/* Psychiatry for Residents: every task, in full. Original content. */
window.TPR_ST=[{"sid":"M4.1","cid":"c-u-01","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Not eating, withdrawn: is it low mood?","who":"Patient","task":"Callum Adeyemi is a 21-year-old male history undergraduate. Two flatmates have brought him to A&E this evening because he has barely eaten for weeks and has shut himself away in his room. They assume he has become depressed. The A&E team have asked the liaison psychiatry service to see him. He has agreed to talk to you and is waiting in a side room. Please speak to Callum, take a focused history to clarify what is going on (including mood, psychotic symptoms, substance use, family history and risk) and form a working diagnosis.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Callum Adeyemi?","p":"Yes. Is this about my body?"},{"d":"Your friends were worried you haven't been eating, so tell me, in your own words, how things have been.","p":"Something's not right. I just don't know what."},{"d":"There's no rush at all.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, open question, why friends brought him, his own view"},{"h":"Eating and self-care","b":"How much, why avoiding food, weight, fluids, washing, sleep"},{"h":"Unusual experiences","b":"Night-time visitors, evidence he relies on, who and why, how certain"},{"h":"Thoughts and perceptions","b":"Thoughts put in via food, voices asked sensitively, perplexity, onset"},{"h":"Mood screen","b":"Low mood, enjoyment, guilt, energy: psychotic depression versus primary psychosis"},{"h":"Background","b":"Cannabis amount, alcohol, past history, uncle's hospital stay, studies"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Self-neglect, suicide, anger at persecutors, police contact, vulnerability"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Reflect back, working diagnosis, next steps, involve friends with consent"}],"bps":{"pre":"Callum sat very still, looked puzzled and answered slowly. He explained that strangers had been coming into his room at night and abusing him, wiping his memory with a 'glove'; he knew because the morning shadows fell in a new place. He ate little because the food 'puts horrible thoughts' in his head. Mood was 'okay'. He smoked cannabis at weekends and an uncle had spent years in a psychiatric hospital. A first episode of psychosis looked most likely.","ask":"So what happens now? Can you find out what they've been doing to me?","lead":"I can see how frightening and exhausting this has been, and we want to help you feel safe and settled again.","bio":["Bloods before treatment: FBC, U&E, LFTs, TFTs, glucose or HbA1c, lipids, prolactin; urine drug screen; weight, BMI, waist, pulse and BP","ECG before an antipsychotic if he is admitted, has cardiac risk factors or the chosen drug needs it","Offer an antipsychotic after discussing side effects, e.g. aripiprazole 5 mg once daily, increasing to 10-15 mg over 1-2 weeks","Monitor weight weekly for 6 weeks, then at 12 weeks with glucose and lipids; check for restlessness and stiffness","Short-term lorazepam 1 mg as needed (max 4 mg/day) only if very distressed or not sleeping"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis: at least 16 one-to-one sessions with a trained therapist in the early intervention team","Family intervention: around 10 sessions over 3-12 months with family or close friends he chooses","Psychoeducation from his care coordinator about psychosis, cannabis and early warning signs","Cannabis harm-reduction work using motivational interviewing"],"social":["Urgent referral to the Early Intervention in Psychosis team (assessment within 2 weeks), with a named care coordinator","Decide with him between home treatment team support and an informal admission, given poor eating","University student support and disability service: extension or interruption of studies","With consent, update his flatmates and family; offer family a carers' assessment","Inform his GP and register for physical health checks"],"safety":["Agree a plan for food and fluids, with daily check-ins from the team","Crisis contact: NHS 111 option 2 or 999 if he feels unsafe or plans to act against the people he fears"]}},{"sid":"M4.2","cid":"c-t-71","area":"Personality and therapies","title":"Frequent attender after an overdose: what's underneath?","who":"Patient","task":"Keira Ashdown is a 22-year-old woman who works shifts in a city-centre bar. She is well known to the A&E team, who have flagged her as a frequent attender. Last night she swallowed twelve 500 mg paracetamol tablets after a row with her boyfriend, then rang an ambulance herself. Her levels were below the treatment line, the medical team have cleared her, and she is now sitting in a side room waiting to go home. The A&E notes mention several earlier visits with cuts to her forearms and one previous small overdose. The liaison psychiatry team have been asked to see her before she leaves. Please speak to Keira, take a history to reach a working psychiatric diagnosis (exploring mood, relationships, self-image, impulsivity, self-harm and possible childhood adversity, and ruling out other conditions) and assess her current risk.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Keira Ashdown?","p":"Yeah. Are you here to tell me off as well?"},{"d":"Not at all. I'm glad you rang for help last night, and I'm glad you're medically okay.","p":"Everyone here's sick of me."},{"d":"I want to hear what life has been like lately. Could we talk for about ten minutes?","p":"Fine."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, non-judgemental stance, acknowledge feeling dismissed, agree time"},{"h":"Last night's overdose","b":"Row trigger, twelve tablets, planning, alcohol, intent, who she told, regret"},{"h":"Self-harm pattern","b":"Cutting, earlier overdoses, what it relieves, frequency, A&E visits"},{"h":"Emotions and relationships","b":"Mood shifts within hours, anger, emptiness, fear of abandonment, intense relationships"},{"h":"Identity and impulsivity","b":"Unstable self-image, spending, binge drinking, drugs, risky sex, dissociation under stress"},{"h":"Rule out other causes","b":"Depressive episode, hypomania, psychosis, PTSD, eating disorder, substance misuse"},{"h":"Background","b":"Childhood adversity, abuse, family history, work, housing, past help, forensic"},{"h":"Risk now","b":"Current suicidal thoughts, access to tablets, protective factors, safeguarding, others at risk"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Working diagnosis gently explained, crisis plan, CMHT referral, GP letter"}],"bps":{"pre":"Keira had begun guarded, expecting another lecture. Over twenty minutes she described feelings that swing from fine to unbearable within an hour, cutting to make the pressure stop, a terror of being left, and never really knowing who she is. There was no sustained low mood, no elation and no psychotic symptoms. She had been hit and shouted at as a child. When I gently named emotionally unstable personality disorder, she went quiet, then looked up.","ask":"So is that it, I'm just broken? Can anything actually help?","lead":"You're not broken: this is a recognised pattern, it usually improves with the right help, and most people get much better over time.","bio":["No medication is licensed or recommended by NICE to treat the personality disorder itself (NICE CG78), so tablets are not the main treatment","Avoid starting a new antidepressant or sedative today; only treat a separate depressive or anxiety disorder if one is clearly present","If a short-term crisis drug is ever needed, a sedating antihistamine such as promethazine 25 mg for up to a week, prescribed in small amounts","GP to prescribe in weekly supplies only, and to review any stockpiled paracetamol or other tablets at home","Check alcohol and drug use; offer brief advice and referral to the local substance service if drinking fuels the overdoses"],"psych":["Dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT): weekly one-to-one plus weekly skills group for about a year, with phone coaching in a crisis","Mentalisation-based treatment (MBT) as an alternative, weekly individual and group sessions over 12-18 months","While waiting, a structured clinical management or STEPPS skills group run by the CMHT personality disorder pathway","Brief psychoeducation now: what EUPD is, that it is common and treatable, plus written information to take away"],"social":["Referral to the CMHT or local personality disorder service, with a named care coordinator","Letter to her GP today with the working diagnosis, risk summary and crisis plan","A shared A&E care plan for frequent attenders, so staff respond consistently and kindly each time","Signpost to Samaritans (116 123), Shout text line (85258) and local crisis café or sanctuary","Ask about safety in the relationship; offer domestic abuse support and safeguarding advice if needed"],"safety":["Written crisis plan agreed with her: warning signs, coping steps, people to call, and NHS 111 option 2 or 999","Remove or limit stored tablets at home; agree that her boyfriend or a friend can hold them"]}},{"sid":"M4.3","cid":"c-t-01","area":"Liaison","title":"Low after a heart attack: teaching a student","who":"Medical student","task":"Graham Whitfield, 63, is recovering on the cardiology ward three weeks after an NSTEMI. You have assessed him for liaison psychiatry. Since the infarct he has felt persistently low, lost all enjoyment, misses playing his guitar, wakes at 4 a.m., eats only when nurses insist and thinks he has lost weight. He blames himself for the heart attack, feels guilty about his wife's long journeys to visit and fears losing his job as an estate agent. He is not engaging with cardiac rehab although his heart is recovering well. He has hypertension and type 2 diabetes, takes amlodipine, metformin, aspirin, clopidogrel, atorvastatin and bisoprolol, drank 3-5 pints nightly and smokes 30 a day. He denies suicidal thoughts. Priya, a final-year medical student, is shadowing you. Please present the case to her, explain the likely diagnosis and management, and answer her questions.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Is it Priya?","p":"Yes, hi. I'm with the team this week."},{"d":"Great. I've just seen Mr Whitfield on cardiology. I'll present him, then talk through diagnosis and plan.","p":"Perfect."},{"d":"Stop me with questions at any point.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Introduce, agree structure: presentation, diagnosis, plan, questions"},{"h":"Presenting complaint","b":"63, three weeks post NSTEMI, low mood, poor rehab progress"},{"h":"Depressive symptoms","b":"Anhedonia, early waking, appetite and weight loss, guilt, self-blame"},{"h":"Background and risk","b":"Hypertension, diabetes, drugs, alcohol 3-5 pints, 30 cigarettes, no suicidal thoughts"},{"h":"Diagnosis and differentials","b":"Moderate depressive episode; adjustment disorder, delirium, hypothyroidism, medication effects"},{"h":"Why it matters","b":"Depression after MI worsens rehab and cardiac outcomes"},{"h":"Management plan","b":"Sertraline, CBT, cardiac rehab, alcohol and smoking, work worries"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, check her understanding, answer questions, suggest reading"}],"bps":{"pre":"I presented Mr Whitfield's story: three weeks after his heart attack, low and flat, waking at 4 a.m., eating little, blaming himself and worrying about work. Priya noticed his heavy drinking and smoking and asked about thyroid and beta-blockers. We agreed this was a moderate depressive episode, not just adjustment, and that no suicidal thoughts were present today.","ask":"So how would you actually treat him while he's still on the cardiology ward?","lead":"Good question: we treat the depression actively, because it is common after a heart attack and slows recovery.","bio":["Bloods: TFTs, B12, folate, LFTs, GGT, U&E, HbA1c; review ECG and QTc before starting an antidepressant","Sertraline 50 mg once daily (best evidence after MI); review at 2 weeks and consider 100 mg after 4-6 weeks","On aspirin plus clopidogrel, sertraline raises bleeding risk: add a PPI such as lansoprazole 15-30 mg and check sodium","Avoid tricyclics and citalopram at high dose because of cardiac effects","Nicotine replacement (patch plus short-acting product) agreed with cardiology; thiamine if alcohol intake confirmed heavy"],"psych":["Behavioural activation and low-intensity CBT on the ward, then Talking Therapies long-term conditions pathway","High-intensity CBT, 16-20 weekly sessions, if moderate symptoms persist","Brief alcohol intervention now, with motivational interviewing about drinking after discharge","Cardiac rehab psychology: graded activity goals linked to things he values, like music"],"social":["Cardiac rehab nurses: restart a paced programme with clear, small targets","Employer and occupational health: fit note, phased return, reasonable adjustments to reduce job fear","Hospital alcohol liaison nurse and the stop smoking service before discharge","Ward manager: let his wife bring in his guitar; offer her a carers' assessment","GP letter with plan and follow-up for mood"],"safety":["Repeat suicide risk review: older man, physical illness, alcohol; reassess if mood worsens","Give him and his wife NHS 111 option 2 for any crisis after discharge"]}},{"sid":"M4.4","cid":"c-u-02","area":"Anxiety, OCD, trauma and dissociation","title":"Worried sick about her health","who":"Patient","task":"Bernadette Kowalski is a 52-year-old woman referred to the CMHT by her GP, Dr Ellison, because of persistent fears that she has a serious illness. Over the past few years she has had X-rays, ultrasound and CT scanning, all of which were normal. Most recently she became convinced her skin was turning yellow; nobody else could see any change and her liver tests came back normal. She still feels something is being missed. Please take a brief history from her, explain the likely diagnosis and outline a management plan, answering her questions.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Bernadette Kowalski?","p":"Yes. I don't know why I've been sent here."},{"d":"Thank you for coming. Your GP tells me you've been very worried about your health, and the tests haven't given answers.","p":"That's exactly it."},{"d":"Could you tell me what has worried you most, then we'll talk about how we can help?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge worry about referral, agree plan"},{"h":"Her health fears","b":"Current fear, yellow skin, liver tests, which illness, how certain"},{"h":"Pattern over time","b":"Earlier fears, scans, doctor visits, internet searching, body checking, reassurance"},{"h":"Impact and mood","b":"Work, family, sleep, low mood, panic, worry generally"},{"h":"Exclude other causes","b":"Delusional certainty, depression, OCD, physical symptoms, family illness history"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts, alcohol, self-medicating, harm from repeated tests"},{"h":"Diagnosis and plan","b":"Health anxiety explained kindly, CBT, single GP, limit testing"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, leaflet, follow-up, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"Mrs Kowalski described years of fearing cancer, then liver disease after she thought her skin looked yellow. She checked her eyes in the mirror many times a day, searched symptoms online at night and felt calm only for hours after normal results. She could accept that the tests were reassuring but felt 'it could still be missed'. There was no depression or psychosis. I explained health anxiety.","ask":"So if it's not my liver, what do I do with all this worry?","lead":"The worry is real and exhausting, and there is a treatment that works well for exactly this.","bio":["No medication first-line: health anxiety responds best to CBT","If symptoms are severe, or depression or OCD coexists, offer sertraline 50 mg once daily, reviewed at 2-4 weeks, up to 200 mg","Warn about early jitteriness and nausea in the first 1-2 weeks of an SSRI","Agree with the GP to avoid repeat tests unless there are new objective findings"],"psych":["CBT for health anxiety: 12-16 weekly sessions via NHS Talking Therapies or CMHT psychology","Work on cutting checking, internet searching and reassurance seeking, with a diary of each urge","Psychoeducation on the worry-check-relief cycle and how normal body sensations are misread","Guided self-help book on health anxiety while waiting"],"social":["One named GP with regular planned appointments, rather than urgent appointments when anxious","With consent, involve her partner so the family stops giving repeated reassurance","Employer: time for therapy sessions; Anxiety UK for peer support","CMHT letter to GP and to hospital teams flagging the plan for tests"],"safety":["If low mood or hopelessness develops, contact GP or NHS 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"M4.5","cid":"c-t-02","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Clozapine for my son: explaining to mum","who":"Mother","task":"Daniel Mensah, aged 25, has paranoid schizophrenia and is on an acute male ward for the second time. After eight weeks he remains unwell: he believes government ministers read his messages and tamper with his bank account, hears other patients commenting on him, and is often verbally aggressive and sometimes violent. Aripiprazole helped first time but he relapsed despite taking it fairly well. Olanzapine this admission did not help, and haloperidol 10 mg twice daily has not either. He also takes clonazepam 1 mg four times daily and promethazine 25 mg twice daily. The ward round has decided to start clozapine, cross-titrating from haloperidol. His mother, Abena Mensah, has asked to meet you. Daniel has agreed you may share his treatment details. Please explain the plan to her and answer her questions.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists on Daniel's ward. Are you Mrs Mensah?","p":"Yes. Abena."},{"d":"Thank you for coming in. Daniel has said I can talk to you about his treatment.","p":"Good, because nobody tells me anything."},{"d":"I'm sorry it's felt that way. Can I hear your worries first, then explain the new plan?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent from Daniel confirmed, acknowledge her frustration"},{"h":"Her understanding","b":"What she knows, how Daniel seems, her main worries"},{"h":"Why a change","b":"Two antipsychotics failed, treatment-resistant schizophrenia, clozapine works best"},{"h":"Starting clozapine","b":"12.5 mg, slow rise, haloperidol reduced, monitoring service registration"},{"h":"Blood tests and checks","b":"Weekly FBC 18 weeks, ECG, troponin, temperature, pulse, BP"},{"h":"Side effects","b":"Drooling, constipation, sleepiness, weight, fever, sore throat, chest pain"},{"h":"Her role","b":"Spot warning signs, smoking changes, carers' support, questions"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, check understanding, leaflet, next meeting, contacts"}],"bps":{"pre":"Mrs Mensah was tearful: Daniel had shouted at her on the last visit and still believed ministers were reading his texts. She had read online that clozapine was 'the last resort' and could 'kill your blood'. She knew he had tried three medicines. I explained treatment-resistant schizophrenia and why the team felt clozapine offered him the best chance.","ask":"But is it safe? What are you going to do to keep him safe on it?","lead":"That's exactly the right question, and safety checks are built into every step of clozapine.","bio":["Register with the clozapine monitoring service; baseline FBC with neutrophils, U&E, LFTs, lipids, glucose, ECG, weight, troponin and CRP","Clozapine 12.5 mg on day 1, increasing by 25-50 mg a day as tolerated to about 300 mg/day over 2-3 weeks; haloperidol tapered as clozapine rises","FBC weekly for 18 weeks, fortnightly to 1 year, then monthly; troponin and CRP weekly for 4 weeks to catch myocarditis","Pulse, BP and temperature daily during titration; plasma level aiming for at least 350 micrograms/L","Clonazepam reduced carefully because benzodiazepines with clozapine can depress breathing; laxative (e.g. senna plus docusate) from the start","Hyoscine 300 micrograms at night if drooling; if more than 48 hours of doses are missed, restart from a low dose"],"psych":["Family intervention for Daniel and his mother: about 10 sessions over 3-12 months","CBT for psychosis once more settled, at least 16 sessions","Psychoeducation for Daniel and his mother about clozapine, its side effects and relapse signs"],"social":["Carers' assessment for Mrs Mensah and a local carers' support group (e.g. Rethink Mental Illness)","Named nurse and ward pharmacist to meet her with Daniel to answer medicine questions","Care coordinator to plan discharge with clozapine clinic follow-up near home","Tell the team if Daniel starts or stops smoking: it changes clozapine levels sharply"],"safety":["Seek urgent help for fever, sore throat or flu-like illness, chest pain or breathlessness, or no bowel opening for 3 days","Ward phone number and NHS 111 option 2 after discharge"]}},{"sid":"M4.6","cid":"c-u-03","area":"Mania and bipolar","title":"Demanding his records in A&E","who":"Patient","task":"Marcus Lindqvist is a 34-year-old man who walked into A&E today asking to see his full medical records immediately. He is not known to mental health services. Nursing staff say he is loud, restless and behaving in an unusual way, and wonder whether he has a mental illness. They have asked the mental health liaison team to assess him. Please carry out a mental state assessment of Marcus, describing what you observe and eliciting key symptoms, then present your findings.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Marcus Lindqvist?","p":"That's me, and I'm very busy, Doctor."},{"d":"Thanks for seeing me. I understand you want your records; I'd like to help with that.","p":"Finally, someone sensible!"},{"d":"Can we sit for a few minutes first so I understand what's been going on?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, align with his request, keep calm, set short time"},{"h":"Appearance and behaviour","b":"Clothing, restlessness, overfamiliar, eye contact, rapport, distractibility"},{"h":"Speech","b":"Rate, pressure, volume, hard to interrupt"},{"h":"Mood and affect","b":"Elated or irritable, labile, subjective mood"},{"h":"Thought form and content","b":"Flight of ideas, grandiose plans, why records, persecutory ideas"},{"h":"Perceptions and cognition","b":"Voices, special messages, orientation, attention"},{"h":"Insight and risk","b":"Sleep, spending, driving, substances, aggression, vulnerability"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Present findings, likely mania, safe next steps"}],"bps":{"pre":"Marcus was dressed in a bright suit, paced the cubicle and spoke rapidly, jumping between topics. He said he needed his records to prove he had been 'chosen' to run a new national health app and had not slept for three nights. He was cheerful but snapped when interrupted, and admitted spending thousands this week. There were no voices. I presented a likely manic episode.","ask":"Right, so can I go now? I've got investors waiting.","lead":"I can see how much energy you have for this, and I want to make sure you don't lose everything you've worked for.","bio":["Bloods and urine drug screen to exclude organic or drug causes: FBC, U&E, LFTs, TFTs, calcium, glucose; consider CT head if neurological signs","Stop any antidepressant he may be taking","Offer an antipsychotic, e.g. olanzapine 10 mg at night (range 5-20 mg), with baseline weight, lipids, glucose and ECG","Short-term lorazepam 1 mg as needed (max 4 mg/day) for agitation or no sleep","Once settled, discuss long-term mood stabiliser such as lithium"],"psych":["Brief explanation of mania once calmer, without arguing about his plans","After recovery, psychological therapy for bipolar disorder focusing on relapse signs and routines","Family or partner involvement in psychoeducation, with his consent"],"social":["Decide between home treatment team and admission; Mental Health Act assessment if he refuses and risk is high","Protect finances: bank card limits, partner help, consider Lasting Power of Attorney later","DVLA: advise him not to drive while unwell and to notify","Employer: fit note and later phased return; CMHT care coordinator on discharge"],"safety":["Risks: overspending, sexual disinhibition, conflict, driving; plan supervision accordingly"]}},{"sid":"M4.7","cid":"c-s-02","area":"Depression and suicide risk","title":"Mum comes instead: collateral on low mood","who":"Mother","task":"Chloe Brennan is a 21-year-old woman referred to the CMHT by her GP with low mood and not eating properly. She did not attend her first appointment today, but her mother, Siobhan Brennan, has come in her place, worried. Chloe has given written consent for the team to speak with her mother. Please take a collateral history from Mrs Brennan about Chloe's difficulties, covering mood, eating, functioning, background and risk, and answer her questions about what happens next.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Mrs Brennan, Chloe's mum?","p":"Yes. She wouldn't come, so I did."},{"d":"Thank you. Chloe has agreed I can talk with you. Anything private she has told us stays private.","p":"That's fine."},{"d":"Could you tell me what you've noticed and what worries you most?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent from Chloe, confidentiality limits, why mum came"},{"h":"Main concerns","b":"What changed, when, triggers, why she didn't come"},{"h":"Mood symptoms","b":"Crying, interest, sleep, energy, concentration, hopeless talk"},{"h":"Eating and weight","b":"Meals skipped, weight loss, body image, purging, exercise"},{"h":"Functioning","b":"University, friends, self-care, staying in room"},{"h":"Background","b":"Past history, family mood problems, alcohol, drugs, personality"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Self-harm, suicidal talk, tablets at home, vulnerability, mum's coping"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, next steps, carers' support, crisis numbers"}],"bps":{"pre":"Mrs Brennan described three months of Chloe staying in her room, crying, missing lectures and eating only toast. She had lost about a stone. She said Chloe did not talk about her weight or shape. Last week Chloe had said 'everyone would be better off without me', which frightened her. There was a family history of depression in her father.","ask":"What can you actually do for her if she won't even come in?","lead":"You've done the right thing coming, and there's a lot we can do, starting with reaching Chloe in a way she can manage.","bio":["Offer Chloe a home visit or phone review to assess her directly before any medication","Physical checks: weight, BMI, pulse, BP, bloods (FBC, U&E, TFTs, glucose) because of poor intake","If moderate-severe depression is confirmed, sertraline 50 mg once daily; as she is under 25, review weekly for the first 4 weeks","Screen for an eating disorder if weight loss continues"],"psych":["High-intensity CBT or behavioural activation, 16-20 sessions, via CMHT psychology or Talking Therapies","Psychoeducation for mother on depression and how to support without pressure","Family sessions if Chloe agrees"],"social":["CMHT duty worker to contact Chloe within days and offer flexible appointments","University wellbeing service and support to defer if needed","Carers' assessment and support for Mrs Brennan","Letter to GP with plan"],"safety":["Lock away or remove spare tablets at home","If Chloe talks of ending her life with a plan: 999, A&E or NHS 111 option 2; Samaritans 116 123"]}},{"sid":"M4.8","cid":"c-u-04","area":"Depression and suicide risk","title":"Sitting in the armchair: psychotic depression","who":"Patient","task":"Stuart Halloran is a 54-year-old man just admitted to the acute ward from the home treatment team, who had been treating him for depression. They were very worried about his self-neglect and minimal eating and drinking, and struggled to get into his flat. He had stopped taking his medicines regularly and spent most days in a soiled armchair in his lounge. He has recurrent depression that has needed ECT before. He has hypertension. His medicines are sertraline 150 mg daily, mirtazapine 15 mg at night and ramipril 5 mg daily. Please assess his mental state and present your findings.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Stuart Halloran?","p":"(Long pause) ...Yes."},{"d":"Thank you, Stuart. I'm sorry things have been so hard at home.","p":""},{"d":"I'd like to understand how you're feeling. Take as long as you need with each answer.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, slow pace, warmth, allow silences"},{"h":"Appearance and behaviour","b":"Self-care, hydration, slowed movements, eye contact, stupor signs"},{"h":"Speech and mood","b":"Latency, quiet, monosyllabic; subjective and objective mood"},{"h":"Biological symptoms","b":"Eating, drinking, sleep, weight, energy, diurnal variation"},{"h":"Thought content","b":"Guilt, worthlessness, nihilistic beliefs, poverty or illness delusions"},{"h":"Perceptions and cognition","b":"Accusing voices, smells, orientation, pseudodementia"},{"h":"Risk and insight","b":"Suicide, food refusal, neglect, medicines adherence, understanding"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Present findings, psychotic depression, urgent physical plan"}],"bps":{"pre":"Stuart was unshaven, dry-lipped and barely moved. Answers came after long pauses in a whisper. He said his insides had 'rotted away', so food was pointless, and that he deserved punishment for ruining his family. He heard a voice calling him worthless. He had stopped his tablets because 'nothing can help a dead man'. I presented severe depression with psychotic features and high physical risk.","ask":"Why bother? Nothing can be done for someone like me.","lead":"I know it feels that way, Stuart, but this kind of depression does get better, and you've recovered before.","bio":["Urgent physical checks: U&E, creatinine, CK, FBC, glucose, LFTs; fluid and food charts, daily weights, NEWS; VTE risk assessment","Restart sertraline 150 mg once supervised and add an antipsychotic, e.g. olanzapine 5 mg at night, increasing to 10 mg","ECT promptly given past response and life-threatening refusal of food and fluid","Pre-ECT: ECG, bloods, anaesthetic review; hold ramipril if dehydrated with renal decline, liaise with medics","Consider lithium augmentation later, with levels 0.4-0.8 mmol/L"],"psych":["Daily supportive contact from his named nurse, short and frequent","Once improving: CBT or behavioural activation, 16-20 sessions","Relapse prevention work and early warning signs with family"],"social":["Mental Health Act assessment if he refuses treatment and lacks capacity or risk is high","Care coordinator and home treatment team to plan discharge and follow-up within 72 hours","Social worker: flat cleaning, environmental health if needed, benefits check","Involve family as carers with consent; carers' assessment"],"safety":["Enhanced observation level for suicide and food refusal; remove means on the ward"]}},{"sid":"M4.9","cid":"c-t-03","area":"Anxiety, OCD, trauma and dissociation","title":"Afghanistan memories that won't settle","who":"Patient","task":"Major Owen Fairbairn is a 45-year-old British Army officer referred by his GP to the CMHT. He is struggling to come to terms with events he experienced on operational deployment in Afghanistan some years ago. He has agreed to an assessment but is uneasy about seeing psychiatry. Please take a history from Major Fairbairn to reach a diagnosis, covering the trauma, current symptoms, comorbidity and risk, and explain your conclusions.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Major Owen Fairbairn?","p":"Owen's fine. I'm not sure I need to be here."},{"d":"Thank you for coming, Owen. Lots of people feel like that at first.","p":""},{"d":"You only need to share what you're comfortable with. Could you tell me what's been troubling you?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, normalise reluctance, control over detail shared"},{"h":"The trauma","b":"Brief outline only, when, threat to life, guilt, no forced detail"},{"h":"Re-experiencing","b":"Flashbacks, nightmares, intrusive images, triggers, physical reactions"},{"h":"Avoidance and numbing","b":"Places, news, talk, detachment, loss of interest, memory gaps"},{"h":"Hyperarousal","b":"Sleep, irritability, startle, hypervigilance, concentration, anger"},{"h":"Comorbidity","b":"Depression, alcohol use, drugs, guilt, physical injuries"},{"h":"Risk and function","b":"Suicide, firearms access, anger at home, work, marriage"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"PTSD explained, treatment works, next steps"}],"bps":{"pre":"Owen spoke briefly about a roadside explosion in which two of his soldiers died. Since then he had nightmares most nights, jumped at loud noises and avoided fireworks and news coverage. He felt cut off from his wife, drank half a bottle of whisky most nights to sleep and blamed himself. He denied current suicidal plans and no longer kept weapons at home. I explained PTSD.","ask":"So is there anything that actually works for this, or do I just live with it?","lead":"Yes: PTSD is very treatable, and the main treatment is a specific talking therapy rather than tablets.","bio":["No medication first-line: trauma-focused therapy works best","If he prefers medication or therapy is not possible, sertraline 50 mg once daily, increasing to up to 200 mg; or venlafaxine","Avoid benzodiazepines; do not start prazosin routinely","AUDIT score and LFTs, GGT; offer alcohol reduction plan, with thiamine if drinking heavily"],"psych":["Trauma-focused CBT: 8-12 weekly sessions of about 90 minutes with a trained therapist","Or EMDR: 8-12 sessions, guided eye movements while recalling the memory","Brief alcohol intervention and motivational interviewing","Psychoeducation on PTSD for him and his wife"],"social":["Op COURAGE, the NHS veterans' mental health service, or Defence Medical Services mental health if still serving","Combat Stress and SSAFA for peer and family support","Employer or chain of command: adjustments while in treatment, with his consent","GP letter; partner support and a carers' assessment"],"safety":["Crisis plan: Combat Stress 24-hour helpline or NHS 111 option 2; avoid alcohol when distressed"]}},{"sid":"M4.10","cid":"c-t-04","area":"Depression and suicide risk","title":"Found on the railway platform at night","who":"Patient","task":"Lukas Petrov is a 30-year-old man brought into A&E by British Transport Police late last night. A rail worker had become concerned about how he was acting near the edge of a crowded platform. He is medically fit and has now slept a few hours. You are covering liaison psychiatry out of hours. Please carry out a suicide risk assessment with him, covering the events, intent, background, protective factors and current risk, and agree a safe plan.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Lukas Petrov?","p":"Yes."},{"d":"Thank you for talking with me, Lukas. I hear the police brought you in last night.","p":"I guess so."},{"d":"Could you help me understand how things got to that point?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, explain role, private space, non-judgemental start"},{"h":"Last night","b":"What happened, planning, final acts, alcohol, intent, who intervened"},{"h":"Current intent","b":"Regret, wish to die now, plans, access to means"},{"h":"Lead-up and mood","b":"Triggers, depression, hopelessness, sleep, psychosis screen"},{"h":"Background risk","b":"Past attempts, self-harm, family suicide, substances, illness"},{"h":"Protective factors","b":"Family, children, faith, future plans, help-seeking"},{"h":"Risk formulation","b":"Static, dynamic, protective; overall risk and plan"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Agree safety plan, follow-up, family, crisis numbers"}],"bps":{"pre":"Lukas said he went to the platform intending to step in front of an express train after losing his job and being left by his partner. He had written a note on his phone. A rail worker spoke to him and he froze. He still felt hopeless, was unsure he was glad to be alive, drank heavily and had no friends locally. His sister in Leeds was supportive.","ask":"So what happens to me now? Are you going to lock me up?","lead":"You're not in trouble, Lukas; our aim is to keep you safe and get you the right help, ideally with you agreeing to the plan.","bio":["Breath or blood alcohol, bloods including LFTs; repeat assessment once sober","No new medication tonight; if a depressive episode is confirmed, sertraline 50 mg once daily with small supplies","Avoid giving large quantities of any medicine; review existing tablets at home","Thiamine and alcohol withdrawal monitoring (CIWA-Ar) if he drinks daily"],"psych":["Collaborative safety plan written with him before he leaves A&E","Follow-up psychological therapy such as CBT for depression, 16-20 sessions, after crisis care","Brief alcohol intervention and referral to local alcohol service"],"social":["Given high risk: informal admission or home treatment team with daily visits; MHA assessment if he refuses and risk remains high","With consent, contact his sister to stay with him or be involved","Jobcentre, benefits and debt advice through a social worker or Citizens Advice","GP letter and follow-up within 72 hours of discharge"],"safety":["Crisis numbers: NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, 999 if in danger","Avoid the railway and remove saved means; reduce alcohol"]}},{"sid":"M4.11","cid":"c-u-05","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Hostel worried he's relapsing after a switch","who":"Patient","task":"Patrick Osei is a 38-year-old man with established paranoid schizophrenia, brought to A&E by staff from the supported hostel where he lives. They say he has been hearing voices and acting strangely for about a week and fear a relapse. He is under the local CMHT, and his antipsychotic was recently changed from risperidone to aripiprazole 10 mg daily. Please carry out a mental state assessment of Patrick and present your findings, including risk.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Patrick Osei?","p":"Yes. They made me come."},{"d":"Thanks for staying to talk. The hostel staff were worried about you.","p":"They're worried about nothing."},{"d":"Then help me understand how things have been for you this week.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, why here, rapport, non-confrontational"},{"h":"Appearance and behaviour","b":"Self-care, guarded, responding to voices, restlessness, eye contact"},{"h":"Speech and mood","b":"Form, rate, affect, irritability, low mood"},{"h":"Thought content","b":"Persecutory beliefs, who, why, certainty, thought interference"},{"h":"Perceptions","b":"Voices: number, person, commands, content, visual experiences"},{"h":"Medication and triggers","b":"Aripiprazole switch, adherence, side effects, cannabis, stress"},{"h":"Cognition, insight, risk","b":"Orientation, relapse insight, harm to self or others, neglect"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Present findings, relapse after switch, next steps"}],"bps":{"pre":"Patrick was dishevelled, watchful and kept glancing at the corner. He said two male voices talked about him and told him the hostel manager was poisoning his food. He felt restless since the change, had missed several doses and was sleeping badly. He denied plans to hurt anyone but was angry. I presented a psychotic relapse following the antipsychotic switch.","ask":"Is it the new tablet's fault? What are you going to do?","lead":"It may well be linked to the change, and we can sort out the medicine and support you through this.","bio":["Bloods and urine drug screen; ECG; check for physical causes of acute change","If risperidone previously kept him well, restart it (e.g. 2 mg, rising to his previous effective dose, often 4 mg) and stop aripiprazole","Or, if he prefers aripiprazole, increase to 15 mg then up to 30 mg, ensuring adherence","Check akathisia from aripiprazole; propranolol 10 mg three times a day if present","Lorazepam 1 mg as needed (max 4 mg/day) for acute distress"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis, at least 16 sessions, once settled","Psychoeducation on the switch and early warning signs","Relapse prevention plan shared with hostel staff"],"social":["Contact CMHT care coordinator and consultant about the switch","Home treatment team with hostel support, or admission if risk to others rises","Hostel key worker to supervise medicines with consent","Benefits and housing review if placement is at risk"],"safety":["Risk plan for anger towards hostel manager; crisis line NHS 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"M4.12","cid":"c-s-03","area":"Medication, emergencies and side effects","title":"The tablets are 'driving her mad'","who":"Patient","task":"Yetunde Clarke is a 31-year-old woman with long-standing paranoid schizophrenia on an acute inpatient unit. Three weeks ago her antipsychotic was switched from aripiprazole to haloperidol. She is now refusing it, saying it is 'driving her mad', and a colleague has not been able to persuade her to take it. Please explore her concerns about the medicine, identify the main problem and discuss possible solutions with her.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Yetunde Clarke?","p":"Yes, and I'm not taking it."},{"d":"That's fine, I'm not here to force anything. I'd really like to understand what's been happening.","p":"Finally."},{"d":"Could you describe what the new tablet does to you?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, reassure no force, invite her concerns"},{"h":"Her concerns","b":"What 'driving me mad' means, start date, other worries"},{"h":"Akathisia features","b":"Inner restlessness, pacing, can't sit, legs, distress, sleep"},{"h":"Other side effects","b":"Stiffness, tremor, sedation, weight, sexual, menstrual changes"},{"h":"Mental state and risk","b":"Psychosis now, agitation, self-harm or suicidal thoughts"},{"h":"Explain akathisia","b":"Name it, common, treatable, not her fault"},{"h":"Solutions together","b":"Lower dose, switch, propranolol, mirtazapine, review timeline"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Agree plan, teach-back, review date, ward contact"}],"bps":{"pre":"Yetunde said that since the switch she felt a terrible inner jitteriness, could not sit through meals and paced the corridor at night. Her legs 'won't stop'. She was frightened she was going mad and had thought she would rather die than feel like this, though she had no plan. Psychotic symptoms were quieter. I explained akathisia.","ask":"So are you going to make me keep taking this poison?","lead":"No: this is a known side effect, it isn't your fault, and we have good ways to fix it together.","bio":["Rate with the Barnes Akathisia Rating Scale and recheck after each change","Reduce the haloperidol dose, or switch to an antipsychotic with lower akathisia risk such as quetiapine or olanzapine, agreed with her","Propranolol 10 mg three times daily, increasing to 20-40 mg three times daily if needed; check pulse, BP and asthma history","Or mirtazapine 15 mg at night; short-term clonazepam 0.5 mg if severe","Anticholinergics like procyclidine help stiffness but are not first choice for akathisia"],"psych":["Psychoeducation on akathisia and shared decision-making about her antipsychotic","Adherence therapy sessions with her named nurse","Daily check-ins to monitor distress and restlessness"],"social":["Ward pharmacist to review options and give written information","Named nurse and ward round informed of the agreed plan","Advocacy (IMHA if detained) if she wants support","Involve family with her consent"],"safety":["Akathisia can increase suicidal thoughts and impulsive acts: tell staff at once if urges increase"]}},{"sid":"M4.13","cid":"c-s-04","area":"Anxiety, OCD, trauma and dissociation","title":"OCD: what it is and how to treat it","who":"Patient","task":"Lance Corporal Ravi Mistry is a 29-year-old soldier recently signed off on medical leave because he spends hours repeatedly washing his hands and his uniform. Your consultant assessed him last month and diagnosed obsessive-compulsive disorder; his Yale-Brown score was 25 out of 40, in the severe range. He is back in the outpatient clinic today wanting to understand the diagnosis and his treatment options. Please briefly explain OCD and talk through the treatments available, answering his questions. History-taking and risk are not needed today.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Ravi Mistry?","p":"Yes, Lance Corporal. Ravi's fine."},{"d":"Thanks, Ravi. I know you saw my consultant last month. What did you understand from that visit?","p":"That I've got OCD, but not much else."},{"d":"Let's go through what it means and how we treat it.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, his understanding, agree focus on diagnosis and treatment"},{"h":"What OCD is","b":"Obsessions, compulsions, anxiety cycle, not weakness, common"},{"h":"His score","b":"Yale-Brown 25, severe, why combined treatment is recommended"},{"h":"Psychological treatment","b":"CBT with exposure and response prevention, sessions, homework"},{"h":"Medication","b":"Sertraline or fluoxetine, higher doses, 12 weeks, side effects"},{"h":"If not improving","b":"Second SSRI, clomipramine, specialist service"},{"h":"Work and family","b":"Military mental health, return to duty, family not reassuring"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, leaflet, OCD-UK, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Ravi said he knew his washing was 'over the top' but felt contaminated after touching anything in public. He had read OCD meant he was 'weak'. I explained how obsessions trigger anxiety and washing brings brief relief, which keeps the cycle going. He nodded and said this exactly matched his days.","ask":"So how do I get rid of it? Do I need tablets or therapy?","lead":"For OCD as strong as yours, the best results come from using both together.","bio":["Sertraline 50 mg once daily, increasing every few weeks up to 200 mg; OCD often needs higher doses","Or fluoxetine 20 mg once daily, up to 60 mg","Allow at least 12 weeks at a good dose before judging; continue 12 months after response","Warn about early nausea, jitteriness and sexual side effects; do not stop suddenly","If two SSRIs fail, clomipramine with ECG and specialist advice"],"psych":["CBT with exposure and response prevention: over 10 therapist hours, usually 12-20 weekly sessions","Graded exposure hierarchy, from touching door handles to wearing uniform without rewashing","Homework practice between sessions; family taught not to give reassurance","Intensive or home-based ERP if clinic-based therapy is not enough"],"social":["Defence Medical Services mental health team (DCMH) for therapy and occupational advice","Chain of command: phased return to duty with adjustments, with his consent","OCD-UK and OCD Action for information and peer support","Letter to his GP and military medical officer"],"safety":[]}},{"sid":"M4.14","cid":"c-s-05","area":"Anxiety, OCD, trauma and dissociation","title":"Sudden terrors out of nowhere","who":"Patient","task":"Adrian Szabo is a 34-year-old male quantity surveyor seen in a general adult outpatient clinic. His GP referral says he has been having sudden, overwhelming episodes of terror that are ruining his quality of life. Please take a history of these episodes, covering their features, triggers, avoidance, physical and substance causes, mood and risk, with the aim of reaching a diagnosis, and explain it to him.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Adrian Szabo?","p":"Yes. Thanks for seeing me."},{"d":"Your GP mentioned some frightening episodes. Could you talk me through the most recent one?","p":"It was on the train last week. I thought I was dying."},{"d":"That sounds awful. Let's go through it step by step.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, invite the most recent episode"},{"h":"The attacks","b":"Onset, peak in minutes, palpitations, breathless, dizzy, fear of dying"},{"h":"Pattern and triggers","b":"Out of the blue or cued, frequency, night attacks"},{"h":"Anticipatory fear and avoidance","b":"Worry about next attack, trains, crowds, agoraphobia"},{"h":"Physical and substance causes","b":"Thyroid, heart, caffeine, alcohol, cocaine, medications"},{"h":"Other conditions","b":"Depression, generalised anxiety, social anxiety, PTSD, OCD"},{"h":"Risk and impact","b":"Work, relationships, suicidal thoughts, alcohol to cope"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Panic disorder explained, next steps"}],"bps":{"pre":"Adrian described attacks for six months: pounding heart, choking, tingling hands and a certainty he would die, peaking within ten minutes. Several came out of the blue, some at night. He now dreaded the next one, avoided crowded trains and drank five coffees a day. A&E heart tests had been normal. I explained panic disorder.","ask":"So it's not my heart? How do I stop these attacks?","lead":"Your heart tests are reassuring, and panic disorder responds very well to the right treatment.","bio":["Check TFTs, FBC, glucose and review the A&E ECG","No benzodiazepines: they worsen long-term outcome","If medication is wanted, sertraline 25 mg once daily for a week then 50 mg, up to 150-200 mg; warn about early increase in anxiety","If an SSRI fails after 12 weeks, imipramine or clomipramine with specialist advice","Cut caffeine gradually and limit alcohol"],"psych":["CBT for panic: 7-14 hours in total, usually weekly 1-2 hour sessions over 4 months","Interoceptive exposure to feared body sensations and gradual return to trains","Guided self-help using CBT workbooks via NHS Talking Therapies first if preferred"],"social":["Self-referral to NHS Talking Therapies","Employer: flexible commuting or hours during treatment","Anxiety UK and No Panic for peer support and helplines","Letter to GP"],"safety":[]}},{"sid":"M4.15","cid":"c-t-05","area":"Depression and suicide risk","title":"Three antidepressants and still low","who":"Patient","task":"Clarissa Oduya is a 45-year-old woman whose GP has treated her for a depressive episode for eight months. She has tried fluoxetine for three months, then citalopram for three months, and has been on mirtazapine for the last two months. She also started a course of talking therapy. She remains depressed and has been referred to you in the CMHT. Please take a history to judge whether this is treatment-resistant depression, and outline suitable next steps in management with her.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Clarissa Oduya?","p":"Yes. I'm starting to think nothing works."},{"d":"I'm sorry, that sounds really disheartening. Thank you for coming.","p":""},{"d":"I'd like to go through how you've been and what you've tried, then talk about next steps.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, empathy, agree plan"},{"h":"Current symptoms","b":"Mood, sleep, appetite, energy, enjoyment, psychotic or bipolar features"},{"h":"Treatments so far","b":"Each drug: dose, duration, adherence, side effects, response; therapy sessions"},{"h":"Other factors","b":"Alcohol, thyroid, pain, life stresses, relationships, work, personality"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts, plans, past attempts, stockpiled tablets"},{"h":"Diagnosis","b":"Treatment-resistant depression: two adequate trials failed"},{"h":"Next steps","b":"Optimise, augment, switch, high-intensity therapy, ECT if severe"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, follow-up, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"Clarissa said her mood had barely lifted. Fluoxetine and citalopram were each taken at 20 mg for three months; mirtazapine was 30 mg. She had attended four therapy sessions before dropping out. She drank little, had no manic episodes and denied plans to end her life, though often wished she could sleep and not wake. I explained treatment-resistant depression.","ask":"So what's left to try for someone like me?","lead":"There are quite a few good options left, and many people improve after a change like this.","bio":["Check TFTs, FBC, B12, folate, U&E, LFTs, calcium; review alcohol and any physical illness","Optimise first: increase mirtazapine to 45 mg at night if tolerated","Or augment with aripiprazole 2-5 mg daily (up to 10 mg) or quetiapine 150-300 mg at night; or lithium aiming for 0.4-0.8 mmol/L with U&E, TFTs, ECG","Or switch to venlafaxine 75 mg, rising to 150-225 mg, with BP checks; or combine an SSRI with mirtazapine","If severe and unresponsive, discuss ECT"],"psych":["High-intensity CBT or IPT, 16-20 sessions, completed properly this time","Behavioural activation to rebuild routine and activity","Combined medication and therapy recommended for more severe depression"],"social":["CMHT care coordinator or regular outpatient follow-up","Employer and occupational health: adjustments or fit note","Benefits advice if off work; family support with consent","GP shared care letter for any monitoring"],"safety":["Safety plan; NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123; limit tablet supplies if risk rises"]}},{"sid":"M4.16","cid":"c-u-06","area":"Mania and bipolar","title":"Lithium: should I switch?","who":"Patient","task":"Ewan Castellano is a 27-year-old man seen in the CMHT for a medication review. He was diagnosed with bipolar affective disorder at 23 and has had five admissions for mania. Quetiapine and then sodium valproate did not prevent frequent manic and depressive relapses. He left hospital six weeks ago after his latest manic episode, treated with olanzapine, which he still takes. He is well but troubled by marked weight gain and tiredness. He has good insight, avoids triggers such as poor sleep, stress, drugs and alcohol, and always takes his medicines and attends. He wants to consider alternatives. Please discuss lithium treatment with him and answer his questions.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Ewan Castellano?","p":"Yes, that's me."},{"d":"Good to see you looking well, Ewan. I hear the olanzapine's caused weight gain and tiredness.","p":"Two stone. I hate it."},{"d":"Let's talk about lithium as an option and go through your questions.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, praise stability, acknowledge weight and tiredness"},{"h":"His view","b":"What he knows about lithium, worries, goals"},{"h":"Why lithium","b":"Best evidence for preventing relapse, reduces suicide risk"},{"h":"Starting checks","b":"U&E, eGFR, TFTs, calcium, weight, ECG if cardiac risk"},{"h":"Starting and levels","b":"400 mg at night, 12-hour level, aim 0.6-0.8 mmol/L"},{"h":"Side effects and toxicity","b":"Thirst, tremor, weight; toxicity signs, NSAIDs, dehydration"},{"h":"Long-term monitoring","b":"Levels 3-monthly, kidneys and thyroid 6-monthly, stopping risks"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, booklet and card, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Ewan said he felt well and wanted to stay that way, but the olanzapine had added two stone and made him sluggish at work. He had read lithium was 'toxic' and worried about blood tests. We agreed he had good insight, takes medicines reliably and that quetiapine and valproate had not worked. I explained lithium is the best-proven medicine for preventing bipolar relapse.","ask":"How would starting lithium actually work, and what are the risks?","lead":"It's a careful, step-by-step process with regular checks, and many people do very well on it.","bio":["Baseline U&E, eGFR, TFTs, calcium, weight, BMI; ECG if heart disease or risk factors","Lithium carbonate (prescribe by brand, e.g. Priadel) 400 mg at night; level 12 hours post-dose after 1 week and after each change, target 0.6-0.8 mmol/L","Levels weekly until stable, then every 3 months in year one, then 6-monthly; U&E, eGFR, TFTs, calcium every 6 months","Taper olanzapine slowly over weeks once lithium is at a therapeutic level","Warn about thirst, passing more urine, fine tremor, weight; avoid NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors and diuretics without advice"],"psych":["Psychoeducation group for bipolar disorder: relapse signs, sleep and stress","Structured psychological therapy for bipolar disorder to strengthen relapse prevention","Written relapse signature and action plan shared with his family"],"social":["Care coordinator and GP shared-care agreement for lithium monitoring","Lithium patient pack: booklet, record book and alert card","DVLA: check current rules for bipolar disorder and notify as required","Employer: time off for blood tests"],"safety":["Toxicity signs: vomiting, diarrhoea, coarse tremor, unsteadiness, confusion; stop and seek urgent help","Keep well hydrated in hot weather or illness; do not stop lithium suddenly"]}},{"sid":"M4.17","cid":"c-s-07","area":"Depression and suicide risk","title":"ECT for Dad: daughter is unhappy","who":"Daughter","task":"Raymond Kowalczyk is a 64-year-old man admitted because his psychotic depression has returned. He has not responded to two courses of high-dose antidepressants, lithium augmentation or an antipsychotic. Over three days he has refused all food and fluid, his creatinine is rising and there is concern about kidney injury and blood clots. He no longer speaks and appears to be in a depressive stupor. The consultant has recommended ECT; the team has judged that he lacks capacity to consent. His daughter, Natalie, is unhappy with this and has asked to see you alone. Please explore her concerns and explain why ECT is advised, its side effects and the alternatives.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists on your dad's ward. Are you Natalie Kowalczyk?","p":"Yes. I'm not happy about this ECT."},{"d":"Thank you for telling me directly. It's right that you ask questions.","p":"I just don't want them frying his brain."},{"d":"I understand. Can you tell me what worries you most, then I'll explain our thinking?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge distress, explore her concerns first"},{"h":"Her concerns","b":"Fears, images from films, past experiences, Dad's wishes"},{"h":"Why now","b":"Stupor, no food or fluid, rising creatinine, clot risk"},{"h":"What ECT involves","b":"Anaesthetic, brief seizure, twice weekly, 6-12 sessions"},{"h":"Side effects","b":"Headache, confusion, memory loss, anaesthetic risk"},{"h":"Consent and law","b":"Lacks capacity, Mental Health Act, SOAD, advance decisions"},{"h":"Alternatives","b":"Other medicines, fluids and feeding, slower and risky"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, her role, next steps, written information"}],"bps":{"pre":"Natalie said she had seen ECT in an old film and was terrified it would damage her father's memory permanently. She said he had never mentioned ECT before. She was shocked to hear he had not drunk anything for three days and his kidney tests were worsening. She accepted he had tried many tablets.","ask":"If not ECT, what else is there? What are you going to do for him?","lead":"We want the safest and fastest route back for your dad, and right now ECT is the most effective option we have.","bio":["ECT twice weekly, usually 6-12 sessions, under short general anaesthetic with a muscle relaxant","Pre-ECT: ECG, U&E, FBC, chest check, anaesthetic review; review or pause lithium (it can prolong seizures and cause confusion)","Meanwhile: IV or subcutaneous fluids, daily U&E, VTE prophylaxis and stockings, pressure-area care","Warn of headache, muscle aches, short-term confusion and memory gaps, usually improving within weeks","Alternatives: further medicine changes (e.g. switch antidepressant or antipsychotic) are slow and risky while he is not eating or drinking"],"psych":["Family meeting with the consultant and ECT nurse; written ECT leaflet for Natalie","Once recovered, psychological therapy and relapse prevention work","Cognitive testing before and during the course to track memory"],"social":["Mental Health Act: as he lacks capacity, a Second Opinion Appointed Doctor (SOAD) must approve; emergency use only if life-threatening","Check for any advance decision refusing ECT, which must be respected","IMHA advocate for Raymond; carers' assessment for Natalie","Involve Natalie in reviews after each session"],"safety":["Daily physical monitoring for dehydration and kidney injury; transfer to medical care if he deteriorates"]}},{"sid":"M4.18","cid":"c-s-09","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Saying odd things: checking first-rank symptoms","who":"Patient","task":"Kieran Abara is a 25-year-old man seen by the Early Intervention in Psychosis team. Over the past year he has become increasingly withdrawn, left his university course and begun saying strange things to his mother, who prompted the referral. Please speak to Kieran with the aim of eliciting Schneider's first-rank symptoms of schizophrenia, asking sensitively about each one. You do not need to complete a risk assessment or explore other diagnoses.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Kieran Abara?","p":"Yeah."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Kieran. Your mum was worried you've been having a hard time this year.","p":"She worries too much."},{"d":"Maybe. I'd like to hear about it from you. Has anything unusual been happening?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, rapport, open question about the past year"},{"h":"Auditory hallucinations","b":"Thoughts aloud, voices arguing, running commentary"},{"h":"Thought alienation","b":"Insertion, withdrawal, broadcasting"},{"h":"Passivity experiences","b":"Feelings, impulses, actions or body controlled by outside force"},{"h":"Delusional perception","b":"Normal perception, sudden new delusional meaning"},{"h":"Clarify each symptom","b":"Examples, conviction, onset, distress, own words"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Reflect back, explain next steps, involve mum with consent"}],"bps":{"pre":"Kieran described hearing a man's voice describing everything he did, and two voices arguing about him. He said thoughts were placed in his head by a tech company and that others could hear his thinking. When a traffic light turned red he 'just knew' he had been chosen for an experiment. He felt confused and exhausted.","ask":"So what's wrong with me? Can you make it stop?","lead":"What you're going through sounds really frightening, and we have good treatments to help these experiences settle.","bio":["Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFTs, TFTs, glucose or HbA1c, lipids, prolactin; urine drug screen; weight, BMI, BP; ECG if indicated","Offer an antipsychotic after discussing side effects, e.g. aripiprazole 5 mg, increasing to 10-15 mg once daily","Weekly weight for 6 weeks, then glucose, lipids and weight at 12 weeks","Consider MRI brain if atypical features or neurological signs"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis: at least 16 sessions","Family intervention with his mother: about 10 sessions over 3-12 months","Psychoeducation on psychosis and warning signs from his care coordinator"],"social":["EIP care coordinator for up to 3 years","Education and employment support (Individual Placement and Support) to return to study or work","Carers' assessment and support for his mother","Benefits advice if needed; GP physical health checks"],"safety":["Crisis contacts: EIP duty line, NHS 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"M4.19","cid":"c-t-06","area":"Liaison","title":"Refusing insulin: does he have capacity?","who":"Patient","task":"Geoffrey Amankwah is a 56-year-old man with paranoid schizophrenia and type 2 diabetes on a medical ward at a teaching hospital. He often does not take his diabetes medicines and has eye, kidney and vascular complications. Three days ago he was found collapsed in the street and admitted with a hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state; after emergency care he is now stable. The endocrinology consultant has told him he needs insulin, but he has refused every dose. The ward has asked liaison psychiatry to decide whether he has capacity to refuse insulin. Please assess his capacity to make this decision.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Geoffrey Amankwah?","p":"Yes. Is this about the injections?"},{"d":"It is. The diabetes team asked me to talk with you about your decision.","p":"I've already told them no."},{"d":"That's your right to say. I'd just like to understand your reasons.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, explain purpose, respect autonomy"},{"h":"His understanding","b":"Diagnosis, why insulin is advised, what happened when collapsed"},{"h":"Retaining information","b":"Repeat key facts back later in conversation"},{"h":"Using and weighing","b":"Risks of refusing: coma, death, complications; benefits, alternatives"},{"h":"Communicating a decision","b":"Clear consistent choice, any form"},{"h":"Mental state influence","b":"Delusions about insulin, mood, cognition, delirium"},{"h":"Capacity conclusion","b":"Decision-specific, time-specific, MCA two-stage test, document"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Explain outcome, alternatives, next steps, team liaison"}],"bps":{"pre":"Geoffrey could explain that he had high sugar and nearly died, and repeated the risks the diabetes team had described. He refused insulin because he believed the nurses had put a tracking chip in the vials to control his thoughts. He could not weigh the risks against this belief. He was otherwise oriented. I concluded he lacked capacity for this decision at this time.","ask":"So can they force me to have the injections now?","lead":"No one will rush you; we'll first look for an approach you can accept, and every step will follow the law.","bio":["Best-interests plan under the Mental Capacity Act, least restrictive option first, agreed with the endocrinology team","Review antipsychotic: olanzapine or clozapine worsen glucose; consider aripiprazole 10 mg once daily if switch is appropriate","Ask the diabetes team about acceptable alternatives (e.g. oral agents such as gliclazide) if he would take tablets","Capillary glucose four times daily, HbA1c, U&E; reassess capacity daily as his mental state changes"],"psych":["Explore his beliefs gently; build trust with consistent staff","Diabetes specialist nurse education using simple written information","Motivational approach to long-term diabetes self-care once more settled"],"social":["Involve family or friends in best-interests decisions; IMCA if he has nobody appropriate","Inform CMHT care coordinator; plan for district nurse support after discharge","Consider Mental Health Act assessment if his psychosis needs treatment in its own right","Legal or Court of Protection advice if restraint would be needed for insulin"],"safety":["Document capacity assessment clearly; if he regains capacity and still refuses, respect his decision and record risks"]}},{"sid":"M4.20","cid":"c-t-07","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"First psychosis: his mum's questions","who":"Mother","task":"Owen Pritchard, a 21-year-old man, was admitted to an adult acute ward this week. He left university six months ago after falling behind, became withdrawn, stopped washing, would only eat food he had made himself and was heard muttering. His parents woke one night to him shouting; he was holding a knife and had cut his upper arm. A&E sutured the wound and the liaison team arranged admission. On today's ward round he explained he was digging out a microchip that MI5 had planted to control his thoughts, as the archangel Gabriel had told him, and that he has spoken with the angel for months. The consultant thinks this is psychosis and has started olanzapine. Owen agrees to information being shared. Please speak to his mother, Bethan Pritchard, about his diagnosis and answer her questions and concerns.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists looking after Owen. Are you his mum, Mrs Pritchard?","p":"Yes, Bethan. Nobody's told me anything."},{"d":"I'm sorry it has felt that way, Bethan. Owen has said I can talk openly with you, so I can explain everything.","p":""},{"d":"Could I first hear what you've noticed at home, and what worries you most?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent from Owen confirmed, acknowledge her shock, agree plan"},{"h":"Her account and worries","b":"Withdrawal since university, self-care, food fears, muttering, the night with the knife"},{"h":"What psychosis means","b":"Hearing and believing things others don't, the microchip belief, Gabriel's voice"},{"h":"Possible causes","b":"Stress-vulnerability, cannabis or drugs, family history, not her fault, schizophrenia possible later"},{"h":"Checks on the ward","b":"Bloods, drug screen, ECG, consider MRI brain if atypical"},{"h":"Treatment and olanzapine","b":"Why olanzapine, sleepiness, weight gain, bloods, weeks to work"},{"h":"Risk and safety","b":"Self-harm risk, the knife, removing sharps at home, leave plan"},{"h":"Early Intervention and outlook","b":"EIP team, CBTp, family intervention, many recover well"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, carers' support, leaflet, ward number, consultant meeting"}],"bps":{"pre":"Bethan described months of Owen hiding in his bedroom, refusing her cooking and talking quietly to someone who wasn't there. She cried describing the night she found him bleeding with a kitchen knife. I explained that his belief about the microchip and the angel's voice are symptoms of psychosis, which is an illness of the brain and not a choice. She held her coat on her lap and asked:","ask":"Will he get better, and what are you actually going to do for him?","lead":"Many young people recover well from a first episode like this, and here is how we will help Owen get there.","bio":["Olanzapine 5 mg at night, increased slowly to 10 mg if needed; NICE suggests the lowest effective dose in first episode","Baseline and 12-week weight, waist, BP, HbA1c, fasting lipids, then yearly; ECG at baseline as part of ward checks","Warn about sleepiness and increased appetite; dietitian and activity support from the start to limit weight gain","Full bloods, urine drug screen, HIV and syphilis serology; MRI brain if neurological signs or atypical features","Continue the antipsychotic for at least 1-2 years after recovery before any planned slow reduction"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis: at least 16 sessions with a trained therapist, exploring the beliefs and voices at his own pace","Family intervention: around 10 sessions over 3-12 months with Owen and his parents, on communication and relapse signs","Psychoeducation for Owen and family on early warning signs and building a relapse prevention plan","Help with cannabis or other drugs if he uses them, as this greatly raises relapse risk"],"social":["Early Intervention in Psychosis team for up to 3 years with a named care coordinator","Carers' assessment for Bethan and her husband, plus Rethink or Mind carer groups","Individual Placement and Support (IPS) and university student support to help Owen return to studies","Ward family meetings and a clear plan before any leave or discharge"],"safety":["Remove knives and sharps safely before home leave; ward will review risk before each leave","If he talks of harming himself or acting on the voices at home, call the ward, the crisis line or 999"]}},{"sid":"M4.21","cid":"c-u-07","area":"Anxiety, OCD, trauma and dissociation","title":"Nose surgery requests: what's going on?","who":"Patient","task":"Adeel Rashid, a 22-year-old man who works in a phone shop, has been sent to the community mental health team by a cosmetic surgeon in private practice, who is uneasy because Adeel has repeatedly asked for an operation to reshape his nose, though there is no visible problem to correct. He has attended several consultations and keeps returning for another opinion. He has agreed to see a psychiatrist. Please take a history of Adeel's concerns about his appearance, including the relevant risk issues, and think about the likely diagnosis.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Adeel Rashid?","p":"Yeah. I don't really know why I'm here."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Adeel. Mr Hale, the surgeon, asked me to see you. It sounds like your nose causes you a lot of distress.","p":"It ruins everything."},{"d":"Could you tell me about it, and how it affects your days?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, why referred, non-judgemental, his own words about the nose"},{"h":"The concern itself","b":"What he sees, onset, hours per day preoccupied, how certain, insight"},{"h":"Checking and hiding","b":"Mirror checking or avoiding, comparing, photos, camouflage, reassurance seeking, skin picking"},{"h":"Impact on life","b":"Work, friends, dating, leaving home, social media use, money spent"},{"h":"Surgery and procedures","b":"Past consultations, previous procedures, expectations, DIY attempts"},{"h":"Mood and other conditions","b":"Depression, OCD, social anxiety, eating disorder, delusional intensity, substances"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts, self-surgery, anger at surgeons, self-harm"},{"h":"Background","b":"Teasing or bullying, family, personality, past help"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Explain body dysmorphic disorder, treatable, next appointment, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"Adeel told me he spends four or five hours a day checking his nose in his phone camera, covers it with his hand in photos and has stopped going to the gym. Three surgeons have turned him down. He admitted thinking life is pointless if it can't be fixed but has no plan. I explained this sounds like body dysmorphic disorder. He frowned and asked:","ask":"So if you won't fix my nose, what are you actually offering me?","lead":"I can't change your nose, but there is good treatment that can make this worry far smaller in your life.","bio":["No medication needed first if mild; CBT is first-line for BDD under NICE","If moderate to severe, or CBT alone not enough: fluoxetine 20 mg each morning, titrated over weeks towards 40-60 mg if tolerated","SSRIs often need higher doses and up to 12 weeks for BDD; review at 2 weeks, warn of initial anxiety and sexual side effects","Under 25: review within 1 week of starting an SSRI because of a small rise in suicidal thinking","Clomipramine as an alternative if an SSRI fails, with ECG; avoid benzodiazepines"],"psych":["CBT with exposure and response prevention for BDD: around 16 weekly sessions of up to 1 hour, often more if severe","Gradually going out without hiding his face and cutting down mirror checking and reassurance-seeking","Building alternative ways of thinking about how others see him; reducing comparison on social media","Specialist BDD service referral if two rounds of treatment have not helped"],"social":["Advise against further cosmetic procedures for now; with consent, write to the surgeon explaining the diagnosis","Care coordinator in the CMHT if moderate or severe","Support to stay in work; GP fit note or occupational health adjustments if needed","BDD Foundation information and peer support"],"safety":["Crisis plan: Samaritans 116 123, NHS 111 option 2, or 999 if he feels he might act on suicidal thoughts","Never attempt any procedure on himself; seek help straight away if urges come"]}},{"sid":"M4.22","cid":"c-t-08","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Why do we die younger? Clozapine and health","who":"Patient","task":"Desmond Achterberg, a 47-year-old man, has paranoid schizophrenia and has taken clozapine for 15 years after two earlier antipsychotics did not work. He has had only mild side effects and his voices and suspicions are well controlled. He lives in supported accommodation, gets on well with staff and is content with his life. He has asked for a clinic appointment because he read in a newspaper that people with severe mental illness die 10-25 years earlier than everyone else. He is shocked and upset, as he sees his GP whenever asked and takes all his tablets, yet no doctor has ever raised this with him. Please explain why this may be true and discuss what he can do to improve his physical health and outlook.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, a psychiatrist in the outpatient team. Are you Desmond Achterberg?","p":"Yes. Des."},{"d":"Thanks for coming in, Des. I hear you read something worrying in the paper. I'm glad you asked us about it.","p":"It really shook me."},{"d":"Could you tell me what you read, then we'll go through it together?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, validate shock, praise him for asking, agree plan"},{"h":"What he read","b":"His understanding, worries, mood since, apology it was not discussed"},{"h":"Is it true","b":"Yes, gap is real, mostly heart disease, diabetes, lung disease, preventable"},{"h":"Why it happens","b":"Smoking, weight, inactivity, diet, clozapine effects, missed screening"},{"h":"His own risks","b":"Smoking, alcohol, weight, constipation, diabetes, BP, family history"},{"h":"What he can change","b":"Stop smoking, activity, diet, sleep, dental care"},{"h":"Monitoring we do","b":"Yearly bloods, weight, BP, ECG, clozapine level, bowel checks"},{"h":"Mood and risk","b":"Low mood since, hopelessness, no reason to stop clozapine"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, GP health check, leaflet, next review"}],"bps":{"pre":"Des had brought the newspaper cutting with him. He told me he smokes 20 a day, has put on weight over the years and rarely leaves the house except to buy cigarettes. He is often constipated. His last bloods showed a borderline HbA1c. I confirmed the statistic is real but said much of it can be changed. He leaned forward and asked:","ask":"Right, so what can I actually do so it doesn't happen to me?","lead":"Quite a lot, Des, and the fact you're asking puts you in a strong position.","bio":["Keep clozapine: it controls his illness well and is linked with lower overall mortality; do not stop suddenly","Stop smoking with varenicline or NRT; warn that clozapine levels can rise sharply when smoking stops, so check a level before and about a week after quitting and reduce the dose if needed","Yearly HbA1c, fasting lipids, weight, waist, BP, LFTs and ECG; Qrisk3 score and statin if indicated","Metformin to limit weight gain if diet and exercise are not enough; consider if HbA1c rising","Constipation plan: regular laxatives such as senna and docusate; report no bowel opening for 3 days, as clozapine bowel obstruction can kill","Full blood count every 4 weeks continues through the clozapine monitoring service"],"psych":["Motivational interviewing approach with his key worker to set small, specific goals","Smoking cessation advisor sessions weekly for around 12 weeks","Healthy living group or structured exercise programme, e.g. a walking group 3 times a week","Problem-solving support to plan meals and activity with accommodation staff"],"social":["Annual severe mental illness physical health check at the GP practice, and a named health lead in the team","Local stop smoking service and free exercise on prescription schemes","Supported accommodation staff help with healthy shopping and cooking","Dental check every 6-12 months and bowel cancer, eye and other routine screening as offered"],"safety":["Seek urgent help for severe abdominal pain, vomiting, fever, sore throat or chest pain"]}},{"sid":"M4.23","cid":"c-u-08","area":"Medication, emergencies and side effects","title":"Stopping sertraline: what's the real problem?","who":"Patient","task":"Shona Gallagher, a 28-year-old woman, has been referred to the community mental health team by her GP. She began sertraline a month ago for depression and is now on 100 mg once daily. Her GP's mood scores show she has improved, so the GP was surprised when she asked to stop it because she 'doesn't like the side effects'. She would not say more to her GP, despite a good relationship, and asked to see a psychiatrist instead. Please speak to Shona, explore her concerns about sertraline and agree a plan with her.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Shona Gallagher?","p":"Yes, hi."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Shona. Dr Kaur said you'd like to stop sertraline. I'm keen to understand why.","p":""},{"d":"Some side effects are awkward to talk about. Everything here is confidential, and nothing will shock me. Is that okay?","p":"Okay. It's a bit embarrassing."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, normalise awkward topics, confidentiality, agree aim"},{"h":"Her side effects","b":"Sexual problems: libido, arousal, orgasm; also nausea, sweating, sleep"},{"h":"Impact on her","b":"Relationship, partner, self-esteem, how it began"},{"h":"Mood now","b":"Response so far, current symptoms, past episodes, suicidal thoughts"},{"h":"Other causes","b":"Contraception, alcohol, relationship strain, depression itself, pregnancy plans"},{"h":"Options for her","b":"Wait and see, lower dose, switch to mirtazapine or vortioxetine"},{"h":"Stopping safely","b":"Discontinuation symptoms, taper slowly, relapse risk, never stop suddenly"},{"h":"Shared decision","b":"Her preference, written plan, GP letter"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, review date, leaflet, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"Shona eventually explained that since starting sertraline she has been unable to reach orgasm and has lost interest in sex. Her boyfriend thinks she has gone off him, and they argue. Otherwise she feels much brighter, is sleeping and back at work, with no suicidal thoughts. She looked relieved but anxious and asked:","ask":"So do I have to choose between being depressed and having a sex life?","lead":"No, you don't, Shona. There are a few good ways round this.","bio":["Do not stop abruptly: sertraline should be continued for at least 6 months after recovery to cut relapse risk","Option 1: reduce sertraline to 50 mg once daily and review mood in 2-4 weeks","Option 2: switch to mirtazapine 15 mg at night (to 30 mg); fewer sexual side effects but weight gain and sleepiness","Option 3: switch to vortioxetine 10 mg daily, which causes less sexual dysfunction; nausea is common early on","If switching, cross-taper over 1-2 weeks; warn of discontinuation symptoms like dizziness, electric-shock feelings and irritability","Pregnancy test if relevant, and check contraception; review in 2 weeks after any change"],"psych":["Continue guided self-help or Talking Therapies CBT to protect against relapse","Psychoeducation about how common sexual side effects are and that they usually reverse","Couples or psychosexual counselling via GP if relationship strain continues","Agree a written relapse plan listing her early warning signs"],"social":["NHS Talking Therapies self-referral for CBT","Letter to her GP with the agreed plan and dose changes","Partner can attend a future appointment if she wishes","Relate or Brook online information on sex and relationships"],"safety":["If mood drops or suicidal thoughts return: GP same day, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, or 999 in emergency"]}},{"sid":"M4.24","cid":"c-u-09","area":"Neurodevelopmental","title":"New editor missing deadlines: is it ADHD?","who":"Patient","task":"Imogen Faulkner, a 37-year-old woman, is a journalist who was a highly regarded features writer until her promotion to editor last year. Since then her output has dropped and she keeps missing deadlines. Her occupational health service has referred her to the adult outpatient clinic. Please take a focused history to reach an initial diagnosis. You do not need to discuss further investigations or management in detail, but she will want to know what happens next.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Imogen Faulkner?","p":"Yes. Imogen."},{"d":"Thank you for coming, Imogen. Occupational health mentioned work has been tough since your promotion.","p":"That's an understatement."},{"d":"Could you tell me what's been happening, in your own words?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, reason for referral, her own account"},{"h":"Current difficulties","b":"Focus, finishing tasks, organising, forgetting, procrastination, restlessness, impulsivity"},{"h":"Why now","b":"Editor role: planning, managing others, less structure; previous coping strategies"},{"h":"Since childhood","b":"School reports, symptoms before 12, two settings, collateral from parent"},{"h":"Impact across life","b":"Work, relationships, finances, driving, home organisation"},{"h":"Other explanations","b":"Depression, anxiety, bipolar, burnout, thyroid, sleep, alcohol, cannabis, stimulants"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Mood, suicidal thoughts, driving accidents, impulsive spending"},{"h":"Background","b":"Family history ADHD, medical history, BP, heart problems"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Likely adult ADHD, further assessment, questionnaires, next steps"}],"bps":{"pre":"Imogen described a lifelong pattern: school reports saying 'bright but daydreams', losing keys daily, starting ten things and finishing none. As a writer she coped with adrenaline and last-minute deadlines, but the editor role means juggling a team and long-term plans. Her mood is fine and she drinks little. I told her this fits adult ADHD. She smiled tiredly and asked:","ask":"If that's what it is, can anything actually be done?","lead":"Yes, quite a lot, and it usually helps people a great deal once the diagnosis is confirmed.","bio":["Confirm diagnosis first with a full specialist assessment, rating scales (e.g. DIVA) and a school report or parent account","Before medication: BP, pulse, weight, heart history and family history of sudden death; ECG if cardiac concerns","First-line in adults: lisdexamfetamine 30 mg each morning, increasing by 20 mg weekly to max 70 mg; or methylphenidate modified-release","Warn about poor appetite, insomnia, raised pulse and BP; check BP, pulse and weight at each dose change and every 6 months","Atomoxetine as a non-stimulant alternative if stimulants not suitable"],"psych":["ADHD-focused CBT or coaching: around 12 weekly sessions on planning, prioritising and breaking down tasks","Psychoeducation about ADHD for her and her partner","Practical systems: calendars, reminders, list apps, body-doubling, short focused work blocks"],"social":["Reasonable adjustments at work under the Equality Act: quieter space, written instructions, protected focus time","Access to Work application for coaching or software","Inform the DVLA if ADHD affects her driving; tell her insurer","ADHD UK and local adult ADHD support groups"],"safety":[]}},{"sid":"M4.25","cid":"c-s-14","area":"Medication, emergencies and side effects","title":"NMS: telling his mother what happened","who":"Mother","task":"Kwabena Asante, a 24-year-old man with schizoaffective disorder, was admitted after a relapse when he stopped his medication and heard voices telling him to harm others. Haloperidol had worked well for him before with no problems, so it was quickly increased to his old dose of 6 mg. Over the next few days he became confused and agitated, and last night he was rigid, drowsy, febrile at 38.1°C with a pulse of 132. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome was diagnosed, haloperidol stopped and his CK came back at 1022 IU/L. After talking with the medical registrar he went to intensive care, where he is having IV fluids and cooling; the ICU team expect a good recovery. His mother, Abena Asante, has come to the psychiatric unit. He has agreed to full information sharing. Please explain what has happened and the likely next steps.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists from Kwabena's ward. Are you his mother, Mrs Asante?","p":"Yes. What happened to my son?"},{"d":"I can see how frightened you are. Kwabena has asked us to share everything with you, so I will be completely open.","p":""},{"d":"Could I first ask what you've been told so far?","p":"Only that he's in intensive care."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent confirmed, acknowledge fear, check what she knows"},{"h":"What happened","b":"Confusion, stiffness, fever 38.1, fast pulse, ICU transfer"},{"h":"What NMS is","b":"Rare serious reaction to antipsychotics, muscles, temperature, CK 1022"},{"h":"Why it happened","b":"Rapid dose increase, dehydration, agitation; unpredictable, previous tolerance"},{"h":"Treatment now","b":"Haloperidol stopped, IV fluids, cooling, CK and kidneys monitored, good outlook"},{"h":"Next steps","b":"Antipsychotic-free at least 2 weeks, cautious restart, low dose"},{"h":"Her concerns","b":"Was it our fault, complaint route, Duty of Candour, incident review"},{"h":"His mental health meanwhile","b":"Voices, risk to others, ward plan, lorazepam if needed"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, ICU visit, ward contact, consultant meeting"}],"bps":{"pre":"Abena listened with her hands pressed together. I explained that Kwabena had a rare but serious reaction to haloperidol called NMS, that the medicine was stopped straight away and that ICU are pleased with his progress. She was angry the dose had gone up so fast and asked whether he could die. When I said the outlook is good, she asked:","ask":"But he still needs medicine for the voices. What will you give him now?","lead":"We will keep him safe from another reaction and still treat his illness; here is how we'll do it.","bio":["No antipsychotic for at least 2 weeks after NMS resolves; CK, U&Es, temperature, pulse and BP monitored in ICU","Lorazepam 1 mg orally up to four times daily (max 4 mg/24h) for agitation meanwhile; also helpful in NMS","Restart later with a low-potency antipsychotic, e.g. quetiapine 25 mg, titrated very slowly; avoid depots and high-potency drugs","Check temperature, pulse, BP, rigidity and CK regularly in the first weeks of any restart","Record NMS clearly as an adverse reaction in his notes and report via MHRA Yellow Card"],"psych":["Explain to Kwabena what happened once he is well enough, so he understands future risks","Continue CBT for psychosis on the ward once recovered, focused on his voices","Family meeting with the consultant and named nurse to answer questions and plan"],"social":["Datix incident report and serious incident review; Duty of Candour letter to the family","PALS and the complaints route if she wishes to raise concerns","Carers' assessment and support for Abena; ward to keep her updated daily","Care coordinator to share the NMS history with his GP and community team"],"safety":["If he becomes stiff, hot or confused after any restart, staff will stop the drug and get urgent medical help"]}},{"sid":"M4.26","cid":"c-t-09","area":"Liaison","title":"After the crash: a changed son","who":"Mother","task":"Liam Doherty, a 27-year-old man, is an inpatient on a surgical ward after a road traffic collision. The surgical team have asked for a psychiatric review because his behaviour towards staff has worried them. His mother, Maureen Doherty, has concerns about how he has changed and has asked to talk to a psychiatrist. Liam has agreed to his mother being spoken to. Please take a collateral history from her, consider the possible causes of his behaviour and address her worries.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists from the liaison team. Are you Liam's mum, Mrs Doherty?","p":"Yes, Maureen. He's not himself at all."},{"d":"Thanks for waiting, Maureen. Liam's happy for us to talk. The surgeons asked us to see him because of his behaviour.","p":""},{"d":"Could you tell me what Liam was like before the crash, and what's different now?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent from Liam, her worry, aim of conversation"},{"h":"The accident","b":"Head injury, loss of consciousness, length of memory gap, scans, ICU stay"},{"h":"Changes noticed","b":"Irritability, disinhibition, aggression, apathy, confusion, sleep, memory"},{"h":"Before the crash","b":"Personality, work, relationships, mood, past mental health"},{"h":"Alcohol and drugs","b":"Drinking, withdrawal signs, cannabis, cocaine; drink-driving"},{"h":"Other causes","b":"Delirium, pain, infection, opioids, frontal injury, PTSD, depression"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Aggression to staff, self-discharge, self-harm, driving"},{"h":"Her concerns","b":"Will he return to normal, home support, her own stress"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Differentials, next assessment, team liaison, leaflet, contact"}],"bps":{"pre":"Maureen explained that Liam, normally easy-going, has been swearing at nurses, pulling at his cannula and making crude comments to young staff. He was knocked out for some minutes in the crash and remembers nothing of the day. He drinks most evenings. Nurses say he is worse at night. She twisted her rings and asked:","ask":"Is this brain damage? Will I get my son back?","lead":"It's too early to say for sure, but many changes like this improve, and here is how we'll find out and help.","bio":["Delirium screen: bloods (FBC, U&Es, calcium, LFTs, glucose, CRP), urine, oxygen, review opioids and anticholinergic drugs","Repeat CT or MRI brain with the surgical team if new or worsening signs","If alcohol withdrawal: chlordiazepoxide reducing regime by CIWA-Ar score and IV Pabrinex for 3-5 days, then oral thiamine","Avoid antipsychotics where possible; if severe distress and risk, haloperidol 0.5-1 mg orally once ECG checked","Good pain control and treat constipation and urinary retention"],"psych":["Non-drug delirium care: orientation, glasses and hearing aids, familiar faces, quiet nights, consistent staff","Neuropsychology assessment of memory, planning and impulse control once delirium settles","Brain injury rehabilitation with OT and psychology; behavioural plan agreed with ward staff","Alcohol brief intervention and later referral to the alcohol service"],"social":["Headway brain injury charity for Maureen and Liam","Carers' assessment and support for Maureen","DVLA must be told about a significant head injury; no driving until cleared","Employer and fit note; community neurorehabilitation team after discharge"],"safety":["If he tries to leave, ward will assess capacity and the liaison team will advise on the legal framework"]}},{"sid":"M4.27","cid":"c-s-15","area":"Liaison","title":"Non-epileptic seizures: talking to her dad","who":"Father","task":"Aisha Mensah, a 26-year-old woman, has had seizures for a year. Neurology tests, including bloods, brain scans and repeated EEGs, have all been normal. Video-EEG telemetry showed events with eyes open, asymmetric movement of all limbs and preserved awareness during a normal EEG, strongly suggesting non-epileptic attacks. Neurology told her two weeks ago that the seizures were 'psychogenic' and referred her to the neuropsychiatry clinic. She has had mild anxiety and depression treated by her GP but never seen a psychiatrist. She refused to come today, feeling the referral means doctors think she is faking. Her father, Kofi Mensah, has come with her agreement to learn about the diagnosis and the plan. Please explain the diagnosis and address his concerns.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the neuropsychiatry clinic. Are you Aisha's father, Mr Mensah?","p":"Yes, Kofi."},{"d":"Thank you for coming, Kofi. I understand Aisha has agreed for us to talk, but felt unable to come herself.","p":"She thinks you'll say she's putting it on."},{"d":"That's important to hear, and I want to be clear that we believe her seizures are real. Could you tell me what you understand so far?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, Aisha's consent, acknowledge her fear of being disbelieved"},{"h":"His understanding","b":"What neurology said, the word psychogenic, his worries"},{"h":"What the attacks look like","b":"Frequency, triggers, injuries, A&E visits, impact on life"},{"h":"What the diagnosis means","b":"Functional or dissociative seizures, real, not faking, not epilepsy"},{"h":"Why they happen","b":"Brain's software not hardware, stress, anxiety, past trauma possible"},{"h":"Treatment and outlook","b":"Psychotherapy, stopping antiepileptics carefully, many improve"},{"h":"Risk and safety","b":"Mood, self-harm, injuries in attacks, first aid at home"},{"h":"Getting Aisha engaged","b":"Letter, choice of appointment, neurologist joint visit"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, neurosymptoms.org, contact, review"}],"bps":{"pre":"Kofi told me Aisha has two or three attacks a week, has stopped work at the nursery and rarely goes out alone. She was crushed when neurology said 'psychogenic' and has been crying since. I explained that functional seizures are common, real and not deliberate, more like a software glitch than damaged hardware. He nodded slowly and asked:","ask":"So if it's not epilepsy, how do you actually treat it?","lead":"The main treatment is a specific talking therapy, and most people improve once they understand the condition.","bio":["No medication treats functional seizures directly; any antiepileptic drugs should be slowly withdrawn by neurology","Treat co-existing depression or anxiety: e.g. sertraline 50 mg daily, review at 2 weeks and titrate to 100-200 mg if needed","Avoid emergency benzodiazepines for typical attacks; give a written care plan to ambulance service and A&E","Check for other causes she may have: sleep problems, migraine, chronic pain"],"psych":["CBT for functional or dissociative seizures: around 12 weekly sessions with a specialist psychologist","Learning warning signs and grounding techniques to stop or shorten attacks","Exploring stresses and any past trauma at her pace; EMDR or trauma-focused CBT if PTSD","Physiotherapy if there are other functional symptoms such as weakness"],"social":["Neuropsychiatry nurse specialist as a point of contact","FND Hope and neurosymptoms.org for information and peer support","DVLA: no driving until attack-free; she must tell DVLA herself","Employer adjustments and phased return; PIP or ESA if unable to work"],"safety":["During attacks: keep her safe from injury, stay calm, time it; call 999 only if injured or attack is unusually long and not typical","If she talks of self-harm or suicide, contact GP, NHS 111 option 2 or 999"]}},{"sid":"M4.28","cid":"c-s-16","area":"Medication, emergencies and side effects","title":"Periods stopped on risperidone","who":"Patient","task":"Natalia Kowalczyk, a 37-year-old woman with paranoid schizophrenia, has been well in the community for 12 months since leaving hospital, with no current psychotic symptoms. She takes risperidone 6 mg daily without missing doses. Olanzapine had been stopped earlier because of side effects. At her last clinic visit she mentioned problems with her periods over several months, so bloods were sent. Her prolactin is 1100 mIU/L (normal 100-410); before risperidone it was normal. Please take a brief history of her symptoms and discuss a management plan with her in the outpatient clinic.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Natalia Kowalczyk?","p":"Yes, Natalia."},{"d":"Thanks for coming back, Natalia. Your blood results are in, and I'd like to go through them with you.","p":"Okay. Is it bad?"},{"d":"Nothing dangerous, and we can sort it. First, could you tell me more about your periods?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, results back, reassure, agree plan"},{"h":"Period changes","b":"Irregular or absent periods, how long, pregnancy possible, contraception"},{"h":"Other prolactin effects","b":"Breast milk, breast tenderness, libido, sexual problems, acne, hair"},{"h":"Other causes","b":"Pregnancy, pituitary tumour signs: headaches, vision; thyroid, kidney, other drugs"},{"h":"Mental state now","b":"Stable, voices, paranoia, adherence, relapse history, risk"},{"h":"Long-term risks","b":"Bone thinning, fertility, possible breast effects"},{"h":"Options","b":"Reduce dose, switch to aripiprazole, add aripiprazole, endocrine referral"},{"h":"Shared decision","b":"Her preference, relapse plan, monitoring"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, repeat bloods, GP letter, next review"}],"bps":{"pre":"Natalia told me her periods stopped five months ago, and she has noticed milky fluid from her breasts and less interest in sex. She has no headaches or visual problems and uses condoms. She is pleased to be well and fears relapse. I explained that risperidone raises a hormone called prolactin. She asked:","ask":"Do I have to stop the risperidone? I don't want to get ill again.","lead":"No, we won't stop anything suddenly; we'll pick a safe option together that keeps you well.","bio":["Pregnancy test today; TFTs, U&Es, repeat prolactin taken properly (not stressed, ideally morning)","Prolactin over 1000 with symptoms: options are reducing risperidone gradually to 4 mg daily or switching","Switch to aripiprazole: start 5-10 mg daily, cross-taper over 2-4 weeks, target 10-15 mg; or add aripiprazole 5 mg to risperidone","Warn aripiprazole can cause restlessness (akathisia) and nausea; restored fertility means contraception matters","Recheck prolactin about 3 months after any change; MRI pituitary and endocrinology referral if prolactin stays high or headaches/visual change","If amenorrhoea over 6 months, consider DEXA scan for bone density"],"psych":["Relapse prevention work with her care coordinator: early warning signs and action plan during the change","Brief psychoeducation about prolactin and why the switch is worth it","Continue any psychological therapy or family work already in place"],"social":["Care coordinator in the CMHT to see her more often during the switch","Letter to GP about the plan and contraception advice; sexual health clinic if needed","Pharmacist review of all her medicines"],"safety":["If voices or suspicions return during the switch, contact her care coordinator, the crisis line or NHS 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"M4.29","cid":"c-s-17","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Depot injections: his father's questions","who":"Father","task":"Callum Rennie, aged 25, who has paranoid schizophrenia, came onto the ward a few days ago. He lives with his father, Graham Rennie, who noticed weeks of decline. Callum now believes the police are hunting him again and that his father is helping them, so he will not speak to him. He talks loudly to himself, says he is not ill, and is isolating and neglecting himself. He has never been violent when unwell. His prescription is risperidone 6 mg daily, yet his father found unopened boxes in his room. This is his third relapse this year after stopping medication once he feels better, despite advice from his team and family. He has refused all medication on the ward. Callum has agreed to his father being informed. Please explain depot (long-acting injectable) antipsychotics to Graham and answer his questions.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists on Callum's ward. Are you his dad, Mr Rennie?","p":"Yes, Graham."},{"d":"Thanks for coming in, Graham. Callum's said we can talk about his care. I hear you've asked about other options to tablets.","p":"Yes, the tablets just don't work for him."},{"d":"Could you tell me what you've seen at home, and what you already know about injections?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent from Callum, acknowledge his efforts, agree plan"},{"h":"His account","b":"Three relapses this year, unopened boxes, police beliefs, his relationship with Callum"},{"h":"What a depot is","b":"Antipsychotic injection into muscle, slowly released, every 2-4 weeks or longer"},{"h":"Why it can help","b":"No daily tablets, reliable dose, staff notice missed doses early, fewer relapses"},{"h":"Choices of depot","b":"Paliperidone, aripiprazole, risperidone, older depots like flupentixol, zuclopenthixol"},{"h":"Side effects","b":"Injection site pain, stiffness, restlessness, weight gain, prolactin; can't stop quickly"},{"h":"Consent and the law","b":"Callum's choice ideally, capacity, Mental Health Act if needed, CTO"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Self-neglect, vulnerability, his father's safety, Callum's stress"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, carers' support, leaflet, meeting with consultant"}],"bps":{"pre":"Graham described finding boxes of risperidone hidden under Callum's bed and the pain of being called a police informer by his own son. He looked exhausted. I explained that a depot is a long-acting injection, given every few weeks, so Callum would not need to remember daily tablets. He asked:","ask":"That sounds better. How would it work, and what if he says no?","lead":"It can work very well for people like Callum; let me take you through the practical side and the choices.","bio":["As he already responded to risperidone, paliperidone palmitate is a good choice: 150 mg day 1, 100 mg day 8 into deltoid, then 75 mg monthly (adjust 25-150 mg)","Check tolerability first if needed, and stop oral risperidone once loading is complete","Alternatives: aripiprazole LAI 400 mg monthly (needs 14 days oral cover), or older depots such as flupentixol decanoate with a test dose","Monitor movement side effects, weight, BP, glucose, lipids and prolactin at baseline, 3 months, then yearly","Warn depot effects last weeks, so side effects can't be stopped quickly; review dose at each clinic"],"psych":["Discuss with Callum when calmer, using motivational interviewing to link medicine to his own goals","CBT for psychosis: at least 16 sessions to help him make sense of the police beliefs","Family intervention: about 10 sessions with Callum and Graham to rebuild trust and spot early signs"],"social":["Care coordinator and depot clinic nurse in the CMHT; home visits if he misses an injection","Carers' assessment for Graham and local carers' group (e.g. Rethink)","If no capacity and refusing, the Mental Health Act may be used; a Community Treatment Order can be considered on discharge","Support with benefits and daily structure: social worker, IPS employment support"],"safety":["Graham to call the CMHT or crisis line if Callum stops eating, washing or seems at risk; 999 if immediate danger"]}},{"sid":"M4.30","cid":"c-u-10","area":"Depression and suicide risk","title":"Paracetamol overdose: assessing risk in A&E","who":"Patient","task":"Chloe Matthews, a 28-year-old woman who works as a receptionist, was brought to A&E after taking an overdose of paracetamol. She has started treatment and the medical team have asked the liaison psychiatry team to see her now that she is alert. Please take a history of the overdose, assess her risk of further self-harm and suicide, and agree a plan with her.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists from the liaison team. Are you Chloe Matthews?","p":"Yeah."},{"d":"Thanks for seeing me, Chloe. I know you've had a hard time. Is now an okay moment to talk?","p":"I suppose."},{"d":"First I'd like to hear what happened; then we can work out together how to keep you safe. Is that alright?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, privacy, consent to talk, comfort check"},{"h":"Before the overdose","b":"Trigger, planning, final acts, note, alcohol, precautions against discovery"},{"h":"The overdose itself","b":"How many tablets, staggered, other drugs, intent to die, beliefs about lethality"},{"h":"After the overdose","b":"How found, who called help, feelings now, regret"},{"h":"Mental state and history","b":"Depression, psychosis, substances, past self-harm, previous attempts"},{"h":"Risk factors","b":"Hopelessness, plans, access to tablets, support, protective factors, children"},{"h":"Formulation","b":"Static and dynamic factors, needs not scores"},{"h":"Plan together","b":"Medical treatment first, safety plan, follow-up, GP, home situation"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, crisis numbers, follow-up within 72 hours"}],"bps":{"pre":"Chloe told me she bought 32 tablets after a row with her partner, took them all over an hour with wine and texted her sister an hour later, who called 999. She left no note. She now feels embarrassed and says she wants to live for her nephew, though she has felt low for months. She looked up from the drip and asked:","ask":"So what happens now? Can I go home once the drip's done?","lead":"Once the medical team are happy, we can plan going home safely together, with proper help in place.","bio":["Complete the acetylcysteine course and repeat LFTs, INR and paracetamol level before medical clearance","If depressed: sertraline 50 mg daily via GP or team, review in 1-2 weeks; warn of early side effects","Limit supplies: weekly prescriptions; no large quantities of paracetamol in the house","Avoid tricyclics and other drugs that are toxic in overdose"],"psych":["Collaborative safety plan before she leaves: warning signs, coping steps, people to contact","NHS Talking Therapies CBT for depression, around 8-16 weekly sessions","Psychological therapy specific to self-harm if repeated episodes, e.g. 4-10 sessions"],"social":["Follow-up by the crisis or home treatment team within 72 hours of discharge","Letter to GP the same day; GP review within a week","With consent, involve her sister in safety planning","If domestic abuse is a factor, IDVA referral and safeguarding advice"],"safety":["Crisis plan: Samaritans 116 123, NHS 111 option 2, or 999 if she acts on suicidal thoughts","Remove extra tablets from home; sister to hold medicines for now"]}},{"sid":"M4.31","cid":"c-u-11","area":"Depression and suicide risk","title":"4 a.m.: he wants to go home","who":"Patient","task":"Gareth Lloyd, a 45-year-old man, was admitted informally to an adult ward last night after a serious suicide attempt. He tried to hang himself in his loft after writing a note and telling no one; the ligature snapped. Neck pain made him call an ambulance. After medical clearance in A&E, the liaison team found that his wife had recently left with their young children, and he felt hopeless and that life was not worth living. He agreed to come in, but soon after reaching the ward he began asking to discharge himself against advice. It is 4 a.m. Please assess his risk and explain your management plan to Gareth.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists on call tonight. Are you Gareth Lloyd?","p":"Yes. I just want to go home."},{"d":"I hear you, Gareth. I'd like to understand why, and talk it through properly before anything is decided.","p":"There's no point me being here."},{"d":"Can we sit down for a few minutes? Tell me what's changed since you arrived.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge his wish, calm space, agree to talk"},{"h":"Why leave now","b":"Ward environment, shame, specific plan, getting home to act"},{"h":"The attempt","b":"Planning, note, timing to avoid discovery, rope, lethality, regret now"},{"h":"Current intent","b":"Thoughts of dying now, plans, means at home, other methods"},{"h":"Mental state","b":"Depression, hopelessness, sleep, alcohol, psychosis"},{"h":"Risk and protective factors","b":"Separation, children, debts, isolation, firearms, support"},{"h":"Plan and his rights","b":"Stay informally; if he insists, Section 5(2) or nurse's 5(4)"},{"h":"Ward safety","b":"Observation level, ligature risk, belongings search"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Explain decision, morning review by consultant, his questions"}],"bps":{"pre":"Gareth sat by the nurses' office in his coat. He told me the note was to his children and that he went to the loft while his wife was away so no one would find him. He is not sure he is glad to be alive and says he will 'sort it' properly if he goes home. He asked:","ask":"So are you going to let me leave, or not?","lead":"Not tonight, Gareth; I'm worried for your safety, and I'll explain exactly what that means and what happens next.","bio":["If he tries to leave, use Section 5(2) doctor's holding power (up to 72 hours) for a full Mental Health Act assessment","No medication tonight unless distressed; if needed, lorazepam 1 mg orally for anxiety or poor sleep","Antidepressant once depression confirmed: sertraline 50 mg daily or mirtazapine 15 mg at night (safer in overdose than TCAs)","Bloods (FBC, U&Es, LFTs, TFTs), alcohol history and breath test; neck injury reviewed by medics"],"psych":["Daily talks with his named nurse; morning review by consultant","CBT for depression and hopelessness once settled, typically 16-20 sessions","Collaborative safety plan before any leave"],"social":["Enhanced observations, e.g. continuous or intermittent within eyesight; search belongings and remove ligature risks","Social worker for separation, housing and access to children; family mediation later","With consent, contact his brother or friend for support and collateral","Approved Mental Health Professional (AMHP) if Mental Health Act assessment needed"],"safety":["If he tries to leave the ward, staff use the nurse's holding power 5(4) until a doctor arrives","Remove items like belts, laces and cords in line with ward policy"]}},{"sid":"M4.32","cid":"c-t-10","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Handover from his community nurse","who":"Community nurse","task":"Darren Okafor, a 35-year-old man, has just been admitted to an adult acute ward. His community psychiatric nurse, Paul Henshaw, from the community mental health team, has worked with him for the last two weeks and has been worried about how much his mental state has deteriorated. Paul has come to the ward to hand over. Please take a collateral history from Paul about Darren's recent presentation, background and risks, and agree an initial plan.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors on the ward. Are you Paul Henshaw, Darren's CPN?","p":"Yes, that's me."},{"d":"Thanks for coming over, Paul. Darren arrived an hour ago, so your view is really valuable.","p":""},{"d":"Could you give me the main picture first, then I'll ask some specific questions?","p":"Sure."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, role, reason for handover, structured approach"},{"h":"Recent change","b":"Timeline over two weeks, symptoms, voices, paranoia, sleep, self-care"},{"h":"Background","b":"Diagnosis, previous admissions, relapses, what triggers them"},{"h":"Medication","b":"Current antipsychotic, adherence, side effects, last depot, response history"},{"h":"Substances","b":"Cannabis, alcohol, stimulants, recent changes"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Self-harm, suicide, violence, weapons, vulnerability, children, driving"},{"h":"Social situation","b":"Housing, family, finances, employment, Mental Health Act history"},{"h":"Ward plan","b":"Observations, leave, bloods, medication review, contact with CMHT"},{"h":"Hand over and close","b":"Summary back to Paul, next contact, CPA meeting"}],"bps":{"pre":"Paul explained that Darren, diagnosed with schizophrenia eight years ago, stopped his olanzapine three weeks ago, started smoking cannabis again and now believes his neighbours are filming him. He has been sleeping in his car. He threatened a neighbour last week. Paul asked:","ask":"So what's the plan for him while he's in with you?","lead":"We'll keep him safe on the ward and get him back on effective treatment; let me tell you the steps.","bio":["Restart olanzapine 10 mg at night, the dose he responded to before, reviewing within days","If he refuses and lacks capacity, consider treatment under the Mental Health Act; lorazepam 1-2 mg orally for acute agitation","Bloods (FBC, U&Es, LFTs, glucose, lipids), urine drug screen and ECG on admission","Discuss depot with Darren later if non-adherence keeps causing relapses"],"psych":["Engagement with named nurse daily; CBT for psychosis when settled","Cannabis brief intervention and referral to substance misuse service","Family work if family is involved"],"social":["CMHT and care coordinator to stay involved; CPA review before discharge","Housing support or homeless prevention as he has been sleeping in his car","Police and neighbours: share risk information per policy; safeguarding if children around","DVLA: advise not driving while unwell"],"safety":["Observation level set by risk of aggression and absconding; review daily"]}},{"sid":"M4.33","cid":"c-t-11","area":"Depression and suicide risk","title":"Hotel room hanging: is he safe to go?","who":"Patient","task":"Roger Whitfield, a 52-year-old man, was brought to hospital after hotel staff found him collapsed on the floor of his room following an attempt to hang himself. He has been medically cleared. Paramedic notes record no psychiatric or medical history, and his home is a long way from the hotel. He now wants to go home but has agreed to wait to see the liaison psychiatry team. A nurse thinks he seems strangely cheerful given what happened and worries he may be acting well to get discharged. He has cooperated with staff. Please take a history of the attempt and assess his risk.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists from the liaison team. Are you Roger Whitfield?","p":"Yes. I'm fine now, honestly."},{"d":"Thank you for waiting, Roger. I know you'd like to head home. I'd like to understand what happened first.","p":"Fair enough."},{"d":"Could we start with how you came to be at the hotel?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, thanks for staying, his wish to leave, agree plan"},{"h":"Why the hotel","b":"Distance from home, booked for purpose, alone, planned not impulsive"},{"h":"The attempt","b":"Planning, method, ligature, note, final acts, why it failed, alcohol"},{"h":"Now and the brightness","b":"Feelings now, regret, relief of decision made, unresolved plans"},{"h":"Mental state","b":"Depression, biological symptoms, hopelessness, psychosis, alcohol"},{"h":"Life stresses","b":"Debt, job loss, relationship, illness, legal problems, bereavement"},{"h":"Risk and protective factors","b":"Male, middle-aged, isolated, access to means, family, reasons to live"},{"h":"Plan","b":"Not safe to leave, admission informally, Mental Health Act if refuses"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Explain decision, contacts, consultant review"}],"bps":{"pre":"Roger admitted that he lost his business a month ago, owes a large sum and has hidden this from his wife. He booked the hotel 80 miles away so she would not find him, and left letters at home. He calmly said he still sees no way out and feels at peace now. He asked:","ask":"So can I just go home now?","lead":"I'm really glad you told me all of this, Roger, and I'm worried enough that I think you need to stay for now.","bio":["Advise informal admission; if he refuses and is at high risk, Mental Health Act assessment (Section 2)","Bloods (FBC, U&Es, LFTs, TFTs, B12, folate, calcium, glucose); alcohol history and breath test","If depression confirmed: sertraline 50 mg daily, review in 1-2 weeks; mirtazapine 15 mg at night if poor sleep","Check for alcohol withdrawal and give chlordiazepoxide if needed"],"psych":["Supportive talks with his named nurse; CBT for depression once settled","Problem-solving therapy about the debt and business loss","Safety plan before any leave"],"social":["With his consent, contact his wife; she may not know what is happening","StepChange or Citizens Advice debt advice; Money Advice Service","Social worker and AMHP if a Mental Health Act assessment is needed","GP informed the same day; home treatment team if discharge is later considered"],"safety":["Close observation while in A&E; remove ligature risks and belongings","Do not let him leave alone; security to be aware if he tries"]}},{"sid":"M4.34","cid":"c-u-12","area":"Liaison","title":"Out-of-body spells and odd face movements","who":"Patient","task":"Funmilayo Bankole, a 22-year-old woman, has a GP referral to the neuropsychiatry outpatient clinic. For two years she has had short, strange episodes in which she feels she is watching herself from above and that everything around her is unreal. Each lasts a few minutes, and onlookers have noticed unusual movements of her face during them. Please take a focused history and explain what you think the most likely diagnosis is.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in this clinic. Are you Funmilayo Bankole?","p":"Yes. Funmi's fine."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Funmi. Your GP mentioned some unusual episodes. They sound unsettling.","p":"They really are."},{"d":"Could you walk me through one, from the very start?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, validate, focused aim"},{"h":"Describe an episode","b":"Warning signs, rising stomach, smell, déjà vu, fear, out-of-body, unreality"},{"h":"During the episode","b":"Awareness, responsiveness, lip smacking, chewing, hand movements, speech"},{"h":"Afterwards","b":"Confusion, tiredness, headache, memory gap, how long to recover"},{"h":"Pattern","b":"Frequency, duration, triggers, sleep, alcohol, worsening, generalised seizures"},{"h":"Background","b":"Febrile convulsions, head injury, meningitis, family history epilepsy"},{"h":"Other causes","b":"Depersonalisation disorder, panic, migraine, drugs, dissociation"},{"h":"Risk and impact","b":"Driving, swimming, work, injuries, mood"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Likely temporal lobe seizures, tests, neurology, driving"}],"bps":{"pre":"Funmi described a rising feeling in her stomach and a strange smell before each spell. Her flatmate says she stares, smacks her lips and doesn't respond for a minute, then is confused for 20 minutes. She had febrile convulsions as a toddler. I said this sounds like focal seizures from the temporal lobe. She asked:","ask":"Epilepsy? So what will you do about it?","lead":"We'll confirm it with tests and get you to the right treatment, which usually works well.","bio":["Urgent neurology referral, ideally first seizure clinic; sleep-deprived EEG and MRI brain with epilepsy protocol","Bloods: FBC, U&Es, glucose, calcium, magnesium; ECG to exclude cardiac causes","Antiseizure medicine started by neurology, e.g. lamotrigine 25 mg daily titrated slowly, or levetiracetam 250 mg twice daily","Discuss contraception and pregnancy: avoid sodium valproate in women of childbearing potential (MHRA)"],"psych":["Psychoeducation about epilepsy and triggers: sleep, alcohol, stress","CBT if she develops anxiety or low mood related to seizures","Epilepsy nurse specialist sessions for practical coping"],"social":["DVLA: she must stop driving and inform the DVLA until seizure-free per guidance","Epilepsy Action and Epilepsy Society support","Employer adjustments; safety at work around heights or machinery","Showers not baths, and swimming only with supervision"],"safety":["Call 999 if a seizure lasts more than 5 minutes or she is injured"]}},{"sid":"M4.35","cid":"c-s-18","area":"Anxiety, OCD, trauma and dissociation","title":"Home visit: why has he stopped going out?","who":"Patient","task":"Babatunde Oyelaran, a 57-year-old IT consultant, has been referred by his GP for a home visit. His daughter told the GP that for about two months he has grown more and more withdrawn, has stopped buying food and now spends all his time indoors. He still logs in to work remotely. Please visit him at home and take a relevant history to reach a likely diagnosis.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Babatunde Oyelaran?","p":"Yes. Tunde."},{"d":"Thank you for letting me in, Tunde. Your daughter and GP were worried about you, so I came to see how you are.","p":"She worries too much."},{"d":"I'd like to hear from you how things have been lately. Is that alright?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, why visiting, permission, his view"},{"h":"Not going out","b":"When started, what happens when he tries, fear, avoidance, places"},{"h":"Anxiety symptoms","b":"Panic attacks, palpitations, breathlessness, fear of collapse, safety behaviours"},{"h":"Trigger","b":"Panic in shop or train, health scare, bereavement, job change"},{"h":"Other causes","b":"Depression, psychosis, cognitive decline, alcohol, physical illness, heart problems"},{"h":"Self-care and food","b":"Eating, weight, groceries, money, bills"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts, self-neglect, alcohol, isolation"},{"h":"Support and impact","b":"Work from home, family, daughter, friends"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Agoraphobia with panic, treatment options, next contact"}],"bps":{"pre":"Tunde explained that two months ago he had a sudden pounding heart and breathlessness on a crowded train, sure he was having a heart attack. A&E found nothing. Since then he can't face shops or trains and works from home. He orders little food. He has no suicidal thoughts. I said this sounds like panic disorder with agoraphobia. He asked:","ask":"Can this actually be treated, or am I stuck at home?","lead":"This is very treatable, Tunde, and most people get back to going out.","bio":["GP physical check first: ECG, BP, TFTs, FBC, glucose to rule out heart and thyroid causes","If needed: sertraline 25 mg daily for a week, then 50 mg; warn anxiety may briefly worsen; review at 2 weeks","Avoid benzodiazepines for panic disorder (NICE)","Reduce caffeine and alcohol, which worsen panic"],"psych":["CBT for panic disorder: 7-14 hours over 4 months, with exposure to feared places step by step","Guided self-help first, from NHS Talking Therapies, possibly by video at home initially","Breathing and grounding techniques for panic attacks"],"social":["NHS Talking Therapies referral; home or video sessions initially","Daughter involved, with consent, in graded outings and shopping","Online grocery delivery short-term; Anxiety UK and No Panic helpline","Employer adjustments while he recovers"],"safety":["If he feels hopeless or suicidal, contact GP, NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans 116 123"]}},{"sid":"M4.36","cid":"c-s-19","area":"Liaison","title":"Brain tumour found: telling his sister","who":"Sister","task":"Kofi Boateng, a 43-year-old driving instructor, was sent by his GP to the community team because a first psychotic episode was suspected after two months of behaviour change and growing paranoia about his neighbours. A CT head was requested as routine screening before his appointment. It shows a frontal lobe meningioma. The radiologist advises urgent discussion with the patient, has arranged an MRI with contrast and recommends urgent neurosurgical and oncology referral. After talking to your consultant you arranged an urgent appointment. Kofi lives with his mother and his sister, Efua Boateng, who has come alone today at his request and with his consent. Please explain the results to Efua and answer her questions and concerns.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Kofi's sister, Efua Boateng?","p":"Yes. Kofi asked me to come."},{"d":"Thank you, Efua. Kofi has agreed I can share his results with you. Before I do, what do you know so far?","p":"Only that he had a scan."},{"d":"I'm afraid the scan has shown something important. Is it okay to go through it now?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent from Kofi, check understanding, warning shot"},{"h":"The result","b":"Meningioma in frontal lobe, usually non-cancerous, slow-growing"},{"h":"Link to symptoms","b":"Frontal lobe affects behaviour, paranoia may be caused by tumour"},{"h":"Next tests","b":"MRI with contrast arranged, neurosurgery and oncology referral today"},{"h":"Treatment possibilities","b":"Surgery, radiotherapy or watchful waiting, team decides"},{"h":"Her questions","b":"Is it cancer, will he die, telling Kofi and mum, work"},{"h":"Risk and safety","b":"Seizures, driving, worsening symptoms, red flags"},{"h":"Support","b":"Psychiatric support continues, Macmillan, Brain Tumour Charity"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, appointment for Kofi, contact"}],"bps":{"pre":"Efua listened quietly as I explained that the scan showed a meningioma, a growth from the brain's lining that is usually not cancer and grows slowly, and that its position may explain Kofi's suspicious thoughts. She cried, then steadied herself and asked:","ask":"So does this mean it's not mental illness? What happens now?","lead":"It may well be the main cause, and here is what will happen next to treat it.","bio":["MRI brain with contrast arranged urgently; neurosurgery and oncology referral sent today","Possible surgery to remove it; sometimes radiotherapy or monitoring; neurosurgeons decide","Hold off antipsychotics unless distress or risk; if needed, low-dose risperidone 0.5-1 mg after discussion with neurosurgery","Dexamethasone and antiseizure drugs only as directed by neurosurgery"],"psych":["Psychiatric team continue to see Kofi for support and mood","Psychological support for Kofi and family through diagnosis and surgery","Neuropsychology assessment after treatment if changes persist"],"social":["DVLA: Kofi must stop driving and stop teaching; he must inform the DVLA","Macmillan and The Brain Tumour Charity for information and support","Carers' assessment for Efua and mother; employer and fit note","Clinical nurse specialist in neuro-oncology as key contact"],"safety":["Call 999 for a seizure, sudden severe headache, vomiting, new weakness or confusion"]}},{"sid":"M4.37","cid":"c-t-12","area":"Women's and gender health","title":"Gender clinic: first assessment","who":"Patient","task":"Nadia Bright, a 33-year-old who was assigned male at birth and registered as Nathan, comes to the gender identity clinic on a GP referral. The GP writes that she has long felt she was in the wrong body, identifies fully as a woman, has read about surgery and hormones and would like specialist advice. She has no notable physical or mental health problems on record. Please assess her motivation and readiness for gender-affirming care, and explain what happens next.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the gender clinic. What name and pronouns would you like me to use?","p":"Nadia, and she/her."},{"d":"Thank you, Nadia. Your GP referred you to talk about hormones and surgery. I'm here to understand your journey.","p":""},{"d":"Could you tell me about how you've felt about your gender over time?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, pronouns, welcoming, aim"},{"h":"Gender history","b":"Childhood feelings, puberty, persistence, current identity"},{"h":"Social transition","b":"Name, clothing, living as female, who knows, support"},{"h":"Goals of treatment","b":"Hormones, surgery, expectations, fertility, realistic outcomes"},{"h":"Mental and physical health","b":"Mood, anxiety, eating, substances, self-harm, medical conditions"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts, self-medication with online hormones, discrimination, abuse"},{"h":"Support and consent","b":"Family, partner, work, capacity, informed consent"},{"h":"Next steps","b":"Further assessments, endocrinology, fertility, peer support"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, questions, follow-up, contact"}],"bps":{"pre":"Nadia told me she has felt female since early childhood, dressed in her sister's clothes, and has lived fully as Nadia for two years with her partner's support. She has no mental illness and wants hormones and later surgery. She asked:","ask":"So what happens now? Can I start hormones soon?","lead":"Yes, we can plan that; here is the usual path and what we'll check first.","bio":["Oestradiol prescribed after endocrine advice, e.g. transdermal gel or patches (safer for clots than oral), with a GnRH analogue or anti-androgen if testosterone stays high","Baseline bloods: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, lipids, HbA1c, testosterone, oestradiol, prolactin","Warn of clot risk, especially with smoking; recheck hormone levels, lipids and LFTs every 3-6 months in the first year","Discuss fertility and sperm storage before hormones"],"psych":["Optional sessions with the clinic psychologist or counsellor to explore expectations and cope with stigma","Voice and communication therapy with a speech and language therapist, often 6-10 sessions","Discussion of realistic surgical outcomes and recovery, with time to reflect before any surgical referral"],"social":["Gender identity clinic follow-up; a second specialist opinion is needed before genital surgery referral","NHS fertility clinic referral for sperm storage before hormones","Help updating her name and gender on her NHS record via her GP; Gender Recognition Certificate information","Workplace protection under the Equality Act; LGBT Foundation or local trans peer groups"],"safety":["Avoid online hormones; if distressed or suicidal, call GP, NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans"]}},{"sid":"M4.38","cid":"c-t-13","area":"Physical examination","title":"Frontal lobe check after head injury","who":"Patient","task":"Callum Okafor is a 27-year-old man seen in the neuropsychiatry outpatient clinic. Just over a year ago he was badly hurt as a car passenger in a motorway crash and suffered a traumatic brain injury, followed by several months in hospital and rehabilitation. He now lives with his parents. His GP referral says the family find him more impulsive than before and that he makes jokes that upset people, though Callum himself notices little change. Your consultant wants a bedside picture of how his frontal lobes are working before formal neuropsychology testing is booked. Please carry out a focused frontal lobe assessment with Callum and summarise what you find.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Can I check you're Callum Okafor?","p":"Yep, that's me. Callum."},{"d":"Thanks, Callum. I'd like to do some short thinking and movement tasks, some a bit odd, to see how the front of the brain is working since the crash. Is that alright?","p":"Sure, go for it."},{"d":"Some are meant to be tricky, so don't worry about mistakes. Tell me if you need a break.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent, explain odd tasks, hand hygiene, glasses and hearing"},{"h":"Brief history","b":"The crash, changes others notice, sense of smell, jokes, spending, temper"},{"h":"Verbal fluency","b":"F words in one minute, then animals; under ten words is low"},{"h":"Abstract thinking","b":"Proverb meaning, similarities such as table and chair, notice literal answers"},{"h":"Estimates and sequencing","b":"Cognitive estimate question, Luria fist-edge-palm, alternating pattern copy"},{"h":"Response inhibition","b":"Go/no-go tapping, conflicting instructions, watch for perseveration and impulsivity"},{"h":"Primitive reflexes","b":"Grasp, palmomental, pout and snout, note utilisation behaviour"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Thank him, findings, offer smell test, FAB, ACE-III, neuropsychology referral"}],"bps":null},{"sid":"M4.39","cid":"c-t-14","area":"Physical examination","title":"Leg weakness: lower limb neuro check","who":"Patient","task":"Gillian Mbatha is a 43-year-old woman on a medical ward who suddenly lost strength in her left leg a week ago. Stroke was first suspected, but the neurologists have found no structural cause on scans and tests and have asked liaison psychiatry to see her. Their letter adds that she has just been through a painful and hostile divorce. When you reach the ward, her named nurse tells you Gillian feels the leg has become even weaker since this morning. Please carry out a full neurological assessment of both of her lower limbs, explaining each step as you go.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors working with the medical team. Are you Gillian Mbatha?","p":"Yes. The nurse said you'd come."},{"d":"I'm sorry the leg feels worse today. That must be frightening. I'd like to check your legs carefully, the nerves, muscles and reflexes. Is that alright?","p":"If it helps, yes."},{"d":"Is anything painful right now? I'll ask a nurse to stay with us as a chaperone.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge worse leg, consent, chaperone, pain, expose legs with dignity"},{"h":"Inspection and gait","b":"Wasting, fasciculation, scars, posture; gait only if safe, dragging pattern"},{"h":"Tone","b":"Roll the legs, quick knee lift, ankle clonus, compare sides"},{"h":"Power","b":"Hip, knee, ankle by root; Hoover's sign, hip abductor sign, give-way"},{"h":"Reflexes","b":"Knee and ankle jerks with reinforcement, plantar responses"},{"h":"Coordination","b":"Heel down the shin, note effort and consistency"},{"h":"Sensation","b":"Light touch, pinprick by dermatome, vibration, joint position, non-anatomical patterns"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Thank her, findings, Hoover's result, feed back to neurology, gentle explanation"}],"bps":null},{"sid":"M4.40","cid":"c-u-13","area":"Physical examination","title":"Chest pain on the ward: heart check","who":"Patient","task":"Brenda Oyelaran is a 63-year-old woman who was admitted to an acute adult mental health ward a few days ago with repeated bouts of anxiety in which she becomes short of breath. This afternoon she told the nursing staff she has pain in her chest, and you are the doctor asked to see her. The healthcare assistant is recording her observations while you go to her room. Please review her and carry out a cardiovascular assessment, keeping her informed as you go.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the ward psychiatrists. Are you Brenda Oyelaran?","p":"Yes. My chest, doctor, it's happening again."},{"d":"I'm glad you told the nurses. Is the pain there right now, and are you short of breath at this moment?","p":"It's eased a bit, but I'm scared."},{"d":"I'm going to check your heart properly, starting with your hands and pulse. May I, with a nurse here too?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, pain now, breathless now, consent, chaperone, call for help if unwell"},{"h":"Quick safety check","b":"Onset, character, radiation, sweating; NEWS2 observations, 12-lead ECG requested"},{"h":"General inspection","b":"Distress, colour, breathing rate, sweating, GTN spray, oxygen nearby"},{"h":"Hands and pulse","b":"Capillary refill, clubbing, splinters, radial rate and rhythm, BP both arms"},{"h":"Face and neck","b":"Pallor, central cyanosis, JVP at 45 degrees, carotid pulse character"},{"h":"Praecordium","b":"Scars, apex beat, heaves, thrills, four valve areas timed with carotid"},{"h":"Lungs and legs","b":"Bibasal crackles, sacral and ankle oedema, calf tenderness"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Findings, ECG, troponin, medical on-call if concerning, explain kindly"}],"bps":null},{"sid":"M4.41","cid":"c-s-20","area":"Medication, emergencies and side effects","title":"Slow and shuffling on haloperidol","who":"Patient","task":"Desmond Ofori is a 57-year-old man with long-standing schizoaffective disorder. During his latest admission he was started on haloperidol, now 5 mg twice a day. His mother phoned the ward because she thinks he has become 'slow', looks 'blank' in the face and 'shuffles' when he walks. He is still an inpatient and agrees to see you. You do not need to assess his mental state or risk today. Please take a short focused history and offer an appropriate physical assessment to look for extrapyramidal side effects.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Desmond Ofori?","p":"Yes. Des."},{"d":"Thanks, Des. Your mum mentioned you've seemed slower lately and are walking differently. I'd like to ask how the haloperidol has been for your body. Is that okay?","p":"Yes, my legs feel like wood."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, mother's concern, consent, focus on body side effects"},{"h":"Medication timeline","b":"Haloperidol start, rise to 5 mg twice daily, onset, other tablets, procyclidine"},{"h":"Parkinsonism","b":"Stiffness, slowness, shuffling, tremor, drooling, falls, blank face, small handwriting"},{"h":"Akathisia and dystonia","b":"Inner restlessness, pacing, neck or jaw spasm, eyes rolling up"},{"h":"Tardive dyskinesia","b":"Lip smacking, tongue movements, grimacing, writhing fingers, onset"},{"h":"Impact and adherence","b":"Getting out of bed or chair, daily tasks, embarrassment, skipping doses"},{"h":"Offer physical assessment","b":"Gait, arm swing, cogwheel rigidity, glabellar tap, Simpson-Angus, BARS, AIMS"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Likely parkinsonism, plan with consultant, NMS warning signs, mother updated"}],"bps":{"pre":"Des described two weeks of stiff arms and legs since his haloperidol dose was doubled. He found it hard to rise from a chair and had nearly fallen in the corridor. On assessment he had a fixed expression, reduced arm swing, a shuffling gait and cogwheel stiffness at both wrists, but no fever, no restlessness and no abnormal mouth movements. He then looked up and asked his question.","ask":"So it's the tablets doing this? Do I have to put up with it?","lead":"No, you don't have to put up with it, and there are several things we can change.","bio":["Review haloperidol with the consultant: return to the lowest dose that kept him well (for example 5 mg once daily) and reassess in one to two weeks","If symptoms persist or relapse risk allows, cross-taper to an antipsychotic with less EPSE such as aripiprazole, starting 10 mg daily","Procyclidine 2.5-5 mg up to three times daily only as a short-term bridge; avoid routine use as it adds confusion, constipation and may worsen tardive dyskinesia","Rate weekly with the Simpson-Angus scale; check CK, U&Es and temperature if rigidity worsens with fever, confusion or sweating (possible NMS)","Falls review: lying and standing BP, postural drop, sensible footwear while stiff"],"psych":["Ward nurse and pharmacist sit with him for a 20-minute side effect talk and give a Choice and Medication leaflet on haloperidol","Shared decision-making on the next antipsychotic, using his own priorities (staying well, walking normally)","Continue planned individual CBT for psychosis with the ward psychologist, weekly sessions, including coping with side effects"],"social":["Update his mother with Des's consent and thank her for spotting the change early; offer a carers' assessment","Ward physiotherapist for gait and falls advice while stiff","Care coordinator to plan medication review after discharge in the CMHT clinic","Occupational therapist to check he can manage washing and dressing safely"],"safety":["Tell staff at once if he develops a high temperature, severe stiffness, confusion or a racing heart: this is an emergency","Report any painful neck or eye spasm immediately so it can be treated the same day"]}},{"sid":"M4.42","cid":"c-u-14","area":"Physical examination","title":"Swollen neck on lithium","who":"Patient","task":"Kerry-Ann Mensah is a 39-year-old woman with bipolar affective disorder who was admitted to an acute adult ward during a depressive episode. She has been on lithium for several years as her mood stabiliser and it was restarted at the usual dose on admission. Over the past few weeks she has noticed her neck looking fuller and has mentioned it to the nurses. Please take a short medical history about the swelling in her neck and then carry out a thyroid assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the ward psychiatrists. Are you Kerry-Ann Mensah?","p":"Yes, that's me."},{"d":"The nurses told me you've noticed some swelling in your neck. I'd like to ask a few questions about it, then have a look and feel. Is that alright?","p":"Yes please. It's been bothering me."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent, explain history then neck check, chaperone offered"},{"h":"Neck swelling story","b":"When noticed, growing, pain, swallowing, breathing, hoarse voice, lumps elsewhere"},{"h":"Underactive thyroid symptoms","b":"Tiredness, weight gain, feeling cold, constipation, dry skin, slowed thinking"},{"h":"Overactive and background","b":"Heat, palpitations, tremor; lithium years, last level, TFTs, family thyroid"},{"h":"Inspection and swallow","b":"Scars, swelling, sip of water, tongue out, eyes from side"},{"h":"Palpation from behind","b":"Size, texture, nodules, tenderness, lymph nodes, trachea, percuss, listen for bruit"},{"h":"Peripheral signs","b":"Pulse rate, hands, fine tremor, slow-relaxing reflexes, lid lag, proximal power"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Likely goitre, TFTs, lithium level, calcium, ultrasound, keep taking lithium"}],"bps":{"pre":"Kerry-Ann described a painless fullness of her neck over a month, with tiredness and some weight gain despite a steady appetite. She had no trouble swallowing or breathing. Her neck showed a smooth, symmetrical, non-tender swelling that rose on swallowing, with no nodules or enlarged nodes. Her pulse was 58 and ankle reflexes relaxed slowly. She touched her neck and asked.","ask":"Is the lithium doing this? Will I have to come off it?","lead":"Lithium can affect the thyroid quite commonly, and the good news is it is very treatable without usually needing to stop it.","bio":["Bloods today: TSH and free T4, thyroid antibodies, U&Es and eGFR, adjusted calcium, and a lithium level taken 12 hours after the last dose","If hypothyroid, start levothyroxine around 50 micrograms each morning on an empty stomach and recheck TSH after 6-8 weeks, adjusting in 25-50 microgram steps","Usually continue lithium at a level of 0.6-0.8 mmol/L; never stop it suddenly as this risks rebound mania","Thyroid ultrasound if the swelling is uneven, nodular or keeps growing; urgent ENT if stridor or difficulty swallowing","Ongoing lithium checks: levels every 3 months, TFTs, U&Es and calcium every 6 months, weight at each review"],"psych":["Ward nurse goes through the lithium information booklet and record book with her, including thyroid and toxicity symptoms","Explain that an underactive thyroid can drag mood down, so treating it may also help her depression","Bipolar psychoeducation group (around 8-10 weekly sessions) after discharge to learn early warning signs and medication routines"],"social":["GP informed for shared care of thyroid tests and levothyroxine after discharge","Care coordinator to track blood test dates and remind her of appointments","Community pharmacist to check for interacting medicines such as ibuprofen or ACE inhibitors"],"safety":["Seek urgent help if she becomes very drowsy, confused, unsteady, vomits repeatedly or develops coarse tremor (possible lithium toxicity)"]}},{"sid":"M4.43","cid":"c-u-15","area":"Physical examination","title":"Sudden visual loss: history and fundoscopy","who":"Patient","task":"Leila Haddad is a 28-year-old woman admitted under the medical team after waking two days ago unable to see out of her left eye. The physicians have found nothing so far and believe her symptoms may have a psychological basis, so they have referred her to liaison psychiatry. Referrals to neurology and ophthalmology have been sent, but no specialist has seen her yet, and the notes do not say whether anyone has looked at the back of her eyes. Please take a history of her visual symptoms and then carry out fundoscopy.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors working alongside the medical team. Are you Leila Haddad?","p":"Yes. I still can't see from this eye."},{"d":"I'm sorry, that sounds really worrying. I'd like to hear exactly what happened, then look into the back of your eyes with a light. Is that okay?","p":"Okay. Nobody has looked yet."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge fear, consent, plan history then eye check"},{"h":"Visual loss story","b":"Woke with it, one eye, total or patchy, pain on moving eye, flashes, floaters"},{"h":"Linked symptoms","b":"Headache, jaw pain on chewing, weakness, numbness, past episodes, tingling"},{"h":"Background and stress","b":"Contact lenses, diabetes, blood pressure, medicines, recent stresses, mood"},{"h":"Preparation","b":"Dim room, acuity each eye, pupils and RAPD, consent before any dilating drops"},{"h":"Fundoscopy","b":"Red reflex, disc colour, cup and margins, vessels, four quadrants, macula last"},{"h":"Compare eyes","b":"Repeat right eye, look for swollen disc, haemorrhages, pale disc"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Findings, eye team same day, no assumptions, update medical team"}],"bps":{"pre":"Leila said she woke on Monday with her left eye completely dark, with no pain, headache or other symptoms. She had lost her job the week before. Visual acuity in the left eye was 'no perception of light', yet her pupils reacted equally with no relative afferent defect, and both red reflexes, discs, vessels and maculae looked normal. She looked straight at you and asked.","ask":"So is there something wrong with my eye or not?","lead":"The back of your eye looks healthy, which is reassuring, but the eye specialists still need to see you before anyone gives this a name.","bio":["No medication from psychiatry at this stage; request same-day ophthalmology for slit-lamp assessment, visual fields and OCT","Neurology to consider MRI brain and orbits to exclude optic neuritis or other causes","Visual evoked potentials if the eye team want objective confirmation of an intact pathway","Basic bloods if not already done: FBC, CRP, glucose, B12"],"psych":["If functional visual loss is confirmed, a clear explanation by neurology and liaison psychiatry that the eye and brain are structurally healthy but not working properly, with the neurosymptoms.org information","Liaison psychiatry follow-up to explore the job loss, mood and anxiety over two or three ward visits","Referral for psychological therapy for functional neurological disorder (often 8-12 weekly sessions) if symptoms persist"],"social":["Fit note from the medical team and signposting to Citizens Advice on benefits after losing her job","Involve her partner or a friend in explanations with her consent","Advise no driving until the eye team confirm she meets the visual standard; tell DVLA if loss persists","GP letter so follow-up continues after discharge"],"safety":["Report at once any new headache, pain on moving the eye, weakness or loss of vision in the other eye"]}},{"sid":"M4.44","cid":"c-u-16","area":"Physical examination","title":"Feverish and breathless: chest check","who":"Patient","task":"You are the on-call doctor covering a women's acute adult psychiatry ward overnight. Sinead Fairweather, aged 39, was admitted with a relapse of psychosis and was started on a new antipsychotic three weeks ago. Nursing staff report that she has been bringing up green phlegm, is now breathless, and her temperature has just been recorded at 39.6°C. Please assess her and carry out a respiratory assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, the psychiatry doctor on call tonight. Are you Sinead Fairweather?","p":"Yes. I can't get my breath."},{"d":"I'm sorry you feel so poorly. I'd like to listen to your chest and check your breathing. Is that okay, with a nurse here too?","p":"Okay."},{"d":"Can you sit up for me? Let me know if you need to stop at any point.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent, chaperone, sit up, NEWS2 and sepsis screen started"},{"h":"From the end of bed","b":"Distress, breathing rate, accessory muscles, sputum pot, oxygen saturation"},{"h":"Hands and pulse","b":"Cyanosis, clubbing, CO2 flap, pulse rate, BP, temperature"},{"h":"Face and neck","b":"Central cyanosis, lips, trachea position, lymph nodes, JVP"},{"h":"Front of chest","b":"Expansion, percussion note, vocal resonance, breath sounds, crackles, bronchial breathing"},{"h":"Back of chest","b":"Repeat expansion, percussion and auscultation, compare both sides"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Likely pneumonia, sepsis six, CXR, cultures, CURB-65, medical transfer, antipsychotic review"}],"bps":null},{"sid":"M4.45","cid":"c-u-17","area":"Physical examination","title":"Clozapine and no bowel movement","who":"Patient","task":"You are a junior doctor on an adult inpatient ward and the nurse in charge asks you to see Lewis Marangoni urgently. He is 45, takes clozapine, and is due to be discharged soon. Staff have just learned that he has not opened his bowels for 12 days, and he is now complaining of tummy pain. Please carry out an abdominal assessment on him straight away.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Lewis Marangoni?","p":"Yeah. My stomach really hurts."},{"d":"I'm sorry. The nurses say you haven't been to the toilet for nearly two weeks. I need to check your tummy now. Is that okay, with a nurse here?","p":"Fine. Please don't stop my discharge."},{"d":"Let's make sure you're safe first. Where is the pain, out of ten?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent, chaperone, pain score, reassure about discharge worry"},{"h":"Brief red flags","b":"Last bowel action, wind, vomiting, eating, overflow, laxatives, clozapine dose"},{"h":"General inspection","b":"Distress, distension, dehydration, NEWS2, temperature, lying flat"},{"h":"Hands to face","b":"Pulse, BP, dry mouth, jaundice, conjunctival pallor"},{"h":"Inspect and palpate","b":"Distension, scars, hernias; light then deep, guarding, rebound, masses"},{"h":"Percuss and listen","b":"Tympanic note, shifting dullness, absent or tinkling bowel sounds"},{"h":"Offer remaining checks","b":"Hernial orifices, rectal check with chaperone, external genitalia"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Possible clozapine bowel obstruction, emergency surgical review, bloods, imaging"}],"bps":null},{"sid":"M4.46","cid":"c-s-22","area":"Medication, emergencies and side effects","title":"Long QTc on a routine ECG","who":"Patient","task":"You are covering a community outpatient clinic, where part of your job is to review the routine ECGs taken by the healthcare assistants. One of today's tracings belongs to Marjorie Bello, a 74-year-old woman with schizophrenia, high blood pressure and type 2 diabetes. Her bloods from this morning are normal apart from an adjusted calcium of 1.90 mmol/L (range 2.20-2.60). She takes quetiapine MR 800 mg daily, raised a few weeks ago from 600 mg, plus nifedipine 20 mg daily and metformin 1 g daily. Her ECG shows a prolonged QTc. Please explain to Marjorie in plain language what the ECG shows and agree a plan with her. No physical assessment is needed.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in clinic. Are you Marjorie Bello?","p":"Yes, love. Is it my heart tracing?"},{"d":"It is. Thank you for waiting. First, how are you feeling today? Any dizziness, fluttering in the chest or blackouts?","p":"No, I feel fine."},{"d":"That's good. I've looked at the tracing and want to explain what I found and what we'll do.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, how she feels, purpose, check what she knows"},{"h":"Read the tracing","b":"Rate, rhythm, axis, PR, QRS, QTc over 500 ms by Fridericia"},{"h":"Symptom check","b":"Palpitations, faints, dizziness, chest pain, tingling lips, cramps, extra tablets taken"},{"h":"Plain explanation","b":"Heart's electrical reset is slow; risk of dangerous irregular rhythm"},{"h":"Likely causes","b":"Quetiapine rise to 800 mg, calcium 1.90, check magnesium and potassium"},{"h":"Plan","b":"Same-day medical review, calcium replacement, reduce quetiapine, repeat ECG, consultant"},{"h":"Risk and safety","b":"Faint or palpitations means 999; avoid other QT-prolonging medicines"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, voices worry addressed, GP letter, leaflet"}],"bps":{"pre":"Marjorie felt well and had no palpitations or blackouts, but admitted she sometimes forgets whether she has taken her tablets. The tracing showed sinus rhythm with a QTc of about 520 ms. You explained that the heart's electrical recovery between beats was taking too long, probably from the higher quetiapine dose and her low calcium. She was alarmed and asked.","ask":"Is my heart going to stop? And please don't take away the tablet that keeps the voices quiet.","lead":"This is something we can fix quickly, and we will keep your mental health steady while we do it.","bio":["Same-day medical assessment in A&E or the acute medical unit for cardiac monitoring; IV 10% calcium gluconate 10-20 ml in 50-100 ml of 5% glucose over 10 minutes if advised, then oral calcium","Check magnesium, potassium, PTH, vitamin D, albumin and TFTs; correct any low magnesium or potassium","Reduce quetiapine MR back to 600 mg, the dose she tolerated, after discussion with the consultant; consider later switch to aripiprazole if QTc stays long","Repeat ECG after calcium correction and one to two weeks after any dose change; aim for QTc under 470 ms in women","Avoid adding QT-prolonging drugs such as citalopram, macrolide antibiotics or ondansetron; pharmacist to check all her medicines"],"psych":["Plain-language explanation with teach-back and a written summary so she remembers the plan","Relapse signature work with her care coordinator so early return of voices is spotted after any dose reduction","Weekly contact for the first month after the change to monitor mental state and reassure her"],"social":["Dosette box or blister pack from the community pharmacy to prevent accidental double doses","Care coordinator to accompany her or arrange transport to the medical review","GP letter covering calcium follow-up, ECG results and medication change","Involve family or a neighbour, with her consent, to help with tablets"],"safety":["Call 999 if she faints, has a pounding or irregular heartbeat, or chest pain","Do not take extra quetiapine if unsure whether a dose was taken"]}},{"sid":"M4.47","cid":"c-s-23","area":"Medication, emergencies and side effects","title":"Heart attack seen on a clinic ECG","who":"Patient","task":"You are the doctor in a community mental health outpatient clinic, checking routine ECGs recorded by the healthcare assistants. One belongs to Gwendolyn Afolabi, a 74-year-old woman with schizophrenia, hypertension and type 2 diabetes. Blood tests from earlier in the week show adjusted calcium 2.31 mmol/L, HbA1c 68 mmol/mol, total cholesterol 7.2 mmol/L, HDL 1.5 mmol/L, non-HDL 5.7 mmol/L and triglycerides 6.0 mmol/L. She takes quetiapine MR 400 mg daily, nifedipine 20 mg daily and metformin 1 g daily. The ECG shows changes of an acute myocardial infarction. Please explain the findings to Gwendolyn in plain words and discuss the plan with her. No physical assessment is needed.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in clinic. Are you Gwendolyn Afolabi?","p":"Yes, dear. Gwen."},{"d":"Gwen, I've just looked at your heart tracing. Before I explain, do you have any pain or discomfort in your chest right now?","p":"Only my indigestion. I took two tablets for it this morning."},{"d":"Thank you for telling me. I'm going to stay with you while we sort this out.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, chest pain now, call for help, stay with her"},{"h":"Read the tracing","b":"Rate about 108, sinus, ST elevation V1-V4 and lead I, inferior reciprocal depression"},{"h":"Quick clinical check","b":"Burning chest pain, breathlessness, sweating, nausea, onset time, observations"},{"h":"Plain explanation","b":"Blocked heart artery, heart muscle short of oxygen, emergency"},{"h":"Immediate actions","b":"999 ambulance, aspirin 300 mg if not allergic, oxygen only if low, ECG copy"},{"h":"Why it happened","b":"Diabetes, raised cholesterol, blood pressure, age, antipsychotic weight and lipid effects"},{"h":"Her questions","b":"Can't go home, contact family, medicines continue, mental health team stays involved"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, handover to paramedics, GP and care coordinator informed"}],"bps":{"pre":"Gwen said she had burning in her chest since early morning that she put down to indigestion. Her tracing showed a fast sinus rhythm with marked ST elevation across the front chest leads and reciprocal changes inferiorly. You explained gently that a blood vessel to her heart seemed to be blocked and that this was a heart attack happening now. She gripped her handbag.","ask":"A heart attack? But I feel alright. Can't I just go home and rest?","lead":"I know you feel fairly well, but this needs treating right now, and I'll stay with you until the ambulance arrives.","bio":["Call 999 for transfer to the nearest heart attack centre for primary PCI (opening the artery with a balloon and stent)","Give aspirin 300 mg to chew straight away if no allergy or active bleeding; GTN spray only if BP is adequate; oxygen only if saturation is under 94%","Send the ECG and medication list with the paramedics; the cardiology team will add a second antiplatelet such as ticagrelor","After PCI expect atorvastatin 80 mg, an ACE inhibitor, a beta-blocker and dual antiplatelets for about 12 months","Continue quetiapine unless cardiology advise otherwise; later review her antipsychotic with metabolic risk in mind and tighten diabetes control with the GP"],"psych":["Cardiac rehabilitation programme (usually 6-12 weeks of exercise and education sessions) after discharge","Mental health team to monitor her mood and psychosis during the stress of a cardiac admission","Simple written explanation for her and her family about what happened and why"],"social":["Contact her next of kin with her permission to meet her at hospital","Care coordinator to update the cardiac ward on her mental health plan and visit after discharge","GP to lead diabetes, cholesterol and blood pressure care afterwards","Driving: usually no driving for at least one week after successful angioplasty, longer otherwise, as advised by cardiology"],"safety":["Do not let her travel by bus or taxi: 999 ambulance only","Stay with her and repeat observations while waiting; start basic life support if she collapses"]}},{"sid":"M4.48","cid":"c-s-24","area":"Medication, emergencies and side effects","title":"Talking a consultant through an ECG","who":"Consultant","task":"You have just started as a doctor in a community psychosis team. Your consultant, Dr Imogen Rafferty, wants to be sure you can read ECGs safely because many of the team's patients take medicines that affect the heart. She hands you a recent tracing from Kwame Asante, a 45-year-old man who is stable on a therapeutic dose of clozapine, and says she will ask questions as you go. Please talk Dr Rafferty through a systematic reading of the ECG, then answer her questions about the cardiac checks needed before and during clozapine treatment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. You're Dr Rafferty? Thanks for making time.","p":"Yes. Talk me through this one."},{"d":"This is a 12-lead ECG for Kwame Asante, 45, on clozapine, taken last week. Paper speed 25 mm per second, standard calibration.","p":""},{"d":"I'll go through rate, rhythm, axis, intervals, then ST segments and T waves.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Patient details, date, paper speed 25 mm/s, calibration 10 mm/mV"},{"h":"Rate and rhythm","b":"300 divided by big squares, regular, P wave before each QRS"},{"h":"Axis and P waves","b":"Leads I, II and aVF positive; P waves normal shape and width"},{"h":"Intervals","b":"PR 120-200 ms, QRS under 120 ms, QTc by Fridericia, under 450 ms men"},{"h":"ST segments and T waves","b":"Elevation, depression, T inversion, Q waves; this tracing is normal"},{"h":"Before clozapine","b":"Baseline ECG, pre-existing arrhythmia, conduction block, recent MI, cardiomyopathy history"},{"h":"Risks on clozapine","b":"Myocarditis early, cardiomyopathy later, heart attack via weight and lipids, QTc"},{"h":"Hand over and close","b":"Normal ECG, annual repeat, sooner if symptoms, thank consultant"}],"bps":{"pre":"You read the tracing as sinus rhythm at 78 a minute, normal axis, PR 160 ms, QRS 90 ms, QTc 420 ms and no ST or T wave changes. Dr Rafferty agreed, then asked about myocarditis, how it shows up and whether the risk ever ends. She then leaned forward.","ask":"So how would you build heart safety into someone's clozapine care from start to finish?","lead":"I'd think of it in three stages: before starting, the first two months, and long term.","bio":["Before starting: baseline ECG, troponin, CRP, BP, pulse, weight, lipids and HbA1c; cardiology advice if there is arrhythmia or heart disease","During titration: temperature, pulse and BP daily, and troponin and CRP weekly for at least the first four weeks; stop and seek cardiology if troponin is over twice normal or CRP over 100","Watch for resting pulse over 120, fever, chest pain or breathlessness, which can signal myocarditis in the first eight weeks","Long term: ECG at least yearly and after dose changes, echocardiogram if heart failure features appear (cardiomyopathy can occur later)","Treat metabolic risk: lipids and HbA1c at three months then yearly; statin by QRISK3; consider metformin for weight gain"],"psych":["Teach the patient and family the warning signs of myocarditis using a clozapine information leaflet","Smoking cessation support with a stop-smoking adviser, as stopping raises clozapine levels and needs a level check and dose review","Healthy lifestyle sessions (diet, activity) through the team's physical health nurse"],"social":["Clozapine clinic nurse to coordinate bloods and the monitoring service registration","GP to share the annual physical health check and cardiovascular risk management","Care coordinator to make sure the patient attends ECG and blood appointments","Community pharmacy to flag interacting medicines and changes in smoking"]}},{"sid":"M5.1","cid":"c-u-18","area":"Old age","title":"Dad's memory: talking to his daughter","who":"Daughter","task":"Tariq Hussain is a 68-year-old retired bus driver referred by his GP to the older adult community mental health team because of problems with his memory. His daughter, Zainab Hussain, has come to the team base to help with the assessment, and Tariq has given his consent for you to speak with her. He lives with his wife, who is well. Please take a collateral history from Zainab about the changes she has seen in her father, keeping in mind the likely causes and any risks.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the older adults team. Are you Zainab Hussain?","p":"Yes, Tariq's daughter."},{"d":"Thank you for coming, Zainab. Your dad has agreed that we can talk about him. Anything you share helps us understand what's going on.","p":"I'm glad. We've been so worried."},{"d":"Could you start by telling me what first made you notice something had changed?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, his consent confirmed, purpose, her worries first"},{"h":"Memory timeline","b":"First change, how long, gradual or stepwise, worse lately, missed appointments"},{"h":"Other thinking skills","b":"Getting lost, word-finding, recognising people, money, planning, repeating himself"},{"h":"Daily living","b":"Shaving, dressing, cooking, bills, tablets, driving, hobbies dropped"},{"h":"Screen other causes","b":"Low mood, hallucinations, fluctuation, falls, tremor, personality change, alcohol"},{"h":"Medical and family","b":"Strokes, heart disease, diabetes, head injury, medicines, family dementia"},{"h":"Risk and carers","b":"Wandering, cooker, scams, driving, medicines, mother's strain"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Likely Alzheimer's type, tests planned, leaflet, family contact"}],"bps":{"pre":"Zainab described a year of steady decline: her dad missed appointments, then got lost driving to the mosque he had attended for decades. He now struggles to find words and often leaves his beard untrimmed, which is unlike him. There were no hallucinations, falls, stepwise drops or heavy drinking, and his mood seemed flat but not low. She paused, then asked.","ask":"Is this dementia? What happens to him now?","lead":"What you describe fits a memory illness, possibly Alzheimer's disease, but we need a few tests before we can say for sure.","bio":["Memory clinic work-up: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, TFTs, B12 and folate, HbA1c, calcium; review medicines with anticholinergic effects","Cognitive testing with ACE-III and an MRI brain (CT if MRI not possible)","If Alzheimer's is confirmed, donepezil 5 mg at night for four weeks then 10 mg; check pulse first and ECG if slow or faints; warn about nausea and vivid dreams","Optimise blood pressure and diabetes with the GP"],"psych":["Cognitive stimulation therapy group: 14 sessions, twice weekly, run by the memory service","Post-diagnosis support sessions for him and family from a dementia adviser","Life story work with the family to help him stay connected to who he is"],"social":["Stop driving until assessed and tell DVLA if dementia is diagnosed","Carers' assessment for his wife through adult social care","Lasting power of attorney (welfare, and property and finance) while he can still decide","Occupational therapy home safety visit; Alzheimer's Society and Admiral nurse contact"],"safety":["Consider a GPS locator and the Herbert Protocol form with local police in case he gets lost","Turn off gas at the cooker if he leaves it on"]}},{"sid":"M5.2","cid":"c-t-15","area":"Old age","title":"Alzheimer's diagnosis: explaining to daughter","who":"Daughter","task":"Margaret Oduya, aged 78, was seen recently in the older adult community team clinic. Her ACE-III score was 46/100 and HADS showed anxiety 4/21 and depression 1/21. MRI reported marked global atrophy for her age, with bilateral mesial temporal and hippocampal atrophy and clear parietal involvement. All bloods, including B12, folate, thyroid and syphilis serology, were normal. She has osteoporosis and diverticular disease and takes alendronic acid 70 mg weekly, calcium with colecalciferol, paracetamol 1 g four times daily and lactulose. She lives alone in her three-bedroom house, does not drink, has no carers and manages her own daily tasks. She has consented to you speaking with her daughter, Claire. Please explain the diagnosis and management of Alzheimer's disease to Claire.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the older adults team. Are you Claire Oduya?","p":"Yes, Margaret's daughter."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Claire. Your mum has said I can talk to you about her results. How has she been, and what do you know so far?","p":"Only that the scan's back. I'm nervous."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, mother's consent, what she knows, her worries"},{"h":"Results in plain words","b":"Memory score 46, scan shows shrinkage in memory areas, normal bloods, low mood unlikely"},{"h":"Diagnosis","b":"Alzheimer's disease, moderate stage, progressive, not her fault, not normal ageing"},{"h":"Medication","b":"Donepezil, how it helps, side effects, pulse check, memantine later"},{"h":"Non-drug help","b":"Cognitive stimulation, routines, memory aids, activity, dementia adviser"},{"h":"Living alone and risk","b":"Cooking, medicines, falls with osteoporosis, scams, wandering, driving"},{"h":"Planning ahead","b":"Lasting power of attorney, care package, carers' assessment, advance wishes"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, leaflet, Alzheimer's Society, follow-up date"}],"bps":{"pre":"You explained that Margaret's memory score was well below the expected range and that her scan showed shrinkage in the brain's memory and spatial areas, with normal blood tests and no sign of depression. You named this as Alzheimer's disease at a moderate stage. Claire's eyes filled, and she said her mother insists she is fine at home alone.","ask":"Is there anything that can slow it down, and is she safe on her own?","lead":"There is treatment that can help, and we can work together on keeping her safe at home for as long as possible.","bio":["Donepezil 5 mg at night for four weeks, then 10 mg if tolerated; check pulse before starting and ECG if under 60 or any faints","Warn about nausea, diarrhoea, poor appetite, vivid dreams and dizziness; slow pulse plus osteoporosis raises fracture risk from falls","If donepezil is not tolerated, memantine 5 mg daily rising weekly to 20 mg; it can also be added later as the illness progresses","Keep anticholinergic load low; pharmacist to set up a weekly blister pack and review lactulose and paracetamol use"],"psych":["Cognitive stimulation therapy: 14 group sessions, twice weekly, with the memory service","Dementia adviser visits to build routines, calendars, reminder clocks and labelled cupboards","Support sessions for Claire on understanding the illness and looking after herself"],"social":["Occupational therapy home safety visit, telecare (falls sensor, cooker cut-off) and a pendant alarm","Adult social care needs assessment for a care package as needs grow; carers' assessment for Claire","Lasting power of attorney for both health and welfare and property and finances while she has capacity","Bank and scam protection (third-party mandate), DVLA notification if she drives, Alzheimer's Society contacts"],"safety":["Agree a daily check-in call and what to do if Margaret is found lost or confused","Fire service home safety visit and smoke alarms"]}},{"sid":"M5.3","cid":"c-s-25","area":"Old age","title":"Vascular dementia: talking to a doubtful son","who":"Son","task":"Patricia Wojcik is a 78-year-old woman recently assessed by a colleague in the older adult community team after her GP raised concerns about memory and behaviour. Her husband noticed she had grown irritable and recently found her wandering outside, unsure where she was heading. Screening bloods and urine were normal. She has type 2 diabetes, hypertension, raised cholesterol and a previous stroke with residual right arm weakness. ACE-III was 68/100; HADS depression 2/21, anxiety 5/21. MRI showed extensive periventricular and deep white matter changes consistent with marked small vessel disease and moderate generalised volume loss. She lives with her husband, manages her own daily tasks, still drives and goes to a pensioners' club twice weekly. Please explain the diagnosis to her son, Daniel Wojcik, and answer his questions about management.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the older adults team. Are you Daniel Wojcik?","p":"Yes. Dad said Mum's got dementia, and frankly I don't buy it."},{"d":"Thank you for being honest, Daniel. It must have been a shock. Your mum has agreed we can talk. Could you tell me what you've noticed yourself?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent, acknowledge shock and doubt, what he has seen"},{"h":"How we reached it","b":"Husband's account, memory score 68, normal bloods, mood not low"},{"h":"Scan and diagnosis","b":"Small vessel damage, past stroke, vascular dementia, why it can look patchy"},{"h":"Course and outlook","b":"Stepwise or gradual decline, slowed by controlling risk factors"},{"h":"Treatment","b":"No memory tablets for pure vascular type; blood pressure, statin, clopidogrel, diabetes"},{"h":"Lifestyle and support","b":"Activity, club, routine, cognitive stimulation, dementia adviser, carer support"},{"h":"Risk and practical issues","b":"Wandering, driving and DVLA, medicines, lasting power of attorney"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, leaflet, second opinion if wanted, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Daniel said his mum seemed perfectly normal when he visited three months ago. You went through her husband's account of irritability and getting lost, the memory score, and the scan showing widespread damage from small blood vessel disease linked to her stroke, diabetes and blood pressure. He became quieter, accepted it could be real, and asked.","ask":"Alright. So is there a tablet for this, or do we just watch her get worse?","lead":"There's no tablet that reverses it, but there's a lot we can do to protect her brain from further damage.","bio":["No donepezil or memantine for pure vascular dementia (NICE), unless features of Alzheimer's or Lewy body disease appear","Blood pressure target below 140/90 with the GP; statin such as atorvastatin 20-80 mg at night; clopidogrel 75 mg daily for secondary stroke prevention if not already on it","Diabetes control with an individual HbA1c target, avoiding hypoglycaemia which worsens confusion","Annual review of bloods, lipids, ECG and weight; check for depression and apathy at each review"],"psych":["Cognitive stimulation therapy: 14 sessions, twice weekly, through the memory service","Keep her going to the pensioners' club and walking daily; regular social contact helps","Psychoeducation sessions for the family on how vascular dementia behaves and how to respond to irritability"],"social":["She must tell DVLA about the diagnosis and should stop driving until DVLA decides","Carers' assessment for her husband through adult social care","Lasting power of attorney for health and finances while she can still make that choice","Dementia adviser or Admiral nurse, Alzheimer's Society and Stroke Association contacts"],"safety":["Herbert Protocol form and a phone with location sharing in case she wanders again","Call 999 for any new weakness, facial droop or speech problems (FAST)"]}},{"sid":"M5.4","cid":"c-s-26","area":"Old age","title":"Lewy body dementia: explaining to daughter","who":"Daughter","task":"Chandran Pillai is a 75-year-old man admitted two months ago to an older adult mental health ward with worsening disturbed behaviour and memory difficulties. He was first treated on a medical ward for presumed delirium due to constipation, but once that settled an underlying cognitive disorder was clear. On arrival he was seeing birds flying through the hospital. His ACE-III is 42/100. Last week's DaT scan showed symmetrically reduced striatal uptake, consistent with Lewy body dementia, though Parkinson's disease or a related condition can look similar. He has no obvious movement disorder. His daughter, Anjali Pillai, has asked to know more. Please help Anjali understand what Lewy body dementia is and how it will be managed.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the ward psychiatrists. Are you Anjali Pillai?","p":"Yes. Chandran's daughter."},{"d":"Thank you for coming, Anjali. Your father is happy for us to talk. I know you'd like to hear about his scan and diagnosis. What have you been told so far?","p":"Just that it's some kind of dementia."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent, what she knows, her concerns"},{"h":"How we got here","b":"Delirium treated, memory still poor, score 42, birds seen, DaT scan result"},{"h":"What it is","b":"Lewy body dementia, protein clumps, fluctuations, hallucinations, stiffness, sleep acting out"},{"h":"Course","b":"Progressive, varies day to day, falls, swallowing later, no cure"},{"h":"Medication","b":"Rivastigmine or donepezil, avoid antipsychotics, sensitivity, pulse and BP checks"},{"h":"Managing symptoms","b":"Hallucinations, lighting, constipation, falls, sleep, low BP on standing"},{"h":"Discharge and support","b":"OT, care package, carers' assessment, LPA, capacity, Lewy Body Society"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, leaflet, consultant meeting, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"You explained that after his constipation and delirium were treated, Chandran's memory remained poor, with a score of 42 and vivid visions of birds. The DaT scan showed reduced dopamine activity deep in the brain, which together with his symptoms points to dementia with Lewy bodies. Anjali said his alertness changes hour to hour and asked.","ask":"Can anything help him, and why won't the ward just give him something to stop the visions?","lead":"There is medicine that can help, and we're deliberately avoiding some sedating drugs because they can be dangerous in this condition.","bio":["Rivastigmine 1.5 mg twice daily with food, increasing every two weeks to 3-6 mg twice daily (or donepezil 5 mg rising to 10 mg); may reduce hallucinations and fluctuations","Check pulse and lying and standing BP before and during treatment; ECG if pulse under 60","Avoid antipsychotics such as haloperidol: risk of severe sensitivity, rigidity, falls and death; flag this clearly in his records","Regular laxatives (for example macrogol) to prevent constipation and further delirium; memantine if cholinesterase inhibitors fail","Neurology review if stiffness or slowness develops; levodopa used cautiously as it can worsen hallucinations"],"psych":["Explain to him and family that the birds are part of the illness; calm reassurance rather than arguing","Good lighting, glasses checked, fewer shadows and clutter to reduce misperceptions","Cognitive stimulation and meaningful activity on the ward with the occupational therapist"],"social":["Discharge planning meeting with ward social worker, OT and family; capacity and best interests decisions about where he lives","Carers' assessment for Anjali and any family carers","Lasting power of attorney if he has capacity, or deputyship if not","Lewy Body Society and Alzheimer's Society information; community mental health team follow-up after discharge"],"safety":["Falls plan: physiotherapy, sensor mat, slow standing; carry a card warning about antipsychotic sensitivity"]}},{"sid":"M5.5","cid":"c-t-16","area":"Old age","title":"Husband's violence: what's changed?","who":"Wife","task":"You are the liaison psychiatry doctor in A&E. Angela Thornbury has been brought in by police after calling 999 this morning when her 65-year-old husband, Clive, attacked her at home and grabbed her by the throat. The emergency team have checked her neck and given her pain relief. She says he has never been violent before but has 'not been himself' for some time. Please take a history from Angela about the recent changes in Clive's behaviour and carry out a risk assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors here in A&E. Are you Angela Thornbury?","p":"Yes."},{"d":"I'm so sorry about what happened this morning. How is your neck now? Are you comfortable to talk?","p":"It hurts. I just don't understand him any more."},{"d":"Take all the time you need. Everything you say helps us keep you safe and understand Clive.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, neck pain, comfort, privacy, safe space, tissues"},{"h":"This morning","b":"What happened, strangulation, loss of consciousness, weapons, triggers, where he is now"},{"h":"Behaviour changes","b":"Onset, gradual, disinhibition, rude remarks, loss of empathy, apathy"},{"h":"Frontal features","b":"Sweet cravings, overeating, compulsions, spending, poor hygiene, poor insight"},{"h":"Other possibilities","b":"Memory, language, mood, psychosis, alcohol, head injury, family history"},{"h":"Her risk","b":"Previous incidents, fear, children, guns, DASH questions, support network"},{"h":"Wider risk","b":"Driving, finances, self-neglect, harm to others, self-harm"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Likely frontal dementia, her safety plan, police, safeguarding, next steps"}],"bps":{"pre":"Angela cried at first, then described two years of change: Clive making crude remarks to strangers, losing interest in their grandchildren, eating whole packets of biscuits and spending heavily online. He showed no concern when she was upset. This morning he grabbed her throat when she hid his car keys. There had been no memory loss or hallucinations. She asked, still holding her neck.","ask":"What's wrong with him? And is it safe for me to go home tonight?","lead":"Your safety comes first today, and what you describe sounds like an illness affecting the front of his brain, which we can look into properly.","bio":["For Angela: full medical review of the strangulation, as swelling and vessel injury can appear later; imaging if advised","For Clive: urgent psychiatric assessment, bloods (FBC, U&Es, LFTs, TFTs, B12, calcium, syphilis), MRI brain and referral to neurology or a cognitive disorders clinic","If frontotemporal dementia is confirmed, avoid cholinesterase inhibitors as they do not help and may worsen behaviour","SSRI such as sertraline 50 mg daily may reduce disinhibition and compulsive eating; antipsychotics only short-term for severe risk"],"psych":["Plain explanation to Angela of possible frontotemporal dementia and how it changes empathy and judgement","Talking Therapies or specialist trauma support for Angela after the assault","Behaviour strategies from the community team: avoid confrontation, use distraction, remove triggers"],"social":["Safeguarding adults referral and DASH risk assessment; independent domestic violence adviser offered","Police and family agree she does not stay alone with him until he is assessed; emergency respite or family stay","DVLA must be told and he should not drive; check finances and consider lasting power of attorney later","Carers' assessment and Rare Dementia Support contact"],"safety":["Call 999 if he becomes threatening again or if she develops voice change, trouble swallowing or breathing","Remove or secure any weapons and car keys; agree a safe place she can go"]}},{"sid":"M5.6","cid":"c-s-28","area":"Old age","title":"Antipsychotic in dementia: angry brother","who":"Brother","task":"Reginald Fairbairn, 83, has had Alzheimer's disease for four years and has lived in a care home for one year. Over the past month he has become irritable and has hit staff and residents. A colleague started risperidone 0.5 mg at night, which helped at first, but this week he is unsettled again and staff want the dose increased. Last night's notes say he was awake all night, struck his head against his hand, hit carers who intervened, threw his meals, but is drinking and toileting normally. He is tearful and calls out for his late wife. At your care home visit, his brother Stanley asks to see you. He is angry that Reginald is on an antipsychotic and believes he is oversedated. Please explain to Stanley how behavioural changes in dementia are managed.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists from the older adults team. Are you Stanley, Reginald's brother?","p":"I want answers! Why is he being drugged?"},{"d":"I can see you're really upset, and you're right to ask. Please, shall we sit down? I'd like to hear your worries first, then explain everything.","p":"Fine. But I want the truth."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge anger, sit down, his relationship, consent and next of kin"},{"h":"His concerns","b":"Newspaper fears, sleepy brother, why risperidone, why more"},{"h":"What's behind behaviour","b":"Pain, infection, constipation, grief for wife, low mood, sleep, environment"},{"h":"Risperidone honestly","b":"Licensed short-term, stroke and death risk, six-week review, no increase"},{"h":"Physical review","b":"Pain scale, urine, bloods, teeth, ears, eyes, constipation, falls"},{"h":"Non-drug care","b":"Behaviour charts, routine, music, life story, bereavement, family visits"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Residents' and staff safety, his head banging, falls, eating, safeguarding"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, best interests meeting, review date, contact number"}],"bps":{"pre":"Stanley calmed once you sat and listened. He had read that these drugs are dangerous in dementia. When you mentioned the notes about Reginald calling for his wife, he wept and said she died suddenly of cancer and his brother never got over it. You agreed that pain, grief or low mood might sit behind the change. He wiped his eyes and asked.","ask":"So what are you actually going to do, apart from giving him more of that drug?","lead":"We are not going to increase it; first we look for the reasons behind his distress and treat those.","bio":["No increase in risperidone; review at six weeks with a plan to taper and stop, as MHRA warns of higher stroke and death risk in dementia","Structured pain assessment (Abbey or PAINAD) and regular paracetamol 1 g up to four times daily if over 50 kg, to see if distress settles","Rule out physical causes: urine test, FBC, U&Es, CRP, constipation check, dental, ear and eye review","If low mood persists after pain and grief are addressed, consider sertraline 50 mg daily with review at 4-6 weeks"],"psych":["ABC behaviour charts kept by care staff for one to two weeks to find triggers and patterns","Person-centred care plan with the care home liaison team: calm approach, music, life story book, meaningful activity","Bereavement-focused support: talk about his wife, photos, reminiscence with Stanley during visits"],"social":["Best interests meeting with Stanley, care home manager, GP and the team about medication and care","Check for lasting power of attorney and DoLS authorisation","Care home staff training on dementia distress from the care home liaison team","Safeguarding check to protect other residents and staff, without blaming Reginald"],"safety":["Report urgently any sudden weakness, facial droop, drowsiness or falls while on risperidone"]}},{"sid":"M5.7","cid":"c-s-29","area":"Old age","title":"Mild cognitive impairment: talking to husband","who":"Husband","task":"Wendy Chaudhry is a 68-year-old retired school administrator who was recently assessed in the memory clinic and diagnosed with mild cognitive impairment. Her ACE-III score was 80/100, and HADS showed anxiety 7/21 and depression 3/21. Blood tests, including thyroid, B12, folate and syphilis serology, were all normal. She has no past medical history and takes no regular medicines. Wendy has agreed that information can be shared with her husband, Martin. Please discuss the diagnosis and its management with Martin and answer his questions.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the memory clinic. Are you Martin Chaudhry?","p":"Yes, Wendy's husband."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Martin. Wendy has said I can share her results with you. What have you noticed at home, and what's on your mind?","p":"She forgets things. I'm scared it's Alzheimer's."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, Wendy's consent, his observations and fears"},{"h":"Results in plain words","b":"Score 80, normal bloods, mild anxiety, daily life unaffected"},{"h":"What MCI means","b":"More forgetful than expected, not dementia, independence kept"},{"h":"Outlook","b":"Some stay stable or improve, some progress, about 10% a year"},{"h":"Medication","b":"No dementia tablets, check hearing, vascular risk, avoid sedating medicines"},{"h":"Staying well","b":"Exercise, social life, diet, alcohol, sleep, memory aids, anxiety help"},{"h":"Planning and follow-up","b":"Repeat memory test yearly, lasting power of attorney, driving"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, leaflet, contact if things change"}],"bps":{"pre":"You explained that Wendy's memory test was a little below expected for her age, with normal blood tests and no sign of depression, though she was somewhat anxious. Because she still manages cooking, money and driving, this was mild cognitive impairment rather than dementia. Martin looked relieved but uncertain and asked.","ask":"So does that mean it will turn into dementia? Is there anything we can do?","lead":"It doesn't mean it will definitely become dementia, and there are real things that can help keep her memory as strong as possible.","bio":["No cholinesterase inhibitors or memantine: NICE does not recommend them for mild cognitive impairment","GP check of blood pressure, cholesterol and HbA1c with treatment if raised; hearing test and hearing aids if needed","Avoid sedating or anticholinergic medicines such as sleeping tablets and some bladder or allergy remedies","Repeat ACE-III in the memory clinic in about 12 months, sooner if daily function changes"],"psych":["NHS Talking Therapies for her anxiety, for example 6-8 sessions of guided self-help or CBT","Memory strategies from the clinic nurse: diary, phone reminders, a fixed place for keys","Mentally and socially active life: clubs, volunteering, learning new skills together"],"social":["At least 150 minutes of moderate exercise a week, Mediterranean-style diet, alcohol within 14 units, good sleep","Lasting power of attorney now while planning is easy; update wills","MCI alone usually does not need DVLA notification, but review if driving concerns arise","Age UK, local memory cafes and the memory clinic helpline for support"]}},{"sid":"M5.8","cid":"c-u-19","area":"Old age","title":"Sudden confusion: talking to his son","who":"Son","task":"Mr Gordon Whitfield, a 71-year-old widower, came onto a general medical ward two days ago after a neighbour found him outside his house, half dressed and muttering. Since then he has been drowsy at times, has wandered into other bays, and the ward consultant thinks he may be psychotic. He takes tablets for blood pressure and cholesterol and has no psychiatric history that anyone knows of. His son Rhys, aged 35, is visiting and is very worried. Please speak to Rhys and take a collateral history to help clarify what is causing his father's change.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Rhys, Gordon's son?","p":"Yes. What's going on with him?"},{"d":"I can see how worried you are. Your dad has agreed we can talk, and I'd really value your view of how he was before this.","p":""},{"d":"Could you take me back to when you last saw him well?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, confirm consent from Gordon, acknowledge worry, purpose of talk"},{"h":"Timeline of change","b":"Well last week, onset over days, fluctuation, worse evenings, drowsiness"},{"h":"Features of confusion","b":"Attention, disorientation, wrong names, sleep reversed, seeing things, not eating"},{"h":"Possible physical causes","b":"Cough with coloured phlegm, fever, urine, new tablets, alcohol, falls, constipation"},{"h":"Baseline function","b":"Drives, shops, finances, minds grandchildren, no prior memory decline"},{"h":"Grief, mood, background","b":"Wife died last year, self-care slipping, low mood, family dementia history"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Wandering off ward, falls, pulling lines, driving, living alone, self-neglect"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Likely delirium not dementia, infection checks, Rhys's role, questions"}],"bps":{"pre":"Rhys explained that only last week his dad had taken the grandchildren to the park without any trouble, though he had been coughing up green phlegm. On the ward Gordon has called Rhys by his brother's name, dozes through lunch and was found in another bay asking where he was. You explained this pattern fits delirium, most likely from a chest infection, rather than dementia or a new psychotic illness.","ask":"So it's not dementia? How do you actually fix this?","lead":"The most important thing is finding and treating whatever has upset his brain, and most people improve as that settles.","bio":["Delirium screen: 4AT, chest X-ray, FBC, CRP, U&Es, glucose, calcium, LFTs, blood and sputum cultures, urine dip","Treat the chest infection with antibiotics per the local pneumonia guideline; oxygen if saturations are low; fluids and food charts","Drug chart review: stop anticholinergics and sedatives where possible; check for constipation and urinary retention with a bladder scan","Avoid antipsychotics; only if severely distressed or dangerous, haloperidol 0.5 mg orally after an ECG for QTc, lowest dose, shortest time (NICE CG103)","Repeat cognitive testing once recovered (about 6-8 weeks); memory clinic only if he does not return to baseline"],"psych":["Nurse-led reorientation every shift: clock, calendar, glasses and hearing aids in, the same staff where possible","Rhys and family visiting at regular times with familiar photos, calmly telling him where he is and why","Sleep plan: dim lights and quiet at night, sitting out and walking with physio by day","Once well, screen for depression and offer bereavement support through Cruse or NHS Talking Therapies"],"social":["Ward OT and physiotherapy assessment of mobility and daily tasks before discharge","Short-term reablement package at home for up to six weeks if needed","Advise no driving until fully recovered and the GP has reviewed him","Carers' assessment for Rhys through the local authority","Discharge letter asking the GP to recheck memory and mood in about two months"],"safety":["Falls bundle and enhanced observation while he is wandering; side room close to the nurses' desk","If he tries to leave and lacks capacity, staff can lawfully act for him using the Mental Capacity Act and consider DoLS"]}},{"sid":"M5.9","cid":"c-s-30","area":"Old age","title":"Refusing carers: is it her choice?","who":"Patient","task":"Miss Dorothy Pennington, an 82-year-old retired bookkeeper, has been on a general medical ward for several weeks with a stubborn urinary tract infection and is now medically ready to go home. She walks with a stick and fell once during the admission. The occupational therapist recommends carers twice daily to help her wash, dress and prepare meals, and the consultant agrees, but Dorothy is declining. Her recent MMSE was 29/30. She has type 2 diabetes on tablets, hypertension, cataracts in both eyes and osteoarthritis, and takes amlodipine, metformin and simvastatin. Please assess her capacity to refuse carers and explain your conclusion to her.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Is it Miss Pennington?","p":"I am. Dorothy. Are you here about these carers?"},{"d":"I am, Dorothy. Your team asked me to hear your side properly before anything is decided.","p":""},{"d":"Can you tell me what you've been told about the carers, and how you feel about it?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, purpose, her right to decide, hear her view first"},{"h":"Her reasons","b":"Independence, strangers at home, cost, wants to leave hospital, neighbours' help"},{"h":"Impairment of mind?","b":"MMSE 29/30, no delirium now, mood screen, no psychosis"},{"h":"Understand and retain","b":"What carers do, OT and consultant reasons, the fall, recall later"},{"h":"Use and weigh","b":"Falls at home, meals, washing, diabetes, versus her reasons and alternatives"},{"h":"Communicate and risk","b":"Clear choice, hypos, falls, lying on floor, loneliness, self-neglect"},{"h":"Conclusion explained","b":"Has capacity, unwise not equal incapable, choice respected, can change mind"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Safety net, pendant alarm, OT kit, GP follow-up, questions"}],"bps":{"pre":"Dorothy told you she has lived alone in her two-bedroom house for decades, that her neighbours of twenty years shop for her and that she has got herself up after falls before. She explained the carers' role back accurately, weighed the risk of another fall against her wish to keep strangers out, and stayed consistent. You concluded she has capacity to refuse and told her so.","ask":"So nobody can make me have them. What happens now, then?","lead":"You're right, it's your decision, and my job now is to make going home as safe as we can on your terms.","bio":["Lying and standing blood pressure before discharge; if postural drop, review amlodipine dose with the medical team","Diabetes check: HbA1c and kidney function, confirm metformin dose suits eGFR, explain hypo symptoms","Regular paracetamol 1 g up to four times daily (if over 50 kg) for hip and knee pain to help mobility","Bone health after the fall: vitamin D level, FRAX score and consider bone protection with the GP","Chase her cataract surgery date; better vision cuts falls"],"psych":["Respect her decision and record the capacity assessment in full, decision-specific and time-specific","Brief mood screen (GDS-15) as loneliness was mentioned; Talking Therapies if low","Falls service strength and balance programme (e.g. Otago), weekly for about 12 weeks","Offer a no-pressure trial: carers can be started later with one phone call if she changes her mind"],"social":["OT home visit: grab rails, perching stool, raised toilet seat, shower aid","Telecare pendant alarm linked to a response service","Hot or frozen meal delivery and neighbours' agreed visiting rota, with her consent","Social worker to explain Care Act assessment and free reablement for up to six weeks","Age UK befriending service for loneliness"],"safety":["Pendant alarm worn at all times; press it or call 999 after a fall she cannot get up from","GP or district nurse check within one week of discharge"]}},{"sid":"M5.10","cid":"c-s-32","area":"Old age","title":"Not eating: 'my insides are rotten'","who":"Patient","task":"Mrs Gwen Pryce, a 78-year-old widow, is visited urgently at home by a doctor from the older adult community mental health team. Over the past few weeks her GP and her daughter have become very concerned: she has almost stopped eating and drinking and has started saying strange things, insisting that her body is rotting inside. She lives alone in a terraced house in a small town. Please take a history from Mrs Pryce, including a careful risk assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists from the older people's team. Are you Mrs Pryce?","p":"Yes... you needn't have bothered."},{"d":"I'm glad to meet you. Your GP and your daughter are worried you've been very unwell lately.","p":""},{"d":"Could you tell me how things have been for you these past few weeks?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, why visiting, gentle tone, seated at her level"},{"h":"Mood and biology","b":"Low mood, no pleasure, sleep, early waking, energy, weight loss, diurnal"},{"h":"Nihilistic beliefs","b":"Insides rotten, dead or decaying, guilt, poverty, conviction, voices"},{"h":"Food and fluid","b":"How long, fluids per day, urine, dizziness, falls, weight change"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Wish to die, plans, tablets at home, self-neglect, dehydration, alone"},{"h":"Physical and cognition","b":"Bowels, pain, cancer fears, memory, delirium, new tablets, alcohol"},{"h":"Past and support","b":"Previous depression, ECT response, bereavements, family, who visits"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Severe depression with psychosis, urgent admission, physical checks, daughter"}],"bps":{"pre":"Gwen sat in her armchair, thin, with dry lips and a full cup of cold tea beside her. In a flat voice she told you her stomach and bowels have rotted away so food is pointless, that she has drunk very little for four days and that she would rather be gone. She had a similar depression twenty years ago. You explained she has a severe depression with psychotic symptoms.","ask":"There's nothing you can do for a body that's already dead, is there?","lead":"I can hear how completely real this feels, and I believe this is a severe illness that usually responds well to treatment.","bio":["Admit to an older adult ward today; urgent U&Es, FBC, LFTs, glucose, calcium, TFTs, B12, folate and ECG","Rehydrate first: food and fluid charts, medical review for IV or subcutaneous fluids if dehydrated","Antidepressant plus antipsychotic (NICE NG222): e.g. sertraline 50 mg daily with olanzapine 2.5 mg at night, titrated","Check sodium at baseline, 2 and 4 weeks on the SSRI; watch for falls and sedation","ECT is first-line here as she is refusing food and fluid: anaesthetic review, consent or Mental Health Act safeguards"],"psych":["Daily supportive contact from her named nurse; do not argue with the belief, focus on care","Psychoeducation for her daughter about depression with psychosis and its good outlook","Once improving: CBT or behavioural activation adapted for older adults, around 12-16 weekly sessions","Relapse prevention plan before discharge, including early signs such as stopping eating"],"social":["Informal admission if she agrees; if she refuses and lacks capacity, same-day Mental Health Act assessment with an AMHP","Older adult CMHT care coordinator after discharge","Carers' assessment and information for her daughter","Social worker to review meals, shopping and home support before she returns"],"safety":["Do not leave her alone today; remove stockpiled tablets from the house","Daughter to call 999 if she collapses, becomes drowsy or stops passing urine"]}},{"sid":"M5.11","cid":"c-u-20","area":"Mania and bipolar","title":"Crystals and auras: a new mania at 72","who":"Patient","task":"Mrs Rosalind Hartley, a 72-year-old retired primary school teacher, attended A&E for a split thumbnail and has since caused upset, telling staff that the doctor who treated her has a toxic aura. The liaison team is asked to see her urgently. Her son Martin, 45, who lives with her, says that over the past month she has become absorbed in homeopathy and healing crystals, spends hours at the library and confronts other visitors aggressively about cleansing their auras, prompting complaints from staff. She shouts at Martin when he questions her and has been ordering many remedies online. Please take a history from Mrs Hartley to reach a diagnosis, explain it to her and assess risk.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Mrs Hartley?","p":"I am, and I won't be long, I've far too much to do."},{"d":"Thank you for sparing me some time. I gather today has been frustrating.","p":""},{"d":"Could we find somewhere quieter, and you tell me what happened in the department?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, quiet room, acknowledge frustration, let her talk first"},{"h":"Mood and energy","b":"Elation or irritability, sleep, energy, racing thoughts, speech, distractibility"},{"h":"Beliefs and perception","b":"Auras, special powers, mission, grandiosity, voices, insight"},{"h":"Behaviour and money","b":"Library confrontations, online spending, debts, disinhibition, driving"},{"h":"Physical causes","b":"Steroids, antidepressants, head injury, headaches, memory, thyroid, alcohol"},{"h":"Past and family","b":"Previous depression or highs, family bipolar, substances, personality before"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Aggression, exploitation, finances, exhaustion, self-harm, Martin's safety"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Manic episode explained, physical tests, involve Martin, questions"}],"bps":{"pre":"Rosalind spoke rapidly, jumping from crystal frequencies to her former pupils, and laughed loudly before snapping at a passing nurse. She has slept three hours a night for weeks, has spent about two thousand pounds online and believes she can read people's energy. She had one depression in her fifties. You explained this looks like a manic episode, which in later life needs physical causes ruled out.","ask":"If I'm unwell, as you put it, what do you suggest we do?","lead":"I'd like us to work out together why this has come on now, and then calm things down so you can rest.","bio":["Physical work-up: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, TFTs, calcium, glucose, B12, syphilis serology, urine drug screen, ECG, and CT or MRI brain","Stop any antidepressant; check for steroids or other triggering medicines","Antipsychotic first-line (NICE CG185): e.g. olanzapine 5 mg at night, lower than usual adult dose; baseline weight, lipids, glucose","Warn of sedation, dizziness and falls; short-term promethazine 25 mg at night if sleep remains poor","If bipolar is confirmed, discuss lithium later: level 12 hours post-dose, renal, thyroid and calcium every 6 months"],"psych":["Low-stimulation care: quiet space, short calm conversations, consistent staff","Psychoeducation for her and Martin on mania and early warning signs, once she is settling","Structured psychological therapy for bipolar disorder after recovery, individual or group, roughly 16 sessions","Relapse signature and action plan written with her when well"],"social":["Home treatment team if she accepts treatment and Martin feels safe; otherwise older adult ward admission","Protect finances: bank card limits, cancel online accounts with her agreement, discuss Lasting Power of Attorney when well","Carers' assessment and support for Martin","She must not drive while unwell and needs to notify the DVLA about mania","Older adult CMHT care coordinator to liaise with the library about her return"],"safety":["If she refuses help and risks remain high, Mental Health Act assessment","Martin to call 999 if she becomes physically aggressive; crisis line number given"]}},{"sid":"M5.12","cid":"c-t-17","area":"Old age","title":"Found wandering at night: granddaughter's account","who":"Granddaughter","task":"Mr Harold Brennan, a 79-year-old retired mechanic, was brought to hospital this evening after the police found him walking around his estate in his pyjamas. No other doctor has assessed him yet. His granddaughter, Leah Brennan, has come in because her mother is at work. Harold lives alone in a small flat with no carers. Please speak to Leah, take a collateral history and assess the risks to him.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Leah, Harold's granddaughter?","p":"Yes. Is he alright?"},{"d":"He's safe and warm on the ward. Thank you for coming in so quickly.","p":""},{"d":"He's happy for us to talk. Could you tell me what you know about tonight?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, he is safe, consent, confidentiality, her own wellbeing"},{"h":"Tonight in detail","b":"Before, during, after, door left open, how long out, clothing, injuries"},{"h":"Recent months","b":"Self-care slipping, gas hob left on, memory, gradual or stepwise course"},{"h":"Delirium and physical","b":"Recent illness, new tablets, falls, head injury, stroke signs, alcohol"},{"h":"Function and mood","b":"Daily tasks, money, meals, driving, grief after wife's death"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Gas, wandering, cold, falls, exploitation, self-neglect, medication errors"},{"h":"Her feelings","b":"Tearfulness, grandmother's death, fear of decline, reassurance"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Tests tonight, possible dementia or delirium, not home alone, support"}],"bps":{"pre":"Leah told you her grandad has looked unshaven and wears stained clothes lately, and last week she found the gas hob burning when he insisted he had turned it off. Her grandmother died of kidney cancer two months ago. There were no falls or recent illness. Near the end, she began to cry and asked whether this is the beginning of the end.","ask":"Is it dementia? What can you actually do for him?","lead":"It's too early to say for certain, but there is a lot we can do, starting tonight.","bio":["Tonight: 4AT, FBC, U&Es, CRP, glucose, LFTs, calcium, TFTs, B12, folate, urine dip and ECG to look for delirium","Review his blood pressure and cholesterol tablets and any anticholinergic medicines","If dementia is later confirmed as Alzheimer's: donepezil 5 mg at night, rising to 10 mg after 4 weeks; check pulse for bradycardia, warn of nausea and vivid dreams","Brain imaging (CT or MRI) as part of the memory assessment"],"psych":["Memory clinic referral: ACE-III or MoCA testing and a diagnosis discussion with family present","Cognitive stimulation therapy group, 14 sessions, if mild to moderate dementia is diagnosed","Bereavement support for Harold and the family through Cruse","Space for Leah to talk about her grief; GP or Talking Therapies if she struggles"],"social":["Care Act needs assessment by hospital social worker before discharge","Gas cut-off valve, telecare door sensor and pendant alarm","Herbert Protocol form with the local police: photo, routines and places he might go","Alzheimer's Society dementia adviser and carers' assessment for the family","Discuss Lasting Power of Attorney while Harold can still decide"],"safety":["He will not be discharged home alone tonight","If he goes missing again, call 999 straight away and share the Herbert Protocol form"]}},{"sid":"M5.13","cid":"c-u-21","area":"Old age","title":"Poisoning neighbours: an angry admission","who":"Patient","task":"Mrs Maureen Cartwright, a 68-year-old retired dinner lady who lives alone, has been admitted to the older adult psychiatric ward after the police brought her in following months of calls and letters about her neighbours. She is very angry about being in hospital. She has never married and has no children. Please take a history of her current difficulties and carry out a mental state assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Mrs Cartwright?","p":"Get away from me! I've been dragged here for nothing."},{"d":"I'm sorry, that sounds really upsetting. I'm not here to make things worse.","p":"Hmph."},{"d":"I'd genuinely like to hear your side. Would you tell me what's been happening at home?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge anger, give her control, sit at a distance"},{"h":"Her account","b":"Neighbours, rotten-egg smell, missing cat, poisoning plot, since when"},{"h":"Belief in detail","b":"Conviction, evidence, letters, police, council, other explanations, spreading"},{"h":"Perceptions","b":"Smells, voices, visions; handle 'I'm not mad' gently"},{"h":"Mood and cognition","b":"Low mood, sleep, eating, memory, orientation, hearing, eyesight"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Confronting neighbours, weapons, self-harm, not eating for fear, vulnerability"},{"h":"Mental state summary","b":"Irritable, guarded, fixed persecutory delusion, olfactory symptoms, no insight"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"What happens next, physical tests, her concerns, questions"}],"bps":{"pre":"Maureen shouted at first but settled when you listened. She is certain her neighbours buried cats in their garden, explaining the rotten-egg smell, took her beloved cat last year and are now poisoning her because she knows too much. She has written warning letters and rung the police repeatedly. She snapped 'I'm not mad' when asked about voices. Memory seemed intact. You summarised a fixed persecutory belief that is distressing her.","ask":"So you'll give me pills to say I'm mad, will you?","lead":"No, I'm not calling you mad; I can see you've been living with a lot of fear, and I want to help with that.","bio":["Rule out other causes: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, TFTs, calcium, B12, glucose, CT or MRI brain, ACE-III","Hearing and vision checks: deafness is linked with late-onset psychosis","If she agrees: low-dose amisulpride 50 mg daily or risperidone 0.5 mg daily, increased slowly","Baseline ECG, weight, lipids, glucose, prolactin; warn of stiffness, dizziness and falls"],"psych":["Engagement first: one consistent nurse, no arguing about the belief, focus on her fear and sleep","CBT for psychosis adapted for older adults, at least 16 sessions, focusing on distress and safety","Activities on the ward and later a day centre to reduce isolation"],"social":["Older adult CMHT care coordinator to follow her up at home","Gas engineer safety check at her home to rule out a real leak (gas emergency line 0800 111 999)","Audiology referral and hearing aids if needed","With her consent, share a plan with the police and council so repeated calls are handled consistently"],"safety":["Agree what she will do instead of confronting the neighbours if she feels threatened","If she refuses treatment and risks rise, assess under the Mental Health Act"]}},{"sid":"M5.14","cid":"c-t-18","area":"Old age","title":"A house full of journals","who":"Patient","task":"Dr Leonard Okoro, a 67-year-old retired pathologist, has been referred to the older adult outpatient clinic by his GP, who visited the house and was worried about the state of his home. He lives alone in a large house since his wife died eight years ago. He has three adult children; his youngest daughter has raised concerns with the GP. He is puzzled about the referral but curious to meet a psychiatrist. Please take a history and carry out a mental state assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Dr Okoro?","p":"I am. Rather intrigued why I'm here, to be honest."},{"d":"Thank you for coming. Your GP mentioned some worries about how things are at home.","p":"Ha, my daughter's doing, no doubt."},{"d":"I'd like to hear about life at home in your own words.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, collegial respect, reason for referral, curiosity not judgement"},{"h":"The collection","b":"Journals, how many, since when, wife's link, value, pride"},{"h":"Impact on home","b":"Rooms unusable, kitchen, oven unused, fire, trips, hygiene, pests"},{"h":"Discarding and insight","b":"Distress at throwing out, acquiring more, daughter's view, insight"},{"h":"Other explanations","b":"OCD checking, grief, depression, psychosis, memory, frontal changes"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Fire, falls in dark, blocked exits, self-neglect, isolation, gas oven"},{"h":"Mental state","b":"Appearance, rapport, mood, no psychosis, cognition, fair insight"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Hoarding explained, safety first, next steps, family involvement"}],"bps":{"pre":"Leonard lit up talking about his collection, which began with his late wife's psychiatry journals and has grown to over a thousand. He was guarded about numbers at first, then admitted piles fill every room, a small kitchen fire destroyed some, he no longer uses the gas oven and he trips over stacks at night. Mood, sleep and memory seemed fine. You explained this sounds like hoarding disorder.","ask":"So you want me to throw them all out, I suppose?","lead":"No, nobody is going to clear your house without you; the aim is to make it safe while respecting what the journals mean to you.","bio":["No medication first-line for hoarding; SSRI only if clear depression or OCD develops","Cognitive screen (ACE-III) and bloods; brain imaging if frontal signs, as late-onset hoarding can mark early dementia","GP review of falls risk and eyesight given trips in the dark"],"psych":["Motivational interviewing first to build his own reasons for change","CBT for hoarding via psychology, about 20-26 weekly sessions, including practice sorting and letting go at home","Bereavement support (Cruse) as the collection is tied to his wife","Keep-donate plan: e.g. offering duplicates to a medical library so they stay valued"],"social":["Fire and Rescue Service Safe and Well visit: smoke alarms, clear exits, fire-retardant advice","Clutter Image Rating at home visits to track progress","Adult social care involvement under Care Act self-neglect guidance if risk rises, with his agreement","Family meeting with his daughter to agree a realistic pace","Hoarding UK peer support group"],"safety":["Keep the gas oven off until the kitchen is clear; working smoke alarms; torch by the bed"]}},{"sid":"M5.15","cid":"c-u-22","area":"Old age","title":"Memory clinic: a full cognitive check","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Kenneth Lau, a 70-year-old retired restaurant owner, was sent to the memory clinic by his GP. Over the past six months he has been forgetting friends' names and his grandchildren's birthdays, which he finds embarrassing. His wife has come with him but is waiting outside. Please explain the purpose and then carry out a structured cognitive assessment with him, covering all the main domains.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Mr Lau?","p":"Yes, Kenneth."},{"d":"Thanks, Kenneth. I'd like to do some memory and thinking tasks with you today.","p":"Like a test?"},{"d":"Some will feel easy, some harder; that's normal. Do you have your glasses and hearing aid? Shall we start?","p":"Go on then."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, explain purpose, consent, glasses, hearing, quiet room"},{"h":"Brief history","b":"Six months, names, birthdays, long-term memory, mood, alcohol"},{"h":"Attention and orientation","b":"Day, date, place, serial sevens, months backwards"},{"h":"Memory","b":"Register a name and address, delayed recall, recent events, famous people"},{"h":"Language","b":"Naming, repetition, reading, writing two sentences, three-stage command"},{"h":"Visuospatial and executive","b":"Cube or pentagons, clock, letter fluency, similarities, Luria sequence"},{"h":"Feedback","b":"Honest, kind answer to 'how did I do', no premature diagnosis"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Bloods, scan, follow-up with wife, questions"}],"bps":{"pre":"Kenneth was polite and tried hard. He did not know which building he was in, could not recall any of the three words after the distracting tasks and became flustered writing two sentences, though his long-ago memories were vivid. At the end he leaned forward and asked if he had done well. You explained there were some difficulties worth looking into further.","ask":"So what happens now? Is something wrong with my brain?","lead":"Some parts were harder for you, so the next step is to find out why before we put any label on it.","bio":["Bloods for reversible causes: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, calcium, glucose or HbA1c, TFTs, B12, folate","MRI brain (or CT) to look for vascular change or atrophy","If Alzheimer's is diagnosed: donepezil 5 mg at night for 4 weeks then 10 mg; check pulse first, warn of nausea, diarrhoea, vivid dreams","Review any anticholinergic medicines and alcohol intake"],"psych":["Feedback appointment with his wife present to discuss results and diagnosis","Cognitive stimulation therapy group, 14 sessions, if mild dementia is confirmed","Memory aids: diary, phone reminders, calendar for birthdays, OT advice","Mood screen; NHS Talking Therapies if anxiety or low mood about the diagnosis"],"social":["Alzheimer's Society dementia adviser after diagnosis","If dementia is diagnosed he must tell the DVLA and his car insurer","Lasting Power of Attorney for health and finances while he can decide","Carers' assessment for his wife"]}},{"sid":"M5.16","cid":"c-s-33","area":"Physical examination","title":"Parietal lobe bedside assessment","who":"Patient","task":"Miss Edna Morrison, a 75-year-old retired seamstress, is an inpatient on the older adult ward after struggling to cope at home with worsening memory. She has recently been diagnosed with vascular dementia and had a CT head scan, the result of which she has not yet been told. She is cooperative and keen to help. Please carry out a bedside physical assessment of her parietal lobe function, explaining each step to her as you go.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Is it Miss Morrison?","p":"Yes, love. Edna."},{"d":"Lovely to meet you, Edna. I'd like to do some short tasks to see how parts of your brain are working.","p":"Alright."},{"d":"Some involve touching your hands. Is that okay? Are you right- or left-handed?","p":"Right."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, hand hygiene, consent, handedness, glasses, seated comfortably"},{"h":"Gerstmann features","b":"Finger naming, right-left orientation, simple sums, writing a sentence"},{"h":"Non-dominant features","b":"Neglect, dressing apraxia, denial of deficit, copying pentagons or clock"},{"h":"Cortical sensation","b":"Objects in hand eyes closed, numbers on palm, two-point, sensory inattention"},{"h":"Apraxia","b":"Wave goodbye, mime combing hair, folding letter into envelope"},{"h":"Visual fields","b":"Confrontation testing for lower quadrant loss, visual inattention"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Thank her, hand hygiene, findings: acalculia and agraphia suggest dominant parietal"}],"bps":null},{"sid":"M5.17","cid":"c-s-34","area":"Physical examination","title":"Temporal lobe bedside assessment after seizure","who":"Patient","task":"Miss Grace Adebayo, a 68-year-old retired librarian, was admitted to the elderly care ward this morning after her first ever seizure at home. She is a little confused and frightened about what has happened, but willing to help. The medical team have asked for psychiatric input into her cognition. Please carry out a bedside physical assessment of her temporal lobe function, explaining each part as you go.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Is it Miss Adebayo?","p":"Yes. I'm a bit muddled, sorry."},{"d":"Please don't apologise, Grace; you've had a frightening morning.","p":""},{"d":"I'd like to try some memory and language tasks with you. We can stop whenever you like. Is that alright?","p":"Okay."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, reassure, hand hygiene, consent, glasses, hearing, handedness"},{"h":"Memory","b":"Name and address registration and recall, breakfast, journey here, long-past events"},{"h":"Dominant language","b":"Comprehension of commands, reading aloud, writing a sentence, naming"},{"h":"Non-dominant function","b":"Recognising familiar faces, tunes, visual memory of a drawing"},{"h":"Visual fields","b":"Confrontation testing for upper quadrant loss"},{"h":"Seizure-related symptoms","b":"Déjà vu, odd smells or tastes, rising stomach feeling, lost time"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Thank her, hand hygiene, reading, writing and recall problems suggest dominant temporal; EEG, MRI"}],"bps":null},{"sid":"M6.1","cid":"c-s-35","area":"Neurodevelopmental","title":"Struggling at school: is it ADHD?","who":"Mother","task":"Mrs Sinead Gallagher has come to the CAMHS outpatient clinic about her 12-year-old son Callum, who is in Year 8 at a local secondary school. His teachers report that he is falling behind and often in trouble in lessons, and he has recently been moved into lower sets. Callum is at school today. Please take a collateral history from Mrs Gallagher with a view to establishing a likely diagnosis, and explore her worries.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the children's team. Are you Mrs Gallagher?","p":"Yes, Sinead. I'm at my wits' end."},{"d":"I'm glad you've come, Sinead. It sounds like a tough time for you both.","p":""},{"d":"Could you tell me what's been happening with Callum at school?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge worry, confidentiality, plan for the session"},{"h":"School difficulties","b":"Not listening, distracted, calling out, leaving seat or class, detentions, sets"},{"h":"Core ADHD features","b":"Attention, hyperactivity, impulsivity, at home and school, since before 12"},{"h":"Development and health","b":"Pregnancy, prematurity, milestones, hearing, vision, sleep, epilepsy, heart"},{"h":"Rule out alternatives","b":"Learning difficulty, autism traits, low mood, anxiety, defiance, drugs, trauma"},{"h":"Family and social","b":"Parenting, discipline, family history ADHD, siblings, friendships, police contact"},{"h":"Risk and impact","b":"Exclusion, self-esteem, school refusal, accidents, roads, bullying, self-harm"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Possible ADHD, rating scales, school report, her fears answered"}],"bps":{"pre":"Sinead described Callum as a whirlwind since toddlerhood, born four weeks early, who cannot settle to anything for more than fifteen minutes, blurts things out then apologises, and forgets homework. Primary school noticed similar things but secondary school is far worse. There are no reading, social or mood problems. You explained the picture fits ADHD and needs a formal assessment.","ask":"If it is ADHD, will he have to go on tablets?","lead":"Not necessarily straight away; the first step is to confirm it, and then we'd decide together what helps him most.","bio":["Complete assessment: Conners rating scales from parents and school, school observation, then a diagnostic review","Before any medicine: height, weight, BP and heart rate on centile charts; cardiac history; ECG only if indicated","If confirmed and impairing: methylphenidate first-line (NICE NG87), e.g. modified-release 18 mg each morning, titrated over 4-6 weeks","Warn about reduced appetite, poor sleep, headache and stomach ache; monitor height 6-monthly and weight 3-6 monthly"],"psych":["ADHD information session for Sinead, Callum's father and Callum together","Group parenting programme focused on ADHD, roughly 10-12 weekly sessions","Work on Callum's self-esteem and school dread; CBT if anxiety persists"],"social":["School SENCo: SEN support plan, seat near the front, movement breaks, homework planner","Request an Education, Health and Care needs assessment if support is not enough","Ask the school to use reasonable adjustments and avoid exclusion while assessment is under way","Signpost to the ADHD Foundation and local parent support groups"],"safety":["Road safety and supervision given his impulsivity"]}},{"sid":"M6.2","cid":"c-s-36","area":"Neurodevelopmental","title":"ADHD explained to a father","who":"Father","task":"Mr Raymond Ho is the father of Ethan, aged 9, who was referred to the CAMHS community team this year after his school and parents raised concerns about poor concentration and over-activity. Ethan often acts before thinking, which has put him at risk, including near roads. After a detailed history from both parents, observation in clinic and Conners questionnaires, he meets the criteria for ADHD. He struggles to follow instructions and cannot stay seated beyond a few minutes, and is described as a bundle of energy. He has no other diagnoses. Please explain ADHD and its management to Mr Ho and answer his questions.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the children's team. Are you Mr Ho, Ethan's dad?","p":"Yes, Raymond."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Raymond. I know you'd like to hear more about Ethan's assessment.","p":""},{"d":"Could you tell me what you already know about ADHD, and what's on your mind?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, purpose, his understanding, his worries, plan"},{"h":"What ADHD is","b":"Attention, over-activity, impulsivity, brain development, common, not bad parenting"},{"h":"How we decided","b":"Both parents' history, clinic observation, Conners scales, home and school"},{"h":"Risks to Ethan","b":"Roads, accidents, falling behind, friendships, self-esteem"},{"h":"Non-drug help","b":"Parent programme, routines, praise, school plan, road safety teaching"},{"h":"Medication","b":"Methylphenidate, how it works, heart and growth checks, side effects"},{"h":"Outlook","b":"Many improve, some continue into adulthood, strengths, regular review"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, teach-back, leaflet, follow-up date, contact number"}],"bps":{"pre":"Raymond listened carefully and said he had wondered if it was his fault for being too soft. You explained ADHD as a difference in brain development, not a parenting failure, and how Ethan's difficulties showed up at home, school and in clinic. He nodded at the road-safety examples, describing Ethan darting off the kerb last month.","ask":"So what's the plan? Are you going to put him on Ritalin?","lead":"Medication may well help, but let's look at the whole plan, because tablets work best alongside other support.","bio":["Methylphenidate first-line from age 5 (NICE NG87): e.g. immediate-release 5 mg once or twice daily, titrated weekly over 4-6 weeks, or modified-release 18 mg mornings","Baseline and follow-up: height, weight, BP and heart rate on centile charts; ECG only if cardiac history or symptoms","Side effects to watch: poor appetite, trouble sleeping, headache, tummy ache, low mood; give with breakfast","If methylphenidate fails after an adequate trial, lisdexamfetamine is next"],"psych":["ADHD-focused parent programme for Raymond and his wife, about 10-12 weekly group sessions","Home strategies: short clear instructions, routines, visual timetables, immediate praise","Ongoing CAMHS reviews to check progress and Ethan's self-esteem"],"social":["School SENCo: individual support plan, movement breaks, seat away from distractions","Teacher training resources from the ADHD Foundation shared with school","Check eligibility for Disability Living Allowance for children","Local ADHD parent support groups"],"safety":["Hold hands near roads and supervise around traffic and water until his impulse control improves"]}},{"sid":"M6.3","cid":"c-s-37","area":"Neurodevelopmental","title":"Late speech and lining up toys","who":"Mother","task":"Mrs Natalie Brooks has come to the CAMHS clinic about her 5-year-old son Oscar, an only child who started Reception class last year. She is worried that his speech has always been different from other children's and that he does not seem to connect with others in the same way. His hearing and vision tests were normal. Please take a collateral history from Mrs Brooks about Oscar's development and difficulties. A risk assessment is not needed today.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the children's team. Are you Mrs Brooks, Oscar's mum?","p":"Yes, Natalie. I'm so worried about him."},{"d":"I can hear that, Natalie. Thank you for bringing him to us.","p":""},{"d":"Could you start by telling me about his speech and what you've noticed?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge worry, confidentiality, purpose"},{"h":"Speech and language","b":"Late babbling, first words, made-up words, uses speech only for needs"},{"h":"Social interaction","b":"Eye contact, sharing interest, response to name, affection, plays alone"},{"h":"Play and routines","b":"Spinning wheels, lining up, intense interests, distress at change, tantrums"},{"h":"Sensory and behaviour","b":"Textures, food fussiness, clothing, noises, head-hitting during meltdowns"},{"h":"Development and health","b":"Pregnancy, birth, milestones, hearing and vision normal, sleep, seizures"},{"h":"Family and school","b":"Family history, school progress, sets, friendships, parents' coping"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Possible autism, formal assessment, her worries, questions"}],"bps":{"pre":"Natalie described Oscar talking late, using invented words and now speaking mainly to ask for things. He avoids eye contact, pulls away from cuddles, spins the wheels of toy cars and lines them up, and once only played with dinosaurs. Changes to meals or routine trigger long tantrums when he hits his head. Pregnancy and other milestones were normal. You explained these features suggest autism and need a full assessment.","ask":"Does that mean he's autistic? Is there anything that can help him?","lead":"It does sound like autism may be part of the picture, and there is a lot of help we can put in place whatever the assessment shows.","bio":["No medication treats core autism; medicines only for specific co-existing problems","Paediatric review: physical check, consider genetic testing (microarray, fragile X) if learning disability or dysmorphic features","Look for pain causes of head-hitting: ear infections, constipation, dental problems","Sleep difficulties: sleep routine first; melatonin only via specialist if this fails"],"psych":["Multidisciplinary autism assessment (paediatrician, speech and language therapist, psychologist) using ADOS-2 and ADI-R","Speech and language therapy with visual supports such as picture exchange","Parent-mediated social communication programme over several months","Clinical psychologist to work out triggers for meltdowns and plan visual timetables and warnings before change"],"social":["School SENCo and request for an Education, Health and Care needs assessment","Occupational therapy assessment for sensory sensitivities","National Autistic Society EarlyBird parent programme","Carers' assessment and information on Disability Living Allowance for children"]}},{"sid":"M6.4","cid":"c-s-38","area":"Neurodevelopmental","title":"Autism diagnosis explained to a mother","who":"Mother","task":"Ms Helen Fraser is the mother of Isla, aged 14, who has just finished an autism assessment. Her parents sought help because Isla seemed different socially from other girls her age: her friendships centre on shared hobbies and she shows little curiosity about other people's lives, including her mother's. Her Social Communication Questionnaire score was 16, just above the cut-off of 15, and an autism-specific observational assessment followed; she narrowly met the criteria for autism spectrum disorder. Isla has agreed that her mother can attend alone and that all information can be shared. Please explain the diagnosis and its management to Ms Fraser.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the children's team. Are you Ms Fraser, Isla's mum?","p":"Yes, Helen."},{"d":"Thanks, Helen. Isla has said she's happy for me to share everything with you today.","p":""},{"d":"Before I explain the results, could you tell me how you've been feeling about the assessment?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, Isla's consent to share, her expectations and feelings"},{"h":"The result","b":"Autism spectrum disorder, narrowly met, SCQ 16 above 15, observation"},{"h":"What autism means","b":"Social communication, narrow interests, sensory, lifelong difference, strengths"},{"h":"Girls and autism","b":"Masking, later diagnosis, exhaustion, anxiety, friendships by interest"},{"h":"Support options","b":"Post-diagnostic group, school plan, social skills, sensory adjustments"},{"h":"Co-existing problems","b":"Anxiety, low mood, eating, sleep, self-harm, ADHD to watch"},{"h":"Telling Isla and others","b":"Explaining to Isla, her choice to disclose, siblings, school"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, teach-back, leaflets, National Autistic Society, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Helen said she half expected the result but felt guilty for finding Isla hard to read. You explained autism as a lifelong difference in how Isla processes social information and the world, with real strengths such as focus and loyalty, and why girls are often diagnosed later. Helen asked whether 'narrowly' means it might be a mistake, and you said the diagnosis was made carefully.","ask":"So what now? Is there any treatment for it?","lead":"Autism isn't something we cure, but understanding it opens the door to practical support for Isla at home and school.","bio":["No medicine treats autism itself","Watch for co-existing anxiety or depression; if moderate to severe depression later, CAMHS may offer fluoxetine 10 mg daily, rising to 20 mg after a week, alongside therapy","Sleep problems: routine first; melatonin only via specialist if needed","Routine health checks with the GP; screen for eating difficulties given restricted preferences"],"psych":["Post-diagnostic psychoeducation group for parents and a separate one for young people","Help Isla understand her diagnosis at her own pace, using her interests","CBT adapted for autism if anxiety develops, usually 12-16 sessions","Social skills group to practise conversation and friendship skills"],"social":["School SENCo: autism support plan, quiet space, adjusted test arrangements, named key adult","National Autistic Society resources and local parent support groups","Isla chooses who to tell; school told only with her agreement","Plan for transition to adult services and further education from around age 16"]}},{"sid":"M6.5","cid":"c-u-23","area":"Child and adolescent","title":"Paracetamol overdose at 16: risk assessment","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Amira Hussain, a 16-year-old Year 11 pupil, was brought to A&E by ambulance this evening with her parents after deliberately taking an overdose of paracetamol. You are the out-of-hours mental health liaison doctor. She has already spoken to the A&E doctors and nurses and is finding the department overwhelming. Please take a history from Amira about the overdose and her circumstances, and assess her current level of risk.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Amira?","p":"Yeah."},{"d":"I know you've told your story a few times already tonight. I'm sorry; it's a lot.","p":""},{"d":"This stays between us, unless I'm worried you're not safe. Could we talk about what happened?","p":"Okay."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge fatigue, confidentiality limits, see alone"},{"h":"The overdose","b":"What, how many, when, where from, planned, intent, note, alone"},{"h":"After the overdose","b":"Went to bed hoping not to wake, told parents, regret, view now"},{"h":"What led up to it","b":"Online bullying, three months low mood, school, friends, family"},{"h":"Mood and mental state","b":"Depression symptoms, sleep, appetite, hopelessness, psychosis, eating"},{"h":"Self-harm and risk","b":"Arm scratching, past attempts, current thoughts, plans, access to tablets"},{"h":"Protective factors","b":"Parents, future plans, reasons to live, help-seeking, safeguarding"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Risk formulation, medical treatment, parents involved, plan"}],"bps":{"pre":"Amira kept her arms folded at first but softened. She took 16 paracetamol at 7 pm in her bedroom after girls who have bullied her online for three months posted cruel comments. She went to sleep hoping not to wake, then felt sick, panicked and told her parents. She now regrets it, has no current plan but still has fleeting thoughts of not being here, and has been scratching her arm.","ask":"Do I have to stay here? What happens to me now?","lead":"Tonight is about keeping your body safe first, and then making sure you have proper help with what's been happening.","bio":["Paracetamol level at 4 hours post-ingestion with LFTs, INR, U&Es, glucose; acetylcysteine if at or above the treatment line","Stay on the paediatric or medical ward until medically cleared","Clean and dress the forearm scratches","No antidepressant tonight; if moderate to severe depression persists after therapy, CAMHS may add fluoxetine 10 mg daily, rising to 20 mg"],"psych":["Full psychosocial assessment with Amira alone and with her parents before discharge (NICE NG225)","CAMHS follow-up within a few days of discharge","CBT for depression, around 8-12 weekly sessions, or family therapy","Coping skills for urges to self-harm, written safety plan"],"social":["Parents involved with her knowledge; safeguarding check of school and home","School designated safeguarding lead to act on the bullying","Report abusive posts to the platform; police if threats or images involved","Childline 0800 1111, Shout text 85258, Papyrus HOPELINE247"],"safety":["Parents to lock away all medicines and sharp items at home","Call 999 or attend A&E if she takes anything else or feels she cannot stay safe"]}},{"sid":"M6.6","cid":"c-s-39","area":"Child and adolescent","title":"Overdose at 15: plan with the charge nurse","who":"Paediatric A&E charge nurse","task":"Ms Jasmine Okonkwo, a 15-year-old girl, is in paediatric A&E overnight after an ibuprofen overdose. A&E doctors have reviewed her, bloods and physical checks are reassuring and no treatment is needed. This is her first contact with mental health services. Late this evening a friend let slip that everyone had a group chat without her; after weeks of low mood she felt it was unbearable and that she would be better off dead. She has not harmed herself since arriving and wants to go home. Her parents are divorced; her mother, who has sole custody, had gone out and is now travelling in. Charge nurse Owen Pritchard is planning the shift. Please discuss the likely outcome and management options with him.","opening":[{"d":"Hi, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors on the liaison team tonight. Are you Owen?","p":"Yes. I need to know what's happening with Jasmine."},{"d":"Of course. I've read the notes and will see her once her mum arrives.","p":""},{"d":"Let me give you the likely outcome and options so you can plan beds and staffing.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, role, his needs for planning, brief SBAR structure"},{"h":"Situation and background","b":"Ibuprofen overdose, medically cleared, first presentation, trigger, weeks low"},{"h":"Current risk picture","b":"Stated wish to die, no further attempts, wants home, unknown factors"},{"h":"Assessment plan","b":"See alone and with mother, depression, intent, safeguarding, home supervision"},{"h":"Likely outcome","b":"Home with mother and safety plan, CAMHS follow-up within days"},{"h":"Other options","b":"Paediatric ward overnight, Tier 4 bed rarely, Mental Health Act unlikely"},{"h":"Nursing needs now","b":"Observation level, remove ligatures and sharps, don't leave unaccompanied"},{"h":"Hand over and close","b":"Timings, documentation, who to call, update after assessment"}],"bps":{"pre":"Owen explained that cubicles are full and he needs to know whether to hold a paediatric bed. You summarised the overdose, the group-chat trigger and the weeks of low mood, and said you would see Jasmine with and without her mother. You noted that, provided risk is manageable and her mother can supervise, the most likely outcome is discharge home with close CAMHS follow-up.","ask":"So, is she going home tonight or am I finding her a bed?","lead":"Most likely home with her mum, but I'll confirm once I've seen them both; it would help to keep a paediatric bed in mind.","bio":["No further medical treatment needed; confirm the ingested dose in mg/kg, renal function and GI symptoms are documented","No psychotropic medication tonight; low mood is treated first with psychological therapy","Mother to remove all medicines, including over-the-counter painkillers, from the home"],"psych":["Full psychosocial assessment once her mother arrives, seeing Jasmine alone and together (NICE NG225)","CAMHS follow-up within 48-72 hours of discharge","CBT for low mood, around 8-12 sessions, if depression is confirmed","Joint safety plan written with Jasmine and her mother before she leaves"],"social":["Safeguarding check: known to children's social care, supervision at home when it happened","School pastoral lead informed with consent to support friendships","Crisis contacts given: Childline 0800 1111, Shout text 85258, Papyrus HOPELINE247","Paediatric ward admission overnight if her mother cannot supervise or risk rises"],"safety":["Until assessed: within sight of staff, belongings checked, not left alone in the toilet for long","If she tries to leave before assessment, staff stop her under common law duty of care and call me"]}},{"sid":"M6.7","cid":"c-s-40","area":"Child and adolescent","title":"Headache and a tracker in his brain","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Jordan Okafor, a 16-year-old college student, came to A&E with a headache that started yesterday. Physical checks and investigations have found nothing worrying, but the A&E doctor is concerned by what Jordan believes is causing the headache and has asked for a psychiatric review. He lives at home with his parents. Please take a history from Jordan with a view to reaching a diagnosis.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Jordan?","p":"Yeah. Is anyone else listening?"},{"d":"Just us. The door's closed. I'm here to hear about your headache and how you've been.","p":""},{"d":"Can you tell me, your way, what's been going on?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, privacy, reassure, explain role, let him lead"},{"h":"His explanation","b":"Headache, implanted device, government, since when, evidence, conviction"},{"h":"Delusions and passivity","b":"Monitoring, thought broadcast, insertion, control of body, reference"},{"h":"Hallucinations","b":"Voices about him, commenting, commanding, location; muttering in room"},{"h":"Thought form","b":"Made-up words, derailment, clarify gently without challenge"},{"h":"Mood, drugs, health","b":"Mood, sleep, cannabis, other drugs, development, family history psychosis"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Dumped phone and laptop, act on voices, self-harm, others, college drop-out"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Likely first-episode psychosis, involve parents, next steps"}],"bps":{"pre":"Jordan kept glancing behind him and muttered under his breath. He explained the government implanted a tracker in his brain while he slept, after he threw his phone and laptop in the canal; it lets them read his thoughts and share them. For a week he has heard voices saying he is watched. Once he used a word you didn't recognise. You explained this sounds like a first episode of psychosis.","ask":"If you don't believe me, what are you actually going to do?","lead":"I believe how frightening this is for you, and I'd like to help you feel safer and sleep better while we work out what's going on.","bio":["Bloods: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, TFTs, glucose, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin; urine drug screen; consider MRI if neurological signs","Antipsychotic after discussion with Jordan and parents: aripiprazole 2 mg daily, increasing to 10 mg over about 4 days","Baseline and ongoing: weight, BMI, waist, BP, pulse weekly for 6 weeks then at 12 weeks; ECG if indicated","Warn about restlessness (akathisia), nausea and sleep changes"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis, individual, at least 16 sessions","Family intervention with his parents, at least 10 sessions over 3-12 months","Psychoeducation about psychosis and recovery, at his pace"],"social":["Early Intervention in Psychosis team referral, assessed within 2 weeks with a named care coordinator","College liaison for a phased return and reasonable adjustments","Carers' support and information for his parents","Support to stay off cannabis"],"safety":["Do not discharge alone; parents informed and involved tonight","Call 999 if he acts on voices or tries to harm himself or others"]}},{"sid":"M6.8","cid":"c-s-41","area":"Child and adolescent","title":"First psychosis at 16: talking to Dad","who":"Father","task":"Mr Patrick Doherty has asked to speak with you after you assessed his 16-year-old son Liam in A&E today. Paramedics were called by passers-by and found Liam frightened, pacing up and down the street, talking loudly to himself and barely making sense. In A&E his blood tests, physical checks, ECG and CT head were normal. You think this is most likely a first psychotic episode; Liam has no insight and lacks capacity to decide about the treatment plan. You think he needs admission for further assessment and treatment and are considering detention under the Mental Health Act. Liam lives with both parents; his mother is working an ICU shift and asked you to speak to Patrick. Please explain Liam's diagnosis and plan to him.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Mr Doherty, Liam's dad?","p":"Patrick. Please, is he alright?"},{"d":"He's safe and being looked after. I know this has been a frightening day.","p":""},{"d":"Liam's mum asked me to update you. Can I first ask what you've heard so far?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, he is safe, what Patrick knows, confidentiality, involving Liam's mother"},{"h":"What happened today","b":"Paramedics, pacing, talking to himself, confused speech, frightened"},{"h":"Tests and diagnosis","b":"Normal bloods, ECG, CT; first-episode psychosis explained simply"},{"h":"Background from Dad","b":"Recent changes, drugs, stress, sleep, family history, school"},{"h":"Capacity and admission","b":"No insight, lacks capacity, why hospital, adolescent unit"},{"h":"Mental Health Act","b":"Section 2, AMHP, parents consulted, rights, Tribunal, advocate"},{"h":"Treatment and outlook","b":"Antipsychotic, therapy, EIP team, many recover well"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, teach-back, visiting, contact numbers, questions"}],"bps":{"pre":"Patrick listened, white-faced, and said Liam had been quieter and up all night for a few weeks but they put it down to his age. You explained that tests ruled out a physical cause and that Liam is experiencing psychosis, frightened and unable to see he is unwell. You said he needs hospital, and that because he is refusing and lacks capacity, the Mental Health Act may be needed.","ask":"Sectioned? He's sixteen. Can't we just take him home and look after him?","lead":"I completely understand wanting him home, and I'll explain why hospital is the safest place for him right now and how you'll stay involved.","bio":["Antipsychotic once agreed in hospital, e.g. aripiprazole 2 mg daily titrated to 10 mg over a few days","Baseline physical monitoring: weight, BMI, waist, BP, pulse, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin, ECG","If very distressed: oral lorazepam 0.5-1 mg per the rapid tranquillisation policy","Urine drug screen and repeat checks; side effects such as restlessness and sleepiness explained"],"psych":["Daily nursing support and a calm environment on the adolescent unit","CBT for psychosis once settled, at least 16 sessions","Family intervention with Patrick and Liam's mother, at least 10 sessions","Education continued through the hospital school"],"social":["Section 2 assessment by an AMHP and two doctors; parents consulted as nearest relative","Age-appropriate bed on a CAMHS inpatient unit, which may be some distance away","Liam's rights: Mental Health Tribunal appeal and an independent mental health advocate","Early Intervention in Psychosis team follow-up after discharge","Carers' assessment and support for both parents"],"safety":["If Liam leaves A&E before assessment, staff will alert the police","Parents to call 999 if he becomes unsafe at any point"]}},{"sid":"M6.9","cid":"c-s-42","area":"Child and adolescent","title":"Teenager in A&E: high, fast and sleepless","who":"Patient","task":"Amira Kowalczyk-Reid is 15 and lives with her mum and stepdad. They brought her to A&E tonight because over roughly a week she has become unusually cheerful, restless and overfamiliar, talks rapidly, has stopped going to school because she thinks she has outgrown her teachers, and is sure she has worked out a groundbreaking physics theory. She is sleeping three or four hours a night, hardly eating and spending heavily on clothes and make-up. There is no previous mental illness, no low episodes and no drug or alcohol use reported. She agreed to talk to you on her own while her parents wait nearby. Please carry out a full mental state assessment with her, check for risk, and be ready to say what you think is going on.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Amira?","p":"Yep! Love your lanyard, by the way. Is this going to take ages?"},{"d":"Not too long, I promise. Your mum and stepdad are just outside. I want your own version of how things have been.","p":"Fine, but I'm honestly fine. Better than fine."},{"d":"Good to hear. I'll ask lots of quick questions, and you can tell me if any feel annoying.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, privacy and its limits, parents nearby, her view first"},{"h":"Appearance and behaviour","b":"Overfamiliar, restless, distractible, eye contact, disinhibition, clothes and make-up"},{"h":"Speech and mood","b":"Pressured, rhyming, hard to interrupt; elated or irritable; her own mood rating"},{"h":"Thoughts and beliefs","b":"Flight of ideas, physics theory as grandiose belief, conviction, outgrown teachers"},{"h":"Perception and cognition","b":"Voices, visions, passivity; orientation, attention with months backwards"},{"h":"Biological and context","b":"Sleep 3-4 hours, appetite, spending, sexual risk, drugs, school stopped"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Self-harm, suicide, exploitation online or in person, running away, vulnerability"},{"h":"Insight","b":"Does she feel unwell, accepts help, would she stay or come back"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Likely manic episode, next steps, involve parents, CAMHS on-call review"}],"bps":{"pre":"Amira talked fast, laughed, rhymed and kept drifting to her physics theory. She bristled when pressed on it. She had slept three hours a night all week and saw no problem. There were no voices, no drugs and no past low spells. Her parents were called in once she agreed, and her mum looked shaken by what she had heard.","ask":"Her mum: \"Is this some kind of breakdown? What do we do now?\"","lead":"From what Amira and you have told me, this looks like a manic episode, a period of very high mood and energy, and it is treatable.","bio":["Rule out physical and drug causes first: urine drug screen, FBC, U&Es, LFTs, thyroid function, glucose, and neurological check if anything is atypical.","If an antipsychotic is needed, the CAMHS consultant leads; aripiprazole is licensed from 13 for mania, usually started at 2 mg a day and built up over days towards 10 mg.","Baseline and ongoing physical monitoring: weight, height, BMI centile, waist, pulse, BP, fasting glucose or HbA1c, lipids and prolactin, then repeated at 6 and 12 weeks.","Short-term help with sleep may be offered, such as a low-dose benzodiazepine for a few nights under specialist advice.","Stop and review any antidepressant if one were ever started; avoid stimulants and caffeine-heavy drinks."],"psych":["Clear explanation to Amira and family of mania, early warning signs and why sleep matters, from the CAMHS care coordinator.","Once she settles, structured psychological work for bipolar-type illness: weekly sessions over several months on mood monitoring, routine and relapse prevention.","Family sessions with the CAMHS team to agree house rules on money, phone use and sleep while she is unwell.","Regular mood diary or app shared with her care coordinator to spot changes early."],"social":["Urgent referral to CAMHS crisis or intensive home treatment team; consider an adolescent inpatient bed if she cannot be kept safe at home.","School liaison: agree a medical absence and a supported return plan with the head of year and SENCo.","Practical limits on spending: parents to hold bank cards for now and check online orders.","Support for parents and stepdad, including Young Minds parent helpline and a carer contact in the team."],"safety":["Parents to stay with her tonight; if she leaves, becomes aggressive or talks of harming herself, call 999.","Out of hours, use NHS 111 option 2 or the CAMHS crisis line on the card given."]}},{"sid":"M7.1","cid":"c-t-19","area":"Neurodevelopmental","title":"Care home worker worried about a quiet resident","who":"Support worker","task":"The community learning disability team has been asked to see Josh Okonkwo-Banks, 32, who has a moderate learning disability and has lived in a supported care home for five years. Staff say he has changed over about six weeks: he stays in his room, speaks less, skips meals and has lost weight, has stopped going to the day centre he loved, wakes crying at night, and recently hit a staff member when encouraged out of his room. He has also been banging his head against the wall. His key worker, Declan Fairweather, has come in to discuss him. Please take a collateral history from Mr Fairweather and address any worries he raises.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the learning disability team. Are you Declan?","p":"Yes, Josh's key worker. Thanks for seeing me."},{"d":"Thank you for coming in. Josh has agreed we can talk about him. You know him well, so your view really matters.","p":"I've been really worried about him."},{"d":"Tell me what you've noticed, and then I'll ask some more detailed questions.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, role, consent from Josh, how long Declan has known him"},{"h":"Baseline Josh","b":"Usual personality, communication, skills, routine, what he enjoys"},{"h":"What has changed","b":"Six weeks: withdrawal, less speech, crying at night, lost interest, day centre stopped"},{"h":"Biological signs","b":"Sleep, meals missed, weight lost, energy, any pain, constipation, toothache"},{"h":"Other causes","b":"Physical illness, new meds, life events, staff changes, bereavement, abuse, psychosis, mania"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Head-banging, hitting staff, self-neglect, weight, safeguarding, bruising"},{"h":"Background","b":"Past mental health, epilepsy, medication, family, previous similar spells"},{"h":"Declan's concerns","b":"Staff safety, next incident, what staff should do now"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Likely depression, rule out physical, plan, review date, contact numbers"}],"bps":{"pre":"Declan described a once sociable man who now stays in his room, cries at night and has lost weight. There was no sign of voices, mania or abuse. No new medication or illness had been noted. Josh lost a favourite day centre worker two months ago. Declan was tired and shaken by the incident in which Josh hit out.","ask":"\"So what do you think it is, and what can we actually do for him?\"","lead":"From what you've described, this sounds most like depression, though we must check for a physical cause first.","bio":["GP physical review: weight, pulse, BP, dental and ear check, abdomen for constipation, plus bloods FBC, U&Es, LFTs, TFTs, glucose, calcium, B12 and folate.","If depression is confirmed, sertraline 25 mg each morning for a week, then 50 mg, reviewed at 2 and 4 weeks for agitation or sleep upset.","Start low, go slow: increase no faster than every 2-4 weeks and use a simple mood and sleep chart to judge effect.","Avoid adding antipsychotics just for behaviour (STOMP principles); review any existing sedating medication."],"psych":["Learning disability team psychologist or nurse to offer adapted behavioural activation, weekly, using pictures and short sessions.","Functional assessment and a positive behaviour support plan to understand head-banging and the hitting incident.","Easy-read information about feeling sad, shared with Josh by his key worker."],"social":["Gradual return to the day centre with a familiar staff member; restore valued routines such as meals in the dining room.","Speech and language therapist review of how Josh is communicating distress.","Care home manager to log incidents, review staffing and agree safe ways to prompt him from his room.","Weekly weight check and food and fluid chart, with dietitian input if loss continues."],"safety":["If Josh injures his head, stops eating or drinking, or staff cannot keep him safe, call the team the same day or 111 out of hours; 999 in an emergency."]}},{"sid":"M7.2","cid":"c-u-24","area":"Liaison","title":"Refusing hip surgery: does he have capacity?","who":"Patient","task":"Callum Ashworth-Nye, 35, has a mild learning disability (IQ around 63) and communicates well in speech. He came off his bike yesterday and broke his right hip. The orthopaedic team say he needs an urgent operation, otherwise the bone may not mend and its blood supply may fail. He has said no. His pain has now been controlled, he is calmer and he has agreed to speak with liaison psychiatry. He lives with his elderly mother, who is housebound. Please assess whether Mr Ashworth-Nye has capacity to decide to refuse the operation on his hip, and then explain the result of your assessment to him.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Callum?","p":"Yes. Can I go home now?"},{"d":"I know you want to get home. I'm here to talk about the hip operation and what you think about it.","p":"I don't want it."},{"d":"That's okay to say. I'll use simple words, and stop me if anything is confusing.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, why here, plain words, his wish to go home"},{"h":"His understanding","b":"What happened, what the bone doctors said, what the operation is"},{"h":"Fill the gaps","b":"Broken hip, blood supply, may not heal, operation risks, simple drawings"},{"h":"Retain and weigh","b":"Repeat back later, pros and cons, his reasons, faith, mother at home"},{"h":"Communicate decision","b":"Clear choice, consistent, any pressure from others"},{"h":"Diagnostic test","b":"Learning disability as impairment, link to any functional failure"},{"h":"Explain outcome","b":"Plain result, what happens next, his views still count"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Best interests or respect refusal, mother informed, surgeons, review"}],"bps":{"pre":"Callum explained that the doctors wanted to cut his leg, but he believed prayer would mend it, and it already hurt less. With pictures he could repeat that the bone would not join without surgery and the blood supply could fail. A few minutes later he could not recall this, and he said nothing bad could happen because God would fix it.","ask":"\"So can I go home now, then?\"","lead":"Callum, I can see how much your faith and your mum matter to you, so I'll be honest about what I've found.","bio":["Outcome: today he cannot retain or weigh the key facts despite support, so he lacks capacity for this decision; this is recorded on the capacity form.","Surgery may then go ahead in his best interests (Mental Capacity Act 2005, s4), taking his wishes, faith and his mother's views into account.","Keep pain well controlled: regular paracetamol 1 g four times daily, a fascia iliaca nerve block, and oral morphine as needed per surgical chart.","Avoid unnecessary sedation; any restraint must be necessary and proportionate, and is a last resort."],"psych":["Explain the outcome to Callum simply and kindly, with easy-read pictures of the hip and the operation.","Involve the hospital learning disability liaison nurse to prepare him for theatre and reduce fear.","Offer the hospital chaplain or his own faith leader, so prayer and treatment can sit side by side."],"social":["Best interests meeting today with surgeons, anaesthetist, liaison psychiatry and his mother by phone.","If no one appropriate can be consulted, instruct an Independent Mental Capacity Advocate (IMCA).","Social worker to arrange care for his housebound mother while he is in hospital.","Reassess capacity if anything changes; if he regains it, his refusal must be respected."],"safety":[]}},{"sid":"M7.3","cid":"c-t-20","area":"Neurodevelopmental","title":"Down's syndrome: forgetful, low and getting lost","who":"Mother","task":"Owen Brightwell, 39, has Down's syndrome and has always lived with his mother, Bernadette Lyle, who is his main carer. Over the past month she has noticed he seems low and increasingly forgetful, and she has asked the community learning disability team for an urgent review because he has changed so quickly. She has come to clinic with him and is waiting to talk to you while Owen has a drink with a nurse. Please take a collateral history from Ms Lyle with the aim of reaching a likely diagnosis.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Bernadette, Owen's mum?","p":"Yes. I'm sorry, I'm a bit on edge."},{"d":"That's completely understandable. Owen is happy for us to talk. You know him better than anyone.","p":"I just want to know what's happening to him."},{"d":"Let's start with what you've noticed, and I'll ask more as we go.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent from Owen, acknowledge her worry, plan"},{"h":"Usual Owen","b":"Baseline skills, speech, independence, day centre football, any baseline memory test"},{"h":"Memory and function","b":"Days muddled, losing blanket, got lost, buttons, recognises family"},{"h":"Mood and biology","b":"Low, withdrawn, less enjoyment, sleep broken, night walking, appetite"},{"h":"Other causes","b":"Thyroid, hearing, vision, sleep apnoea, pain, infection, medication, life events"},{"h":"Psychosis and seizures","b":"Voices, odd behaviour, new fits or jerks, continence"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Wandering, road safety, cooking, self-neglect, carer strain, safeguarding"},{"h":"Her coping","b":"Support at home, her health, respite, who else helps"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Possible early dementia or depression, tests, plan, next appointment"}],"bps":{"pre":"Bernadette described a cheerful man who in a few weeks began muddling days, misplacing his blanket, struggling with buttons and once getting lost outside. He was low, not enjoying football, up at night and eating less. There were no voices or fits. She had been keeping the door locked and was clearly exhausted.","ask":"\"Is it dementia? I've read people with Down's get it early. What happens now?\"","lead":"You're right that people with Down's syndrome are at higher risk, but we need to check for other treatable causes before we decide.","bio":["Bloods via GP or clinic: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, thyroid function, calcium, glucose, B12 and folate; urine dip for infection.","Hearing and eye checks, and a sleep study referral if he snores or stops breathing at night.","Brain scan (MRI, or CT if he cannot tolerate MRI) and baseline ECG before any medication.","If depression is the main finding: sertraline 25 mg daily, increasing to 50 mg after a week or two if tolerated.","If Alzheimer's is confirmed: donepezil 5 mg at night, increase to 10 mg after a month; check pulse for slowing and watch for diarrhoea."],"psych":["Formal memory and skills assessment with a Down's-specific tool (such as DSQIID or CAMDEX-DS) by the team psychologist, repeated over time.","Simple behavioural activation: planned enjoyable activities with his mum and day centre each week.","Visual timetable and familiar routines at home to reduce confusion."],"social":["Carer's assessment for Bernadette from adult social care, with respite options.","Herbert Protocol form and a door sensor or telecare alarm to protect against getting lost.","Occupational therapy home visit for dressing aids and kitchen safety.","Discuss future planning: who would make decisions if his capacity changes."],"safety":[]}},{"sid":"M7.4","cid":"c-s-43","area":"Neurodevelopmental","title":"Starting ADHD medicine: what and why","who":"Patient","task":"Priya Halloran, 24, has a moderate learning disability. Last month she told the community learning disability clinic she was finding it very hard to focus and sit still during her all-day weekend adult education course. Assessments have since confirmed ADHD and she knows the diagnosis. The plan is to start methylphenidate at a low dose and increase it as needed, most likely moving to a long-acting form that covers her whole class day. Before starting, her blood pressure, pulse and weight will be measured, then checked every six months. She has no known heart problems, but her family has a history of heart rhythm problems, so an ECG has been requested. Please explain the medication plan, how it will work, and why the checks and ECG are needed.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Priya?","p":"Yes. Why have I come back so soon?"},{"d":"Good question. Last time we found out you have ADHD. Today is about something that could help with that.","p":"Okay."},{"d":"I'll use simple words. If I say anything tricky, please stop me.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, reason for visit, simple words, check who is with her"},{"h":"ADHD recap","b":"What ADHD means for her, class struggles, her questions"},{"h":"The medicine","b":"Methylphenidate as stimulant, helps focus, start low, long-acting for class days"},{"h":"How it starts","b":"Low dose, small steps weekly, review, long-acting tablet each morning"},{"h":"Body checks","b":"Pulse, blood pressure, weight before starting, then every six months"},{"h":"The ECG","b":"Sticky pads, painless, family heart rhythm history, safe, quick"},{"h":"Side effects and worries","b":"Appetite, sleep, headache, how long to take, if it fails"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Teach-back, easy-read leaflet, carer involved, next appointment"}],"bps":{"pre":"Priya listened carefully and said ADHD meant her brain \"jumps about\". She was willing to try a tablet but grimaced at the word ECG and asked if the wires might hurt her heart. She wanted to know how long she would need it and what would happen if it didn't help.","ask":"\"So what will you actually do, and is the heart test going to hurt?\"","lead":"The heart test doesn't hurt at all, Priya, and I'll explain every step of the plan.","bio":["Before starting: pulse, blood pressure, height and weight on a growth or adult chart, and a 12-lead ECG because of family heart rhythm problems.","Start methylphenidate 5 mg in the morning, adding a lunchtime 5 mg; increase by 5-10 mg a week, reviewing effect and side effects.","Once the right dose is found, switch to a long-acting tablet once each morning (for example 18 mg, adjusted in steps within BNF limits) to cover the whole class day.","Recheck pulse and BP after each dose change, then pulse, BP and weight every six months; stop and review if pulse stays high or BP rises.","Warn about poor appetite, trouble sleeping, headaches and tummy ache; take it after breakfast and not late in the day."],"psych":["Easy-read ADHD and medication leaflet, gone through with her and her support worker.","Simple strategies with the learning disability nurse: short tasks, movement breaks, visual timetables.","Rating scale completed by Priya, carer and tutor before and after dose changes to judge benefit."],"social":["Reasonable adjustments at her adult education course: breaks, seat near the front, clear written steps.","Carer or support worker to help with a pill organiser and keep tablets locked away.","Learning disability nurse to accompany her to the ECG appointment to keep her calm."],"safety":[]}},{"sid":"M8.1","cid":"c-t-21","area":"Forensic","title":"Police custody: naked in the garden","who":"Patient","task":"Gareth Mulvaney, 47, is out of work and separated from his wife last year. He has been arrested on suspicion of indecent exposure after his neighbour, who works from home and can see his garden from her kitchen, told police he has been sunbathing naked most days for a fortnight. He is angry about the arrest and says he can do as he likes on his own property. The custody sergeant has asked the liaison and diversion service for a psychiatric view. Please take a history from Mr Mulvaney in the custody suite and carry out a risk assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists working with the custody team. Are you Gareth Mulvaney?","p":"Yes. And I don't see why I need a shrink."},{"d":"That's fair to ask. I'm not police. My job is to check how you are and whether you need any support.","p":"Fine. Go on."},{"d":"Anything you tell me may be shared if there's a risk to someone. Is that okay?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, independent role, limits of confidentiality, appropriate adult, solicitor"},{"h":"The incident","b":"Naked in garden, two weeks, neighbour's view, his explanation"},{"h":"Sexual motivation","b":"Arousal at being seen, erections, masturbation, fantasies about the neighbour"},{"h":"Past pattern","b":"Earlier exposure, age started, other paraphilias, escalation, convictions"},{"h":"Mental state","b":"Mood since separation, psychosis, mania, drink, drugs, cognition, learning difficulty"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Approach or contact with neighbour, stalking, sexual violence, children, self-harm"},{"h":"Protective factors","b":"Insight, shame, remorse, supports, work, willingness to engage"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Likely exhibitionism, fit for interview, diversion advice, plan"}],"bps":{"pre":"Once Gareth saw the assessment was not a trap he opened up. Since the separation he had become attracted to his neighbour. Being seen naked aroused him, and he had exposed himself to strangers in his teens. He had fantasised about going round but not acted. He was not psychotic or depressed, and accepted she might feel frightened.","ask":"\"So what happens to me now? Am I some kind of pervert?\"","lead":"No, Gareth; what you've described has a name, and there is help available alongside whatever the police decide.","bio":["No medication is first-line for exhibitionism; treatment is mainly psychological.","If urges stay strong despite therapy, a forensic psychiatrist may offer an SSRI such as fluoxetine 20 mg daily, rising to 40-60 mg, to reduce sexual preoccupation.","Screen for and treat any depression or harmful drinking since the separation; check AUDIT score.","Antilibidinal hormones are reserved for high-risk cases and only with specialist input and full consent."],"psych":["Assessment by a forensic psychologist, then group or individual CBT-based sex offending work (for example Horizon via probation if convicted).","Work on triggers, fantasies, empathy for the neighbour and a relapse prevention plan.","Self-referral to Stop It Now for confidential advice in the meantime."],"social":["Liaison and diversion report to custody: fit to be interviewed, no appropriate adult needed, no mental illness requiring admission.","Advise police and court of the risk picture; bail conditions may include no contact with the neighbour.","If convicted, probation and MAPPA arrangements may apply.","GP letter and offer of support with low mood or drinking after the separation."],"safety":["Police to consider the neighbour's safety and inform her of bail conditions.","Gareth to call his GP, NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans on 116 123 if he feels hopeless after release."]}},{"sid":"M8.2","cid":"c-u-25","area":"Forensic","title":"A man in A&E who won't say why","who":"Patient","task":"Stuart Pembridge, 30, is a caretaker at a primary school. He has walked into A&E this evening asking to speak to a mental health professional but has refused to tell the triage nurse what is wrong. He appears tense and keeps checking the door. He has never had contact with psychiatric services. Please see Mr Pembridge, find out what is troubling him, and carry out a risk assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Stuart Pembridge?","p":"Yes. Is this private?"},{"d":"We keep what you say confidential, with one exception: if someone, especially a child, might be at risk, I'd have to share it.","p":"Right. Okay."},{"d":"You've come in for a reason, and that took courage. What's been happening?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, private room, confidentiality and its limits, praise attending"},{"h":"His concerns","b":"Why now, what he fears, sexual urges towards young girls"},{"h":"Nature of interest","b":"Age preference, exclusive or not, onset, fantasies, arousal"},{"h":"Recent events","b":"Babysitting friend's daughter, arousal, fantasies since, any touching"},{"h":"Images and offending","b":"Indecent images online, pen drive, contact offences, arrests"},{"h":"Mental state","b":"Shame, mood, suicidal thoughts, drink, drugs, psychosis, impulsivity"},{"h":"Risk to children","b":"Access via school, babysitting, grooming, plans, protective factors"},{"h":"Safeguarding steps","b":"Explain duty to share, police and children's services, his consent"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Paedophilic disorder likely, honest plan, support, next contact"}],"bps":{"pre":"Stuart said he had been sexually drawn to girls under 12 for as long as he could remember. He had recently babysat a friend's 8-year-old and became aroused, though he did not touch her. He admitted keeping images of children on a memory stick. He felt disgusted with himself, had no suicidal thoughts and wanted help.","ask":"\"What happens now? Are you going to call the police?\"","lead":"Stuart, you came here to stop harm happening, and I'll be honest with you about what has to happen next.","bio":["No medicine cures this, but an SSRI such as fluoxetine 20 mg daily, rising to 40-60 mg, can reduce intrusive urges, prescribed by a specialist.","In high-risk cases a forensic psychiatrist may consider antilibidinal treatment with full consent and ECG, bone and liver monitoring.","Screen for and treat any depression, anxiety or alcohol misuse that lowers self-control."],"psych":["Referral to a specialist forensic or sexual behaviour service for CBT-based work on urges, fantasies and relapse prevention.","Confidential support from Stop It Now (0808 1000 900) in the meantime.","Supportive work on shame and self-esteem, as hopelessness raises both suicide and offending risk."],"social":["The images held are a crime and a child has been put at risk: police and local children's social care must be informed (LADO, because he works at a school).","Inform Stuart directly and encourage him to cooperate; record the reasoning under GMC confidentiality guidance.","Safeguarding lead to be informed tonight; he must not babysit or be alone with children.","GP to be updated with his consent where possible."],"safety":["Agree he will not have unsupervised contact with any child.","If he feels suicidal after disclosure, call 999 or NHS 111 option 2; Samaritans 116 123."]}},{"sid":"M8.3","cid":"c-t-22","area":"Forensic","title":"Can't sleep, sure his partner is cheating","who":"Patient","task":"Wesley Thorne, 35, is a plasterer. He saw his GP two weeks ago asking for something to help him sleep, saying he felt stressed. The GP was worried enough about what he said to refer him to the community mental health team. He lives with his partner of three years; they have no children. Please see Mr Thorne, take a focused history to reach the likely diagnosis, and carry out a risk assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Wesley Thorne?","p":"Yeah. I thought I'd just get some sleeping pills."},{"d":"I understand. Your GP wanted us to look at the stress behind the poor sleep. Can you tell me about it?","p":"It's my partner, Shona."},{"d":"Go on. I'm listening, and I'd like the whole picture.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, why referred, his expectation of sleeping tablets"},{"h":"The stress","b":"Partner's alleged affairs, when it began, the aftershave, his evidence"},{"h":"Belief quality","b":"Conviction, number of lovers, alternative explanations, other delusions"},{"h":"Behaviours","b":"Checking phone, following her, confrontations, interrogations"},{"h":"Mental state","b":"Mood, sleep, voices, alcohol, drugs, erectile problems, cognition"},{"h":"Risk to partner","b":"Threats, 'one way or another', force, truth serum, weapons, past violence"},{"h":"Other risks","b":"Rivals, suicide, children, driving, past forensic history"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Morbid jealousy, safety plan, further assessment, partner's safety"}],"bps":{"pre":"Wesley described six months of certainty that his partner was sleeping with hundreds of men, based on a scent on her coat and her greeting to the postman. He checked her phone and followed her. He had decided to force a confession from her, and had been looking up injectable \"truth serums\". He saw no other explanation.","ask":"\"I just need to sleep. What can you give me?\"","lead":"Wesley, I can see how exhausting this has been, but the bigger problem is the belief itself, and Shona's safety.","bio":["Antipsychotic for delusional jealousy, for example risperidone 1 mg at night, increasing to 2-4 mg over 1-2 weeks; check weight, BP, glucose, lipids, prolactin and ECG.","Rule out organic and substance causes: alcohol history, urine drug screen, bloods, and neurological history.","No sleeping tablets on their own; short-term sleep advice only."],"psych":["Once safe, individual CBT for delusional beliefs with a clinical psychologist, around 16 weekly sessions.","Psychoeducation for Wesley about morbid jealousy and how it drives checking and following.","Sleep hygiene advice and a regular routine, reviewed by his care coordinator, rather than hypnotics."],"social":["Given the threat of force and research into injectable substances, consider urgent Mental Health Act assessment; informal admission if he accepts.","Duty to warn: the partner must be told of the risk; involve police and a MARAC referral via the domestic abuse lead.","Recommend separate living for now; Shona to be offered an IDVA (independent domestic violence adviser).","Care coordinator in CMHT, with consultant review within days."],"safety":["Shona to call 999 if threatened; safety plan agreed with domestic abuse services.","Crisis line for Wesley via NHS 111 option 2."]}},{"sid":"M8.4","cid":"c-u-26","area":"Forensic","title":"Out of hours: insisting on seeing one doctor","who":"Patient","task":"Dominic Arrieta, 28, makes a living as a professional online gamer. He has been waiting in A&E for hours this evening and is getting more and more agitated because staff will not fetch a particular female doctor who saw him months ago for an asthma attack. He has turned up repeatedly with minor complaints since then. You are the psychiatry doctor on call. Please take a focused history to identify the likely diagnosis and complete a risk assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Dominic Arrieta?","p":"Yes. I've waited hours. I just want to see Dr Fenwick."},{"d":"I'm sorry about the long wait. I can see you're frustrated. Can we sit down and talk?","p":"Fine, but quickly."},{"d":"Tell me about Dr Fenwick, and why it matters so much tonight.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge wait and anger, safe seating, staff aware"},{"h":"The relationship","b":"One meeting, blink and eyebrows as 'signs', love of his life"},{"h":"Belief quality","b":"Conviction, secret love, unanswered friend request, other delusions"},{"h":"Pursuit","b":"Repeat attendances, social media searches, home address, letters, flight to Venice"},{"h":"Mental state","b":"Mood, mania, voices, drugs, alcohol, gaming, sleep, cognition"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Force if stopped, martial arts, weapons, risk to doctor and staff"},{"h":"Background","b":"Past relationships, past mental health, forensic history, stalking"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Erotomania, de-escalate, security plan, senior and police input"}],"bps":{"pre":"Dominic explained that the doctor had blinked twice and raised her eyebrows, which proved she loved him. Since then he had come back with minor complaints to see her. She ignored his friend request, which he took as her keeping it secret. He had bought flights to Venice for tomorrow and said he would use force, being trained in mixed martial arts.","ask":"\"Just tell me when she's coming. Why won't anyone let me see her?\"","lead":"Dominic, I can hear how strongly you feel, but she isn't going to come down tonight, and I'd like to explain what happens next.","bio":["If agitation escalates, offer oral lorazepam 1-2 mg or promethazine 25-50 mg first, in line with NICE NG10 for rapid tranquillisation.","Antipsychotic for erotomanic delusional disorder once assessed, for example aripiprazole 5-10 mg daily or risperidone 2 mg; baseline ECG and metabolic bloods.","Urine drug screen and basic bloods to rule out stimulant or organic causes."],"psych":["Once settled, individual CBT for psychosis focused on the belief and its consequences.","Psychoeducation and engagement work with a community care coordinator; stalking-specific work through a forensic psychologist if needed.","Brief family work if relatives are involved, so they understand the belief and know when to seek help."],"social":["Security to remain nearby; consider police if he threatens staff or refuses to leave.","Mental Health Act assessment if he will not accept informal care and risk is high.","Inform the doctor concerned, her clinical lead and the trust security team; consider a stalking protection order via police.","Flag his records so future attendances are managed safely without her."],"safety":["Staff should not be alone with him; call 999 if violence is threatened.","Personal safety advice for the doctor concerned, including at home."]}},{"sid":"M8.5","cid":"c-u-27","area":"Forensic","title":"Burnt hands after a bin fire","who":"Patient","task":"Rhiannon Achebe, 20, is in her second year at university. She came to A&E with minor burns on both hands and told the triage nurse she got them after lighting a fire in a recycling bin outside her student house. She would not say why she did it but agreed to talk to the psychiatric liaison team. She has never been known to mental health services. Please explore what happened, work out the most likely diagnosis, and complete a risk assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Rhiannon?","p":"Yes. Are my hands going to be okay?"},{"d":"The burns team say they're minor and should heal well. I'd like to understand how today happened.","p":"Does this go to the police?"},{"d":"This is private between us; I'd only share if someone faced real danger, and I'd tell you first.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, burns first, confidentiality and its limits"},{"h":"Today's events","b":"Ex with new partner, wine, urge, lit bin, burnt hands"},{"h":"The fire-setting","b":"Tension before, relief after, watching fire engines, arousal, planning"},{"h":"Past fire-setting","b":"Previous fires, childhood interest, escalation, fascination with fire"},{"h":"Rule out causes","b":"Revenge, psychosis, mania, intoxication, depression, personality, learning difficulty"},{"h":"Stress and support","b":"Coursework, father's cancer, family, friends, alcohol use"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Further fires, harm to housemates, self-harm, suicide, legal consequences"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Possible pyromania, honest about reporting, plan, support"}],"bps":{"pre":"Rhiannon was tense and guarded at first. Stress over coursework and her father's cancer had been building, and today she saw her ex kissing someone. She drank wine, felt an overwhelming urge and lit the bin. The flames eased the tension; watching the fire engines excited her, then frightened her. No one had been hurt.","ask":"\"Is there something wrong with me? What happens now?\"","lead":"Rhiannon, you've been carrying a lot, and what you describe is something we can help with.","bio":["No medicine is licensed for pyromania; treatment is mainly psychological.","If an underlying depressive or anxiety disorder is found, an SSRI such as sertraline 50 mg daily may help, reviewed in 2-4 weeks.","Burns care from A&E: dressings, analgesia (paracetamol 1 g up to four times daily) and review at the burns clinic.","Advise against alcohol, which lowered her control tonight."],"psych":["Referral to a forensic psychologist or specialist service for CBT on fire-setting, around 12-20 weekly sessions on triggers, tension and alternatives.","Skills for managing distress (such as from DBT) if urges are linked to emotional tension.","Support through university counselling and Talking Therapies for stress and her father's illness."],"social":["University wellbeing team and personal tutor for extensions and support, with her consent.","Explain honestly that arson is a crime; the fire service may investigate, and she should get legal advice.","Carers' support such as Macmillan for the strain of her father's cancer.","GP follow-up within a week."],"safety":["Safety plan: if the urge returns, contact the crisis line or NHS 111 option 2; avoid alcohol and stay with friends.","Remove lighters and matches from her room for now."]}},{"sid":"M8.6","cid":"c-s-45","area":"Forensic","title":"Knife and wedding dress at the palace","who":"Consultant","task":"Lucy Vandermeer, 21, works as a healthcare assistant in a care home and lives alone. Police brought her to A&E after finding her hiding in bushes in palace grounds in London, wearing a wedding dress and carrying a knife. You have assessed her: she believes a senior royal, whose hand she shook at a charity event six months ago, loves her and plans to marry her, and that relatives intercepted her letters. The knife was for protection. Mood was normal, there were no hallucinations and she lacked insight. She has PCOS, takes the combined pill and smokes cannabis nightly. Please present your assessment to your consultant, Dr Iqbal, giving the likely diagnosis and a management plan.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors on call. Is that Dr Iqbal?","p":"Speaking. Go ahead."},{"d":"I'm calling about Lucy Vandermeer, 21, brought in by police from palace grounds with a knife. I'm worried about risk.","p":"Okay, take me through it."},{"d":"I'll give the situation, background, my assessment and a plan.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, patient, reason for call, SBAR structure"},{"h":"Situation","b":"Palace grounds, wedding dress, knife, police brought her in"},{"h":"Background","b":"Six-month belief, handshake, letters intercepted, PCOS, combined pill, nightly cannabis"},{"h":"Mental state","b":"Euthymic, no thought disorder, fixed erotomanic delusion, no hallucinations, no insight"},{"h":"Diagnosis","b":"Delusional disorder, erotomanic type; differentials cannabis psychosis, schizophrenia, mania"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Knife, determination, fear of assassination, celebrity target, self-neglect"},{"h":"Plan","b":"Mental Health Act assessment, admission, antipsychotic, bloods, FTAC referral"},{"h":"Hand over and close","b":"Agree plan, consultant review, documentation, police liaison"}],"bps":{"pre":"Lucy was calm and friendly but entirely convinced the royal loved her and that his family were blocking their marriage. She had travelled to London to accept his proposal. She brought the knife in case relatives tried to kill her. She had no mood symptoms or hallucinations, smoked cannabis nightly and saw no reason to stay.","ask":"Dr Iqbal: \"Fine. What's your plan?\"","lead":"My view is this is a delusional disorder of the erotomanic type with a significant risk to others, so I'd recommend admission.","bio":["Antipsychotic: aripiprazole 5-10 mg daily, raising to 15 mg if needed; chosen to avoid raising prolactin, which may worsen PCOS symptoms.","Baseline weight, BMI, waist, BP, pulse, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin and ECG; repeat weight weekly for six weeks.","Bloods and urine drug screen to rule out other causes; consider brain imaging if anything atypical.","Review the combined pill with her GP, as PCOS and weight gain on antipsychotics add vascular risk."],"psych":["Engagement work to build trust before challenging the belief; then CBT for psychosis, at least 16 sessions with a psychologist.","Cannabis harm reduction and motivational work with the dual diagnosis worker.","Family or friends involved, with consent, to support engagement and spot warning signs after discharge."],"social":["Mental Health Act assessment for admission under Section 2, as she refuses informal admission and carries a knife.","Refer to the Fixated Threat Assessment Centre (FTAC), the police and NHS unit for people fixated on public figures.","Liaise with police about the knife offence and any protection of the royal household.","Inform her employer only with consent or if required; her care home role may need review given vulnerable residents."],"safety":["Knife removed and searched on arrival; observation level set by the ward."]}},{"sid":"M8.7","cid":"c-s-47","area":"Forensic","title":"Assault on the ward: why and what next","who":"Patient","task":"Terence Odhiambo, 45, lives alone, is out of work and has few friends. He was admitted to a general adult ward overnight under the Mental Health Act after police entered his home with doctors. This morning he attacked another patient, punching and kicking him until nurses restrained him. The other patient is being seen by the medical team. You are the ward doctor. Please assess Mr Odhiambo's mental state and carry out a violence risk assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors on the ward. Are you Terence?","p":"Who are you really working for?"},{"d":"I'm a doctor here, not police or anyone else. I'd like to understand what happened this morning.","p":"Why should I trust you?"},{"d":"You don't have to yet. Two nurses will stay nearby, and we can stop whenever you need.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, safe room, staff present, exit route, honest role"},{"h":"The assault","b":"What happened, why, the patient seen as assassin, intent to kill"},{"h":"Psychotic symptoms","b":"MI5 plot, surveillance, whispering voices, ward full of assassins"},{"h":"Mood and other","b":"Fear, sleep, depression, mania, suicidal thoughts, substances, cognition"},{"h":"Current intent","b":"Willing to repeat, targets, weapons, regret or not"},{"h":"Violence history","b":"Past violence, convictions, weapons, substance use, adherence"},{"h":"Risk formulation","b":"Command or threat delusions, no insight, specific targets, high risk now"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Immediate safety plan, medication, observations, PICU, team handover"}],"bps":{"pre":"Terence was frightened and suspicious. He believed MI5 had arranged his admission and that the patient who greeted him was an assassin, so he attacked him to survive. He heard whispering all night, thought the ward was bugged and said more killers were posing as staff and patients. He felt no remorse and would do it again.","ask":"The nurse in charge: \"What's the plan? We can't keep him safe like this.\"","lead":"I agree the risk is high right now, so here's what I suggest for the next few hours and beyond.","bio":["Offer oral medication first: lorazepam 1-2 mg or promethazine 25-50 mg, or olanzapine 10 mg if no antipsychotic has been given; follow NICE NG10.","If refused and risk persists, IM lorazepam 1-2 mg, or IM haloperidol 5 mg with IM promethazine 50 mg if an ECG is available; monitor pulse, BP, RR, temperature, sats every 15 minutes for an hour.","Start regular antipsychotic, for example olanzapine 10 mg at night, with baseline ECG, weight, glucose, lipids and prolactin.","Urine drug screen and routine bloods to rule out other causes."],"psych":["Explain to Terence what happened and why the team is acting, without arguing with the delusions.","Post-incident debrief for staff and the other patient; psychology input to support staff.","Once settled, structured risk assessment (HCR-20) and later CBT for psychosis."],"social":["Increase to 1:1 or within-eyesight observations; consider transfer to a psychiatric intensive care unit (PICU).","Incident report (Datix); safeguarding referral for the injured patient and offer to support him reporting to police.","Inform the responsible clinician and nearest relative; review section status.","Separate Terence from the patient he attacked; move one of them if needed."],"safety":["Staff never alone with him; alarms worn; seclusion only as a last resort and per policy."]}},{"sid":"M8.8","cid":"c-s-44","area":"Forensic","title":"Telling his partner about morbid jealousy","who":"Partner","task":"Craig Lennox, 32, was treated in A&E for minor cuts after a cycling accident when staff became worried about him. You have assessed him: he is convinced his partner, Fiona Dalgleish, is having an affair with a work colleague, based on a photo from a party five months ago. He checks her phone, logs her movements, has followed the man home and has bought a flick-knife, saying it is for protection. Records show a past stalking and assault conviction against an ex-partner, a two-year stay in a medium-secure unit for morbid jealousy, and that he stopped antipsychotic treatment. He refuses community follow-up and informal admission. He has consented to you talking to Fiona, who has arrived. Please explain the likely diagnosis and discuss a management plan with Ms Dalgleish.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Fiona Dalgleish?","p":"Yes. Is Craig hurt? Nobody's told me anything."},{"d":"His cuts are minor. Craig has agreed I can talk to you about him, and some of it is difficult.","p":"Okay. What's going on?"},{"d":"Can I first ask what you've noticed at home lately?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent from Craig, physical injuries minor, her view first"},{"h":"What she has seen","b":"Accusations, phone checks, timing her, the photo, her fears"},{"h":"Explain the diagnosis","b":"Morbid jealousy, delusional belief, not open to reason"},{"h":"The past","b":"Previous stalking and assault, secure unit, stopped medication"},{"h":"Risk to others","b":"Followed the man, flick-knife, risk to her and the colleague"},{"h":"Management plan","b":"Mental Health Act assessment, antipsychotic, police, warn the colleague"},{"h":"Her safety","b":"Separate living, domestic abuse support, MARAC, safety plan"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, questions, contact numbers, leaflet, next update"}],"bps":{"pre":"Fiona said Craig had been accusing her for months, checking her phone and timing her journeys. She had told him he was being paranoid. She did not know about the knife, or that he had followed her colleague home. She had heard little about his past stay in hospital and was shocked. Fiona became tearful.","ask":"\"I had no idea. What are you going to do with him? Am I safe?\"","lead":"Fiona, I'm worried about your safety and your colleague's, and I'll explain exactly what we plan to do.","bio":["Restart an antipsychotic, for example risperidone 2 mg at night, increasing as needed, or the medication that helped him in the secure unit.","If adherence is the problem, consider a long-acting injection later, such as aripiprazole or paliperidone depot.","Baseline ECG, weight, BP, glucose, lipids and prolactin before restarting."],"psych":["Once safe, CBT for psychosis with a forensic psychologist to address the jealous belief.","Psychoeducation for Fiona, so she understands the illness is driving his actions, not anything she has done.","Counselling for Fiona through Victim Support or her GP, given the shock and fear."],"social":["Mental Health Act assessment today, as he refuses admission and carries a knife; likely detention in hospital.","Inform police of the knife and the risk to the colleague; duty to warn the colleague, with legal advice.","Domestic abuse referral: IDVA and MARAC for Fiona; she should consider staying elsewhere.","Forensic team and his former secure-unit team for records and advice; MAPPA check."],"safety":["Fiona to call 999 if Craig threatens or follows her; National Domestic Abuse Helpline 0808 2000 247.","Do not challenge him alone about the affair belief."]}},{"sid":"M9.1","cid":"c-t-23","area":"Liaison","title":"Day three after back surgery: frightened and shaking","who":"Patient","task":"Margaret Penrose, 64, is a retired bank manager on the orthopaedic ward, three days after lumbar decompression surgery. The nurses have called liaison psychiatry because over the last few hours she has become suddenly confused, agitated and shaky. She is restless in her chair and keeps stamping on the floor. Please carry out a mental state assessment to reach a diagnosis.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Margaret Penrose?","p":"Can't you see them? They're everywhere!"},{"d":"I can see you're very frightened, Margaret. You're safe here, and I'm staying with you.","p":"On the floor. Look."},{"d":"Can you look at me for a moment? I'd like to ask you a few questions.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, calm reassurance, low stimulation, nurse present"},{"h":"Appearance and behaviour","b":"Restless, stamping, sweating, tremor, distractible, fearful"},{"h":"Mood and thoughts","b":"Fear, sense of doom, persecutory ideas, suicidal thoughts"},{"h":"Perception","b":"Insects on floor and walls, crawling feeling on legs, voices"},{"h":"Cognition","b":"Orientation time, place and person, attention, 4AT, memory since surgery"},{"h":"Alcohol history","b":"Several bottles of wine daily, last drink before surgery, past withdrawal, fits"},{"h":"Physical","b":"Obs, temperature, pulse, BP, infection, pain, meds, bloods"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Delirium tremens, urgent medical plan, tell team, family"}],"bps":{"pre":"Margaret was terrified, sweating and tremulous, seeing insects pour from the walls and feeling them crawl up her legs. She did not know the date or the hospital and kept losing track of questions. She admitted drinking several bottles of red wine daily since retiring and last drank the day before surgery. Her husband arrived during the assessment.","ask":"Her husband: \"What on earth is happening to her? Is it the anaesthetic?\"","lead":"This is a serious form of alcohol withdrawal called delirium tremens, and it needs treating straight away.","bio":["Chlordiazepoxide reducing regime, for example 20-30 mg four times daily titrated to symptoms with CIWA-Ar scoring; use lorazepam instead if liver function is poor.","IV Pabrinex 2 pairs three times daily for 3-5 days to prevent or treat Wernicke's encephalopathy, then oral thiamine 100 mg three times daily.","Bloods: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, magnesium, phosphate, glucose, clotting, CK; correct electrolytes and give IV fluids.","Monitor obs regularly with NEWS2; consider HDU if seizures or very unstable signs."],"psych":["Reorientation: clock, calendar, familiar faces, glasses and hearing aids, calm explanations repeated.","Once recovered, brief intervention and motivational interviewing about her drinking.","Explain afterwards what happened, as many people are distressed by memories of the hallucinations."],"social":["Inform the orthopaedic team and consultant; alcohol liaison nurse review.","Involve her husband in reassurance and in planning abstinence after discharge.","Referral to community alcohol service; consider acamprosate after detox."],"safety":["Side room close to the nurses' desk, low bed, falls precautions, and no unnecessary restraint."]}},{"sid":"M9.2","cid":"c-u-28","area":"Addictions","title":"Bank holiday, no methadone, rising withdrawal","who":"Patient","task":"Lee Gartside, 39, is out of work and lives alone in a rented flat. He has come into A&E on a bank holiday Monday demanding to see a doctor and says he will not go until he is given methadone. The nurses have asked the liaison psychiatry team to see him because he is restless and irritable. Please explore Mr Gartside's concerns and take a full substance use history.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Lee Gartside?","p":"Yes. I need my methadone, now."},{"d":"I can see you're feeling rough. Let's sort out what's happened and what we can safely do.","p":"The chemist's shut. I've had nothing since yesterday."},{"d":"That sounds awful. Can I ask some questions to work out the best plan?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge distress, aim to help safely"},{"h":"Today's problem","b":"Missed doses, bank holiday closure, last dose time, withdrawal symptoms"},{"h":"Methadone details","b":"Dose 60 mg, prescriber, pharmacy, supervised or not, duration"},{"h":"Drug history","b":"Heroin onset, route, injecting, sharing, cost, other drugs, last use"},{"h":"Other substances","b":"Alcohol units, tobacco, benzodiazepines, cannabis, crack"},{"h":"Complications","b":"Overdose, blood-borne viruses, abscesses, DVT, mental health"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Relapse to heroin, overdose after lost tolerance, suicide, driving"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Confirm script, plan for today, naloxone, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Lee had been on methadone 60 mg daily for two years and had stopped injecting heroin. The pharmacy was shut for the bank holiday and he had gone over 24 hours without a dose. He had a runny nose, sweats, cramps and nausea and feared he would buy heroin. He drank two cans a night and smoked heavily.","ask":"\"So are you going to give me my methadone or not?\"","lead":"Lee, I want to get you through this safely, and the first step is confirming your prescription.","bio":["Confirm the dose with his drug service or pharmacy (or out-of-hours records) before prescribing; never give methadone on his word alone.","If confirmed and he is in withdrawal, a single supervised dose may be given per local policy; if not confirmed, a smaller dose (for example 10-20 mg) with observation, and no take-home doses.","Measure withdrawal with the COWS scale; give symptomatic help: loperamide for diarrhoea, metoclopramide 10 mg for nausea, paracetamol for aches.","Urine drug screen; check for co-prescribed sedatives and that he has not used heroin today."],"psych":["Motivational interviewing to support his two years without heroin and prevent relapse.","Keyworker sessions at his drug service, plus peer support such as Narcotics Anonymous or SMART Recovery.","Contingency management or structured psychosocial sessions if he relapses, through his drug service."],"social":["Phone his drug service when it reopens to arrange his next dose and review pharmacy access for holidays.","Naloxone kit and training before he leaves.","Check blood-borne virus testing and hepatitis B vaccination status; stop smoking service referral.","Advise about not driving while withdrawing or after dose changes, and DVLA rules."],"safety":["Warn that tolerance falls quickly; using heroin on top of methadone or after missing doses can cause fatal overdose. Call 999 if someone cannot be woken."]}},{"sid":"M9.3","cid":"c-t-24","area":"Addictions","title":"Nursery rhymes at night after stopping drink","who":"Patient","task":"Natalia Petrenko, 49, works as a cook at a primary school. Her GP has referred her to the community mental health team because of a recent decline in her mental health, mainly odd beliefs about her neighbour. The GP letter mentions a history of heavy drinking and abnormal liver tests. She is puzzled about why she has been sent to a psychiatrist. Please take a focused psychiatric history to establish the likely diagnosis.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Natalia Petrenko?","p":"Yes. I don't know why the doctor sent me here."},{"d":"Your GP was concerned about how you've been sleeping and some worries about your neighbour. Can you tell me about that?","p":"He sings to me at night."},{"d":"Thank you. I'd like to hear it all, and then ask about some other parts of your life.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, why referred, her understanding"},{"h":"The experiences","b":"Neighbour's voice singing nursery rhymes at night, through keyhole, no one there"},{"h":"Nature of voices","b":"Clarity, insight, other voices, visions, delusions of persecution, onset timing"},{"h":"Alcohol history","b":"Two bottles of wine a day for years, since bereavement, dependence features"},{"h":"Stopping drinking","b":"Last drink a week ago, shakes, sweats, fits, confusion, timing"},{"h":"Mental state and causes","b":"Mood, grief, cognition, other drugs, physical illness, liver problems"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Confronting neighbour, self-neglect, suicide, relapse, work safety"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Alcohol-related psychotic disorder, plan, abstinence support, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Natalia had been getting through roughly two bottles of wine daily for four years after her husband died. She stopped a week ago on her GP's advice and was shaky for a few days. Since then she had heard her neighbour's voice singing nursery rhymes at night through the keyhole, though no one was there. She was clear-headed, not confused and not depressed.","ask":"\"So am I going mad? What can you do about this neighbour?\"","lead":"No, Natalia, you're not going mad; this is a recognised effect of heavy drinking and stopping, and it usually settles.","bio":["Oral thiamine 100 mg three times daily; IV Pabrinex if there is any sign of Wernicke's (confusion, unsteady walk, eye movement problems).","Bloods: FBC (MCV), LFTs including GGT, U&Es, clotting, magnesium; liver ultrasound via GP.","If voices persist or distress is high, a short course of antipsychotic such as olanzapine 5 mg at night or haloperidol 1.5-3 mg, reviewed within weeks.","Relapse prevention medication once abstinent, such as acamprosate 666 mg three times daily (if 60 kg or over) for up to a year."],"psych":["Motivational interviewing and relapse prevention with the community alcohol service, weekly at first.","Bereavement counselling, for example Cruse, for the loss that triggered her drinking.","Mutual aid groups such as Alcoholics Anonymous or SMART Recovery."],"social":["Referral to community alcohol service with a named keyworker.","Occupational health discussion about returning to work safely in the school kitchen.","GP to monitor liver function and mood; care coordinator if symptoms continue."],"safety":["Ask her not to confront the neighbour; call NHS 111 option 2 if voices become threatening or she feels unsafe."]}},{"sid":"M9.4","cid":"c-s-50","area":"Addictions","title":"Woke suddenly after GHB coma","who":"Patient","task":"Ravi Sandhu-Morgan, 29, is a solicitor recovering on a medical ward. He was found unconscious at a bus stop with drug-taking equipment beside him, had a GCS of 4 in A&E and needed intensive care and ventilation. About four hours later he woke abruptly; three hours on he is calm and talking with the medical team, who suggested he see the mental health team, and he has agreed. Please take a substance use history and carry out a risk assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Ravi?","p":"Yes. I'm so sorry for wasting everyone's time."},{"d":"You're not wasting anyone's time. You were very unwell, and we want to help you stay safe.","p":"I can't remember much."},{"d":"That's okay. Can we go through what you do remember, and then talk about drugs and alcohol?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, reassure, non-judgemental, confidentiality"},{"h":"Last night","b":"Party, what was taken, amounts, drink, vomiting, collapse, memory gap"},{"h":"GHB or GBL use","b":"Five years, chemsex, three times weekly, dosing, tolerance, dependence"},{"h":"Withdrawal","b":"Tremor, sweats, insomnia, anxiety, confusion between doses, dosing round clock"},{"h":"Other drugs","b":"Crystal meth, injecting, needle use, alcohol, other sedatives"},{"h":"Sexual health and harms","b":"Unprotected sex, consent, BBV testing, PrEP, PEP, assault"},{"h":"Mental state and risk","b":"Mood, suicidal thoughts, overdose intent, work, isolation"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Risks, harm reduction, services, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Ravi admitted he had been at a chemsex party, drank beer and took GHB in a drink. He uses GHB whenever he has sex, at least three times weekly, and needs more than before. He denies withdrawal symptoms. He injects crystal meth monthly, using clean needles, and has never had a BBV test. He wanted help to cut down.","ask":"\"I want to get control of this. What help is there?\"","lead":"Ravi, asking for help after such a frightening night is a big step, and there's specialist support for exactly this.","bio":["Watch closely for GHB withdrawal, which can start within hours and be dangerous: tremor, sweating, agitation, delirium, seizures.","If withdrawal develops, diazepam in high doses titrated to symptoms under medical and addictions advice; baclofen may be added by specialists.","Never plan detox at home without specialist support; planned withdrawal is done in a specialist or inpatient setting.","Offer hepatitis B vaccination and HIV, hepatitis B and C and syphilis testing; PEP if risk in the last 72 hours."],"psych":["Referral to a chemsex-specialist service or LGBTQ+ drug service for psychosocial support and counselling.","Motivational interviewing and CBT-based relapse prevention, around weekly sessions over 3 months.","Explore links between sex, confidence and drug use with a counsellor."],"social":["Sexual health clinic for full screening, PrEP and ongoing care.","Needle and syringe programme, and harm reduction advice for injecting.","Support with employment issues if he wishes; occupational health is confidential.","GP letter with consent."],"safety":["Harm reduction: never mix GHB with alcohol or other sedatives; measure doses with a syringe, leave at least 2 hours between doses, never use alone.","If a friend cannot be woken, put them in the recovery position and call 999."]}},{"sid":"M9.5","cid":"c-s-49","area":"Addictions","title":"Delirium tremens: talking it through with the nurse","who":"Ward nurse","task":"Mr Harjit Sandhu, a 64-year-old retired bus driver who lives alone, was brought to the acute medical unit last night after a neighbour found him on his kitchen floor, probably down for about two days. There are no fractures, but he is badly bruised. His head CT is clear; bloods show dehydration, a high CK and a raised GGT. He is tachycardic at 110, BP 150/95, temperature 37.4. He has little English, is frightened, sweaty and tremulous, cannot hold attention, and appears to be shouting at and grabbing at things that are not there. His neighbour says he drinks heavily every day. You have reviewed him for liaison, suspect delirium tremens and have already told the medical team. His nurse, Rosa, has not come across this condition. Please explain to her how you reached the diagnosis and talk through the management plan.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Rosa, looking after Mr Sandhu in bay four?","p":"Yes, that's me. I'm a bit worried about him."},{"d":"You're right to be. I think he has delirium tremens, a severe form of alcohol withdrawal. Do you have ten minutes?","p":"Yes, go ahead."},{"d":"Tell me what you know so far, then I'll explain the diagnosis and the plan.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Introduce, her concerns, what she knows, agree the plan"},{"h":"What DTs are","b":"Severe alcohol withdrawal with delirium, usually 48-72 hours after the last drink"},{"h":"How I reached it","b":"Daily heavy drinking, two days without, confusion, hallucinations, tremor, sweats, tachycardia"},{"h":"Ruling out other causes","b":"Clear CT head, infection screen, glucose, sodium, Wernicke's, rhabdomyolysis from the long lie"},{"h":"Medication now","b":"Oral lorazepam or chlordiazepoxide, IV Pabrinex before glucose, fluids, avoid sedation overshoot"},{"h":"Nursing on the ward","b":"Side room, good lighting, CIWA-Ar scoring, NEWS2, falls and seizure precautions, interpreter"},{"h":"Risks and escalation","b":"Seizures, arrhythmia, aspiration, kidney injury; call medics or outreach early"},{"h":"Hand over and close","b":"Check her understanding, written plan, alcohol care team, I'll review tomorrow"}],"bps":{"pre":"Rosa had caught me at the nurses' desk after Mr Sandhu pulled at his cannula and tried to climb out of bed. I had gone through the neighbour's account of his daily drinking, the long lie, the shaking and the sweating, and the way he kept swiping at the curtain. She nodded, but looked worried, and said she had never nursed anyone with this before.","ask":"So what do we actually do for him tonight?","lead":"The good news is that this is treatable, and the first few hours of nursing make a big difference.","bio":["Oral lorazepam first line for delirium tremens (NICE), e.g. 1-2 mg, repeated against a symptom-triggered chart; parenteral lorazepam if he cannot swallow","If agitation persists despite lorazepam, the medical team may add haloperidol 0.5-1 mg with a baseline ECG for QTc","IV Pabrinex two pairs three times daily for 3-5 days, given before any glucose, then oral thiamine 100 mg three times daily","IV fluids for dehydration and high CK; daily U&Es, CK, magnesium, phosphate, glucose and LFTs; correct magnesium and potassium","Infection screen (CXR, urine, cultures) and repeat observations; watch for over-sedation and respiratory depression"],"psych":["Calm, short, repeated reorientation by the same nurses where possible, through a professional interpreter","Brief intervention about alcohol once he is clear-headed, from the hospital alcohol care team","Motivational interviewing before discharge to explore whether he wants to stop or cut down"],"social":["Book a Punjabi interpreter (telephone or in person) for every key conversation; not family","Hospital alcohol care team referral today, then the local community drug and alcohol service at discharge","Occupational therapy and social work before discharge: he lives alone and lay on the floor for two days","With his consent, update the neighbour or family; consider a community alarm (pendant) at home"],"safety":["Seizure, falling saturations, chest pain or a new drop in consciousness: call the medical team or outreach at once","Do not leave him unattended near the ward exit while delirious; document capacity decisions under the Mental Capacity Act"]}},{"sid":"M9.6","cid":"c-s-51","area":"Addictions","title":"Low mood and drinking: what comes first?","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Dariusz Kowalczyk is a 46-year-old warehouse supervisor referred to the community mental health clinic by his GP. The letter describes about six weeks of feeling low and losing interest in things he used to enjoy, such as five-a-side football and fishing with his brother. His past medical history includes an episode of pancreatitis two years ago and high blood pressure. He smokes about fifteen cigarettes a day and the GP notes that he drinks most days, though the amount is not recorded. He has come to the appointment alone. Please take a history of his low mood and his drinking, and reach a likely diagnosis; treatment does not need to be discussed in detail today.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Mr Kowalczyk?","p":"Yes. Darek is fine."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Darek. Your GP says you've been feeling low for a few weeks. Is that right?","p":"Yeah, I'm just fed up."},{"d":"I'd like to hear how things have been, and ask about drinking and your health too, as they often link. Is that okay?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, reason for referral, agree to ask about mood and alcohol"},{"h":"Low mood now","b":"Onset six weeks, mood, interest, sleep, appetite, energy, guilt, concentration"},{"h":"Drinking pattern","b":"Units per day, typical day, first drink, binges, timeline of increase"},{"h":"Dependence features","b":"Craving, tolerance, morning shakes, relief drinking, narrowing, persistence despite harm"},{"h":"Complications","b":"Withdrawal fits, DTs, pancreatitis, blackouts, work, driving, relationships, money"},{"h":"Timeline and background","b":"Which came first, sober periods and mood, family history, past episodes"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts, plans, alcohol with tablets, driving, neglect, others"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Likely alcohol dependence with depressive symptoms, check understanding, next steps"}],"bps":{"pre":"Darek had been guarded at first, then explained that he now drinks about two bottles of strong cider and some vodka each day, shakes in the mornings until his first can, and lost his licence last year. The low mood started after his partner moved out. He admitted the pancreatitis was alcohol-related and that he once had a fit when he stopped suddenly. He denied any plans to end his life.","ask":"So is it depression, or is it the drink? And what can I do about it?","lead":"It sounds like the drinking has taken hold, and the drink itself can cause a lot of low mood, so we start there.","bio":["No antidepressant yet: reassess mood after 3-4 weeks without alcohol, as alcohol-induced low mood often lifts","Planned assisted withdrawal, not stopping suddenly: past fit means inpatient or closely supervised chlordiazepoxide reducing regimen","Thiamine: oral 100 mg three times daily during and after withdrawal; IV Pabrinex if inpatient detox","Bloods: LFTs, GGT, MCV, U&Es, amylase or lipase, glucose; repeat BP; avoid disulfiram because of his hypertension","After withdrawal, acamprosate 666 mg three times daily (if over 60 kg) or naltrexone 50 mg daily to prevent relapse, with LFTs"],"psych":["Motivational interviewing with his drug and alcohol keyworker before and during detox","CBT-based relapse prevention, weekly for around 12 weeks: triggers, cravings, high-risk situations","Mutual aid: SMART Recovery or Alcoholics Anonymous meetings, ideally weekly","If mood stays low after a month sober, high-intensity CBT for depression through NHS Talking Therapies"],"social":["Referral to the local community drug and alcohol service for assessment and a detox plan","Occupational health or HR: fit note during detox, phased return to work","DVLA: he must declare alcohol dependence; he should not drive until advised","Stop-smoking service at the GP or pharmacy, and involve his brother as support with consent"],"safety":["Never stop drinking abruptly at home; call 999 for a fit, confusion or hallucinations","Crisis line or 111 option 2 if suicidal thoughts grow, especially when drinking"]}},{"sid":"M9.7","cid":"c-s-52","area":"Physical examination","title":"Checking the cerebellum after years of drinking","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Clifford Osei-Bonsu is a 60-year-old man on an older adult psychiatry ward with many years of heavy, dependent drinking. Over the past fortnight the nursing team have noticed that he is unsteady, has become more forgetful and seemed briefly confused on admission. The team are worried about Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome and want a clear record of his cerebellar signs before he sees the neurologist. He is calm, cooperative and sitting in the clinical room. Please carry out a physical assessment of his cerebellar function, explaining each step to him as you go.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Can I check your name, please?","p":"Clifford Osei-Bonsu."},{"d":"Thank you, Clifford. I'd like to check your balance and coordination, as you've been a little unsteady. It takes about ten minutes. Is that alright?","p":"Fine by me."},{"d":"Any pain anywhere? And would you like a chaperone?","p":"No pain. I'm okay without."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, explain, consent, chaperone offered, pain, hand hygiene"},{"h":"General inspection","b":"Posture, walking aids, tremor, signs of liver disease, alertness"},{"h":"Speech","b":"Slurred or scanning speech; repeat 'baby hippopotamus', 'West Register Street'"},{"h":"Eyes","b":"Nystagmus on held lateral gaze, ophthalmoplegia, saccades, pursuit"},{"h":"Arms","b":"Tone, outstretched arms, finger-nose, dysdiadochokinesia, rebound"},{"h":"Legs","b":"Tone, reflexes, heel-shin slide, foot tapping"},{"h":"Stance and gait","b":"Broad-based gait, heel-to-toe, Romberg, turning, guard against falls"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Thank him, findings, thiamine and further tests, hand over to team"}],"bps":null},{"sid":"M10.1","cid":"c-s-54","area":"Personality and therapies","title":"Thinking traps after a bad match","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Kieran Ashworth is a 23-year-old county cricketer referred to the psychiatry outpatient clinic by his club doctor. Three weeks ago he dropped a simple catch in the final over of a cup semi-final and his team lost. Since then he has felt low, slept badly and refused to go back to training or selection, telling his coach he is 'done'. The club doctor notes he keeps describing himself and the match in harsh, sweeping ways. There is no previous psychiatric history. Please see him, identify the unhelpful thinking patterns in what he says, and explain them to him in a way that makes sense to him.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Kieran Ashworth?","p":"Yeah."},{"d":"Thanks for coming in, Kieran. I gather the last few weeks have been really tough since the semi-final.","p":"You could say that."},{"d":"Could you talk me through what happened and how you've been since? Then we'll look at it together.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge the match, agree the plan"},{"h":"The match and since","b":"What happened, mood, sleep, appetite, training, team contact"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Hopelessness, suicidal thoughts, alcohol, isolation, protective factors"},{"h":"Spot the thinking traps","b":"All-or-nothing, catastrophising, mind reading, overgeneralising, labelling, personalising"},{"h":"Thoughts, feelings, actions","b":"Simple cycle using his own words, thoughts are not facts"},{"h":"Test one thought","b":"Evidence for and against, what would he tell a teammate"},{"h":"Next steps","b":"Thought diary, CBT referral, gradual return to the nets"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, follow-up, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"Kieran had told me the dropped catch 'cost the whole season', that everyone in the dressing room now thinks he is useless, and that he 'always bottles it'. We had named a few of these patterns together, and he laughed at the idea that he was 'reading minds'. He said he had no thoughts of ending his life, but felt stuck at home on his phone.","ask":"Right, so how do I actually get out of this?","lead":"You've already done the first bit, which is noticing the thoughts, so let's build on that.","bio":["No antidepressant now: NICE advises a psychological treatment first for less severe depression","Repeat PHQ-9 in two weeks; if moderate to severe or not improving, consider sertraline 50 mg once daily","If started, review within one week because he is under 25 (higher risk of suicidal thoughts early on)","Cut back on alcohol and keep a fixed wake time; GP bloods (FBC, TFTs) only if fatigue persists"],"psych":["CBT with a therapist, weekly 50-minute sessions for about 8-12 weeks, through NHS Talking Therapies","Thought record homework: write the situation, the thought, the feeling, then the evidence for and against","Behavioural activation: planned activities each day, starting with light fitness and seeing friends","Sport psychology input from the club on performance pressure and handling mistakes"],"social":["Club welfare officer and the players' association confidential counselling line","Graded return: watch training, then nets, then selection, agreed with his coach","Fit note from his GP if needed, and tell family or a close friend how he is doing"],"safety":["If hopeless or suicidal thoughts appear, call 111 option 2 or Samaritans on 116 123, or 999 if in danger"]}},{"sid":"M10.2","cid":"c-s-55","area":"Anxiety, OCD, trauma and dissociation","title":"Agoraphobia: a new plan at home","who":"Patient","task":"Mrs Siobhan Adeyemi is a 42-year-old woman with panic and agoraphobia whom you have inherited on a CMHT caseload. She has missed most appointments for a year, but you managed a phone review last week and your consultant asked you to visit her at home today. Two years ago she attended an online self-help course and psychoeducation group regularly and was keen, but improved very little. She has taken an SSRI for 18 months with only slight, brief benefit. Two months ago she lost her job in a supermarket after repeatedly arriving late, sometimes frozen at the front door in terror, convinced she was going to die. She now shops online and only goes out with her husband, who is very supportive. She is desperate for help. Please review her past treatment with her and agree a new plan, explaining both systematic desensitisation and graded exposure.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Siobhan Adeyemi?","p":"Yes. Come in, sorry about the mess."},{"d":"Thank you for having me at home. I know getting to clinic has been really hard.","p":"It's impossible, honestly."},{"d":"I'd like to hear what's helped or not helped so far, then plan something new together. Is that okay?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, thank her, acknowledge difficulty, agree the plan"},{"h":"Panic and avoidance now","b":"Attacks, fear of dying, door freezing, online shopping, husband as escort"},{"h":"Past treatment","b":"Online course, groups, SSRI name, dose, adherence, why it fell short"},{"h":"Impact and risk","b":"Job loss, mood, alcohol, suicidal thoughts, money"},{"h":"Explain the fear cycle","b":"Panic is harmless, avoidance keeps fear alive, escape feels like relief"},{"h":"Systematic desensitisation","b":"Relaxation skills, fear ladder, imagined then real steps, pairing calm with fear"},{"h":"Graded exposure","b":"Real-life steps up the ladder, stay until anxiety falls, daily practice"},{"h":"Medication and support","b":"SSRI review, avoid benzodiazepines, home-based therapist, husband's role"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Agreed plan, teach-back, next visit, crisis numbers"}],"bps":{"pre":"Siobhan had described standing at the front door with her heart racing, sure she was dying, before going back to bed. She said the online course 'was just reading', and she takes sertraline 50 mg most days. We had drawn her fear ladder on the back of an envelope, from opening the door to riding the bus alone, and she looked both curious and scared.","ask":"Do you really think I can get back out there?","lead":"Yes, I do, and the steps we've just written down are exactly how people do it.","bio":["Check adherence and dose: increase sertraline in 50 mg steps every 2-4 weeks, up to 200 mg daily if tolerated","If still little benefit at a full dose, switch to another SSRI (e.g. escitalopram 10-20 mg), or later venlafaxine","Avoid benzodiazepines and propranolol for panic disorder (NICE); warn about early jitteriness when the dose rises","Ask the GP for TFTs and a caffeine review, as both can worsen panic"],"psych":["High-intensity CBT for panic disorder, 7-14 hours in total, weekly, with the first sessions at home","Systematic desensitisation: learn slow breathing and muscle relaxation, then climb the ladder while relaxed","Graded in vivo exposure: practise each step daily until fear drops by half, then move up","Husband trained as a co-therapist at first, then gradually stepping back so she goes alone"],"social":["CMHT care coordinator or assistant practitioner to support the first exposure trips","Universal Credit and a fit note; later Access to Work or a supported return to work scheme","Carer's assessment for her husband through the local authority","Anxiety UK or No Panic helpline and peer support"],"safety":["Panic attacks are frightening but not dangerous; if chest pain is different from usual, call 111 or 999","Crisis line (111 option 2) if mood drops or hopeless thoughts start"]}},{"sid":"M10.3","cid":"c-t-25","area":"Anxiety, OCD, trauma and dissociation","title":"OCD at work: exposure and response prevention","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Tobias Mensah, a 24-year-old chef at a busy bakery, has been referred to the psychiatry clinic by his GP. Four months ago he developed severe irritant dermatitis on both hands from cleaning products. He eventually told the GP, with embarrassment, that intrusive thoughts tell him the work surfaces carry germs; he wipes each surface twice with detergent after every batch and stays late scrubbing the areas his colleagues use. Although he partly sees this as silly, he fears customers will catch HIV unless he cleans this way, and now washes his hands repeatedly for the same reason. Since his skin cracked, the fears about HIV getting in have become stronger. He has taken fluoxetine for 12 weeks, recently raised from 20 mg to 40 mg, and wants to stay on it. Please discuss a treatment plan with him, focusing on exposure and response prevention and CBT.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Tobias Mensah?","p":"Yes. Toby."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Toby. Your GP told me a bit about the cleaning and your hands. That must be really hard to talk about.","p":"It's embarrassing, to be honest."},{"d":"You're not alone in this. Can we go over how things are now, then talk through treatment?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, normalise embarrassment, agree plan"},{"h":"Obsessions and compulsions","b":"HIV fears, wiping twice, staying late, handwashing, time taken, insight"},{"h":"Impact and risk","b":"Skin damage, work, mood, sleep, suicidal thoughts, alcohol"},{"h":"Name it and explain","b":"OCD, the anxiety-ritual loop, rituals keep the fear going"},{"h":"How ERP works","b":"Fear ladder, facing triggers, not cleaning, anxiety rises then falls"},{"h":"CBT around it","b":"Inflated responsibility, HIV facts, thought-action fusion, behavioural experiments"},{"h":"Medication and skin","b":"Continue fluoxetine 40 mg, can rise to 60 mg, emollients, dermatology"},{"h":"Work and support","b":"Occupational health, agreed cleaning standard, OCD-UK, family"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, therapy referral, review date"}],"bps":{"pre":"Toby had explained that he spends nearly two extra hours a day cleaning, that his hands bleed after a shift, and that he knows HIV does not survive on a counter but 'can't take the chance'. He rated the urge to wipe at 9 out of 10. He felt the fluoxetine had taken the edge off slightly and had no thoughts of harming himself.","ask":"So what's this therapy you mentioned, and will it actually stop it?","lead":"It's the treatment with the best evidence for exactly what you've described, and most people improve a great deal.","bio":["Continue fluoxetine 40 mg; it can be increased to 60 mg daily if partial response, reviewing after 4-6 weeks","Give at least 12 weeks at a good dose before judging; continue for at least 12 months after improvement","If no response to two SSRIs plus therapy, consider clomipramine with a baseline ECG","Emollients and a short steroid cream from the GP; dermatology if the dermatitis does not settle"],"psych":["CBT including ERP with a trained therapist, more than 10 hours for moderate OCD (NICE), usually weekly 60-90 minute sessions","Build a fear ladder: e.g. touching the counter once without wiping, then leaving a worktop uncleaned","Response prevention: stay with the anxiety without cleaning or washing until it falls on its own","Cognitive work on inflated responsibility and HIV facts; daily homework between sessions"],"social":["Occupational health at work: agreed food-hygiene cleaning standard, so 'normal' is clear","Temporary adjustments (gloves, less detergent work) while hands heal","OCD-UK for information and peer support; involve his partner or family with consent"],"safety":["If mood drops or suicidal thoughts start, contact the team or 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"M10.4","cid":"c-u-29","area":"Personality and therapies","title":"Is interpersonal therapy right for me?","who":"Patient","task":"Professor Rohan Iyer is a 64-year-old retired engineering lecturer who self-referred to NHS Talking Therapies two months ago with mild to moderate depression. He attended eight sessions of CBT and took part well, but felt little better and was discharged. In the discharge letter to his GP, though not to him, the therapist suggested he might suit interpersonal therapy (IPT) if further therapy were tried. He went back to his GP asking for more help, and the GP has referred him to your community mental health team on that basis. This is his first appointment with you. Please explain what IPT is and when and why it is used, and assess and discuss whether it suits him.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Professor Iyer?","p":"I am. Rohan, please."},{"d":"Thank you, Rohan. I understand the CBT didn't help as much as you'd hoped, and your GP mentioned another talking therapy.","p":"Yes, something called IPT. I'd never heard of it."},{"d":"I'll explain it, but first could I hear how you've been and what's been happening in your life?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge CBT experience, agree the plan"},{"h":"Mood now","b":"Low mood, interest, sleep, appetite, energy, PHQ-9, how long"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts, plans, alcohol, isolation, protective factors"},{"h":"Life and relationships","b":"Retirement, bereavement, conflicts, loneliness, who he is close to"},{"h":"What IPT is","b":"Mood and relationships affect each other, here and now, time-limited"},{"h":"IPT focus areas","b":"Grief, role dispute, role transition, isolation; which fits him"},{"h":"Suitability","b":"Clear focus, motivation, can link mood to events, weekly attendance"},{"h":"Practicalities","b":"About 16 weekly sessions, three phases, homework, alternatives"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recommendation, teach-back, referral, leaflet, crisis numbers"}],"bps":{"pre":"Rohan had told me his mood dipped soon after he retired last spring, when he lost the daily contact with colleagues and students, and that he and his wife now argue about how he spends his days. CBT worksheets had felt 'abstract'. His PHQ-9 was 14, he had no thoughts of ending his life, and he became animated when talking about his old department.","ask":"So would this IPT actually suit someone like me?","lead":"From what you've told me, I think it could suit you well, because so much of this links to the big change of retiring.","bio":["An antidepressant could be offered alongside therapy, e.g. sertraline 50 mg once daily, reviewed at 2-4 weeks","Check sodium before and 2-4 weeks after starting an SSRI, as over-60s are at higher risk of hyponatraemia","Baseline GP bloods: FBC, TFTs, B12, folate, glucose, calcium, to rule out physical causes of low mood","If he prefers therapy alone first, that is reasonable at this severity"],"psych":["IPT: about 16 weekly 50-minute sessions with a trained IPT therapist in Talking Therapies or the CMHT","Early phase: mood timeline and 'interpersonal inventory' of key relationships, agree one focus (role transition)","Middle phase: grieve the old role, build a new one, and work on the disagreements with his wife","Final phase: review gains, warning signs and a plan for setbacks; alternatives include counselling or behavioural activation"],"social":["Talking Therapies or CMHT referral for IPT, with the waiting time explained","Ideas for a new role: University of the Third Age, volunteering, mentoring or part-time teaching","Invite his wife to one session if he wishes; carer support if needed","GP to keep reviewing mood while he waits"],"safety":["If thoughts of self-harm start, call 111 option 2 or Samaritans on 116 123"]}},{"sid":"M10.5","cid":"c-s-56","area":"Personality and therapies","title":"'Therapy is making me worse': exploring transference","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Freya Nakamura, a 29-year-old postgraduate student, has come to A&E late in the evening and the liaison team has been asked to see her. Three months into a planned year of psychodynamic psychotherapy for low mood linked to the pressure of her degree, she says she feels much worse and wants you to tell her therapist she is stopping. Her therapist had told her to come to A&E if she ever felt she could not cope; she has not harmed herself. The latest therapy note describes a transference that seems to be getting in the way of the work and needs careful attention. The notes say she is engaging and progressing as expected, the therapist expects a good outcome and does not suggest ending. Her next session is tomorrow. Please talk with her about her experience of therapy and, addressing her concerns, explore the part transference may be playing. A risk assessment is not needed.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors working in A&E. Are you Freya Nakamura?","p":"Yes."},{"d":"Thank you for waiting, Freya. I hear you've been finding therapy really hard and you're thinking of stopping.","p":"I just want someone to tell her I'm done."},{"d":"That sounds important. Could you tell me what's been happening in the sessions?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, validate distress, hear her request"},{"h":"Her experience of therapy","b":"How sessions feel, what changed, why tonight, mood now"},{"h":"Feelings towards the therapist","b":"Anger, rejection, neediness, who the therapist reminds her of"},{"h":"Explain transference","b":"Old feelings replayed with the therapist, normal and expected, useful material"},{"h":"Why worse before better","b":"Painful material surfaces, a sign of work, not failure"},{"h":"Her choices","b":"Take feelings to tomorrow's session, review meeting, planned ending if still wanted"},{"h":"Brief safety check","b":"Coping tonight, who is at home, crisis numbers"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, letter to therapist with consent, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Freya had told me her therapist 'doesn't care', ends sessions bang on time and went on holiday without warning, which made her furious. She then said her father was often away and 'never there when it mattered'. When I wondered aloud whether those old feelings might be showing up in therapy, she went quiet, then said that was 'weirdly accurate'. She said she was safe tonight.","ask":"So what am I supposed to do, just go back tomorrow and pretend I'm fine?","lead":"Not pretend at all; the most useful thing you could do is take exactly what you've told me tonight into tomorrow's session.","bio":["No new medication tonight; her low mood does not need an urgent change","If low mood deepens, her GP could consider an SSRI such as sertraline 50 mg daily, reviewed at 2-4 weeks","Avoid benzodiazepines or sleeping tablets for distress; short-term sleep hygiene advice instead"],"psych":["Continue weekly 50-minute psychodynamic sessions; transference is explored in the room, not avoided","Tell her therapist directly about the anger and feeling abandoned; therapists are trained for this","If she still wants to stop, ask for a review meeting so the ending is planned rather than sudden","Therapist uses supervision to think about the transference and her reactions to it"],"social":["With consent, a short liaison letter to her therapist and GP about tonight's visit","University wellbeing service and disability adviser; mitigating circumstances for deadlines","Lean on a trusted friend tonight rather than being alone"],"safety":["If she feels unsafe, call 111 option 2, Samaritans on 116 123, or return to A&E"]}},{"sid":"M10.6","cid":"c-s-57","area":"Child and adolescent","title":"Family therapy: talking with Dad","who":"Father","task":"Mr Gareth Llewellyn is the father of Maisie, a 13-year-old girl under the CAMHS team with moderate depression. She has had a long course of individual CBT plus fluoxetine but is still unwell. Your consultant feels family difficulties are holding her back. Her parents often disagree about her care, and Maisie says they undermine her CBT homework: last week, when she asked her dad to help list her negative thoughts, he told her not to worry as she has a great life; her mum overheard and shouted at him, and Maisie cried in her room while they argued. She thinks CBT will not work unless her parents change too. Her parents used to take turns bringing her, but lately only her dad comes, and Maisie believes they are separating. Please discuss family therapy with Mr Llewellyn, describe how it works and consider with him whether Maisie would benefit.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors in the team. Are you Mr Llewellyn, Maisie's dad?","p":"Yes, Gareth."},{"d":"Thank you for coming, Gareth. Maisie knows we're meeting, and I'll keep the details of her sessions private, but we can talk about how to help her.","p":"Okay."},{"d":"Could I hear how you think she's doing first?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, confidentiality, Maisie aware, his view of her"},{"h":"How Maisie is","b":"Mood, school, sleep, CBT progress, fluoxetine, self-harm, risk"},{"h":"Family picture","b":"Home atmosphere, parental disagreement, homework incident, possible separation"},{"h":"What family therapy is","b":"Whole family, patterns not blame, how everyone responds to her low mood"},{"h":"How it runs","b":"Two therapists, screen or team, sessions every 2-3 weeks, about 6-12"},{"h":"Is it suitable","b":"Willingness of both parents, Maisie's wish, safety, separation not a barrier"},{"h":"Concerns and alternatives","b":"Blame worries, privacy, parenting support, couple counselling separately"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, invite mum, leaflet, crisis numbers"}],"bps":{"pre":"Gareth had admitted, looking at his hands, that he and Maisie's mum are 'going through a rough patch' and may split up. He said he only told Maisie she had a good life because he couldn't bear seeing her sad. He had not known she cried that night. He said he would do anything to help, but worried family therapy meant being told he was a bad father.","ask":"So what happens now? Is this family therapy going to blame us?","lead":"No, it's not about blame; it's about helping all of you pull in the same direction for Maisie.","bio":["Continue fluoxetine (10 mg, increased to 20 mg daily), the first-line antidepressant for under-18s (NICE)","Watch for agitation, self-harm or new suicidal thoughts, especially after any dose change; CAMHS reviews regularly","Do not stop fluoxetine suddenly; any change is planned with her psychiatrist"],"psych":["Systemic family therapy: about 6-12 sessions every 2-3 weeks, with both parents and Maisie","Two family therapists, sometimes a reflecting team behind a screen, with the family's consent","Explores patterns at home, e.g. reassuring versus listening, and agrees how to support her CBT homework","Continue her individual CBT alongside; parents may get a session on how to help with thought diaries","Couples or separation counselling (e.g. Relate) kept separate from Maisie's therapy"],"social":["Invite Maisie's mum to a meeting; both parents ideally attend, even if separating","School: head of year or pastoral lead, with consent, for support and a quiet space","If the parents separate, agree a shared plan so Maisie's care stays consistent"],"safety":["If Maisie talks of ending her life or self-harms, call CAMHS, 111 option 2, or 999 in an emergency","Keep medicines locked away at home; Childline 0800 1111 for Maisie"]}},{"sid":"M11.1","cid":"c-s-59","area":"Eating disorders","title":"Explaining anorexia to a worried mum","who":"Mother","task":"Mrs Patricia Haworth is the mother of Ellie, 18, whom you assessed in clinic when her GP was worried she might have an eating disorder. Ellie was a sporty teenager who played netball, ran and swam. In her first months at university she ate a lot of takeaways and gained weight, which upset her because she had always seen herself as slim. She began counting calories, became preoccupied, lost a lot of weight and now exercises out of fear of gaining. She denies vomiting, laxatives or diet pills. She has paused her course with her tutor's agreement and lives at home. ECG was normal; bloods are normal apart from a white cell count of 3.2; BP 95/70, pulse 60, BMI 15. You have diagnosed anorexia nervosa. Ellie accepts she has a problem but fears treatment and weight gain, and has agreed you may share her assessment with her mum. Please explain the diagnosis to Mrs Haworth and answer her questions.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Mrs Haworth, Ellie's mum?","p":"Yes, Trish. Is she alright?"},{"d":"Thank you for coming, Trish. Ellie has agreed I can share what we found. What have you noticed at home?","p":"She's so thin and won't sit with us to eat."},{"d":"That sounds worrying. I'll explain what's going on, then answer your questions.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent from Ellie, her concerns and observations"},{"h":"The diagnosis","b":"Anorexia nervosa: restriction, fear of weight gain, BMI 15, driven exercise"},{"h":"Why it happens","b":"Mix of personality, dieting, stress, transition to university; no one's fault"},{"h":"Physical risks","b":"Low white cells, low BP and pulse, bones, heart, periods, fertility"},{"h":"Treatment plan","b":"Eating disorder team, CBT-ED or SSCM, dietitian, regular monitoring"},{"h":"Family role","b":"Supportive mealtimes, not arguing about food, carer skills, Beat"},{"h":"Red flags and risk","b":"Fainting, chest pain, rapid loss, self-harm; call 111 or 999"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, leaflet, next appointment, her own support"}],"bps":{"pre":"Trish had listened carefully as I explained anorexia nervosa, and became tearful when I said Ellie's BMI was 15 and her white cells were low. She said she had blamed herself for buying 'diet' food. She had noticed Ellie running at 6am and cutting food into tiny pieces. I reassured her it was not her fault, and she took a breath.","ask":"So how do we get her better? What happens next?","lead":"She can recover, and families are one of the most important parts of that.","bio":["No medication treats anorexia itself; the main treatment is regular eating and gradual weight restoration","Dietitian meal plan: three meals and two to three snacks a day, aiming for steady weekly weight gain","Weekly weight, BP, pulse and bloods at first (FBC, U&Es, phosphate, magnesium, glucose), ECG if risk rises","DEXA bone scan if underweight for two years or more; calcium and vitamin D if intake is poor"],"psych":["Adult eating disorder therapy: CBT-ED, about 40 sessions over 40 weeks, or specialist supportive clinical management, about 20 sessions","Family involvement: carers join some sessions, with Ellie's agreement, to learn how to help at meals","Carer skills workshops run by the eating disorder service or Beat","Ellie's motivation is built gently through motivational work early in therapy"],"social":["Referral to the community eating disorder service; early help is linked to better recovery","Beat helpline and carer support groups for Trish and the family","University: student support and a planned return once she is physically safe","Carer's assessment for Trish through the local authority"],"safety":["Call 111 or 999 for fainting, chest pain, palpitations, confusion or very fast weight loss"]}},{"sid":"M11.2","cid":"c-u-30","area":"Eating disorders","title":"Very low weight on the medical ward","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Tamsin Bello is a 19-year-old woman admitted to a general medical ward with abdominal pain. Her BMI is 14. Her potassium was low on arrival and has been corrected; the medical team say all other investigations are unremarkable and have asked the liaison psychiatry team to see her. She is sitting up in bed, covered in blankets, and has agreed to talk. Please take a psychiatric history from Ms Bello and complete a risk assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors who visit the wards. Are you Tamsin Bello?","p":"Yes."},{"d":"Thank you for seeing me, Tamsin. The medical team asked me to come because they're worried about your weight and your tummy pain.","p":"I'm fine, really."},{"d":"I'd just like to understand how things have been with food and how you're feeling. Is that okay?","p":"I suppose."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, why liaison came, reassurance, consent"},{"h":"Eating now","b":"Typical day, restriction, calorie counting, fluids, rules, binges"},{"h":"Weight and compensations","b":"Weight loss speed, vomiting, laxatives, diet pills, exercise, water loading"},{"h":"Beliefs and body image","b":"Fear of gain, feeling fat at BMI 14, weighing, checking"},{"h":"Physical symptoms","b":"Dizziness, falls, palpitations, periods, cold, hair, abdominal pain"},{"h":"Mood and background","b":"Depression, OCD traits, self-harm, alcohol, family, onset, previous help"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Medical risk, suicidal thoughts, self-harm, leaving hospital, refusing feeds"},{"h":"Insight and capacity","b":"Views on treatment, capacity, MHA if refusing life-saving care"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, eating disorder team, family with consent"}],"bps":{"pre":"Tamsin had told me she eats an apple and black coffee most days, walks for three hours, and has been taking laxatives nightly for months, which explained the low potassium. She has not had a period for a year. She cried when she described feeling 'huge', and said she sometimes wishes she would not wake up, though she has no plan.","ask":"Can I just go home now my potassium's back to normal?","lead":"I can see you want to get home, but your body is in a dangerous place right now, so let me explain what needs to happen first.","bio":["Refeeding guided by MEED guidance: dietitian plan starting around 1,400-2,000 kcal/day, avoid underfeeding","Daily phosphate, potassium, magnesium, glucose and U&Es for the first week, then twice weekly; replace as needed","Thiamine 100 mg three times daily and a multivitamin before and during early refeeding","ECG for QTc and bradycardia; lying and standing BP, temperature; stop laxatives with a bowel plan"],"psych":["Daily liaison psychiatry reviews and early contact from the specialist eating disorder team","Supportive mealtimes with trained nursing staff, post-meal supervision to prevent purging","Motivational work now, then CBT-ED (about 40 weekly sessions) in the eating disorder service after discharge"],"social":["Referral to the specialist eating disorder unit if she does not gain on the medical ward","With consent, involve her family or partner and offer carer support (Beat)","If she lacks capacity or refuses life-saving treatment, consider a Mental Health Act assessment","University or employer: medical leave and fit note"],"safety":["Discuss passive death wishes, agree she tells staff if thoughts worsen; check for stored laxatives","Medical team to escalate falling potassium, phosphate, pulse under 40, or chest pain immediately"]}},{"sid":"M11.3","cid":"c-u-31","area":"Eating disorders","title":"NG feeding and refeeding risk: briefing the nurse","who":"Ward nurse","task":"Ms Imogen Darko, 22, has severe anorexia nervosa and has spent several months on a specialist eating disorder unit. Despite intensive therapy she still restricts heavily and her BMI is 13. With her physical health worsening, the multidisciplinary team has decided to start nasogastric feeding. She has been moved to a medical ward in the general hospital for this, because on a previous admission she developed refeeding syndrome and needed intensive care. The hospital dietitian has seen her and written a feeding plan. She is detained under the Mental Health Act and is judged to lack capacity to consent to treatment. You are the liaison psychiatrist who has just reviewed her. Please go through her management plan with Callum, the ward nurse, making sure he understands the risks of refeeding syndrome.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors from the liaison team. Are you Callum, Imogen's nurse today?","p":"Yes, hi."},{"d":"I've just seen Imogen. She's very unwell and at high risk of refeeding syndrome. Have you got fifteen minutes to go through the plan?","p":"Sure. I've not nursed anyone on NG feeds for anorexia before."},{"d":"That's fine. Tell me what you know so far.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Introduce, SBAR summary, his experience, agree the plan"},{"h":"Situation and background","b":"BMI 13, months on ED unit, previous refeeding syndrome needing ICU"},{"h":"Legal frame","b":"Detained, lacks capacity, NG feeding as treatment, least restrictive, document"},{"h":"What refeeding syndrome is","b":"Shift of phosphate, potassium, magnesium into cells when feeding restarts"},{"h":"Feeding plan","b":"Dietitian regimen, slow start, vitamins and thiamine, NG position checks"},{"h":"Monitoring","b":"Daily bloods, ECG, obs, fluid balance, oedema, glucose, weight"},{"h":"Warning signs","b":"Confusion, palpitations, weakness, breathlessness, seizures, swelling; escalate"},{"h":"Behaviour and safety","b":"Tube pulling, hiding feed, exercising, enhanced observation, supportive approach"},{"h":"Hand over and close","b":"Check understanding, written plan, contacts, review time"}],"bps":{"pre":"Callum had read the dietitian's plan but admitted he was unsure why the feed started so low. I explained that Imogen's last refeeding episode ended in intensive care with a dangerously low phosphate. He mentioned she had tried to pull at the tube during insertion and was crying, saying staff were 'making her fat'. He looked uneasy about restraint.","ask":"So what exactly do I need to be watching for, and what do I do if she pulls it out?","lead":"Let's take it in two parts: keeping her body safe, and keeping the tube in as kindly as we can.","bio":["Dietitian plan: start no more than 5-10 kcal/kg/day given BMI 13 and past refeeding syndrome (NICE CG32), increase slowly over 4-7 days","Thiamine 200-300 mg daily (or IV Pabrinex) before the first feed and for 10 days, plus vitamin B compound strong and a multivitamin","Phosphate, potassium, magnesium, glucose and U&Es before starting, then daily for the first week; replace per hospital protocol","Baseline ECG and repeat if electrolytes fall; NEWS2 at least four-hourly, strict fluid balance, daily weight","Check NG tube position with aspirate pH 5.5 or below before every feed and after any coughing or vomiting"],"psych":["Calm, consistent, non-judgemental approach; explain each step to Imogen even though she is detained","Eating disorder unit staff to visit and advise; liaison psychiatry review daily","Distraction and support during and after feeds; reward progress, avoid arguments about weight"],"social":["Legal: feeding is treatment for her mental disorder under the Mental Health Act; record each decision","Ensure she knows her rights and has an independent mental health advocate (IMHA)","Keep her family updated, with her agreement where possible, and offer carer support","Agree with the ward manager the observation level and staff trained in safe holding"],"safety":["Escalate at once: phosphate under 0.5 mmol/L, new arrhythmia, chest pain, confusion, seizure, rapid oedema","If the tube is pulled out, stop feeding, inform the doctor, do not re-pass without a plan; restraint only as last resort and recorded"]}},{"sid":"M11.4","cid":"c-t-26","area":"Eating disorders","title":"Bingeing and vomiting: what's going on?","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Hannah Mwangi, a 24-year-old account manager at a media agency, was sent by her GP to the community eating disorder service with two years of bingeing and making herself sick. Her weight has troubled her since she was a teenager and tried several diets at university. The move into a demanding job with long days and after-work drinks led to weight gain and comments from family and colleagues. She began exercising more and skipping food in the day, then arrives home ravenous and eats large amounts quickly, feeling out of control. Afterwards she feels guilty and ashamed and vomits using her fingers, now every weekday. She is more preoccupied with food, though her weight is stable and BMI is 22. Observations are normal and she has never seen a psychiatrist. Please discuss the diagnosis and treatment options with her. A risk assessment is not needed today.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Hannah Mwangi?","p":"Yes."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Hannah. Your GP shared a bit about the bingeing and being sick. I know that can be hard to talk about.","p":"I'm so embarrassed."},{"d":"There's nothing to be embarrassed about here. Can I check a few things, then explain what I think is happening?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, reduce shame, agree the plan"},{"h":"Brief check","b":"Pattern of binges, vomiting, other purging, weekends, feelings before and after"},{"h":"The diagnosis","b":"Bulimia nervosa: binges, loss of control, vomiting, weight-focused self-worth"},{"h":"The cycle","b":"Daytime restriction, hunger, binge, guilt, vomiting, more restriction"},{"h":"Physical effects","b":"Low potassium, heart rhythm, teeth, throat, swollen glands, dehydration"},{"h":"Treatment options","b":"Guided self-help first, then CBT-ED, regular eating, food diary"},{"h":"Medication and checks","b":"No tablets first-line, potassium and ECG, dental advice"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, referral, Beat, review"}],"bps":{"pre":"Hannah had described skipping breakfast and lunch, eating cereal, crisps and chocolate in one go most evenings, then vomiting. She was relieved to hear it had a name. When I drew the restrict-binge-purge circle, she said, 'That's literally my week.' She worried she would never stop and that work would find out.","ask":"So how do I actually break this cycle?","lead":"The most powerful first step sounds simple: eating regularly through the day, and we'll support you with that.","bio":["No medication as the sole or first treatment for bulimia (NICE)","U&Es for potassium now and repeat if vomiting continues; ECG if potassium is low or she has palpitations","Dental: rinse with water after vomiting, avoid brushing for an hour, register with a dentist","If therapy alone is not enough, fluoxetine 60 mg daily is licensed as an add-on (BNF)"],"psych":["Bulimia-focused guided self-help: a book-based programme with 4-9 short sessions over about 16 weeks","If no improvement after 4 weeks or it is not acceptable, CBT-ED: about 20 sessions over 20 weeks","Regular eating: three meals and two to three snacks, no gaps longer than 3-4 hours","Food and mood diary to spot triggers, plus alternative activities after meals"],"social":["Community eating disorder service referral; GP to monitor bloods","Workplace: occupational health or reasonable adjustments for regular breaks, only if she wishes","Beat helpline and online support groups","Tell a trusted friend or family member to support regular meals"],"safety":["Seek urgent help (111 or 999) for palpitations, fainting, muscle cramps or vomiting blood"]}},{"sid":"M12.1","cid":"c-s-61","area":"Perinatal","title":"Lithium and planning a baby","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Naomi Fairweather is a 29-year-old with bipolar affective disorder whom her GP has referred to the perinatal mental health team for advice about lithium and pregnancy. In her late teens and early twenties she was unwell for long spells and tried several antipsychotics; despite taking them, she relapsed three times and was detained twice for mania. Those episodes were frightening and disruptive and she wants to avoid them again. Five years ago she started lithium, and since then she has had no manic episodes, only a brief depressive dip last year. She has no side effects. She and her partner now want to start trying for a baby, and she wants to know if she can safely carry on with lithium. Please address her worries, answer her questions and agree a management plan.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists, working with the perinatal team. Are you Naomi Fairweather?","p":"Yes, hi."},{"d":"Thank you for coming before trying for a baby, Naomi. That's a really sensible step.","p":"I just want to do it right."},{"d":"Could I hear a bit about your bipolar and how lithium has been, then we'll go through the options and your questions?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, praise planning ahead, agree the plan"},{"h":"Her illness history","b":"Onset, admissions, antipsychotics tried, relapse signs, lithium since, last episode"},{"h":"Lithium now","b":"Dose, level, kidney and thyroid tests, adherence, contraception now"},{"h":"Risks to baby","b":"Small rise in heart defects, Ebstein's anomaly, neonatal effects, levels"},{"h":"Risks of stopping","b":"High relapse risk, especially after birth; postpartum psychosis"},{"h":"Options","b":"Stay on lithium with monitoring, switch, or gradual stop; her choice"},{"h":"Pregnancy and birth plan","b":"Folic acid, levels every 4 weeks then weekly, scans, delivery, feeding"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, written plan, team contacts, relapse signs"}],"bps":{"pre":"Naomi had described the terror of her last manic admission, and said lithium had 'given her life back'. Her last level was 0.7, her kidney and thyroid tests were normal, and she is on the implant. She had read online that lithium causes heart problems in babies and her partner wanted her to stop. She looked torn.","ask":"So do I have to come off lithium to have a baby?","lead":"Not necessarily; for some women, staying on lithium is the safest choice for both mum and baby, and we can plan it carefully.","bio":["Risk: lithium slightly raises the chance of heart defects (roughly 1-2 extra per 100 births); most babies are unaffected","If continuing: lithium levels every 4 weeks, then weekly from 36 weeks; keep levels at the lowest effective range","If stopping: reduce gradually over at least 4 weeks, never suddenly; an antipsychotic such as quetiapine may cover","Folic acid 400 micrograms daily from now; detailed anomaly scan and fetal heart scan at around 20 weeks","Birth: hospital delivery, level within 12 hours of the last dose, fluids in labour; breastfeeding usually not advised"],"psych":["Pre-conception counselling sessions with the perinatal team to weigh options at her pace","Relapse prevention work: early warning signs, sleep protection, with her partner involved","Psychoeducation for her partner about bipolar and the postnatal period"],"social":["Shared perinatal care plan with her obstetrician, midwife, GP and health visitor","Plan for sleep and night feeds after birth, with partner or family support","Bipolar UK and Action on Postpartum Psychosis for peer information"],"safety":["Seek help urgently for poor sleep, racing thoughts or feeling high in pregnancy or after birth","Contact the perinatal team, 111 option 2, or 999 if she or the baby is at risk"]}},{"sid":"M12.2","cid":"c-u-32","area":"Perinatal","title":"Methadone and a new pregnancy","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Jodie Pennington, a 31-year-old woman, has been referred to the perinatal mental health team for advice. She stopped injecting heroin daily four years ago and has been stable on methadone since, now 30 mg a day collected from her pharmacy without supervision. She takes no other prescribed medication and uses no illicit drugs. She had a depressive episode in her early twenties but no other mental illness, and no physical health problems, though GP records show a past positive hepatitis C test. She has just found out she is eight weeks pregnant. The pregnancy was not planned but she is in a stable relationship and is happy. She has stopped smoking and drinking and wants to do the best for her baby. She is worried about the effect of methadone and would like to stop all medication, but fears withdrawal and going back to heroin. Please discuss her options and answer her questions.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists, working with the perinatal team. Are you Jodie Pennington?","p":"Yeah, that's me."},{"d":"Congratulations, Jodie. I hear you've already stopped smoking and drinking. That's brilliant.","p":"Thanks. I'm trying."},{"d":"I know you're worried about the methadone. Can we talk about where you're at, then go through the options?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, congratulate, praise changes, agree the plan"},{"h":"Drug history","b":"Heroin years, injecting, methadone dose, stability, cravings, other drugs"},{"h":"Pregnancy and health","b":"Eight weeks, dating scan, hepatitis C, blood-borne virus tests, midwife"},{"h":"Mood and risk","b":"Depression, anxiety, relapse risk, overdose, domestic abuse"},{"h":"Methadone and baby","b":"Stopping risks relapse and withdrawal; staying on is safest; NAS explained"},{"h":"Options","b":"Continue, adjust dose later in pregnancy, slow reduction only if stable"},{"h":"After birth","b":"Baby observed several days, breastfeeding usually fine, safeguarding honestly"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, shared care plan, contacts"}],"bps":{"pre":"Jodie had told me she has not used heroin in four years and feels 'proud but scared'. She had read that babies 'come out withdrawing' and felt guilty. Her partner works and is supportive. She had not yet booked with a midwife. She was also frightened that social services would take the baby.","ask":"So should I just come off the methadone now?","lead":"I understand why you'd want to, but for most women the safest thing for the baby is to stay stable on methadone.","bio":["Continue methadone 30 mg daily; stopping or a fast reduction risks withdrawal, relapse and harm to the pregnancy","Dose may need increasing or splitting in the third trimester as the body clears methadone faster","If she still wants to reduce, only small, slow steps, ideally in the second trimester, with close monitoring","Hepatitis C viral load, HIV and hepatitis B tests; hepatology referral; baby tested after birth","After birth: baby observed for neonatal abstinence syndrome for at least 3-5 days; breastfeeding encouraged unless nipples crack and bleed"],"psych":["Regular keyworker sessions at the drug and alcohol service for relapse prevention","CBT or counselling for anxiety and guilt about the pregnancy","Antenatal education on caring for a baby with withdrawal symptoms (skin-to-skin, quiet room)"],"social":["Specialist substance misuse midwife and early booking with maternity services","Children's social care pre-birth assessment explained honestly as support, not punishment","Health visitor and family support; involve her partner","Safe storage of methadone in a locked box at home; naloxone kit for the household"],"safety":["If she relapses, tell the team at once; tolerance is lower and overdose risk is high","Call 999 for any overdose; 111 option 2 if mood or coping worsens"]}},{"sid":"M12.3","cid":"c-u-33","area":"Perinatal","title":"Low after the baby: taking the history","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Ffion Dempsey, a 32-year-old teaching assistant, has been referred to the psychiatry outpatient clinic by her GP. She gave birth to her second son six weeks ago and has felt low since. The GP is especially worried that she does not seem to be bonding with the new baby. Her partner is looking after both children in the waiting area. Please take a psychiatric history from Ms Dempsey to reach the most likely diagnosis, including a check of risk to herself and the children.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Ffion Dempsey?","p":"Yes. Sorry, I'm a bit of a mess."},{"d":"Please don't apologise, Ffion. Having a new baby is a lot, and your GP said you've been feeling low.","p":"I just don't feel how I should."},{"d":"How have things been for you since he arrived?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, normalise, why referred, agree the plan"},{"h":"Mood since birth","b":"Onset, low mood, tearfulness, interest, sleep beyond baby, appetite, energy"},{"h":"Bonding and baby","b":"Feelings toward baby, feeding, care, guilt, intrusive thoughts of harm"},{"h":"Pregnancy and birth","b":"Planned, complications, delivery, feeding, baby's health, partner support"},{"h":"Exclude other causes","b":"Baby blues, psychosis, mania, OCD, anaemia, thyroid, birth trauma"},{"h":"Background","b":"Past depression, previous postnatal illness, family history, alcohol, stressors"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts, self-harm, harm to baby or toddler, neglect, safeguarding"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Likely postnatal depression, teach-back, next steps, crisis numbers"}],"bps":{"pre":"Ffion had told me she cries most days, cannot sleep even when the baby sleeps, and feels 'nothing' when she holds him, which fills her with guilt. Her first pregnancy was followed by a milder low spell. She has had fleeting thoughts that the family would be better off without her, but no plans, and no thoughts of harming the baby. She is breastfeeding.","ask":"Is something wrong with me? What happens now?","lead":"What you're describing is postnatal depression; it's common, it's not your fault, and it gets better with help.","bio":["Sertraline 50 mg once daily is suitable with breastfeeding; review at 2 weeks, increase to 100 mg if needed","Warn about early nausea and anxiety in the first two weeks; continue at least 6 months after recovery","Bloods via GP: FBC (anaemia after birth), TFTs (postpartum thyroiditis), ferritin, vitamin D","Monitor the baby for feeding or sleep changes while on an SSRI; very low levels pass into milk"],"psych":["High-intensity CBT through NHS Talking Therapies, prioritised in the perinatal period, about 12-16 weekly sessions","Parent-infant work to build bonding, e.g. video interaction guidance with the perinatal team","Guided self-help or a postnatal group if she prefers lower intensity at first"],"social":["Referral to the specialist perinatal mental health team given previous postnatal low mood","Health visitor informed with consent for extra visits and baby checks","Partner support at night (expressed feeds) to protect her sleep; Home-Start volunteer","PANDAS Foundation or local mums' groups; children's centre activities"],"safety":["Crisis plan shared with partner; call 111 option 2 or 999 if she feels unable to keep herself or the children safe","Rapid review if new thoughts of harming the baby, confusion or unusual beliefs appear"]}},{"sid":"M12.4","cid":"c-u-34","area":"Perinatal","title":"'That's not my baby': assessing the mother","who":"Patient","task":"Mrs Deepa Raghunathan, 31, is on a postnatal ward five days after an emergency Caesarean section for fetal distress, complicated by heavy bleeding that needed a transfusion. Her daughter spent time in the neonatal unit but did well and was returned to her yesterday. While apart, Mrs Raghunathan was tearful, low and sleeping badly; since her daughter came back she has become more agitated and distressed, keeps asking where her 'real' baby is and is not bonding. In her early twenties, after her mother died, she was treated with sertraline for depression and anxiety; this was stopped when she became manic, and she had a brief admission with antipsychotic treatment and recovered fully. She lost touch with mental health services after moving areas and has been well since. She takes no regular medication. The obstetric team has asked liaison psychiatry to see her. Please complete a mental state assessment and risk assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors working in the hospital. Are you Mrs Raghunathan?","p":"Why? Who sent you?"},{"d":"The midwives asked me to check how you're doing after a very hard few days. I'm here to help, not to take anything away.","p":"Everyone keeps saying that."},{"d":"I understand. Could you tell me how things have been since your daughter was born?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, reassurance, why here, build trust despite suspicion"},{"h":"Appearance and behaviour","b":"Agitated, restless, guarded, eye contact, care of baby"},{"h":"Speech and mood","b":"Pressured speech, 'petrified', fear, low or elated, sleep, appetite"},{"h":"Thought content","b":"Baby swapped, misidentification, persecutory ideas about midwives"},{"h":"Perception","b":"Whispering voices through walls, crying baby in vents, other modalities"},{"h":"Cognition and insight","b":"Orientation, attention (delirium?), belief she is unwell, accepts treatment"},{"h":"Risk","b":"To baby, plan to leave and find 'real' baby, harm to others, self, absconding"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Explain concern, urgent plan, senior and team informed, constant observation"}],"bps":{"pre":"Mrs Raghunathan had spoken fast and in a whisper, saying the midwives call her a bad mother through the walls and that they swapped her baby. She heard her 'real' daughter crying in the air vent last night. She was orientated, denied suicidal thoughts, but said that once her wound allows she will leave and get her real baby back by any means, even if someone gets hurt.","ask":"Are you going to lock me up again? I just want my baby.","lead":"I can see how frightened you are, and I want to get you well so you can be with your daughter; that means you need treatment today.","bio":["Rule out delirium and physical causes: FBC (post-haemorrhage anaemia), CRP, cultures, U&Es, glucose, TFTs, calcium, wound review","Start an antipsychotic, e.g. olanzapine 5 mg at night, titrating to 10 mg; baseline ECG, weight, lipids, HbA1c","Lorazepam 0.5-1 mg orally for acute agitation (max 4 mg/24h) with obstetric team agreement","No antidepressant (past switch to mania); lithium may be considered later as a mood stabiliser","Breastfeeding plan discussed with her and the obstetric pharmacist for each medication"],"psych":["Calm, consistent nursing with clear explanations; avoid arguing with her beliefs","Psychoeducation for her husband about postpartum psychosis and recovery","Later: perinatal CBT and mother-infant bonding work once the psychosis settles"],"social":["Urgent Mental Health Act assessment (AMHP and two doctors) as she lacks insight and risk is high","Admission to a Mother and Baby Unit as first choice, so she can stay with her daughter safely","Safeguarding: baby cared for by husband or staff, never alone with mother for now; children's social care informed","Specialist perinatal team and health visitor; Action on Postpartum Psychosis for the family"],"safety":["Constant one-to-one observation; ward security and neonatal staff alerted to abduction risk","Escalate to the consultant today; call security or 999 if she tries to leave with a baby"]}},{"sid":"S1.1","cid":"c-t-27","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Found in the road at 3am: mental state","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Declan Ferris, a 30-year-old man, was found by police walking in the middle of a main road at 3am. He told the officers he was getting away from people who were shouting at him, and they noticed a strong smell of cannabis. He was detained under section 136 of the Mental Health Act and brought to the hospital's place of safety for assessment. He has not been seen by mental health services before. He is now sitting in the suite with a support worker nearby. Please carry out a mental state assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Can I check your name?","p":"Declan. Why am I here?"},{"d":"The police brought you here because they were worried about your safety on the road tonight. Are you hurt anywhere?","p":"No. They were shouting at me."},{"d":"I'd like to understand what's been happening. Could you tell me about tonight?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, why here, injuries, explain section 136 simply"},{"h":"Appearance and behaviour","b":"Self-care, clothing, agitation, guarding, responding to unseen stimuli"},{"h":"Speech and mood","b":"Rate, coherence, subjective and objective mood, anxiety, irritability"},{"h":"Thought form and content","b":"Disorganisation, persecutory beliefs, reference, passivity, thought interference"},{"h":"Perception","b":"Voices: who, how many, second or third person, commands, other senses"},{"h":"Cannabis and substances","b":"Type, amount, recent use, skunk, other drugs, alcohol"},{"h":"Cognition and insight","b":"Orientation, attention, intoxication, belief he is unwell, treatment"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Road danger, self-harm, suicide, harm to others, commands, vulnerability"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Findings, likely psychosis with cannabis, MHA assessment, next steps"}],"bps":{"pre":"Declan had told me that for three weeks people outside his flat have been shouting that he is a 'grass' and that they will get him. He hears them clearly even when alone, and believes the TV is sending him warnings. He smokes skunk daily, more since losing his job. He was orientated, denied suicidal thoughts, and ran into the road to escape the voices.","ask":"So what now? Can I go home?","lead":"I can see how scared you've been, and I don't think it's safe for you to go home tonight without more help.","bio":["Urine drug screen and bloods: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, glucose, TFTs, HIV and syphilis if consented; physical health check","If antipsychotic agreed: aripiprazole 10 mg daily or olanzapine 5-10 mg at night, starting low in first episode","Baseline before treatment: weight, BMI, waist, BP, pulse, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin, ECG","Lorazepam 1 mg orally if very distressed; avoid high doses; review response over 4-6 weeks"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis, at least 16 one-to-one sessions, from the early intervention in psychosis team","Family intervention, around 10 planned sessions with family or a close friend","Motivational interviewing to cut down or stop cannabis, with the drug service"],"social":["Mental Health Act assessment with an AMHP within the section 136 time limit; admission likely","Referral to early intervention in psychosis (EIP) service and a care coordinator","Housing and benefits advice (Universal Credit) after job loss","DVLA: he should not drive and must declare psychosis; contact family with consent"],"safety":["Stay in the place of safety until assessed; observation level agreed with staff","Crisis line (111 option 2) and the EIP team number after discharge"]}},{"sid":"S1.2","cid":"c-t-28","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Back in the hostel and withdrawing again","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Marcus Adeyemi is 43 and has lived with paranoid schizophrenia for many years. After a three-month inpatient stay for a relapse, he moved six weeks ago into a supported hostel as a step towards living on his own. For the past three weeks the hostel staff have seen him keep to his room, skip group meals and avoid the key workers. They worry he is becoming unwell again and have asked the CMHT for an urgent review, which takes place in a quiet room at the hostel. Please take a history of what has happened since discharge and assess his mental state.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Marcus Adeyemi?","p":"Yeah. What's this about?"},{"d":"Thanks for seeing me, Marcus. The staff here say you've mostly stayed in your room lately, and they wanted to check you're alright.","p":""},{"d":"Could you tell me how things have been since you left the ward?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, reason for visit, put him at ease, plan"},{"h":"Since discharge","b":"Move to hostel, routine, sleep, appetite, staff, residents"},{"h":"Medication and use","b":"Adherence, depot or tablets, side effects, cannabis, alcohol"},{"h":"Early warning signs","b":"Compare with last relapse, triggers, stress, insight into change"},{"h":"Psychotic symptoms","b":"Voices, commands, persecutory ideas about staff, passivity, thought interference"},{"h":"Mood and negative symptoms","b":"Low mood, hopelessness, motivation, self-care, social withdrawal"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicide, self-neglect, harm to others, vulnerability, fire safety"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, plan for medication, CMHT follow-up, crisis numbers"}],"bps":{"pre":"Marcus sat on his bed with the curtains drawn and food cartons on the desk. He admitted he had stopped his olanzapine a month ago because of weight gain. Over two weeks he began hearing a man's voice commenting on him and became convinced staff were reading his post. He denied plans to harm anyone and said he stays in to keep safe. Insight was partial; he agreed he was 'stressed'.","ask":"So what happens now? I'm not going back to hospital.","lead":"I hear you, Marcus, and our aim is to get you feeling settled here, not to rush you back onto the ward.","bio":["Restart antipsychotic he will accept: discuss aripiprazole 10 mg each morning, less weight gain than olanzapine","If adherence keeps slipping, offer long-acting injection, e.g. aripiprazole 400 mg monthly after an oral tolerability trial","Bloods at restart: FBC, U&E, LFT, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin; weight and BP baseline then at 12 weeks","Baseline ECG if cardiac risk factors; warn about restlessness (akathisia) and to report it","Urine drug screen with his agreement if any cannabis use"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis: at least 16 one-to-one sessions with a psychologist, working on the voice and suspicions","Relapse prevention work with care coordinator: written early warning signs card","Family intervention, 10 sessions over 3-12 months, if he wants his family involved","Healthy living sessions on diet and exercise to address his weight worries"],"social":["Care coordinator visits twice weekly for now; joint plan with hostel key worker","Home treatment team (crisis resolution) for daily contact if symptoms worsen","Structured day: day centre, IPS employment specialist, gradual activities outside his room","Check benefits and that the hostel tenancy is secure"],"safety":["Agreed plan: if voices become commanding or he feels unsafe, tell staff or call crisis line / 111 option 2","Staff to check room for fire hazards and monitor eating and self-care daily"]}},{"sid":"S1.3","cid":"c-u-35","area":"Forensic","title":"Jealous husband with a knife in custody","who":"Detained man","task":"Mr Gareth Pryce is a 36-year-old warehouse supervisor arrested late last night after police pulled his car over for driving well above the speed limit. He told the officers he was racing to the hospital where his wife is an emergency department nurse, to put a stop to what he believes is her affair with the senior charge nurse. A kitchen knife was found under his seat. While in the custody suite he has seemed agitated and preoccupied, and the custody healthcare team have asked for a psychiatric assessment. Please assess his mental state and carry out a full risk assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Gareth Pryce?","p":"Yes. Why am I seeing a shrink?"},{"d":"Officers were concerned about you overnight, so I've come to hear your side of things.","p":""},{"d":"I need to say that what you tell me may need to be shared if anyone could be in danger. Do you understand?","p":"Fine, whatever."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, role, limits of confidentiality, his account"},{"h":"Last night","b":"Why driving, intention, the knife, what he planned to do"},{"h":"Jealousy beliefs","b":"Evidence, conviction, checking phone and underwear, following, confrontation"},{"h":"Other psychopathology","b":"Other delusions, hallucinations, mood, alcohol, cocaine, organic causes"},{"h":"Background and forensic","b":"Past violence, domestic abuse, police contact, weapons, children at home"},{"h":"Risk to wife and others","b":"Threats, plans, rival, children, suicide, homicide-suicide risk"},{"h":"Insight and capacity","b":"Alternative explanations, willingness for help, acceptance of admission"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, admission advice, police, informing wife, safeguarding"}],"bps":{"pre":"Gareth spoke fast and stared at the door. For eight months he had been sure his wife was sleeping with her manager: he cited her new perfume and a car 'always' in their street. He had been checking her phone and following her shifts. He drank heavily most nights. He said he took the knife 'to make him admit it' and could not rule out using it. He accepted nothing else could explain it.","ask":"So what are you going to do with me?","lead":"Gareth, I think you are unwell and I'm worried for your safety and for others, so I want to get you proper care in hospital.","bio":["Likely delusional (morbid) jealousy: admission for assessment; Mental Health Act assessment if he declines","Antipsychotic once assessed, e.g. risperidone 1 mg rising to 2-4 mg daily; or aripiprazole 10 mg","Baseline bloods (FBC, LFT, GGT, U&E, glucose, lipids, prolactin), ECG and urine drug screen","Alcohol withdrawal plan: CIWA-Ar scoring and chlordiazepoxide if dependent; thiamine 100 mg three times daily","Consider organic screen (CT head) if neurological signs or late cognitive change"],"psych":["Engagement and trust building on the ward before challenging the belief directly","CBT for psychosis later, focusing on jealous beliefs and checking behaviours","Alcohol work: motivational interviewing with addictions team","Couple sessions only once risk is low and his wife chooses to take part"],"social":["Duty to protect: share risk with police and warn his wife (GMC public interest disclosure)","Safeguarding referral if children live at home; MARAC if domestic abuse risk is high","Police to keep the knife; review firearms licence or weapons access","Forensic psychiatry or liaison and diversion team advice; consider MAPPA if charged","Geographical separation advised on discharge; care coordinator in CMHT"],"safety":["Do not release him home: risk to wife judged high until assessed in hospital","Wife given advice and a contact number; 999 if he approaches her"]}},{"sid":"S1.4","cid":"c-t-29","area":"Old age","title":"Neighbours plotting against her at 74","who":"Patient","task":"Mrs Dorothy Pennington is a 74-year-old retired dinner lady who lives alone in a terraced house. Her son and daughter took her to the GP after she told them her neighbours were trying to drive her out, and the GP made an urgent referral to the older adults community mental health team. She is seen in clinic and is irritated to be there, insisting it is the people next door who need looking at, not her. Please assess her mental state focusing only on any abnormalities of thought and perception. A full history is not needed.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Mrs Dorothy Pennington?","p":"I am. And I shouldn't be here."},{"d":"Thank you for coming anyway, Mrs Pennington. It sounds like things with your neighbours have been very stressful.","p":"They have."},{"d":"I'd like to understand what's been happening, in your own words.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge her annoyance, avoid arguing, invite her story"},{"h":"The neighbours","b":"What they do, since when, evidence, conviction, other people involved"},{"h":"Other beliefs","b":"Partition delusions, through walls, gas, poisoning, theft, reference, grandiosity"},{"h":"Hallucinations","b":"Voices, knocking, smells, tastes, visual, tactile; timing and clarity"},{"h":"Passivity and thought","b":"Thought insertion, withdrawal, broadcast, control by others"},{"h":"Senses and cognition","b":"Hearing, eyesight, orientation, brief memory screen"},{"h":"Risk and coping","b":"Retaliation, barricading, eating, suicide, vulnerability, police calls"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, tests, follow-up, involve family with consent"}],"bps":{"pre":"Mrs Pennington explained that for a year her neighbours had pumped a sweet smell through the bedroom wall and taken her post. She heard them talking about her at night, by name, when the house was quiet. She was certain and angry. She wore hearing aids but rarely used them. Orientation and recall were good. She had not harmed anyone but had shouted at them.","ask":"If you won't stop them, what are you suggesting I do?","lead":"I can't promise to change the neighbours, but I can help with how unsettled and unsafe you're feeling at night.","bio":["Exclude organic causes: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, calcium, B12, folate, glucose, urine dip; MRI or CT head","Low-dose antipsychotic if she agrees: amisulpride 50 mg daily or risperidone 0.5 mg at night, slowly titrated","Baseline ECG and repeat after dose changes; check postural BP and falls risk","Warn about stiffness, sleepiness and falls; review at 4-6 weeks"],"psych":["Engagement first: regular visits from the same clinician to build trust without challenging the beliefs","CBT for psychosis adapted for older adults, around 16 sessions, if she is willing","Practical coping: ways to settle at night, radio, planned activities"],"social":["Older adults CMHT care coordinator and home visits","Audiology review and properly fitted hearing aids; optician check","Family meeting with her consent; support for her son and daughter","Housing officer and neighbour liaison only if she wishes; social prescribing to reduce isolation"],"safety":["Plan if she feels in danger at home: call family, GP or 111 option 2; 999 if emergency"]}},{"sid":"S1.5","cid":"c-u-36","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Refusing his depot and losing weight","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Kofi Mensah is a 35-year-old man with paranoid schizophrenia who moved into supported accommodation eight weeks ago after an admission for relapse. Over the past three weeks support workers have noticed him withdrawing from staff and other residents, living mostly on the odd takeaway and looking thinner. He refused his last depot injection and has not let the CMHT nurse in. His care coordinator has arranged for you to see him at the accommodation. Please assess his mental state, concentrating on psychotic symptoms and signs.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Kofi Mensah?","p":"...Yes."},{"d":"Thanks for letting me in, Kofi. I won't take long. Staff say you've been keeping to yourself and missing meals.","p":""},{"d":"I'd like to understand how things are for you. Is that alright?","p":"Okay."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, reason, calm approach, observe room and appearance"},{"h":"Appearance and behaviour","b":"Weight loss, self-care, guardedness, eye contact, responding to voices"},{"h":"Food and the depot","b":"Why takeaways only, poisoning beliefs, why injection refused"},{"h":"Delusions","b":"Persecution by staff, reference, control, grandiosity, conviction and evidence"},{"h":"Hallucinations","b":"Voices: number, content, commands, running commentary, other senses"},{"h":"Thought and mood","b":"Form of speech, thought interference, low mood, negative symptoms"},{"h":"Risk and insight","b":"Self-neglect, suicide, harm to staff, vulnerability, acceptance of illness"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, offer treatment options, next contact, crisis numbers"}],"bps":{"pre":"Kofi was thin and unshaven, glancing at the ceiling vent while we spoke. He said staff were 'putting something' in shared food so he buys sealed takeaways. He thought the depot was used to control him. A voice warned him not to trust the nurse. He denied wanting to hurt anyone or himself. He accepted he was not sleeping but not that he was ill.","ask":"If I don't have the injection, what then?","lead":"That's a fair question, Kofi, and I'd rather find a treatment you're comfortable with than force anything.","bio":["Offer oral antipsychotic he chooses, e.g. aripiprazole 10 mg daily, as a step back to treatment","Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, glucose, albumin, lipids, prolactin; weight, BMI and ECG","Check for refeeding risk if intake very low; dietitian if BMI falling","If he lacks capacity and risk rises, consider Mental Health Act assessment or community treatment order recall if on CTO"],"psych":["Engagement: same small team, short frequent visits, avoid confrontation over beliefs","CBT for psychosis (16+ sessions) once he is more settled","Psychoeducation on relapse signs, shared with him in writing"],"social":["Care coordinator to increase contact; home treatment team for daily visits if needed","Support staff to offer sealed or self-prepared food so he can eat safely","Advocacy (IMHA if detained) and involve family if he consents","Check benefits and day activities"],"safety":["Escalate if he stops eating or drinking, voices command harm, or he leaves the accommodation","Crisis line and 111 option 2 shared with staff and him"]}},{"sid":"S1.6","cid":"c-t-30","area":"Depression and suicide risk","title":"Writing a safety plan together","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Rhys Hollingworth is 43 and has had suicidal thoughts on and off for about ten years, usually without a firm plan or strong intent. He has been under his local mental health service for a long time and has tried many medicines and talking treatments, none with a lasting effect. His GP has referred him back because the thoughts are persisting. You are seeing him in the CMHT clinic. A history, mental state assessment and review of past treatment have already been done. Please work with him to draw up a written plan he can use when the thoughts arrive, and discuss ways he can manage them himself.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Rhys Hollingworth?","p":"Yes."},{"d":"Thanks for coming in, Rhys. I know you've lived with these thoughts for a long time, and coming back takes effort.","p":"It's the same old thing."},{"d":"Today I'd like us to write a plan together for the hard moments. Would that be okay?","p":"Go on then."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge long struggle, explain purpose, collaborative tone"},{"h":"Current risk check","b":"Thoughts now, intent, plans, access to means, protective factors"},{"h":"Warning signs","b":"Triggers, thoughts, feelings, times of day before a crisis"},{"h":"Own coping steps","b":"Distraction, grounding, exercise, music, delay, urge surfing"},{"h":"People and places","b":"Friends, family, safe places to go, who to tell"},{"h":"Professional contacts","b":"Care coordinator, GP, crisis line, 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123"},{"h":"Making it safer","b":"Remove or lock away tablets, ligatures, alcohol, safe storage"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Copy of plan, phone photo, review date, crisis numbers"}],"bps":{"pre":"Rhys said the thoughts arrive most evenings after drinking alone, often after rows with his ex-wife about access to his son. He had no plan now, and his son was his main reason to stay. Together we wrote down his warning signs, three things that help, and two friends he could text. He admitted he still kept old tablets in a drawer.","ask":"This is all useful, but is there anything else that would actually help long term?","lead":"Yes, Rhys, there are a few things alongside the plan that we can try, and some of them are different from before.","bio":["Review current antidepressant with him; if on one, keep a single, lower-toxicity agent such as sertraline 50-200 mg","Avoid tricyclics and large supplies: weekly or fortnightly prescriptions via GP","Pharmacy disposal of stockpiled tablets; review alcohol use and offer support to cut down","Check physical health: bloods, sleep, pain that might drive the thoughts"],"psych":["Safety plan (Stanley-Brown style) reviewed at every contact and kept on his phone","Psychological therapy aimed at suicidal thinking: CBT for suicide prevention or DBT skills group, weekly","Mindfulness-based approaches or Talking Therapies group for distress tolerance","Brief contact interventions: planned calls or texts after hard times"],"social":["Care coordinator with planned regular reviews, not only at crisis","Family mediation or legal advice on contact with his son","Alcohol service referral; Recovery College courses; peer support groups","Debt, employment or housing advice if stresses arise"],"safety":["Remove and return extra tablets to pharmacy today; agree to limit alcohol at home","Crisis line, 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123; 999 or A&E if he feels he cannot keep safe"]}},{"sid":"S1.7","cid":"c-s-46","area":"Forensic","title":"Fire setting on an acute ward","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Connor Fitzgerald is a 28-year-old man admitted to an acute adult psychiatric ward under section 2 four days ago, after his family took him to A&E because his behaviour had become strange. This afternoon staff found him standing in the activity room moments after he had set fire to a pile of papers; the fire was put out quickly and nobody was hurt. You are the duty doctor and have been asked to see him now in a safe room with a nurse present. Please explore the abnormal psychopathology and carry out a risk assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors on the ward. Are you Connor Fitzgerald?","p":"Yes."},{"d":"Connor, staff told me about the fire earlier. I'm glad nobody was hurt, including you.","p":""},{"d":"I'd like to understand what was going on for you. Can we talk it through?","p":"Okay."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, nurse present, calm, non-judgemental, invite his account"},{"h":"The fire","b":"What happened, why, lighter source, intended target, planning"},{"h":"Psychotic phenomena","b":"Voices, commands about fire, delusions, purification or religious themes"},{"h":"Mood and arousal","b":"Elation, irritability, low mood, sleep, agitation"},{"h":"Substances and organic","b":"Cannabis, stimulants on leave, withdrawal, head injury, epilepsy"},{"h":"Past fire and violence","b":"Previous fire setting, arson, aggression, forensic history"},{"h":"Current risk","b":"Further fire, self, others, staff, absconding, insight and remorse"},{"h":"Hand over and close","b":"Summary, observation level, search, nurse in charge, consultant"}],"bps":{"pre":"Connor whispered that a voice had told him the activity room was 'contaminated' and fire would cleanse it. He believed staff were agents of the devil. He had hidden a lighter from his tobacco after a visit. He set a small fire as a child but nothing since. He showed little concern for the danger and said the voice might ask again.","ask":"Are you going to lock me up now?","lead":"Connor, you're not in trouble with me; my job is to keep you and everyone else safe and get your treatment working.","bio":["Start or optimise antipsychotic, e.g. olanzapine 10 mg at night up to 20 mg, after baseline bloods and ECG","Rapid tranquillisation plan per NICE if acutely disturbed: oral lorazepam 1-2 mg first, IM options documented","Urine drug screen, physical obs; consider EEG/CT if any organic features","Review section: likely change to section 3 if longer treatment is needed"],"psych":["Daily one-to-one nursing time to explore the voices and beliefs","CBT for psychosis once settled; later fire-setting specific work with forensic psychology","Relapse plan including fire-related warning signs"],"social":["Enhanced observations (within eyesight or arm's length) and search of room and belongings","No lighters or matches; smoking cessation with NRT on ward","Incident (Datix) report; inform family with consent; police only if needed per policy","Consider PICU transfer or forensic opinion if risk persists"],"safety":["Ward fire plan and staff briefing; review observation level at each shift"]}},{"sid":"S2.1","cid":"c-u-37","area":"Depression and suicide risk","title":"Third overdose in six weeks","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Leah Cartwright is a 23-year-old retail assistant brought to A&E last night after swallowing a number of diazepam tablets. This is the third time she has overdosed in the past six weeks. The emergency team have finished her medical treatment, judged her physically fit, and want to know whether she can be discharged home. You are the liaison psychiatry doctor seeing her in a side room in the department. Please take a history, judge her level of risk and decide whether she needs to be admitted to a psychiatric ward.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists working with A&E. Are you Leah Cartwright?","p":"Yeah."},{"d":"Thanks for talking to me, Leah. How are you feeling physically this morning?","p":"Tired. Groggy."},{"d":"I'd like to understand what led to last night, and how you're feeling now. Is that okay?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, physical wellbeing, privacy, explain purpose"},{"h":"This overdose","b":"Trigger, tablets, source, planning, alone, note, how found"},{"h":"Intent and now","b":"Expected to die, regret, current thoughts, plans"},{"h":"Earlier overdoses","b":"Pattern over six weeks, escalation, self-harm, help sought"},{"h":"Mood and background","b":"Depression, psychosis, alcohol, drugs, personality, trauma"},{"h":"Supports and protective","b":"Family, friends, children, work, reasons to live"},{"h":"Risk and decision","b":"Formulation, admission versus home treatment, her views"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, plan, follow-up, crisis numbers, GP letter"}],"bps":{"pre":"Leah said she took about twenty of her mother's diazepam after her partner left, waited until she was alone and wished she had not woken up. The two earlier overdoses also followed rows. She drinks most nights, has stopped eating properly and sleeps badly. She has cut her arms since her teens. She still has thoughts of dying but agreed to talk.","ask":"So can I go home, or what?","lead":"Leah, I'm worried about how unsafe you still feel, and I think you need more help than going home alone tonight.","bio":["Admission (informal if she agrees) given rising frequency, intent and ongoing thoughts; MHA assessment if she refuses and risk stays high","No benzodiazepines prescribed; family to return or dispose of her mother's supply","If depressive episode: sertraline 50 mg daily, weekly prescriptions; warn about early agitation in under-25s","Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, GGT; alcohol withdrawal monitoring (CIWA-Ar) on ward"],"psych":["Psychosocial assessment completed and shared with her (NICE self-harm guidance)","Brief psychological follow-up then structured therapy, e.g. DBT if emotional instability is confirmed","Collaborative safety plan before discharge","Alcohol brief intervention and motivational interviewing"],"social":["If she declines admission and risk allows: home treatment team visits daily","Involve family or friend with her consent to stay with her","CMHT referral with care coordinator; GP informed within 24 hours","Housing or relationship support; alcohol service referral"],"safety":["Remove access to medicines at home; locked storage","Crisis line, 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123; 999 in an emergency"]}},{"sid":"S2.2","cid":"c-s-10","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Pregnant and afraid of passing it on","who":"Patient","task":"Mrs Nadia Rahman is a 27-year-old woman with schizophrenia who is supported by the community mental health team and has been stable for a year. She is now 12 weeks pregnant with her first child. She has asked to see you in clinic because she is thinking of ending the pregnancy, fearing her baby would inherit her illness. Her husband has no mental illness. Please explain to her what is known about the causes of schizophrenia, including inheritance, and address her worries.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Nadia Rahman?","p":"Yes."},{"d":"Congratulations on the pregnancy, Nadia. I understand you've got some big worries about it.","p":"I can't bear the idea of my baby going through this."},{"d":"That's very understandable. Shall we talk through what's actually known, and your questions?","p":"Please."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, congratulate, acknowledge fear, agree plan"},{"h":"Her worries","b":"What she has read, family history, partner's views, mood, risk"},{"h":"Causes of schizophrenia","b":"Many genes plus environment; no single gene; stress-vulnerability model"},{"h":"Actual risks","b":"General population about 1%; one parent about 10%; most children unaffected"},{"h":"Reducing risk","b":"Avoid cannabis, healthy pregnancy, stable home, early help if worried"},{"h":"Her own care","b":"Keep medication, folic acid, perinatal team, relapse risk postnatally"},{"h":"Her decision","b":"Her choice, no pressure, time, partner, ongoing support"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, leaflet, perinatal follow-up, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"Nadia had read online that schizophrenia is 'genetic' and assumed her child would definitely develop it. She has been well on aripiprazole for a year. Her husband is supportive and wants the baby. Her mood was a little low with worry but she had no thoughts of self-harm. After hearing that about nine in ten children of one affected parent stay well, she became tearful and relieved.","ask":"If I keep the baby, how do I make sure I stay well and the baby's okay?","lead":"That's exactly the right question, Nadia, and you won't be doing this on your own.","bio":["Continue aripiprazole at her current dose; do not stop suddenly because relapse risk is higher than drug risk","Folic acid 5 mg daily if on antipsychotic with raised BMI, otherwise 400 micrograms, until 12 weeks","Glucose tolerance test at 24-28 weeks (antipsychotic and gestational diabetes risk)","Avoid valproate; plan postnatal medication and breastfeeding advice with perinatal pharmacist"],"psych":["Psychoeducation with her and husband: stress-vulnerability model and relapse signs","Perinatal mental health team: regular reviews through pregnancy and the first year","Parenting and bonding support, e.g. parent-infant work if needed","Counselling to support her decision, without pressure either way"],"social":["Perinatal mental health team and care coordinator liaise with midwife and GP","Pre-birth planning meeting by 32 weeks; mother and baby unit if needed","Health visitor early contact; children's centre and family support","Partner and family involved with her consent; carers' assessment for husband"],"safety":["Seek urgent help if sleep fails, voices or suspicions return, especially in first weeks after birth"]}},{"sid":"S2.3","cid":"c-s-31","area":"Liaison","title":"Can he manage his own money?","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Owen Tredwell is a 39-year-old man with paranoid schizophrenia who has taken quetiapine for two years and lives alone in a rented flat. He has paid no rent for six months and his landlord has complained to the police. The police, working with the council, have asked for an opinion on whether he can manage his finances. You are seeing him at the CMHT office. Please assess his capacity to make decisions about handling his own money and explain your conclusion to him.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Owen Tredwell?","p":"Yes. Is this about the rent?"},{"d":"Partly, yes. Your landlord and the police are worried, and I'd like to hear your side.","p":""},{"d":"I'll ask some questions about how you manage money, then explain what I think. Okay?","p":"Alright."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, purpose, consent, presume capacity"},{"h":"The rent story","b":"Why unpaid, beliefs about landlord, letters, understanding of consequences"},{"h":"Money in and out","b":"Income, benefits, bills, budgeting, debts, who helps"},{"h":"Understand and retain","b":"Knows amounts, rent due, eviction risk; recalls information given"},{"h":"Weigh and communicate","b":"Uses information to decide; any delusions or low mood interfering"},{"h":"Mental state and treatment","b":"Adherence, psychotic symptoms, cognition, substances, exploitation"},{"h":"Outcome explained","b":"Capacity finding, reasons, support options, his views"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, written report, next steps, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Owen knew his rent was £550 a month and roughly how much Universal Credit he got. He stopped paying because he believed the landlord was 'spying through the smoke alarm' and money would 'fund' it. He could not weigh the risk of eviction against this belief. He had missed quetiapine doses for months. I explained that, today, his illness was stopping him making this decision.","ask":"So what happens to my money now? You're not taking it off me.","lead":"No, Owen, nobody is taking your money; we want to protect your home while you get well, and we'll keep checking.","bio":["Restart quetiapine at his effective dose, re-titrating if several days missed (e.g. 50 mg, then 100, 200, 300 mg)","Consider switching if adherence or sedation is the barrier; offer long-acting injection option","Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, glucose, lipids; ECG at dose change","Reassess capacity once psychosis improves; capacity is decision- and time-specific"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis to work on beliefs about the landlord","Psychoeducation and adherence work with care coordinator","Budgeting skills sessions with occupational therapy"],"social":["Best-interests plan: DWP appointeeship (often a family member or council) or Court of Protection deputy if longer term","Ask DWP for managed payment of housing costs direct to landlord","Housing officer and Citizens Advice debt advice to stop eviction","Police and safeguarding check for financial abuse or exploitation"],"safety":["If he receives an eviction notice, contact care coordinator or housing team at once"]}},{"sid":"S2.4","cid":"c-s-67","area":"Women's and gender health","title":"Mood swings: bipolar or monthly cycle?","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Ffion Morgan is a 22-year-old trainee accountant referred to the general adult outpatient clinic by her GP, who wonders if she has bipolar disorder because of repeated changes in her mood. She says she feels like 'a different person' for part of each month and it is affecting her work and her relationship. Please take a history to reach a diagnosis, ruling out the important differential diagnoses.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Ffion Morgan?","p":"Yes, hi."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Ffion. Your GP mentioned your mood has been up and down.","p":"It's horrible some weeks."},{"d":"Could you tell me what these changes are like and when they happen?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, purpose, open question about mood changes"},{"h":"Pattern and timing","b":"Days per cycle, link to periods, symptom-free week after menses"},{"h":"Core symptoms","b":"Irritability, low mood, anxiety, tearfulness, bloating, breast pain, cravings"},{"h":"Bipolar excluded","b":"Elevated mood, reduced sleep need, grandiosity, spending, duration"},{"h":"Other differentials","b":"Depression, EUPD, thyroid, PCOS, substances, contraceptive pill"},{"h":"Impact and risk","b":"Work, relationship, self-harm, suicidal thoughts in luteal phase"},{"h":"Background","b":"Gynae history, contraception, family history, previous treatment"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Diagnosis explained, symptom diary, plan, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Ffion described a week before each period of rage, tearfulness and feeling hopeless, with bloating and craving sweets; she picks fights with her boyfriend and calls in sick. Symptoms lift within two days of bleeding starting. She has never had high mood or reduced need for sleep. No self-harm. Her app showed this pattern every month for a year, pointing to premenstrual dysphoric disorder.","ask":"So it's not bipolar? What can I do about it?","lead":"From what you've described, Ffion, this fits premenstrual dysphoric disorder rather than bipolar, and it is very treatable.","bio":["Confirm with prospective daily symptom diary over two cycles (e.g. DRSP)","SSRI: sertraline 50 mg daily, either continuously or luteal phase only (day 14 to period); warn about nausea","Alternative: combined pill with drospirenone (e.g. Yasmin) taken continuously, via GP; check VTE risk","Bloods if indicated: TFT, FBC; if no response, gynaecology referral for GnRH analogue options"],"psych":["CBT, individually or group, around 8-12 weekly sessions, via NHS Talking Therapies","Psychoeducation on the cycle, planning tasks around luteal weeks","Stress management, exercise and sleep routine"],"social":["GP to manage and review at 3 months; discharge from secondary care","Employer adjustments: flexible working in luteal week via occupational health","Partner involvement and couple communication strategies","Support groups, e.g. National Association for Premenstrual Syndrome"],"safety":["If she has suicidal thoughts in the luteal phase, use crisis line or 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"S2.5","cid":"c-u-38","area":"Addictions","title":"Frightened and shaking after his fracture","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Desmond Clarke is a 70-year-old retired bus driver on the orthopaedic ward two days after a fall. A CT head was normal with no sign of head injury. He fractured his wrist, which was fixed in theatre, and he is waiting for an occupational therapy assessment before going home. Overnight the surgical team grew worried because he became frightened, restless and tremulous. Admission bloods showed a normal white cell count, slightly raised CRP, raised MCV, GGT and ALP. You are the liaison psychiatry doctor. Please take a short focused history and assess his mental state to reach a diagnosis.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Desmond Clarke?","p":"Who are you? Get them off the wall!"},{"d":"You're safe, Desmond. You're in hospital, after your fall. I'm a doctor here to help.","p":""},{"d":"Can you tell me what you're seeing? Then I'll ask a few questions.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, reassure, orientate, calm environment"},{"h":"Brief history","b":"Alcohol quantity, last drink, previous withdrawal, fits, falls"},{"h":"Orientation and attention","b":"Time, place, person; months backwards; fluctuation"},{"h":"Perceptions","b":"Visual hallucinations, insects, tactile, voices, misperceptions"},{"h":"Mood and thought","b":"Fear, persecutory ideas, disorganisation"},{"h":"Physical signs","b":"Tremor, sweating, tachycardia, fever, ataxia, eye movements"},{"h":"Diagnosis and differentials","b":"Delirium tremens; exclude infection, head injury, hypoglycaemia, Wernicke's"},{"h":"Hand over and close","b":"Summary to surgical team, treatment plan, monitoring, review"}],"bps":{"pre":"Desmond was sweating and picking at the sheets, convinced spiders were crawling on the wall. He did not know the date or which hospital he was in. His wife later confirmed he drinks about a bottle of whisky every two days and his last drink was the night before his fall. Pulse was 112 and temperature 37.8. The picture was delirium tremens.","ask":"What's wrong with me? Make it stop.","lead":"Desmond, your body is reacting to suddenly going without alcohol, and we can treat it to make you feel safer.","bio":["Chlordiazepoxide, symptom-triggered using CIWA-Ar, e.g. 10-20 mg doses with lower totals in older adults; lorazepam if liver impairment is significant","Pabrinex IV two pairs three times daily for 3-5 days for suspected Wernicke's, before any glucose","Bloods: U&E, magnesium, phosphate, glucose, LFT, clotting; correct electrolytes","Exclude infection and subdural: obs, cultures, repeat CT if confusion persists; ECG"],"psych":["Nurse in a calm, well-lit room with family visits for reorientation","Once recovered: alcohol brief intervention and motivational interviewing","Relapse prevention work with community alcohol team"],"social":["Alcohol care team input on the ward; one-to-one nursing if very agitated","Wife involved in plan with his consent; carers' support","Community alcohol service referral; GP informed","DVLA advice on alcohol dependence before driving again"],"safety":["Falls precautions and seizure plan; escalate to medical team if obs worsen","Avoid sedating antipsychotics alone; haloperidol only as adjunct with ECG check"]}},{"sid":"S2.6","cid":"c-u-39","area":"Forensic","title":"Exposing himself near a playground","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Kieran Holt is a 38-year-old delivery driver brought to A&E by police after he allegedly exposed his genitals to a group of primary school children near a park. Officers want a psychiatric opinion before deciding next steps. You are the duty psychiatry doctor seeing him in a quiet assessment room in A&E. Please explore the factors that may have led to this behaviour and carry out a risk assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Kieran Holt?","p":"Yeah."},{"d":"I'm here to understand what happened today and how you're doing. It may be hard to talk about.","p":""},{"d":"What you tell me may be shared if children or others are at risk. Is that clear?","p":"Okay."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, purpose, confidentiality limits, non-judgemental stance"},{"h":"Today's incident","b":"Circumstances, planning, targeted children, arousal, masturbation, aftermath"},{"h":"Sexual history","b":"Interests, fantasies, child images, previous exposure, relationships"},{"h":"Mental illness","b":"Mania, psychosis, depression, learning disability, autism"},{"h":"Organic and substances","b":"Dementia, frontal injury, epilepsy, alcohol, drugs, medication"},{"h":"Background","b":"Childhood abuse, relationships, work with children, forensic record"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Re-offending, escalation to contact offences, access to children, suicide"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, findings, police, safeguarding, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Kieran said he had felt an urge building for weeks and drove to the park deliberately, feeling aroused by the children's reaction. He had done this twice before without being caught. He denied contact with children but admitted viewing images of under-age girls online. No psychosis or mania; he drank before each episode. He felt ashamed and had thought about suicide since his arrest.","ask":"What happens now? Can anyone help me stop this?","lead":"Kieran, wanting help is important, and there is treatment, though the police will also need to be involved.","bio":["No mental illness found requiring admission; return to police custody with risk information shared","Specialist forensic sexual behaviour assessment; SSRI such as fluoxetine 20 mg rising to 40-60 mg to reduce urges","Antilibidinal drugs (cyproterone or GnRH analogue) only via specialist with informed consent, baseline bloods and bone health","Rule out organic causes if any doubt: bloods, CT head, cognitive screen"],"psych":["Accredited group programme for men with sexual convictions (e.g. Horizon or Kaizen via probation)","Individual CBT focused on urges, relapse prevention and alcohol","Stop It Now confidential helpline and self-help programme"],"social":["Police informed of risk; children's safeguarding referral and LADO if he works near children","Employer and vehicle access considered via police and probation","MAPPA if convicted and placed on sex offenders register","Alcohol service referral; GP informed"],"safety":["Suicide risk after arrest: share with custody staff, crisis line and 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"S2.7","cid":"c-s-60","area":"Eating disorders","title":"Anorexia: what helps her recover","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Imogen Byrne-Walsh is 25 and carries diagnoses of depression and emotionally unstable personality disorder, and more recently anorexia nervosa. Her notes record several overdoses and past inpatient admissions. She is not close to her parents but has a partner and a close friend, and she attends the eating disorder clinic regularly. You are seeing her in that clinic. Please take a brief history aimed at the factors that point to a better or worse outlook for her anorexia, and briefly explore her personality traits.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Imogen Byrne-Walsh?","p":"Yes, Immy."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Immy. I'd like to understand your eating difficulties and what's helped or got in the way.","p":"Okay."},{"d":"Some questions may feel personal. Tell me if you need a break.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, purpose, sensitivity, permission"},{"h":"Onset and course","b":"Age at onset, duration, lowest BMI, current weight trend"},{"h":"Behaviours","b":"Restriction, purging, laxatives, exercise; binge-purge worse than restricting"},{"h":"Physical status","b":"Periods, dizziness, fainting, bone health, bloods, ECG"},{"h":"Comorbidity","b":"Depression, overdoses, self-harm, substances, OCD traits"},{"h":"Personality traits","b":"Perfectionism, impulsivity, emotional instability, relationships, self-image"},{"h":"Supports and motivation","b":"Partner, friend, parents, engagement, readiness to change, risk"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Prognostic summary, plan, follow-up, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"Immy developed anorexia at 22, later than many, and has had it three years. Her BMI is 15.8, she restricts and sometimes vomits. She described perfectionism, rapid mood swings and intense fear of rejection. Good signs: she attends every appointment, her partner and friend are supportive, and she wants to finish her nursing course. Poorer signs: vomiting, low BMI, overdoses and distance from her family.","ask":"So be honest. Am I going to get better, and how?","lead":"Many people do recover, Immy, and you have real strengths on your side; let me explain what we can do.","bio":["No medication treats anorexia itself; avoid using drugs as the main treatment","Physical monitoring: weight, BMI, bloods (U&E, phosphate, magnesium, glucose, LFT), ECG for QTc and bradycardia per MEED","DEXA scan if amenorrhoea beyond a year; calcium and vitamin D","For depression, if needed, a lower-risk SSRI such as sertraline 50 mg with weekly prescriptions; avoid QT-prolonging drugs"],"psych":["Eating disorder focused CBT (CBT-ED), up to 40 sessions over 40 weeks","Or MANTRA or SSCM, about 20 sessions, per NICE for adults","DBT skills for emotional instability, weekly group plus individual work","Motivational work to strengthen her own reasons to recover"],"social":["Eating disorder service key worker and dietitian; GP shared physical monitoring","Partner and friend invited to a carers' session with her consent","University support and occupational health adjustments for her course","Beat helpline and carers' resources"],"safety":["Crisis plan for overdose urges; limited medicine supply at home","Admission criteria explained: falling BMI, fainting, abnormal bloods or ECG"]}},{"sid":"S2.8","cid":"c-t-31","area":"Old age","title":"Galantamine: questions from his son","who":"Son","task":"Mr Harold Whitaker is a 79-year-old retired joiner diagnosed with Alzheimer's disease after assessment in the memory clinic. At today's appointment the team have decided to start galantamine. His son, Mr Neil Whitaker, a 50-year-old teacher, has asked to speak with you about the medicine; his father has given consent for this. Please explain to Neil the benefits and side effects of galantamine and answer his concerns. A history is not needed.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the memory clinic. Are you Neil Whitaker?","p":"Yes, Harold's son."},{"d":"Thanks for staying, Neil. Your dad has said he's happy for us to talk about his treatment.","p":""},{"d":"What do you already know about galantamine, and what's on your mind?","p":"Mainly whether it actually works."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent from father, explore his knowledge"},{"h":"Diagnosis recap","b":"Alzheimer's, mild to moderate stage, what it means"},{"h":"How galantamine works","b":"Boosts acetylcholine, slows symptoms, not a cure"},{"h":"Expected benefits","b":"Memory, daily function, behaviour; about one in three notice benefit"},{"h":"Dosing","b":"8 mg XL with breakfast, rise to 16 mg after 4 weeks"},{"h":"Side effects","b":"Nausea, weight loss, dizziness, slow pulse, fainting, rash"},{"h":"Monitoring and review","b":"ECG if heart disease, pulse, weight, review at 3-6 months"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, leaflet, carer support, Alzheimer's Society, contact number"}],"bps":{"pre":"Neil explained his father had become lost driving home twice and was repeating questions. He had read that these drugs 'don't do much' and was worried about side effects because his dad has a slow heartbeat on bisoprolol. I explained the realistic aims of the drug and that we would check his heart first. He seemed reassured but asked what else could be done.","ask":"Apart from the tablet, what else can we do for Dad?","lead":"Quite a lot, Neil; the medicine is only one part of keeping your dad well and at home.","bio":["Galantamine XL 8 mg once daily with food for 4 weeks, then 16 mg daily; maximum 24 mg","Baseline ECG and pulse given bisoprolol; hold and review if pulse below 50 or fainting","Warn: nausea, poor appetite, weight loss, dizziness; stop and seek help for serious skin rash","Review at 3-6 months with cognition, function and side effects; GP shared care"],"psych":["Group cognitive stimulation therapy: 14 sessions, twice weekly, per NICE","Life story work and reminiscence with memory service","Carer psychoeducation and skills training for Neil"],"social":["Carers' assessment for Neil via the council; Admiral Nurse support","Lasting Power of Attorney for finances and health while Harold can decide","DVLA must be told; driving assessment or stopping driving","Alzheimer's Society support, Attendance Allowance and council tax reduction"],"safety":["Seek urgent help if fainting, falls, black stools or severe vomiting"]}},{"sid":"S3.1","cid":"c-s-56","area":"Personality and therapies","title":"Wants to quit therapy after cancelling","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Graham Ashworth is a 52-year-old former secondary school geography teacher who was made redundant last year and has been having weekly psychodynamic psychotherapy for depression. You are reviewing him in the psychiatric outpatient clinic. He tells you he cancelled last week's session and now wants to stop therapy altogether. Please listen to him and address his concerns, explore whether transference may be playing a part, and agree a plan. There is no need to take a history of his depression.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Graham Ashworth?","p":"That's me."},{"d":"Thanks for coming in, Graham. I hear you've been having second thoughts about your therapy.","p":"I want to finish with it."},{"d":"I'd like to understand what's happened. Could you tell me more?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge his decision, curious open stance"},{"h":"Why he cancelled","b":"Events in last session, feelings, what he wants"},{"h":"The therapist relationship","b":"How therapist comes across, who it reminds him of"},{"h":"Transference explored","b":"Links to headteacher or father, rejection, criticism, redundancy"},{"h":"Explain transference","b":"Normal, useful, often a sign therapy is working"},{"h":"Mood and risk","b":"Current mood, hopelessness, suicidal thoughts, alcohol"},{"h":"Options and plan","b":"Return and discuss, joint review, change therapist, his choice"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, contact therapist, follow-up date"}],"bps":{"pre":"Graham said his therapist glanced at the clock and seemed 'bored', and when she asked about the redundancy he felt judged, just as his headteacher had made him feel. He realised his father was similarly critical. He stormed out, then cancelled. His mood was a little lower but he had no suicidal thoughts. After I explained transference, he looked thoughtful.","ask":"So what should I do now? Go back and face her?","lead":"Graham, you're in charge of this choice, but going back to talk about exactly these feelings could be the most useful session you've had.","bio":["Continue current antidepressant, e.g. sertraline 100 mg daily, unchanged during this period","Review mood at follow-up; no new medication needed for this issue","Check alcohol intake and sleep"],"psych":["Return to the next session and bring these feelings to the therapist; transference worked through in therapy","Joint review meeting with him, therapist and psychiatrist if he prefers","Therapist to take the rupture to clinical supervision","If he still wishes, ending planned over 2-3 sessions or change of therapist or model, e.g. CBT"],"social":["Careers advice, Jobcentre Plus or retraining to address the redundancy","Volunteering or tutoring to rebuild structure and confidence","Peer support group for men, e.g. local community groups"],"safety":["If mood drops or suicidal thoughts arise, contact GP, crisis line or 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"S3.2","cid":"c-s-22","area":"Medication, emergencies and side effects","title":"Quetiapine and a long QTc","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Ibrahim Sesay is a 46-year-old man with long-standing schizophrenia, maintained on quetiapine 700 mg daily. He has come to the rehab team's physical health clinic for his yearly check, and his ECG shows sinus rhythm at 72 per minute with a QTc of 486 ms, longer than last year's 430 ms. He is anxious about what the tracing means. Please review the ECG, explain the findings, ask about risk factors for QTc prolongation and discuss the plan with him. A physical assessment is not required.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Ibrahim Sesay?","p":"Yes. Is my heart okay?"},{"d":"I can see you're worried, Ibrahim. I've looked at your heart tracing and I'll explain it clearly.","p":""},{"d":"First, can I ask a few questions about your health?","p":"Okay."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge anxiety, plan for the talk"},{"h":"ECG reading","b":"Rate 72, sinus rhythm, QTc 486 ms, previous 430 ms"},{"h":"Symptoms","b":"Palpitations, fainting, blackouts, chest pain, breathlessness"},{"h":"Risk factors","b":"Family sudden death, heart disease, low potassium, vomiting, diuretics"},{"h":"Other drugs","b":"Methadone, citalopram, erythromycin, antihistamines, cocaine, alcohol, over-the-counter"},{"h":"Explain findings","b":"Plain meaning, quetiapine link, why it matters, rarely dangerous"},{"h":"Plan","b":"Repeat ECG, bloods, dose reduction or switch, cardiology"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, warning symptoms, follow-up date"}],"bps":{"pre":"Ibrahim had no palpitations or blackouts, but recently started citalopram from his GP and had diarrhoea last week. His uncle died suddenly at 40. I explained the QTc measures how long the heart takes to reset between beats, and that his was longer than ideal, probably from quetiapine and citalopram together. He was worried he would need to stop his medicine.","ask":"What are you going to do about it? I don't want to get ill again.","lead":"Ibrahim, we can make this safer without leaving you unprotected from your illness.","bio":["Repeat ECG within a week; check potassium, magnesium, calcium, TFT today and correct any low levels","Ask GP to stop citalopram; if antidepressant needed, sertraline 50 mg has less QT effect","Reduce quetiapine gradually (e.g. to 500 mg) or switch to aripiprazole, which has little QT effect, by cross-titration","Cardiology referral given QTc above 470 ms and family history of sudden death; urgent if over 500 ms or symptoms"],"psych":["Explain changes in plain language to reduce anxiety and protect adherence","Relapse signs shared with him during any switch","Anxiety management if worry persists"],"social":["Care coordinator and rehab team monitor mental state during switch","GP and pharmacist informed; drug interaction alert on records","Family history: suggest relatives see their GP for ECG"],"safety":["Call 999 if fainting, palpitations or chest pain","Avoid new medicines, including over-the-counter, without checking with pharmacist"]}},{"sid":"S3.3","cid":"c-t-32","area":"Addictions","title":"Back from leave and refusing a drug test","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Tyrone Bailey is a 31-year-old man with paranoid schizophrenia and a history of using several drugs. He is detained on an acute ward and has just come back from section 17 leave in town. Nurses have called you because he is behaving oddly, with strange jerky movements and seeming to respond to things nobody else can see. They suspect he took drugs while out but he will not give a urine sample. Please speak to him and try to persuade him to agree to a urine drug screen. A history and mental state assessment are not needed.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors on the ward. Are you Tyrone Bailey?","p":"What do you want?"},{"d":"The nurses are worried about you since you came back, Tyrone, and I want to check you're okay.","p":"I'm fine. Leave me alone."},{"d":"I'll be quick. Can we sit down and talk for a few minutes?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, calm, acknowledge annoyance, safe space"},{"h":"Explore concerns","b":"Why refusing, fear of losing leave, police, being judged"},{"h":"Why test matters","b":"His safety, odd movements, interactions with medication, right treatment"},{"h":"Address worries","b":"Confidential to care team, not police, honest about leave review"},{"h":"Persuade and negotiate","b":"Motivational approach, options, his choice, time to think"},{"h":"If refusing","b":"Respect refusal, cannot force, observation, physical checks"},{"h":"Risk and safety","b":"Physical obs, overdose signs, aggression, absconding"},{"h":"Hand over and close","b":"Summary, nurse in charge, consultant, document, review"}],"bps":{"pre":"Tyrone eventually admitted he smoked 'spice' with a friend in town and felt strange since. He refused at first because he feared losing all leave and police involvement. I explained the result stays with the care team and helps us keep him safe and treat him properly. He agreed to give a sample if he could do it privately.","ask":"Fine, I'll do it. But what happens next? Am I in trouble?","lead":"You're not in trouble with me, Tyrone; you've done the right thing, and now we'll focus on keeping you well.","bio":["Urine drug screen (synthetic cannabinoids may need specialist lab testing)","Physical observations with NEWS2 hourly then four-hourly, ECG, bloods including CK if agitated","Review antipsychotic; PRN lorazepam 1 mg for agitation, avoiding sedating doses on top of intoxication","Assess abnormal movements: dystonia treated with procyclidine 5 mg, or intoxication if no other cause"],"psych":["Motivational interviewing with dual diagnosis worker about drug use","Psychoeducation: synthetic cannabinoids worsen psychosis","Relapse prevention plan for future leave"],"social":["Section 17 leave reviewed by responsible clinician, not removed as punishment; staff escort for now","Ward search policy explained; visitors and supplier risk considered","Family involvement with consent; community drug service in discharge plan"],"safety":["Escalate to medical team if chest pain, seizures, high temperature or reduced consciousness"]}},{"sid":"S3.4","cid":"c-u-40","area":"Women's and gender health","title":"Asking to start oestrogen","who":"Patient","task":"Sam Kowalski is a 29-year-old software tester, registered male at birth, referred to the general adult clinic by the GP after asking to be prescribed oestrogen. The GP would like a psychiatric opinion before referring on. You are seeing Sam in outpatients. Please take a history to reach a diagnosis, considering important differentials. A mental state assessment is not required.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. What name and pronouns would you like me to use?","p":"Sophie, and she/her please."},{"d":"Thank you, Sophie. Your GP mentioned you'd like to start oestrogen.","p":"Yes, I've waited long enough."},{"d":"I'd like to understand your experience and what you're hoping for. Is that okay?","p":"Sure."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, pronouns, respect, purpose of meeting"},{"h":"Gender identity","b":"Onset in childhood, persistence, feelings about body, how she identifies"},{"h":"Living as herself","b":"Social transition, name, clothing, who knows, work"},{"h":"Goals and hormones","b":"Expectations, any self-sourced hormones, surgery hopes, fertility"},{"h":"Differentials","b":"Fetishistic cross-dressing, psychosis, body dysmorphia, autism, trauma"},{"h":"Mental health and risk","b":"Mood, anxiety, self-harm, suicidal thoughts, substances, harassment"},{"h":"Supports","b":"Family, partner, friends, community groups"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, diagnosis, referral pathway, support, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Sophie said she has felt she was a girl since about six, hid it through school and has lived as a woman full time for two years. There was no sexual arousal linked to clothing. No psychosis or body dysmorphia. She had low mood from waiting and family rejection but no suicidal plans. She had looked at buying hormones online. The picture fitted gender incongruence.","ask":"So will you start me on oestrogen today?","lead":"I can't prescribe it myself today, Sophie, but I can explain the route and what can help while you wait.","bio":["Oestrogen is started via an NHS Gender Dysphoria Clinic (or GP with specialist advice), not by general psychiatry","Warn against unregulated internet hormones: clot, liver and dosing risks; if already taking, tell GP for safety bloods","GP baseline bloods: FBC, LFT, lipids, HbA1c, prolactin, testosterone; BP and smoking advice","Fertility preservation discussion before any hormone treatment"],"psych":["Supportive counselling while waiting; Talking Therapies for low mood","Voice and communication therapy via speech and language therapy, if wished","Peer support groups for trans people"],"social":["GP referral to adult Gender Dysphoria Clinic (self-referral possible in some areas)","Name change by deed poll; NHS records updated","Workplace support and protection under the Equality Act 2010","LGBT+ support services, e.g. Switchboard; family support resources"],"safety":["If low mood worsens or suicidal thoughts arise, contact GP, crisis line or Samaritans 116 123"]}},{"sid":"S3.5","cid":"c-s-68","area":"Women's and gender health","title":"Low mood in perimenopause on lithium","who":"Patient","task":"Mrs Grace Adeyemi, aged 49, has recurrent depressive disorder, and ten years ago had one depressive episode with psychotic features. For the last six years she has been well on lithium carbonate (Priadel) 800 mg at night together with citalopram 20 mg each morning, and every lithium check in that time has come back normal. Over the past few months she has gone back to her GP saying her mood has not felt right, and the GP has asked the community team to see her. She works as a school administrator. Please take a history to work out what is driving her current symptoms.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Grace Adeyemi?","p":"Yes, that's me."},{"d":"Thank you for coming in, Grace. Your GP mentioned your mood has felt different for a few months.","p":"It has. I just don't feel like myself."},{"d":"I'd like to hear what has changed, then look at the possible reasons together. Is that alright?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge the change, agree to explore causes together"},{"h":"Current symptoms","b":"Mood, irritability, tearfulness, sleep, energy, concentration, anxiety, onset and pattern"},{"h":"Relapse or not","b":"Core depressive features, any psychotic symptoms, compare with past episodes"},{"h":"Menopause features","b":"Periods changing, hot flushes, night sweats, brain fog, libido, joint aches"},{"h":"Lithium and physical causes","b":"Adherence, last level, thyroid, calcium, kidneys, NSAIDs, diuretics, weight"},{"h":"Life and stressors","b":"Work, relationship, children leaving, caring roles, bereavement, alcohol"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts, plans, overdose access to lithium, self-neglect"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Likely perimenopause plus checks, bloods, GP link, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"Grace described three months of flat, tearful days, broken sleep from night sweats and feeling foggy at work. Her periods had become irregular and she kept snapping at her husband. She denied voices or odd beliefs and was taking her tablets every night. She had no thoughts of ending her life. She looked worried and asked the obvious question.","ask":"Is this the depression coming back, or is it my age? What can be done?","lead":"It sounds like the perimenopause is playing a big part, and there is a lot we can do, starting with some simple checks.","bio":["Lithium level 12 hours after the night dose (aim 0.6-0.8 mmol/L), plus U&E, eGFR, calcium and TFTs this week","Thyroid function matters: lithium can cause hypothyroidism, which mimics low mood and fatigue; treat with levothyroxine if low","GP or menopause clinic to discuss HRT (oestrogen with progestogen as she has a uterus); HRT does not interact with lithium","If mood stays low once hormones are addressed, citalopram can go from 20 mg to 30-40 mg; check ECG if cardiac risk","Warn her to avoid ibuprofen and similar NSAIDs, and to drink well in hot weather, as both can push lithium levels up"],"psych":["CBT for menopausal symptoms (NICE 2024), about 6 sessions with a psychologist or Talking Therapies, helps mood, sleep and flushes","Relapse signature work with her care coordinator: early warning signs from her psychotic episode written into a plan","Psychoeducation for Grace and her husband on perimenopause and mood, so changes are understood rather than feared"],"social":["GP to lead menopause care and repeat lithium monitoring every 3 months, with kidney and thyroid checks every 6 months","Occupational health and employer adjustments: flexible hours, a desk fan, breaks during flushes","Menopause support groups and the NHS menopause pages for reliable information","Husband invited to a joint appointment if Grace agrees"],"safety":["Lithium supply kept to what is needed; if suicidal thoughts appear, contact the crisis team or NHS 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"S3.6","cid":"c-s-26","area":"Old age","title":"DaTscan result: dementia with Lewy bodies","who":"Sister","task":"Mr Daniel Okafor is a 56-year-old man with mild intellectual disability who lives in supported accommodation. Over the past year his memory has gone up and down from day to day and he has started falling, neither of which is like him. Lately he has said he can see things that are not there, such as little animals on the curtains, though they do not upset him. After an initial assessment he had a DaTscan, which showed less dopamine transporter activity than expected in the basal ganglia. His sister Ruth is his main support, and there is an existing agreement that she may receive information about his care. She has come to clinic for the result. Please explain the findings and the diagnosis and talk through a management plan with her.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Ruth, Daniel's sister?","p":"Yes. Is it bad news?"},{"d":"Thank you for coming, Ruth. Daniel has agreed we can share his results with you, so we can talk openly.","p":""},{"d":"Could you first tell me how he's been lately and what you already know about the scan?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, confirm sharing agreement, what she knows, warning shot"},{"h":"How Daniel is now","b":"Fluctuation, falls, things he sees, sleep, acting out dreams, mood, daily skills"},{"h":"The scan result","b":"Dopamine transporters, reduced uptake, supports Lewy body diagnosis, plain words"},{"h":"Dementia with Lewy bodies","b":"Fluctuation, visions, movement, sleep, progressive course, nobody's fault"},{"h":"Medication plan","b":"Donepezil or rivastigmine, ECG, avoid antipsychotics, careful with levodopa"},{"h":"Falls and daily life","b":"Physiotherapy, OT, home safety, easy-read information, LD team"},{"h":"Future and capacity","b":"Best interests, LPA, advance care planning, carer's assessment"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, her questions, teach-back, leaflet, follow-up date"}],"bps":{"pre":"Ruth described Daniel having good mornings and muddled afternoons, three falls since spring and chatting about the 'little dogs' on his curtains. Staff had seen him shouting in his sleep. She listened quietly while the scan was explained, then said she had read that dementia simply gets worse. She wanted to know what anyone could actually offer him.","ask":"So what can you do for him now? Is there a tablet?","lead":"There is treatment that can help, and a lot of it is about keeping Daniel safe, steady and doing what he enjoys.","bio":["Donepezil 5 mg at night, rising to 10 mg after 4 weeks if tolerated, or rivastigmine (NICE first choices for Lewy body dementia)","Baseline ECG and pulse before starting, as these drugs can slow the heart and add to falls; watch for nausea and vivid dreams","Avoid antipsychotics such as haloperidol: people with Lewy bodies can have severe, sometimes dangerous reactions","Neurology or movement review if stiffness worsens; levodopa at low dose only, as it can increase hallucinations","Medication review to stop anything anticholinergic or sedating that worsens confusion or falls"],"psych":["Easy-read and pictorial explanation of the diagnosis, delivered with the community learning disability nurse","Simple strategies for the things he sees: good lighting, calm reassurance, not arguing with what he sees","Life story work and familiar routines, planned by the LD psychologist with staff, to support memory and mood"],"social":["Community learning disability team and memory service working jointly, with one named lead","Physiotherapy for falls and strength, OT home assessment for rails, lighting and a falls alarm","Supported living staff given a care plan covering bad days, sleep behaviour and when to seek help","Carer's assessment for Ruth; advance care planning and best-interests decisions under the Mental Capacity Act"],"safety":["Sudden worsening, a fall with head injury or new fever: same-day GP or 111, or 999 if very unwell"]}},{"sid":"S3.7","cid":"c-t-33","area":"Neurodevelopmental","title":"Learning disability and autism: behaviour change","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Callum Reid is a 26-year-old man with mild intellectual disability and autism who lives in a supported living flat with daily staff visits. Staff have referred him to the community intellectual disability team because his behaviour has changed over recent weeks and they are worried about his mental health. He has come to clinic with his support worker, who waits outside unless he asks for her. You are the psychiatry trainee in the team. Please take a history to understand what is happening now and what may be causing it. A management plan is not needed in the conversation itself.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Callum?","p":"Yes. Callum Reid."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Callum. I'd like to ask some questions about how things are for you.","p":"OK."},{"d":"If anything is confusing, tell me and I'll say it another way. We can stop for breaks too.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, simple words, breaks, offer support worker in room"},{"h":"What has changed","b":"Behaviour, routines, sleep, eating, withdrawal, aggression, onset, triggers"},{"h":"Psychotic symptoms","b":"Voices, being watched, interference with thoughts, odd beliefs, actions on them"},{"h":"Mood and anxiety","b":"Sadness, pleasure, worry, agitation, compared with his usual autistic baseline"},{"h":"Physical and pain","b":"Toothache, constipation, epilepsy, new medicines, hearing and vision"},{"h":"Life events and stress","b":"Staff changes, move, bereavement, bullying, exploitation, substances"},{"h":"Risk","b":"To self, to others, vulnerability, exploitation, self-neglect"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, check understanding, collateral from staff, next steps"}],"bps":{"pre":"Callum said a man on the bus had put a chip in his phone and now talked to him through the walls at night. He had stopped using his kitchen and was eating only crisps. His support worker later described him pacing and shouting at the wall. There was no clear physical cause and no change in staff. At the end he looked at the doctor and asked a direct question.","ask":"Can you make the man stop talking?","lead":"I believe the voice is really upsetting you, Callum, and there are things that can help it be quieter.","bio":["Bloods (FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, glucose, lipids, calcium) and urine drug screen to exclude physical and substance causes","If psychosis is confirmed, a low starting dose such as risperidone 0.5 mg at night, increased slowly in LD","Baseline weight, BMI, blood pressure, HbA1c, prolactin and ECG before an antipsychotic, repeated at 12 weeks","Check for constipation, dental pain and seizures, which commonly drive behaviour change in people with LD","STOMP principles: no antipsychotic just for behaviour; regular review of need and dose"],"psych":["Adapted CBT for psychosis from the LD psychologist, using pictures and shorter weekly sessions","Positive behaviour support plan written with staff, keeping his routines predictable","Easy-read explanation of what psychosis is and how medicine can help"],"social":["Community LD nurse as care coordinator, with joint working from the early intervention in psychosis team","Supported living staff to keep a sleep, food and behaviour diary for review","Safeguarding check if exploitation or cuckooing is suspected around his flat","Hospital passport updated and reasonable adjustments flagged for all appointments"],"safety":["Crisis plan shared with staff; if he is at risk to himself or others, contact the crisis team or 999"]}},{"sid":"S3.8","cid":"c-t-34","area":"Medication, emergencies and side effects","title":"Angry mother: clozapine and taking him home","who":"Mother","task":"Mr Jamal Hussain, aged 34, has treatment-resistant schizophrenia and has been on the ward for three months. He was started on clozapine and is improving, but slowly, and staff still need to prompt him to wash and change. Before admission he lived with his older brother, who drinks heavily, in a neglected flat, and it seems the brother was taking his money. His liver tests were abnormal on arrival but every blood result for the last three months has been normal. His mother, Mrs Shabnam Hussain, has asked to see a doctor. She has read online that clozapine can damage the bone marrow, is unhappy with his care and wants him discharged to her. Her home is 30 miles away with her husband, who also drinks heavily. Jamal has agreed his mother can be involved. Please meet her and address her concerns.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Mrs Hussain, Jamal's mother?","p":"I am, and I'm not happy."},{"d":"I can see you're upset, and I want to hear why. Jamal has said we can talk with you about his care.","p":""},{"d":"Please tell me what's worrying you most, and we'll go through each thing.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge anger, Jamal's consent, invite her concerns"},{"h":"Her concerns","b":"Internet reading, care on ward, wanting him home, listen fully first"},{"h":"Clozapine and blood","b":"Neutropenia risk, weekly FBC 18 weeks, then fortnightly, then monthly"},{"h":"Why clozapine","b":"Treatment-resistant illness, best evidence, slow gains, liver tests now normal"},{"h":"Care on the ward","b":"Personal care prompting, OT, named nurse, complaints route, PALS"},{"h":"Going home safely","b":"Brother's drinking, money taken, husband's drinking, distance, leave plan"},{"h":"Risk and safeguarding","b":"Relapse if stopped, financial abuse, vulnerability, carer strain"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, family meeting, consultant, carer support, leaflet"}],"bps":{"pre":"Mrs Hussain arrived holding printouts about clozapine and bone marrow. She said Jamal still looked unkempt, staff never phoned her and she could look after him better herself. She calmed a little once the blood monitoring was explained and she heard his recent results were fine. She admitted home was not easy either. Then she asked what the plan really was.","ask":"So what happens now? How long until he can leave?","lead":"I want Jamal home as much as you do, and the safest way is a clear plan we build together.","bio":["Continue clozapine; FBC weekly for the first 18 weeks, fortnightly to one year, then monthly, via the monitoring service","Clozapine plasma level with dose changes (aim at least 350 micrograms/L); smoking changes affect the level","Watch for fever or sore throat (report same day), constipation (laxative prescribed) and drooling","Repeat LFTs, weight, glucose, lipids and ECG as part of his physical health checks"],"psych":["Family intervention: around 10 sessions over 3-12 months with the family, to understand the illness and reduce stress","Psychoeducation for Mrs Hussain on clozapine, early warning signs and why stopping is dangerous","OT-led daily living skills on the ward: washing, laundry, cooking, with steady goals"],"social":["Care coordinator and ward social worker to plan discharge; supported accommodation may suit better than either home","Adult safeguarding referral about the brother taking his money; DWP appointee or bank changes","Graded leave, first day visits then overnight, with clozapine and bloods arranged for each","Carer's assessment for Mrs Hussain, carers' group and named nurse contact each week; PALS if she wishes to complain"],"safety":["If Jamal stops clozapine for more than 48 hours it must be restarted from a low dose by the team"]}},{"sid":"S3.9","cid":"c-t-35","area":"Anxiety, OCD, trauma and dissociation","title":"OCD: obsessions getting worse","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Priya Sharma is a 35-year-old woman whose GP has asked the psychiatry clinic to see her. She has had obsessional symptoms for many years, but they have become much worse in recent months and she is now struggling to keep up with work and home life. She is a pharmacy technician and lives with her partner and their young daughter. Please ask about her obsessional thoughts and compulsive behaviours and find out how they are affecting her day-to-day functioning.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Priya Sharma?","p":"Yes, hi."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Priya. Your GP said some worries have got much harder to manage lately.","p":"They have. It's taking over."},{"d":"I'd like to understand them properly. Some questions may feel personal, so take your time.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, normalise, permission for personal questions"},{"h":"Obsessions","b":"Content, intrusive, own mind, resisted, distress, contamination, harm, order"},{"h":"Compulsions","b":"Washing, checking, counting, reassurance, mental rituals, time per day"},{"h":"Course and triggers","b":"Onset, recent worsening, stress, pregnancy or postnatal, avoidance"},{"h":"Impact","b":"Work, partner, childcare, sleep, skin damage, social life, Y-BOCS severity"},{"h":"Comorbidity and past help","b":"Depression, tics, eating, alcohol, past CBT or SSRIs"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts, harm thoughts about daughter as ego-dystonic, self-neglect"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, diagnosis of OCD, plan, leaflet, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Priya described intrusive pictures of her daughter becoming ill from germs she brought home. She now showered for an hour after work, bleached door handles nightly and asked her partner many times a day for reassurance. She hated the thoughts and knew they were excessive. Her hands were cracked. She had no wish to harm anyone and no suicidal thoughts. She asked what could help.","ask":"Can this actually get better, or is it just who I am?","lead":"This is OCD, it is very treatable, and many people get their lives back with the right help.","bio":["Sertraline 50 mg each morning, increasing in 50 mg steps every few weeks up to 200 mg if needed","OCD usually needs higher doses and 12 weeks at a good dose before judging the effect","Warn about early nausea, jitteriness and sexual side effects; do not stop suddenly","Emollients for her hands; ask GP to check for infected cracked skin"],"psych":["CBT with exposure and response prevention, usually more than 10 therapist hours, weekly sessions","Graded exposure: touching feared items, then resisting washing, starting low on her own hierarchy","Partner joins a session to learn how to step back from giving reassurance"],"social":["NHS Talking Therapies or the local OCD service for the CBT; psychiatrist review in 4-6 weeks","Occupational health and adjustments at work if hygiene rituals are causing lateness","OCD-UK for information and peer support groups","Health visitor or children's services only if her daughter's needs are being missed"],"safety":["If mood drops or she feels hopeless, contact GP urgently or NHS 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"S3.10","cid":"c-u-41","area":"Women's and gender health","title":"Coercive control at home","who":"Patient","task":"Mrs Natalia Kowalski is a 37-year-old woman referred by her GP to the psychiatry outpatient clinic. The surgery has noticed she has missed several routine appointments in a row, including an asthma review, a contraception review and her cervical screening. Staff there know her husband to be angry and controlling, and they suspect he may be abusing her. She has come alone today. Please take a history of the abuse she is experiencing and explain to her what kind of abuse this is.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Natalia Kowalski?","p":"Yes. Natalia."},{"d":"Thank you for coming, Natalia. It's just us here, and what you say stays private unless someone is at serious risk.","p":""},{"d":"Your GP was worried you'd missed appointments. Could you tell me how things are at home?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, alone, confidentiality and its limits, gentle start"},{"h":"Missed appointments","b":"Why missed, who decides, phone and transport access, permission needed"},{"h":"Controlling behaviour","b":"Money, phone checks, isolation from friends, tracking, rules, threats"},{"h":"Other abuse","b":"Physical, sexual, threats to children or pets, strangulation, weapons"},{"h":"Effect on her","b":"Mood, anxiety, sleep, self-blame, alcohol, PTSD symptoms, asthma"},{"h":"Risk","b":"DASH questions, escalation, pregnancy, children at home, suicide risk"},{"h":"Naming the abuse","b":"Coercive control, emotional and economic abuse, a crime, not her fault"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, IDVA, helpline, safe contact, safety plan, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Natalia said her husband kept her bank card, checked her messages each night and cancelled her appointments because she 'didn't need them'. She was no longer allowed to see her sister. He had never hit her, but punched doors when angry. She felt low and stupid. When told this was coercive control, a recognised form of abuse and a crime, she cried, then asked what she could do.","ask":"I didn't think it counted because he doesn't hit me. What can I even do?","lead":"It absolutely counts, you've been very brave to talk, and you don't have to decide everything today.","bio":["No medication for the abuse itself; assess for depression, anxiety and PTSD, which often follow","If moderate depression is confirmed, sertraline 50 mg daily, reviewed in 2 weeks, with a small supply","GP to rebook her asthma review, contraception and cervical screening at times she can safely attend","Make sure her inhalers are in date and that she can get them without his permission"],"psych":["Specialist domestic abuse support worker or IDVA for regular one-to-one support and planning","Freedom Programme or similar 12-week group to understand controlling behaviour","Trauma-focused CBT later, once she is safe, if PTSD symptoms persist"],"social":["DASH risk checklist today; high risk leads to MARAC referral with her knowledge","National Domestic Abuse Helpline 0808 2000 247, free and open around the clock","Clare's Law request to police to ask about his history; legal advice on non-molestation orders","Safeguarding children referral if there are children in the home; refuge or housing advice if she leaves"],"safety":["Safe way to contact her agreed; a bag with documents and medicines kept somewhere safe","In danger: 999, and if unable to speak, press 55 when prompted (Silent Solution)"]}},{"sid":"S3.11","cid":"c-s-37","area":"Neurodevelopmental","title":"Is my toddler different? Development history","who":"Mother","task":"Mrs Hannah Clarke has been referred to the CAMHS outpatient clinic about her son Oliver, who is 3 years old. She told her health visitor she feels something is wrong with him and that he is very unlike his older brother at the same age. You are the core trainee in clinic and Oliver is in the playroom with a nursery nurse. Please take a history from Mrs Clarke to understand the nature of Oliver's difficulties, building a clear timeline of his symptoms with a particular focus on his development.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Hannah, Oliver's mum?","p":"Yes, hello."},{"d":"Thanks for coming. You know Oliver better than anyone, so your observations really matter.","p":""},{"d":"Could you tell me what first made you think something was different?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, praise her noticing, her main worry first"},{"h":"Pregnancy and birth","b":"Illness, alcohol, medicines, gestation, birth weight, special care, feeding"},{"h":"Early milestones","b":"Smiling, sitting, walking, babbling, first words, any loss of skills"},{"h":"Social communication","b":"Eye contact, pointing, showing, name response, pretend play, interest in children"},{"h":"Repetitive behaviours","b":"Lining up, routines, meltdowns at change, spinning, flapping, sensory issues"},{"h":"Health and family","b":"Hearing, vision, seizures, sleep, diet, family autism, ADHD, learning difficulties"},{"h":"Impact and safety","b":"Nursery, sibling, parental stress, wandering, safety awareness, safeguarding"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, possible autism, next assessments, support now, leaflet"}],"bps":{"pre":"Hannah described Oliver babbling at a year, then losing his few words around 18 months. He did not point or bring toys to show her, rarely turned to his name and lined cars up for hours, screaming if they were moved. He hated the noise of the hand dryer. Hearing had never been checked. She asked, voice shaking, what came next.","ask":"Is it autism? And what do we do now?","lead":"Some of what you describe does fit autism, and getting help early really does make a difference.","bio":["No medication is used for the core features of autism in young children","Formal hearing test through audiology before diagnosis, as hearing loss can look similar","Paediatric review for growth, head size and any genetic testing (chromosomal microarray, fragile X) if indicated","Melatonin only if sleep problems persist after a sleep routine, prescribed by the specialist"],"psych":["Multidisciplinary autism assessment including ADOS-2 observation, by the neurodevelopmental team","Parent-mediated social communication programme (NICE recommended), weekly sessions with video feedback","Speech and language therapy for communication, including picture-based tools","Parent course such as EarlyBird from the National Autistic Society"],"social":["Health visitor and nursery SENCO to share observations and start a support plan now, before diagnosis","Education, Health and Care needs assessment through the local authority if needs are significant","Disability Living Allowance for children and a carer's assessment for Hannah","Local parent support groups and National Autistic Society helpline"],"safety":["Safety at home: door locks and gates, as he may wander and has little sense of danger"]}},{"sid":"S4.1","cid":"c-u-42","area":"Forensic","title":"Young woman after a fire in her hostel","who":"Patient","task":"Miss Leah Brennan, aged 18, has been brought to the police custody suite after starting a fire in her room at a supported hostel. Nobody was hurt, but the building was evacuated. Officers are worried she may have a mental illness and have asked the liaison and diversion psychiatrist to see her. An appropriate adult is present. Please take a history focused on the fire-setting that helps you reach a diagnosis.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Leah Brennan?","p":"Yeah."},{"d":"Thanks, Leah. I'm not the police; I'm here to see how you are and whether you need help.","p":"Right."},{"d":"What happened in your room today? Tell me however you like.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, role not police, limits of confidentiality, appropriate adult present"},{"h":"The fire","b":"What she set alight, planned or impulsive, alone, alarm raised, stayed or fled"},{"h":"Motive","b":"Voices, beliefs, revenge, relief of tension, excitement, cry for help, suicidal"},{"h":"Fire history","b":"Previous fires, childhood fire play, interest in fire, arousal"},{"h":"Mental state","b":"Psychosis, mood, self-harm, dissociation, intellectual disability, substances"},{"h":"Background","b":"Childhood, care history, trauma, school, conduct problems, hostel life, forensic"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Repeat fire, suicide, others in hostel, vulnerability, exploitation"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, likely diagnosis, liaise with police and team, next steps"}],"bps":{"pre":"Leah said a voice had told her the room was poisoned and only fire would clean it. For weeks she had felt that the hostel staff were filming her and that the radio sent messages to her. She had not slept for days and had stopped smoking cannabis a month ago. She had no past fires. When it was suggested she might be unwell, she asked what would happen to her.","ask":"Am I going to prison, or what happens to me now?","lead":"You seem unwell rather than bad, Leah, and the first step is getting you somewhere safe to be treated.","bio":["Admission to hospital, informally if she agrees, or assessment under the Mental Health Act if she does not","Bloods, urine drug screen, and baseline weight, BP, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin and ECG before antipsychotic","If first-episode psychosis is confirmed, aripiprazole 10 mg once daily, chosen with her, reviewed over 4-6 weeks","Warn about restlessness (akathisia); short-term promethazine 25 mg for sleep if needed"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis, at least 16 sessions one-to-one with a psychologist","Family intervention, around 10 sessions, if she has family she wants involved","Forensic psychology assessment of fire-setting, looking at triggers and safer coping"],"social":["Liaison and diversion team to tell police and the CPS about her illness and advise on charges","Early intervention in psychosis team with a care coordinator for up to 3 years","Hostel manager and housing team for a safe placement; fire risk plan before any return","Adult safeguarding review given her age, vulnerability and possible exploitation"],"safety":["No lighters or matches on the ward; fire-setting risk written into her care plan"]}},{"sid":"S4.2","cid":"c-u-43","area":"Personality and therapies","title":"DBT: is it right for me?","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Chloe Mensah is a 24-year-old woman with long-standing emotional swings that can shift several times a day depending on what is going on around her. Stress overwhelms her, she has frequent bursts of anger, and at those times she has self-harmed. She describes low self-esteem and spends money impulsively to lift her mood. All of this is straining her relationship with her partner, and she wants help. She has read about dialectical behaviour therapy and would like to know more. Please speak to her, explain what DBT involves and respond to whatever she wants to ask.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Chloe Mensah?","p":"Yes. Chloe's fine."},{"d":"Thank you for coming, Chloe. I hear you've been reading about DBT. That's a great step.","p":""},{"d":"Can you tell me a bit about yourself first? Then we'll cover DBT and anything you want to ask.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, praise her reading, agree the plan"},{"h":"Her difficulties","b":"Mood swings, anger, current self-harm, spending, risk"},{"h":"Past help and options","b":"Tablets, why now, the evidence, DBT as focus"},{"h":"What DBT is","b":"Acceptance plus change, four skill areas, what she gains"},{"h":"Sessions and structure","b":"Individual therapy, skills group, diary card, phone coaching, about a year"},{"h":"Before you start","b":"Assessment, goals, commitment, crisis plan"},{"h":"Recommend and the group","b":"Clear recommendation, honest about the group, no guarantees"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, consultant, leaflet, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"Chloe described moods that flipped within an hour, shouting at her partner then cutting her thighs to calm down, most recently last week. She had run up credit card debt on clothes. She had tried two antidepressants without much change. She had no current plan to end her life. She leaned forward, clearly hopeful, and asked how DBT would actually work for someone like her.","ask":"So would DBT actually help me, and what would I have to do?","lead":"I think DBT is a really good fit for what you've described, so let me explain how it works.","bio":["No medication is first-line for emotional instability; NICE advises against drugs for the core difficulties","Review her current antidepressant: continue only if it treats a separate depression, otherwise taper with GP","Short-term promethazine 25 mg for sleep in a crisis, no more than a week, rather than benzodiazepines","Limit quantities of any tablets at home because of self-harm risk"],"psych":["DBT for about 12 months: weekly one-to-one therapy plus a weekly 2-hour skills group","Four skill sets: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness","Daily diary card tracking urges, self-harm and spending, reviewed at each session","Telephone coaching between sessions to use skills in the moment rather than self-harm"],"social":["Referral to the DBT service via her CMHT, with a care coordinator while she waits","Debt advice through StepChange or Citizens Advice for the credit card spending","Joint session with her partner, if she wishes, to explain the skills she is learning","Employer adjustments for weekly sessions, supported by a fit note if needed"],"safety":["Written crisis plan: distraction skills, partner, crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, A&E if serious injury"]}},{"sid":"S4.3","cid":"c-t-36","area":"Anxiety, OCD, trauma and dissociation","title":"OCD: explaining CBT with ERP","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Tomasz Nowak, a 30-year-old warehouse supervisor, was referred to clinic by his GP because he washes his hands excessively, and he was recently diagnosed with obsessive-compulsive disorder. The consultant has referred him to psychology for cognitive behavioural therapy. He has no idea what this involves and would like an overview before his first appointment. Please talk with him about CBT for OCD and respond to any worries he has.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Tomasz Nowak?","p":"Yes. Tom is fine."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Tom. I understand you've been referred for CBT and want to know what it involves.","p":"Yeah, no idea what it is."},{"d":"Could we start with how the OCD is affecting you now?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, his aim, agree plan"},{"h":"His OCD now","b":"Contamination fears, washing, time, skin, impact on work, mood, risk"},{"h":"How OCD works","b":"Thought, anxiety, wash, relief, cycle strengthens, plain diagram"},{"h":"What CBT with ERP is","b":"Facing fears gradually, not washing, anxiety falls naturally, habituation"},{"h":"How sessions run","b":"Therapist, weekly, about 12-20 sessions, hierarchy, homework, rating anxiety"},{"h":"His worries","b":"Too scary, getting ill, failure, time off work, privacy"},{"h":"Other options","b":"SSRI alongside, sertraline, combining both if severe"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, OCD-UK leaflet, review date, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"Tom said he washed his hands up to 60 times a day, used bleach on his skin and dreaded touching pallets at work. He knew it was excessive but the dread was unbearable until he washed. His hands were raw. He was not depressed and had no thoughts of self-harm. He frowned when facing fears was mentioned and asked how it could possibly help.","ask":"So you want me to touch dirty things and not wash? How does that help?","lead":"It sounds odd at first, but it's done step by step at your pace, and it's the most effective treatment we have.","bio":["Medication is optional alongside therapy: sertraline 50 mg daily, increased gradually up to 200 mg if needed","Give an SSRI 12 weeks at a good dose before judging; warn about early nausea and sexual side effects","If both are needed for severe OCD, combine SSRI and CBT rather than choose one","Emollient cream and gloves for cleaning tasks to let his skin heal"],"psych":["CBT with exposure and response prevention, weekly 60-minute sessions, typically 12-20, from a psychologist","Build a fear ladder together, starting with low-anxiety steps such as touching a door handle","Stay with the anxiety without washing until it settles; repeat daily as homework","Later sessions tackle the beliefs about germs and responsibility, plus a relapse prevention plan"],"social":["NHS Talking Therapies or the local psychology service; check waiting time and offer self-help meanwhile","Occupational health and employer adjustments for therapy time and any homework at work","OCD-UK and OCD Action for information and peer support","Partner or family invited to a session to avoid giving reassurance"],"safety":["If mood falls or he feels hopeless, contact GP or NHS 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"S4.4","cid":"c-t-37","area":"Anxiety, OCD, trauma and dissociation","title":"Work stress not helped by paroxetine","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Adebayo Coker is a 37-year-old man referred to the outpatient clinic by his GP because of stress that is causing problems at work, where he is a train driver. The GP started paroxetine 20 mg four weeks ago, but he says it has made no difference at all. The referral gives little other detail. Please take a history, decide on the diagnosis and screen for any other conditions alongside it.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Adebayo Coker?","p":"Yes. Most people call me Bayo."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Bayo. Your GP said work has been very stressful and the tablets haven't helped.","p":"Not at all."},{"d":"I'd like to understand what's been happening. Where would you like to start?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge frustration, open question"},{"h":"Stress and trigger","b":"What happened, when, trauma at work, threat to life"},{"h":"Re-experiencing","b":"Flashbacks, nightmares, intrusive images, distress at reminders"},{"h":"Avoidance and arousal","b":"Avoiding cab or route, numbing, startle, hypervigilance, sleep, irritability"},{"h":"Comorbidity","b":"Depression, alcohol, drugs, panic, guilt, psychosis, head injury"},{"h":"Treatment so far","b":"Paroxetine dose, adherence, side effects, any therapy"},{"h":"Risk and work","b":"Suicidal thoughts, driving safety, fitness to work, relationships"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, PTSD explained, plan, occupational health, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"Bayo described a man stepping in front of his train five months ago. Since then he had replayed the moment, woke from nightmares most nights and could not face going near the line. He jumped at loud noises and snapped at his children. He drank four cans most evenings to sleep and felt flat. He had passing thoughts that life was pointless but no plans. He asked what this was.","ask":"What's wrong with me, and why haven't the tablets worked?","lead":"What you describe is post-traumatic stress disorder, a normal reaction to an awful event, and therapy is the main treatment.","bio":["Talking therapy is first choice; if he prefers medicine, sertraline 50 mg rising to 100-200 mg, or venlafaxine (NICE)","If switching from paroxetine, cross-taper over 1-2 weeks with GP to avoid discontinuation symptoms","Alcohol: AUDIT score, LFTs and GGT; advise cutting down gradually; no daily drinking to sleep","Avoid benzodiazepines; short-term sleep hygiene rather than hypnotics"],"psych":["Trauma-focused CBT, 8-12 weekly sessions of about 90 minutes, from a trained therapist","EMDR as an alternative, 8-12 sessions, if he prefers","Psychoeducation for Bayo and his wife about PTSD symptoms and recovery"],"social":["Occupational health and his employer's trauma support scheme; not driving trains until assessed fit","Fit note from GP for time off; phased return plan when ready","NHS Talking Therapies or the local trauma service for the therapy","Union representative support and family support through his GP"],"safety":["Crisis plan; if suicidal thoughts grow, contact GP, NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans on 116 123"]}},{"sid":"S4.5","cid":"c-u-44","area":"Addictions","title":"Hearing the neighbour's voice: alcohol","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Gareth Pryce, aged 43, has come to A&E with his wife because he has been hearing a voice, which he recognises as his next-door neighbour's. The problem came to a head when he became very anxious and agitated while they were doing the weekly food shop at the supermarket. He is a delivery driver. The A&E team has asked liaison psychiatry to see him. Please assess the psychopathology and find out what is causing it.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Gareth Pryce?","p":"Yes."},{"d":"Thanks, Gareth. I hear something frightening happened at the supermarket today.","p":"I keep hearing him. My neighbour."},{"d":"That sounds really upsetting. Would you tell me more about it?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge fear, open question"},{"h":"The voice","b":"Whose, second or third person, content, clear consciousness, when started"},{"h":"Other symptoms","b":"Persecutory beliefs, visual hallucinations, mood, insight, orientation"},{"h":"Alcohol history","b":"Units, pattern, last drink, withdrawal, morning drinking, dependence features"},{"h":"Physical and other causes","b":"Withdrawal signs, delirium, head injury, drugs, epilepsy"},{"h":"Background","b":"Family history, past psychiatric history, work, driving, wife's view"},{"h":"Risk","b":"To neighbour, self-harm, driving, seizures, Wernicke's signs"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, likely alcoholic hallucinosis, plan, involve wife"}],"bps":{"pre":"Gareth said his neighbour had been calling him a drunk and a failure for a week, even when he knew the man was away. He was fully alert and oriented. He drank about a bottle of vodka daily for three years and woke shaking, with his last drink this morning. No visions or confusion. His wife looked relieved when told this could be linked to alcohol, and he asked what happens now.","ask":"So I'm not going mad? What can you do about the voice?","lead":"You're not going mad, Gareth; this is a recognised effect of heavy drinking, and it usually settles with the right treatment.","bio":["Admission for medically assisted withdrawal: chlordiazepoxide reducing regimen guided by CIWA-Ar scores","Pabrinex IV one pair of ampoules daily for 3-5 days, then oral thiamine 100 mg three times daily","Bloods: FBC (MCV), LFT, GGT, U&E, magnesium, glucose; ECG if antipsychotic used","If voices persist after withdrawal, short course of olanzapine 5 mg at night, reviewed weekly","After detox: acamprosate 666 mg three times daily or naltrexone 50 mg daily to prevent relapse"],"psych":["Motivational interviewing during admission to build his reasons to stop","CBT-based relapse prevention through the community alcohol service, weekly for about 12 weeks","Mutual aid such as AA or SMART Recovery; Al-Anon for his wife"],"social":["Community drug and alcohol service referral before discharge, with a named keyworker","DVLA: he must stop driving and notify them about alcohol dependence; affects his delivery job","Employer and occupational health support; fit note while he recovers","Wife offered carer support and information on withdrawal warning signs"],"safety":["Do not stop drinking suddenly at home: risk of seizures; call 999 for fits or confusion"]}},{"sid":"S4.6","cid":"c-t-38","area":"Old age","title":"Older man: low mood and weight loss","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Reginald Thompson is a 65-year-old man who retired as a bus driver last year. His GP has referred him to the community mental health service for older people because of recent weight loss and low mood. He lives with his wife. You are the core trainee in the team seeing him at a home visit. Please carry out a focused history and mental state assessment to reach a diagnosis.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Reginald Thompson?","p":"Reg, please."},{"d":"Thank you for having me, Reg. Your GP is worried you've lost weight and felt low.","p":"Can't say I've been right."},{"d":"Would you tell me how things have been for you lately?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, home visit, rapport, open question"},{"h":"Mood and depression","b":"Low mood, anhedonia, energy, sleep, appetite, weight, concentration, guilt"},{"h":"Physical causes","b":"Weight loss amount, pain, bowel change, cough, thyroid, alcohol, medicines"},{"h":"Cognition","b":"Memory, orientation, brief screen, depression versus dementia"},{"h":"Psychotic features","b":"Nihilistic, guilt or poverty delusions, hypochondriacal beliefs, voices"},{"h":"Background and stressors","b":"Retirement, bereavement, isolation, past episodes, family history"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts, plans, means, older male, not eating or drinking"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, depression, physical checks, plan, involve wife"}],"bps":{"pre":"Reg said he had felt useless since retiring, had lost about a stone in three months and woke at 4am every day with dread. He no longer went to his allotment. He moved slowly and spoke little. He denied bowel change or pain, and his memory was intact. He had thought he would be better off dead but had no plan. His wife asked what could help.","ask":"Is there anything that can get him back to himself?","lead":"This looks like depression, which is very treatable at any age, and we'll check his physical health too.","bio":["Bloods with GP: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, calcium, glucose, HbA1c, B12, folate, CRP; GP to look for physical causes of weight loss","Mirtazapine 15 mg at night, rising to 30 mg after 2 weeks if needed; helps sleep and appetite","Check sodium at baseline and after 2-4 weeks as antidepressants can lower it in older people","If psychotic features or refusal to eat develop, urgent review, possible admission and ECT"],"psych":["CBT or behavioural activation, around 16 weekly sessions via NHS Talking Therapies for older adults","Planning small daily activities with his wife, starting with short trips to the allotment","Problem-solving work around the loss of role after retirement"],"social":["CMHT for older people care coordinator, weekly contact while starting treatment","Age UK befriending, men's shed or volunteering to rebuild routine and contact","Carer's assessment for his wife and information on warning signs","Safety review at home: medicines stored safely, any firearms or stockpiles removed"],"safety":["Crisis plan; if he talks of ending his life, call the crisis team, NHS 111 option 2 or 999"]}},{"sid":"S4.7","cid":"c-u-45","area":"Personality and therapies","title":"Is interpersonal therapy right for her?","who":"Patient","task":"Mrs Fiona MacLeod is a 41-year-old woman with a depressive illness who attends the general adult outpatient clinic. She works as a part-time care assistant and is married with two teenage children. She would like to try a talking therapy rather than relying only on tablets. Please explore her depressive symptoms and assess whether she is suitable for interpersonal psychotherapy (IPT).","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Fiona MacLeod?","p":"Yes, that's right."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Fiona. I hear you'd like to try a talking treatment.","p":"I would, yes."},{"d":"Could we talk about how you've been feeling first, then see which therapy fits you best?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, her wish for therapy, agree plan"},{"h":"Depressive symptoms","b":"Mood, interest, sleep, appetite, energy, guilt, concentration, duration, severity"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts, plans, self-harm, protective factors"},{"h":"Grief","b":"Recent deaths, complicated or delayed grief"},{"h":"Role disputes","b":"Conflict with husband, children, family, work, unmet expectations"},{"h":"Role transitions","b":"New job, children growing up, caring, moves, health changes"},{"h":"Isolation and suitability","b":"Few relationships, motivation, weekly attendance, link mood to relationships"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, IPT explained, decision, referral, leaflet, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"Fiona described four months of low mood, poor sleep and losing interest in her choir. It began when her mother moved in after a stroke and Fiona became her carer, cutting her work hours. She felt resentful and guilty, and argued with her husband over sharing the load. She had no suicidal thoughts. She was clear she wanted to understand herself and asked about IPT.","ask":"So would this IPT suit me, and what does it involve?","lead":"It sounds like a good fit, because your mood seems closely tied to the big change in your role and relationships.","bio":["Bloods with GP if not done: FBC, TFT, B12, folate, to exclude physical causes","Sertraline 50 mg daily as an option alongside therapy for moderate depression; review at 2-4 weeks","Warn about early nausea and anxiety; continue for at least 6 months after recovery"],"psych":["Interpersonal psychotherapy, about 16 weekly one-hour sessions with a trained IPT therapist","Focus area agreed early: here, role transition into caring and the dispute with her husband","Early sessions map her relationships; middle sessions work on the focus; final sessions plan for the future","Mood tracked with PHQ-9 at each session"],"social":["NHS Talking Therapies referral for IPT, or CMHT psychology if more complex","Carer's assessment through the local authority; respite and home care for her mother","Local carers' centre and Carers UK for support and benefits advice such as Carer's Allowance","Employer flexible working request to protect her income"],"safety":["If mood worsens or she thinks of self-harm, contact GP or NHS 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"S5.1","cid":"c-t-39","area":"Addictions","title":"Opioid use: dependence history","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Liam O'Connor, a 32-year-old man, was sent by his GP to the drug and alcohol outpatient clinic because he has been using several different drugs over some years. He is out of work and lives in a rented flat with his girlfriend, who is pregnant. Please take a history of his drug use and establish whether he meets the features of a dependence syndrome.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Liam O'Connor?","p":"Yeah."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Liam. Your GP mentioned you've been using a few different drugs.","p":"Mainly gear, a bit of other stuff."},{"d":"I won't judge. I just want to understand, so we can work out what help fits. Shall we start?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, non-judgemental, confidentiality and limits"},{"h":"Drugs used","b":"Each drug, amount, cost, route, injecting, sharing, first use, last use"},{"h":"Dependence features","b":"Craving, control, tolerance, withdrawal, salience, persisting despite harm"},{"h":"Complications","b":"Overdoses, abscesses, BBV testing, mental health, debts, crime"},{"h":"Past treatment","b":"Detox, methadone or buprenorphine, naloxone, longest abstinence"},{"h":"Background","b":"Childhood, trauma, housing, partner pregnant, children, forensic history"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Overdose, mixing with benzodiazepines or alcohol, suicide, safeguarding unborn baby"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, dependence explained, options, naloxone, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Liam said he smoked and sometimes injected about a gram of heroin a day, plus street diazepam. He woke sweating and aching without it, had lost control over how much he used and spent all his benefits on it. He had overdosed once last year. His girlfriend was five months pregnant and he wanted to change before the baby came. He asked what was on offer.","ask":"If I wanted to get off it, what would you actually do?","lead":"It's great that you want to change, Liam, and there's solid treatment that can keep you safe and stable.","bio":["Urine drug screen to confirm opioids before any prescription; BBV tests and hepatitis B vaccination","Opioid substitution: methadone starting 10-30 mg daily, increased gradually to usual 60-120 mg, supervised daily","Or buprenorphine started once in withdrawal (COWS about 12 or more), titrated to around 12-16 mg daily","Naloxone kit given today and family shown how to use it; ECG if methadone is high or QT risks","Diazepam not prescribed long-term; plan a slow supervised reduction if dependent"],"psych":["Keyworker sessions using motivational interviewing, fortnightly to start","Contingency management or psychosocial groups at the drug service","Mutual aid such as Narcotics Anonymous or SMART Recovery"],"social":["Community drug and alcohol service for prescribing and a named keyworker","Needle exchange and safer injecting advice while he is still using","Midwife and safeguarding children referral for the unborn baby; specialist substance misuse midwife","Housing, benefits and debt advice; DVLA must be told about opioid dependence"],"safety":["Overdose risk is highest after a break or if mixed with diazepam or alcohol; never use alone","If someone is unresponsive: give naloxone, call 999"]}},{"sid":"S5.2","cid":"c-t-40","area":"Physical examination","title":"Parietal lobe check after stroke","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Harold Fenwick, aged 68, had a stroke three weeks ago that has left him with weakness of his left arm and leg. He is now on a rehabilitation ward. His family feel he has also changed in his thinking: he ignores people on one side and struggles to dress. The ward team has asked psychiatry to see him. He is right-handed. Please carry out a physical assessment of his parietal lobe function.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Harold Fenwick?","p":"Harry. Yes."},{"d":"Thank you, Harry. Your family wondered if the stroke has affected how you think and notice things.","p":""},{"d":"I'd like to do some short tests with paper and objects. Is that alright, and are you comfortable?","p":"Go on then."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent, handedness, pain, glasses, hearing, chaperone"},{"h":"Neglect and inattention","b":"Line bisection, cancellation task, sensory and visual inattention"},{"h":"Constructional and dressing","b":"Copy intersecting pentagons, draw clock, putting on a gown"},{"h":"Anosognosia","b":"Awareness of weakness, denial of left arm"},{"h":"Cortical sensation","b":"Astereognosis with coin, graphaesthesia, two-point discrimination, eyes closed"},{"h":"Dominant parietal signs","b":"Finger agnosia, left-right confusion, acalculia, agraphia"},{"h":"Visual fields and praxis","b":"Lower quadrant field loss, ideomotor apraxia with commands"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Thank him, findings, right parietal, report to team"}],"bps":null},{"sid":"S5.3","cid":"c-u-46","area":"Anxiety, OCD, trauma and dissociation","title":"Worried her eyes are too far apart","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Amira Haddad is a 27-year-old woman who works as a receptionist. She has been to her GP several times saying that her eyes are set too far apart and that this ruins how she looks. The GP found nothing abnormal and has referred her to the psychiatry clinic. Please take a history to understand what her difficulties are, how far they reach and assess her to clarify the diagnosis.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Amira Haddad?","p":"Yes."},{"d":"Thank you for coming, Amira. Your GP said you've been very worried about your eyes.","p":"Everyone can see it."},{"d":"That sounds really distressing. Could you tell me how it affects you day to day?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, take concern seriously, no arguing about appearance"},{"h":"The concern","b":"What she sees, since when, preoccupation hours, belief strength, insight"},{"h":"Behaviours","b":"Mirror checking, camouflage, make-up, comparing, reassurance, photos, avoidance"},{"h":"Impact","b":"Work, relationships, going out, social media, time lost"},{"h":"Cosmetic procedures","b":"Surgery sought, done, planned, DIY attempts, finances"},{"h":"Differentials and comorbidity","b":"Depression, OCD, eating disorder, social anxiety, delusional disorder"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts, self-harm, self-surgery, alcohol"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, body dysmorphic disorder, plan, leaflet, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"Amira spent four hours a day checking her eyes in mirrors and phone cameras, wore large glasses outside and had stopped dating. She had seen two cosmetic surgeons and was saving for surgery. She could accept she might see it differently from others. She often felt life was not worth living but had no plans. When body dysmorphic disorder was named, she asked about treatment.","ask":"Does that mean surgery won't help? What will, then?","lead":"Surgery rarely eases this kind of worry, but there are treatments that work well and I'd like to explain them.","bio":["Fluoxetine 20 mg each morning, increased over weeks up to 60 mg if needed; BDD often needs higher doses","Allow 12 weeks at a good dose before judging; warn about agitation early and sexual side effects","Continue for at least 12 months after improvement to reduce relapse"],"psych":["CBT including exposure and response prevention for BDD, weekly, usually more than 10 therapist hours","Exposure: going out without glasses; response prevention: cutting mirror checking and camouflage","Work on self-focused attention and comparing herself with others"],"social":["Local specialist OCD/BDD service or NHS Talking Therapies for the therapy","Advise against cosmetic surgery for now; GP to note BDD in referral letters","BDD Foundation for information and peer support","Employer adjustments if checking or avoidance affects work"],"safety":["BDD carries a high suicide risk: crisis plan, Samaritans 116 123, NHS 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"S5.4","cid":"c-t-41","area":"Anxiety, OCD, trauma and dissociation","title":"Memory worries: anxiety rather than dementia","who":"Patient","task":"Mrs Linda Okoro, a 58-year-old former secondary school teacher, was referred by her GP for an assessment of memory difficulties and anxiety. A CT head and blood tests, including a full screen for reversible causes of memory problems, were all normal. She scored 82/100 on the ACE-III a year ago and 88/100 today (sub-scores today: memory 22/26, attention 12/18, language 26/26, fluency 12/14, visuospatial 16/16). She still feels her memory is poor and fears dementia, as her mother developed Alzheimer's disease in her eighties. Please take a history, work out the diagnosis, explain it to her, and explain what her test results mean. A detailed treatment plan is not needed.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Linda Okoro?","p":"Yes. Is it dementia?"},{"d":"I can hear how worried you are, Linda. I'll go through your results with you today.","p":""},{"d":"First, what changes have you noticed in your memory, and how you've been feeling?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge fear, agree plan to explain results"},{"h":"Memory complaints","b":"Examples, forgetting mid-task, losing track, onset, progression, function intact"},{"h":"Anxiety symptoms","b":"Worry about many things, tension, restlessness, sleep, concentration, months"},{"h":"Mood and others","b":"Depression, panic, alcohol, medicines, menopause, thyroid symptoms"},{"h":"Family and background","b":"Mother's Alzheimer's later life, stressors, retirement, health anxiety"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts, self-neglect, alcohol use"},{"h":"Explaining results","b":"Normal CT and bloods, ACE up from 82 to 88, attention mainly"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Generalised anxiety disorder, not dementia, GP plan, questions"}],"bps":{"pre":"Linda said she walked into rooms and forgot why, lost the thread of books and checked the oven repeatedly. She had worried constantly for over a year about money, her health and her son, with tense shoulders and poor sleep. She managed all her own affairs. Her score had risen and dropped points were mostly on attention. Told this pointed to anxiety, she asked what helps.","ask":"So if it's anxiety, how do I get rid of it?","lead":"The good news is that anxiety is very treatable, and as it settles, your concentration should improve too.","bio":["Sertraline 50 mg daily as first-choice medicine (NICE), reviewed at 2-4 weeks; warn about early restlessness and nausea","Avoid benzodiazepines and sedatives, which worsen memory and risk dependence","No dementia medication is indicated; repeat cognitive testing only if new decline appears"],"psych":["NHS Talking Therapies: guided self-help first, then CBT for GAD, around 12-15 weekly sessions","Applied relaxation as an alternative, about 12-15 sessions","Explaining how worry uses up attention and makes memory seem worse"],"social":["GP to coordinate follow-up and review anxiety with the GAD-7 score","Structured activity such as volunteering or a course to rebuild confidence","Reliable information on genetics: late-onset Alzheimer's in one parent only slightly raises risk"],"safety":["If low mood or hopelessness develops, contact GP or NHS 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"S5.5","cid":"c-t-42","area":"Perinatal","title":"New mum, low mood and a frightening home","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Leah Brennan is a 23-year-old woman whose son, Alfie, is six weeks old. Her community midwife has referred her to the perinatal mental health team because of low mood and anxiety since the birth. She had one episode of depression at 19 and has never self-harmed or used drugs. She has told the midwife she does not want medication for now. Her partner Dean, the baby's father, has been drinking heavily and smoking a lot of cannabis; he has shouted at her and, on one occasion, shook Alfie briefly when he would not stop crying. The three of them share a small, overcrowded one-bedroom flat. Please see Ms Brennan at the clinic, take a history focused on the risks to her and to Alfie, respond to her worries, and agree with her a plan to manage those risks.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Leah Brennan?","p":"Yes, and this is Alfie."},{"d":"Hello Alfie. Thank you for coming, Leah. Your midwife asked me to see you because things have felt hard since he arrived.","p":""},{"d":"I'd like to hear how you're both doing, including how things are at home, and then we'll plan together. Is that okay?","p":"I suppose so."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, Alfie in the room, why the midwife referred, agree the plan"},{"h":"Her mood now","b":"Low mood, anxiety, sleep beyond feeds, bonding, intrusive thoughts about harm"},{"h":"Risk to Leah","b":"Suicidal thoughts, self-harm, past depression at 19, alcohol or drugs"},{"h":"Risk to Alfie","b":"Shaking incident: when, injuries, GP or A&E seen, who cares for him"},{"h":"Dean and home","b":"Drinking, cannabis, verbal aggression, any hitting, coercion, cramped flat"},{"h":"Her worries","b":"Fear social services take Alfie, Dean's reaction, feeling judged"},{"h":"Safety and safeguarding","b":"Explain duty to share, children's social care referral today, honest and supportive"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Plan agreed, review in a week, crisis numbers, teach-back"}],"bps":{"pre":"Leah held Alfie close throughout. She described two months of tearfulness, poor sleep and constant worry, with no thoughts of suicide. When asked about home, she went quiet, then said Dean had shaken Alfie a week ago after a night of drinking; Alfie seemed fine afterwards and was not checked by anyone. She said Dean has been shouting more and she feels trapped in the flat. She looked up, frightened.","ask":"What happens now? Are you going to take him off me?","lead":"You've done the right thing telling me, Leah, and my aim is to keep you and Alfie safe together, so let me explain what happens next.","bio":["Alfie needs a same-day medical check (paediatrics via A&E) because shaking a baby can cause hidden head injury","No medication is needed today as she prefers; if depression deepens, sertraline 50 mg once daily is a safe choice while breastfeeding","Screen and monitor with PHQ-9 and GAD-7 at each perinatal team visit","Ask her GP to check thyroid function and a full blood count, as postnatal fatigue can mimic depression"],"psych":["Guided self-help or CBT from NHS Talking Therapies (perinatal priority), about 6-8 weekly sessions","Weekly visits from the perinatal community nurse to monitor mood and bonding","Parent-infant work (for example video interaction guidance) to build confidence with Alfie","Domestic abuse support worker to talk through options and a personal safety plan"],"social":["Safeguarding referral to children's social care today, explained openly; the aim is support, not removal","Health visitor and midwife informed and part of the plan","Independent domestic violence adviser (IDVA); MARAC if risk is judged high","Housing team and local authority about overcrowding; refuge or family stay if needed","Dean to be offered referral to local drug and alcohol services, but Alfie's safety comes first"],"safety":["Alfie should not be left alone in Dean's care until social care have assessed","If Dean becomes violent or Alfie is hurt, call 999; National Domestic Abuse Helpline 0808 2000 247","If Leah's mood worsens or she has thoughts of harm, call the perinatal team or NHS 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"S5.6","cid":"c-s-69","area":"Anxiety, OCD, trauma and dissociation","title":"Unresponsive teenager with normal tests","who":"Mother","task":"Mrs Farah Qureshi is a 46-year-old woman whose daughter Amina, aged 18, collapsed in the street yesterday afternoon and was brought to hospital. Amina is now on the acute medical unit. She has had full medical tests, including bloods, a brain scan and an EEG, and no physical cause has been found. She lies with her eyes closed, does not respond when spoken to, and is refusing all food and fluids. The medical team have asked liaison psychiatry to help. Mrs Qureshi is at the bedside and is very anxious. Please take a brief history from Mrs Qureshi and explain to her the most likely diagnosis.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists working with the medical team. Are you Amina's mother, Mrs Qureshi?","p":"Yes. Is she going to be alright?"},{"d":"I can see how frightening this is. Amina has said before that we can talk with you, so I'd like to hear about her, then explain what we think is happening.","p":"Please."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, liaison role, acknowledge fear, Amina's consent noted, agree plan"},{"h":"Recent events","b":"Collapse yesterday, any warning, stresses, life events, losses, conflict"},{"h":"Trauma and stress","b":"Bullying, abuse, assault, relationship break-up, family changes, gently asked"},{"h":"Past and family history","b":"Previous similar episodes, anxiety, depression, self-harm, family mental illness"},{"h":"Substances and physical","b":"Drugs, alcohol, medicines, epilepsy, eating problems, recent illness"},{"h":"Explain dissociative stupor","b":"Real, not faked, mind switching off under stress, tests normal"},{"h":"What happens next","b":"Fluids and nutrition, physical safety, psychiatry daily, therapy later"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, questions, how to talk to Amina, contact details"}],"bps":{"pre":"Mrs Qureshi explained that Amina had been withdrawn for weeks after a painful break-up and a falling-out with friends, and had stopped sleeping well. There was no history of drug use or seizures. She mentioned a similar brief episode at 15 after a family bereavement. The medical consultant had already told her the scans and bloods were normal. She held Amina's hand and looked at you.","ask":"If nothing is wrong on the tests, why won't she wake up? What can you do?","lead":"Amina isn't pretending, and the normal tests are actually reassuring; let me explain what we think this is and how we help.","bio":["Keep her physically safe: fluids by drip if she will not drink for more than 24 hours, and nutrition support if it lasts longer","Daily checks of observations, pressure areas, fluid balance and urea and electrolytes while she is not eating","Avoid further invasive tests unless a new sign appears; repeated testing can reinforce the illness","A carefully supervised lorazepam challenge (for example 1-2 mg IV) can be considered to separate catatonia from dissociation","No routine medication for dissociation; treat any depression or anxiety once she can engage"],"psych":["Calm, consistent, reassuring explanation at the bedside; speak to her as though she can hear","Liaison psychiatrist reviews daily and gently encourages small steps such as sips of water","Once she responds: an assessment of stresses and trauma, then psychological therapy (CBT-based) over 12-16 sessions","Psychoeducation for the family using a clear, shared explanation of functional and dissociative symptoms"],"social":["Family visits kept calm and supportive; avoid crowding or arguments at the bedside","College or employer informed with consent about absence and a phased return","Referral to community mental health or Talking Therapies on discharge, with liaison follow-up","Carer support and information for Mrs Qureshi, including the neurosymptoms.org guide"],"safety":["If Amina later discloses abuse or self-harm thoughts, staff will follow safeguarding and safety planning"]}},{"sid":"S6.1","cid":"c-t-43","area":"Medication, emergencies and side effects","title":"High fever and stiffness after haloperidol","who":"Father","task":"Mr Kwame Asante is the father of Kofi Asante, an 18-year-old man admitted to an adult psychiatric ward six days ago with a first episode of psychosis. Haloperidol 5 mg three times daily was started three days ago. Kofi then became agitated and tried to leave, and was given haloperidol 10 mg intramuscularly twice under rapid tranquillisation. Admission bloods were normal. Nurses now find him unwell: temperature 41°C, pulse 104, blood pressure 140/90, respiratory rate 22, with rigid muscles. Repeat bloods show white cell count 14 x10^9/L, CK 1024 IU/L (normal 38-174), AST 44 and ALT 82; urine is normal. Kofi has agreed you can speak to his father. Please explain to Mr Asante what is causing this, set out the management plan, and address his concerns. A history is not needed.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors on the ward. Are you Mr Asante, Kofi's father?","p":"Yes. The nurse said he's unwell. What's going on?"},{"d":"Kofi has agreed I can talk to you. I'll be honest: he's become physically unwell and we're acting quickly.","p":""},{"d":"May I explain what we've found and what we're doing, then answer your questions?","p":"Go on."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, Kofi's consent, honest tone, he is being treated now"},{"h":"What we found","b":"Fever 41, fast pulse, stiffness, CK 1024, raised white cells, liver tests"},{"h":"Likely cause","b":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome: rare reaction to haloperidol, especially high doses and injections"},{"h":"Immediate treatment","b":"Stop haloperidol, transfer to medical team, fluids, cooling, monitor CK, kidneys"},{"h":"Possible complications","b":"Kidney injury, breathing problems, clots; most recover within 1-2 weeks"},{"h":"His concerns","b":"Why it happened, blame, psychosis treatment later, complaint route"},{"h":"Future medication","b":"Wait two weeks, low dose different antipsychotic, slow increase, record allergy"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, contact, consultant meeting, PALS, questions answered"}],"bps":{"pre":"You had explained to Mr Asante that his son has a high fever, a fast heart rate and stiff muscles, and that the blood tests show muscle breakdown. He listened with his arms folded, then said he had agreed to hospital so Kofi could get better, not so he could be poisoned by the injections.","ask":"So how are you going to fix this, and what happens to his mind now?","lead":"I understand why you're angry, and the most important thing is that we stop the cause and treat him urgently, so here is the plan.","bio":["Stop haloperidol and all antipsychotics immediately; no further rapid tranquillisation with antipsychotics","Urgent transfer to the acute medical team (high dependency if worsening); IV fluids to protect the kidneys","Active cooling, paracetamol, and monitoring of temperature, pulse, BP and urine output; CK, U&Es and LFTs at least daily","Lorazepam (for example 1-2 mg) for agitation and rigidity; dantrolene or bromocriptine if severe, decided by physicians","Thromboprophylaxis and checks for infection to rule out other causes of fever","After full recovery (at least 2 weeks), restart a different antipsychotic at a low dose with slow increases and close CK and temperature checks"],"psych":["Clear, regular updates for Kofi and family on progress","Psychology input to help Kofi understand psychosis and this reaction once he is well","Family intervention later as part of early intervention care, about 10 sessions over 3-12 months"],"social":["Early Intervention in Psychosis team to stay involved with a named care coordinator","Incident report and duty of candour conversation by the consultant","PALS contact details if the family wish to raise concerns formally","Carers' assessment and information for Mr Asante"],"safety":["Reaction recorded as an adverse drug reaction in notes and on a Yellow Card report","Family to tell staff at once about any new fever, confusion or stiffness"]}},{"sid":"S6.2","cid":"c-s-16","area":"Medication, emergencies and side effects","title":"Breast changes and missed periods on risperidone","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Priya Nair is a 34-year-old woman with schizophrenia diagnosed four years ago. A haloperidol depot caused stiffness, so a year ago she was switched to oral risperidone. Her voices are now much quieter and less frequent and she has no fixed odd beliefs. Early morning dizziness on risperidone has settled. She has noticed her breasts getting bigger and milk leaking from both nipples several times, which embarrasses her, and she worries it may be cancer. She has lost interest in sex, and her boyfriend left six months ago. She has had no periods for eight months; she had a hormonal coil fitted a year ago. Her prolactin is 770 mIU/L (normal up to about 530). Please see her in clinic, explain the result, address her concerns and discuss management. A mental state check is not needed.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Priya Nair?","p":"Yes, hi."},{"d":"Thanks for coming in, Priya. I've got your blood test back, and I know you've had some worrying changes.","p":""},{"d":"Could I check what you've noticed, then explain the result and what we can do?","p":"Okay."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, result is back, she has worries, agree plan"},{"h":"Her symptoms","b":"Breast swelling, milky discharge both sides, no lumps, low libido, no periods 8 months"},{"h":"Her worry","b":"Fear of breast cancer, embarrassment, relationship ending"},{"h":"Explain the result","b":"Prolactin 770, mildly raised, risperidone blocks dopamine, explains symptoms"},{"h":"Other causes checked","b":"Pregnancy test, thyroid, kidney function, coil effect on periods, pituitary if higher"},{"h":"Treatment options","b":"Switch to aripiprazole, or add low-dose aripiprazole, or lower dose"},{"h":"Long-term health","b":"Bone thinning if periods stop long term, DEXA if persistent"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, chosen option, relapse signs, recheck prolactin, leaflet"}],"bps":{"pre":"Priya told you the leaking has happened several times at work and she has started wearing pads. She had not felt any lumps but had read online that discharge can mean cancer. You explained her prolactin is raised and that risperidone commonly does this. She sounded relieved but quickly added that the tablets have finally quietened her voices.","ask":"I don't want to lose what's working. What can we actually do about this?","lead":"There are good ways to fix this without throwing away the progress you've made, so let me go through them.","bio":["Pregnancy test, TFTs, U&Es and LFTs to rule out other causes of raised prolactin","Preferred: switch to aripiprazole, starting 10 mg daily while tapering risperidone over 2-4 weeks, as it lowers prolactin","Alternative: keep risperidone and add aripiprazole 5 mg daily, which often brings prolactin down","Recheck prolactin 6-8 weeks after the change; if it stays above about 1000 or symptoms persist, refer for pituitary MRI","If periods do not return within about 6 months of switching, a DEXA scan for bone density","Warn about restlessness (akathisia) and nausea with aripiprazole; report early"],"psych":["Clear explanation that this is a known, reversible side effect, not cancer, which she can share with her GP","Relapse-prevention work with her care coordinator: early warning signs during the switch","Space to talk about the loss of her relationship and sexual confidence, with psychology if wanted"],"social":["Care coordinator in the CMHT to visit or phone weekly during the switch","GP informed with the plan; coil review with sexual health or GP for contraception advice","Employer: no need to disclose, but a note for a brief adjustment if needed"],"safety":["If voices return or worsen during the switch, contact the care coordinator or crisis team the same day"]}},{"sid":"S6.3","cid":"c-s-06","area":"Depression and suicide risk","title":"Adding lithium to an antidepressant","who":"Patient","task":"Mrs Gemma Hughes is a 34-year-old woman on an adult inpatient ward with recurrent depressive disorder. She has taken amitriptyline 150 mg a day for six weeks with only slight benefit. At today's ward round the team agreed to add lithium carbonate to boost the effect of her antidepressant. She is aware it was mentioned but does not yet know what it involves. Please speak to Mrs Hughes about starting lithium, explain why it is recommended and how it works in practice, go through the side effects, and answer her questions. A history is not needed.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the ward psychiatrists. Are you Gemma Hughes?","p":"Yes."},{"d":"Thanks for seeing me, Gemma. At ward round we talked about adding lithium to your amitriptyline.","p":""},{"d":"Can I check what you already know, then explain why, how it works day to day, and answer your questions?","p":"I've heard it's quite strong."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, ward round decision, her knowledge, agree plan"},{"h":"Why lithium","b":"Six weeks amitriptyline, partial help, lithium boosts response, good evidence"},{"h":"Before starting","b":"U&Es, eGFR, thyroid, calcium, weight, ECG, pregnancy test"},{"h":"Starting and levels","b":"200-400 mg at night, level 12 hours post dose, target 0.4-1.0"},{"h":"Side effects","b":"Thirst, passing urine, tremor, weight, thyroid and kidney long term"},{"h":"Toxicity and interactions","b":"Vomiting, diarrhoea, coarse tremor, confusion; NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, dehydration"},{"h":"Pregnancy and practical","b":"Contraception, plan pregnancy with team, same brand, purple book"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, teach-back, her decision, leaflet, review date"}],"bps":{"pre":"Gemma said she had read online that lithium is for bipolar disorder and that it damages the kidneys. You had reassured her that it is also used to strengthen antidepressants and confirmed she is not currently planning a pregnancy. She has felt flat and exhausted for months and is keen to get home to her two children.","ask":"If I agree, how does it actually work, and what do I need to do?","lead":"It's a careful, step-by-step treatment with regular checks, so let me walk you through exactly what it would look like.","bio":["Baseline bloods before starting: U&Es with eGFR, TFTs, calcium, FBC, weight and BMI; ECG as she has cardiac risk with amitriptyline","Lithium carbonate (Priadel) 400 mg at night to start, same brand always","Level 5-7 days after starting and after each dose change, taken 12 hours after the last dose; adjust to 0.4-1.0 mmol/L (often 0.6-0.8)","Once stable: level every 3 months for the first year, then 6-monthly; U&Es and TFTs every 6 months; calcium yearly","Common side effects: thirst, passing more urine, fine tremor, mild nausea, weight gain; long-term risk to thyroid and kidneys, hence checks","Toxicity signs: vomiting, diarrhoea, coarse tremor, unsteadiness, confusion; stop and get help. Avoid ibuprofen; tell any doctor she takes lithium"],"psych":["Continue psychological support on the ward, with CBT for depression offered after discharge (16-20 sessions)","Psychoeducation sessions with ward pharmacist using the NHS lithium pack and purple record book","Behavioural activation with the ward OT to build routine while the dose is adjusted"],"social":["Named nurse to help her keep a record of levels and doses","GP and community pharmacist informed; shared care for blood monitoring after discharge","Discuss contraception with GP; plan any pregnancy in advance with the perinatal team","Family visits and leave planned around her children; carers' information for her husband"],"safety":["Lithium is dangerous in overdose; on discharge, small supplies and family hold medication if risk returns","During any illness with vomiting or diarrhoea, stop lithium and contact GP or NHS 111"]}},{"sid":"S6.4","cid":"c-t-44","area":"Depression and suicide risk","title":"ECT: will it damage my memory?","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Desmond Clarke is a 65-year-old retired bus driver admitted to an older adults ward with a severe depressive episode. He took fluoxetine 40 mg daily for six weeks and then venlafaxine 150 mg daily for six weeks, and appears to have taken both reliably, but he has not got better. He has high blood pressure controlled on ramipril and mild kidney impairment. His consultant has suggested electroconvulsive therapy. He knows very little about it and is worried it will harm his memory. Please give Mr Clarke clear information about ECT to help him decide whether he wants it, and explore his beliefs, worries and expectations. You do not need to assess his capacity or take his consent today.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the ward psychiatrists. Are you Desmond Clarke?","p":"Yes. Des."},{"d":"Thank you, Des. Your consultant mentioned ECT, and I hear you're worried about your memory.","p":"I am. I've heard terrible things."},{"d":"That's a fair worry. Could I hear what you've heard first, then explain it properly?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consultant's suggestion, his memory worry, agree plan"},{"h":"His ideas and fears","b":"Films, shocks, brain damage, memory loss, being forced"},{"h":"Why ECT now","b":"Two antidepressants failed, severe depression, ECT works well in older adults"},{"h":"What happens","b":"Workup, fasting, anaesthetic, muscle relaxant, brief seizure, twice weekly, 6-12"},{"h":"Memory and side effects","b":"Headache, confusion, gaps around treatment, usually recover, rare lasting loss"},{"h":"Physical health","b":"Blood pressure, ramipril on the morning, kidneys and anaesthetic, ECG"},{"h":"Choice and alternatives","b":"His decision, can stop anytime, other options if he says no"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, leaflet, video, family talk, consultant to discuss consent"}],"bps":{"pre":"Des told you his only picture of ECT came from an old film where a patient screamed and was held down. He said he has done crosswords every morning for forty years and is terrified of losing that. He has stopped eating properly and feels he is a burden. You had acknowledged how hard the last three months have been.","ask":"If I did go for it, what would actually happen, and would I still be me afterwards?","lead":"Modern ECT is very different from that film, and you'd still be you, so let me explain what it really involves.","bio":["Workup: FBC, U&Es and eGFR (given his kidneys), ECG, chest check and anaesthetic assessment","Takes ramipril with a sip of water on treatment mornings unless the anaesthetist advises otherwise; BP checked before and after","Short general anaesthetic with a muscle relaxant; a brief controlled seizure; awake within minutes","Usually twice a week, typically 6-12 treatments, reviewed after each session with a depression rating and memory check (MoCA)","Common effects: headache, aching muscles, confusion for an hour or so; paracetamol helps","Memory: gaps for events around the course are common and mostly recover over weeks; lasting loss is uncommon; right unilateral placement reduces this"],"psych":["ECT nurse visit and the patient leaflet plus video before he decides","Talk with someone who has had ECT, through the ward or service user group, if he wishes","After ECT: continue an antidepressant to prevent relapse and CBT or behavioural activation as he recovers"],"social":["Family meeting with his wife and daughter (with his permission) to answer their questions","Escort to and from the ECT suite and a nurse with him when he wakes","Ward OT to support appetite and daily routine; dietitian if weight keeps dropping","If he chooses not to have ECT: options include lithium augmentation or adding mirtazapine, discussed with his consultant"],"safety":["No driving for at least 48 hours after each treatment (and during the course as advised)","Nurses monitor food and fluid intake and hopelessness daily while he decides"]}},{"sid":"S6.5","cid":"c-s-08","area":"Depression and suicide risk","title":"Is rTMS an option for me?","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Alan Petrovic is a 56-year-old man with recurrent depressive disorder seen in the community mental health clinic. Several antidepressants have helped only a little in the past, including mirtazapine, escitalopram, amitriptyline and sertraline. Community ECT worked well for him previously, but he disliked the effects of the general anaesthetic. He now takes trazodone 300 mg at night, yet his mood has slipped and every day is a struggle, though he has no suicidal thoughts. He has read about repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation (rTMS) and wants to know more. Please speak to Mr Petrovic, talk through his management options including rTMS, and answer his questions and concerns.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Alan Petrovic?","p":"That's me."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Alan. I understand things have slipped lately and you've been reading about rTMS.","p":"Yes, a friend told me about it."},{"d":"Let's hear how you're doing first, then go through rTMS and the other choices.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, his interest, agree plan"},{"h":"How he is now","b":"Mood, sleep, energy, function, hopelessness, confirm no suicidal thoughts"},{"h":"Treatment so far","b":"Four antidepressants, trazodone 300 mg, ECT helped, anaesthetic problems"},{"h":"What rTMS is","b":"Magnetic coil on scalp, stimulates mood area, awake, no anaesthetic"},{"h":"Course and effects","b":"Daily sessions, 20-40 minutes, 4-6 weeks, headache, scalp discomfort, rare seizure"},{"h":"Suitability and access","b":"Metal implants, epilepsy, NHS availability limited, may need private or trial"},{"h":"Other options","b":"Augmentation, switch, ECT with different anaesthetic, psychological therapy"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, his preference, consultant referral, leaflet, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"Alan told you he has been waking at 4 am, finds his allotment pointless and has stopped seeing his brother. He confirmed he has no thoughts of ending his life. He said ECT got him well last time but he felt sick and muddled for days after each anaesthetic and dreads repeating that.","ask":"So is this magnet treatment something I could have, and what else is there?","lead":"rTMS could well be an option for you, and it sits alongside a few others, so let me lay them all out.","bio":["rTMS: awake, seated, coil on the left forehead area; usually 5 sessions a week for 4-6 weeks (newer theta burst sessions take minutes)","Side effects: headache and scalp discomfort (paracetamol helps), rarely a seizure; not suitable with metal in the head, cochlear implants or uncontrolled epilepsy","Medication options: augment trazodone with lithium (levels 0.4-1.0) or aripiprazole 2-5 mg, or switch to venlafaxine or vortioxetine","If severe again, ECT with a different anaesthetic (for example propofol) and anti-sickness cover is worth considering","Baseline checks for any change: U&Es, TFTs, ECG if adding lithium or switching"],"psych":["CBT or behavioural activation through NHS Talking Therapies or psychology in the CMHT, 16-20 sessions","Mindfulness-based cognitive therapy later to reduce relapse risk","Activity scheduling with his care coordinator, starting with short allotment visits"],"social":["Referral to the regional neuromodulation service; NHS access varies, so discuss waiting times and funding honestly","Transport support for daily sessions (hospital transport or family)","Care coordinator in the CMHT to review every two weeks","Employer or occupational health letter for flexible hours during a daily course"],"safety":["If suicidal thoughts appear, contact the CMHT duty worker or NHS 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"S6.6","cid":"c-s-36","area":"Child and adolescent","title":"ADHD in my son: what now?","who":"Mother","task":"Mrs Rebecca Lewis is a 32-year-old part-time medical secretary attending CAMHS with her six-year-old son Oscar, referred by the GP. For a year Oscar has become steadily more overactive: he fidgets, cannot play quietly, interrupts, ignores instructions, loses things and is easily distracted. He is often awake until midnight bouncing on his bed. Teachers report him climbing on desks and disturbing others, unfinished work, and early literacy and number skills below his classmates; there is talk of exclusion. His birth and milestones were normal. He has a four-year-old sister, Maisie, and Mrs Lewis is eight weeks pregnant. The history has been taken and ADHD confirmed. Please explain the diagnosis and the treatments, including medicines, address her worries about her other children, and reassure her.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors in CAMHS. Are you Rebecca, Oscar's mum?","p":"Yes."},{"d":"Thanks for all the detail you gave earlier. I know the last year has been exhausting.","p":"It really has."},{"d":"Can I explain what we think is going on with Oscar, then go through treatment and your questions?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, thank her, acknowledge strain, agree plan"},{"h":"Explain ADHD","b":"Brain development difference, inattention, hyperactivity, impulsivity, not bad parenting"},{"h":"Her understanding","b":"Medical background, what she has read, worries about labels"},{"h":"First steps","b":"Parent training programme, school plan, sleep routine, learning assessment"},{"h":"Medication","b":"Methylphenidate if needed, how started, side effects, height, weight, BP"},{"h":"Siblings and pregnancy","b":"Runs in families, Maisie watch for signs, pregnancy no harm"},{"h":"Her coping","b":"Stress, support, carers' resources, school exclusion fears"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, leaflets, ADHD Foundation, review date, contact"}],"bps":{"pre":"You had confirmed with Rebecca that Oscar meets criteria for ADHD, combined type, at home and at school. She said the school sent him home twice last month and she has been crying in the car after pick-ups. She works in a GP surgery and has heard of Ritalin but worries it will turn him into a zombie. She also asked whether Maisie and the new baby will be the same.","ask":"So what do we actually do now, and does he have to take tablets?","lead":"Tablets aren't the first step at his age, and there's a lot we can do straight away, so let me take you through it.","bio":["At six, NICE advises an ADHD-focused parent training programme first; medication only if symptoms still cause significant problems after that","If needed: methylphenidate immediate release 5 mg once or twice daily, increased weekly to effect (often switched to a long-acting form)","Before starting: height, weight, BP and pulse on centile charts, cardiac history, ECG only if heart concerns","Monitor height and weight every 6 months, BP and pulse at every dose change; watch appetite loss, poor sleep, low mood","Melatonin can be considered for sleep onset problems if a firm bedtime routine fails"],"psych":["ADHD-focused group parent training (for example Incredible Years), around 10-16 weekly sessions","Behaviour strategies at home: short clear instructions, praise, routine, reward chart","Educational psychology assessment for learning difficulties given his letters and numbers","Sleep hygiene plan: fixed bedtime, no screens an hour before, calm wind-down"],"social":["School: SENCo meeting, individual support plan, seating near the teacher, movement breaks; EHCP if needs persist","Exclusion: CAMHS letter to school explaining ADHD and reasonable adjustments","Carers' assessment and local parent support groups; ADHD Foundation and Young Minds parents' helpline","Disability Living Allowance for children can be explored if care needs are high","Siblings: ADHD is partly inherited, so watch Maisie for signs; Rebecca's pregnancy is not affected by Oscar's diagnosis"],"safety":["Close supervision for safety given climbing and impulsivity; GP or 111 for any injury"]}},{"sid":"S7.1","cid":"c-s-13","area":"Neurodevelopmental","title":"Coach asks: can he stop his ADHD tablets?","who":"Coach","task":"Mr Gareth Pugh is a 45-year-old judo coach who trains Ryan Doyle, a 19-year-old man doing well in regional competitions. Ryan has had ADHD since age seven and has taken methylphenidate for years. The judo association says methylphenidate is a banned stimulant in competition, so Mr Pugh wants Ryan switched to something else. At 16 Ryan tried atomoxetine; his symptoms worsened and his liver tests became abnormal, so he went back on methylphenidate. Ryan still makes careless mistakes, struggles to concentrate and follow coaching, talks over teammates, is short-tempered, has got into fights, was stopped for dangerous driving on the motorway and uses cannabis at parties. Ryan saw the psychiatrist this morning and has given consent for you to speak with his coach. Please answer Mr Pugh's questions. A collateral history is not needed.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Gareth Pugh, Ryan's coach?","p":"Yes, thanks for seeing me."},{"d":"Ryan has agreed I can talk to you about his treatment, though some things will stay private to him.","p":"Understood."},{"d":"I know there's a competition worry. Can you tell me what the association said, and then I'll explain the options?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, Ryan's consent, limits of confidentiality, agree plan"},{"h":"His concern","b":"Anti-doping rule, career worry, wants a switch"},{"h":"ADHD and current picture","b":"Residual inattention, impulsivity, temper, fights, driving, cannabis"},{"h":"Why not just stop","b":"Symptoms worsen, accidents, driving risk, atomoxetine failed and liver"},{"h":"Therapeutic Use Exemption","b":"UK Anti-Doping TUE, specialist letter, keep methylphenidate legally"},{"h":"Other options","b":"Guanfacine non-stimulant, monitoring, psychological strategies"},{"h":"Risks to address","b":"Cannabis, driving and DVLA, anger, Ryan's decision"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, Ryan decides, letter, review, contact"}],"bps":{"pre":"Gareth explained that Ryan has a national trial in three months and the association warned him he could be banned. He said Ryan has been snapping at teammates and nearly came to blows with a sparring partner last week. He had assumed the only answer was to swap tablets, and was surprised to hear the earlier switch had gone badly.","ask":"So what can be done so he can compete without his ADHD getting worse?","lead":"There's a route that may let Ryan keep the medicine that works and still compete, so let me explain that first.","bio":["Methylphenidate is prohibited in competition but athletes with a confirmed diagnosis can apply to UK Anti-Doping for a Therapeutic Use Exemption; we provide the diagnostic evidence","Do not stop abruptly before competition without a plan; symptoms, temper and driving risk would worsen","Atomoxetine not to be retried given worsening symptoms and liver injury at 16","If a non-stimulant is still wanted: guanfacine prolonged release 1 mg daily, increased weekly by 1 mg (usually up to 4 mg); check BP and pulse at each change, warn about drowsiness and dizziness","Annual review of weight, BP and pulse while on stimulant"],"psych":["Psychoeducation for Ryan on ADHD in adulthood and how it affects sport and relationships","CBT for adult ADHD (organisation, attention and anger) in 8-12 sessions","Brief intervention about cannabis, which worsens attention and impulsivity, with motivational interviewing"],"social":["With Ryan's consent, coach strategies: short clear instructions, written plans, check understanding","UK Anti-Doping and the club welfare officer about the TUE process and timelines","DVLA: Ryan must tell DVLA if ADHD affects his driving; advice given to Ryan directly","Local drug and alcohol service if cannabis use continues"],"safety":["Ryan to avoid driving after cannabis; fights or threats to be managed by club disciplinary process"]}},{"sid":"S7.2","cid":"c-t-45","area":"Mania and bipolar","title":"Off lithium and feeling unstoppable","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Jamal Whitfield is a 28-year-old marketing assistant who had a manic episode with grandiose delusions two years ago and was detained in hospital under the Mental Health Act. Lithium was started and he recovered fully. He stopped lithium about two months ago. His family took him to the GP, who has referred him urgently to the community clinic. He feels intensely happy and limitless, has much more energy, sleeps about two hours a night, goes to the gym twice daily, works late and is planning a film at home. He has bought expensive camera gear and has been approaching strangers to star in his film. Family and colleagues are worried. He is not using alcohol or drugs. Please talk to Mr Whitfield about the likely diagnosis and draw up a management plan.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Jamal Whitfield?","p":"Yes! Good to meet you, doc. I'm brilliant."},{"d":"Good to meet you too, Jamal. Your GP and family have been worried, and I want to hear it from you.","p":"They worry too much."},{"d":"Let's talk about how things have been, then what might help. Okay?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, GP referral, family worry, his view first"},{"h":"Current symptoms","b":"Elation, two hours sleep, energy, film plans, spending, approaching strangers"},{"h":"Stopping lithium","b":"When, why, side effects, thought he was cured"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Money, debt, driving, vulnerability, sexual risk, aggression, work"},{"h":"Explain diagnosis","b":"Bipolar disorder, manic relapse after stopping lithium, insight gently"},{"h":"Treatment plan","b":"Restart medication, antipsychotic, home treatment or admission"},{"h":"His concerns","b":"Losing creativity, lithium side effects, work, hospital fear"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, family involvement, follow-up, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"Jamal spoke rapidly about his film, which he says will win awards, and showed you photos of a £3,000 camera on his phone. He admitted stopping lithium because he felt better and hated the thirst. He sleeps two hours and feels fine. He said he did not see the point of today but agreed his family were frightened. You explained gently that this looks like the illness returning.","ask":"Okay, say I believe you. What do you want me to do about it?","lead":"I want to help you stay in control of your life and your film, so here's what I'd suggest.","bio":["Start an antipsychotic now: olanzapine 10 mg at night (or quetiapine, aripiprazole or risperidone); warn about sedation and weight gain","Baseline bloods: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, TFTs, glucose or HbA1c, lipids, weight, BP; ECG if indicated","Discuss restarting lithium (for example Priadel 400 mg at night) once settled, levels 12 hours post dose aiming 0.6-0.8, if he agrees","Short-term promethazine or a benzodiazepine such as lorazepam 1 mg for sleep if needed, for a few days only","No antidepressants; avoid alcohol and caffeine"],"psych":["Psychoeducation about bipolar disorder and relapse signatures once calmer","Structured psychological therapy for bipolar disorder (for example group psychoeducation over 10-12 sessions) after the episode","Relapse prevention plan with his family about early warning signs such as reduced sleep"],"social":["Crisis Resolution and Home Treatment Team daily visits; admission if he declines treatment or risk rises (Mental Health Act assessment if needed)","Care coordinator in the CMHT","Sick note from work; avoid major decisions or purchases; return of the camera within the cooling-off period","With his consent, a family meeting; carers' assessment for his parents","DVLA: must stop driving during acute mania and inform DVLA"],"safety":["Crisis line or NHS 111 option 2; family to call 999 if he is in danger"]}},{"sid":"S7.3","cid":"c-u-47","area":"Forensic","title":"Ready for discharge, but he can't go home","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Bernard Kowalski is a 74-year-old man on an older adults psychiatric ward. He was arrested after assaulting his wife; police raised concerns about his memory and mental state, and he was bailed to hospital. He was treated with mirtazapine, then trazodone, for low mood and poor sleep, and after several weeks his mood is better. A CT head showed only age-related atrophy and other physical tests were normal. He no longer needs hospital care. His wife and son have now told the ward that he has drunk heavily for years and been repeatedly violent to family, recently to his wife. She will not have him home and has an injunction stopping him returning, which their children support. Please talk to Mr Kowalski, explore his care needs, plan his discharge and explain your reasons to him. Do not take a history or carry out a risk assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the ward psychiatrists. Are you Bernard Kowalski?","p":"Yes. When can I go home?"},{"d":"That's exactly what I'd like to talk about, Bernard. You've made real progress with your mood.","p":""},{"d":"There's some difficult news about home too. Can we talk about what you'll need and plan it together?","p":"What news?"}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, praise progress, signal difficult news, agree plan"},{"h":"Breaking the news","b":"Wife's decision, injunction, children agree, allow reaction"},{"h":"Daily living needs","b":"Washing, cooking, money, mobility, falls, memory aids"},{"h":"Accommodation options","b":"Temporary housing, supported living, residential care, his preference"},{"h":"Alcohol","b":"Drinking pattern, link to violence, community alcohol team, thiamine"},{"h":"Legal and contact","b":"Bail conditions, injunction rules, police case, solicitor"},{"h":"Mental health follow-up","b":"Mood monitoring, medication review, memory clinic if needed"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, social worker, discharge meeting, rationale explained"}],"bps":{"pre":"You had told Bernard his wife will not have him back and has an injunction. He stared at the floor, then said forty-eight years of marriage cannot end over one argument. He admitted he drinks most days and \"loses it\" sometimes. You noted he manages his own washing and dressing on the ward but has never cooked or handled the bills.","ask":"So where am I supposed to go, then? What happens to me?","lead":"You won't be discharged anywhere unsafe or unplanned; let me explain what we'll put in place and why.","bio":["Continue mirtazapine or trazodone (whichever he takes now) at the current dose; GP to review in 4 weeks","Oral thiamine 100 mg three times daily, and monitor LFTs, GGT and MCV","If drinking resumes heavily, alcohol team assessment; acamprosate or naltrexone can be considered once abstinent","Cognitive screening (ACE-III) once sober and mood stable; memory clinic referral if scores are low"],"psych":["Talking therapy or counselling for loss of his marriage and home","Alcohol-focused work (motivational interviewing, relapse prevention) with community alcohol services","Anger management or a domestic abuse perpetrator programme, discussed honestly and linked to any court process"],"social":["Care Act needs assessment by the ward social worker; occupational therapy kitchen and money skills assessment","Housing: local authority homeless application from hospital; temporary or sheltered accommodation, or residential care if needs are high","Respect the injunction: no contact with his wife except as allowed; liaise with police and his solicitor","Benefits check: pension credit, attendance allowance, housing benefit","Older adults CMHT care coordinator; MAPPA or MARAC information shared as appropriate"],"safety":["Crisis contact and Samaritans 116 123 if low mood or hopelessness returns after this loss"]}},{"sid":"S7.4","cid":"c-t-46","area":"Liaison","title":"Refusing a scope after vomiting blood","who":"Support worker","task":"Mr Liam Fairweather, aged 35, has a mild learning disability, lives in supported housing and takes risperidone, prescribed by his psychiatrist for some years. For six months he has had upper tummy pain. Today he had sudden severe pain, nausea and vomited blood twice. In A&E he was told he is probably bleeding inside his stomach and could die if it continues. He refuses bloods, a drip and an endoscopy: he believes police injected him while he slept, that hospital staff work for the police, that the blood test would make him bleed more and the camera would show police the damage. He understands and remembers what he is told but trusts no one. He talks well, sees his mother only at Christmas and rarely meets his social worker. You have assessed his capacity on the medical ward. Please explain your findings to his support worker, Ms Tessa Oduya, agree a plan and address her concerns.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the liaison psychiatrists. Are you Tessa, Liam's support worker?","p":"Yes. How is he?"},{"d":"He's still bleeding and still refusing tests. I've just assessed whether he can make this decision.","p":""},{"d":"You know him well, so I'd value your view. Can I share what I found and then plan with you?","p":"Of course."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, liaison role, urgency, value her knowledge, agree plan"},{"h":"Her view of Liam","b":"Usual self, recent change, beliefs about police, risperidone adherence"},{"h":"Capacity findings","b":"Understands, retains, communicates; cannot use or weigh due to fixed beliefs"},{"h":"What it means","b":"Lacks capacity for this decision now, not all decisions, time-specific"},{"h":"Best interests plan","b":"Least restrictive, gentle persuasion, Tessa present, urgent bloods, endoscopy"},{"h":"Safeguards and people","b":"Mother consulted, IMCA, social worker, Court rarely, restraint proportionate"},{"h":"Mental health","b":"Possible psychosis, review risperidone, liaison follow-up, MHA not for gut"},{"h":"Hand over and close","b":"Recap, her concerns, documentation, next review"}],"bps":{"pre":"You explained to Tessa that Liam can repeat back what the doctors said, but his belief that staff work for the police stops him weighing the risk of dying. She said he has been more suspicious for a few weeks and may have been missing tablets. She was upset at the thought of him being held down and asked whether the hospital could simply force him.","ask":"So what happens now? Will they just do it to him anyway?","lead":"Nobody wants to force Liam, so we'll start with the gentlest ways to help him agree, and here's how the plan works.","bio":["Best interests decision under the Mental Capacity Act: urgent bloods (FBC, U&Es, clotting, group and save), IV access and fluids","Endoscopy by the gastroenterology team as soon as possible, with sedation planned to reduce distress","IV proton pump inhibitor after endoscopy as directed by the medical team","Restraint only if necessary, proportionate and as brief as possible, documented; consider DoLS if kept on the ward against his wishes","Review risperidone adherence and dose; check prolactin, ECG and LFTs once stable; consider whether a new psychosis is present"],"psych":["Tessa and familiar staff present to explain each step in simple words and pictures (easy-read leaflets)","Learning disability liaison nurse involved today to support communication","Reassess capacity regularly and once bleeding is controlled; record each assessment","Later: trauma-informed debrief with Liam and his community learning disability psychiatrist"],"social":["Mother contacted by phone to share his wishes and values as part of best interests","IMCA referral if no appropriate family or friend can be consulted, though emergency treatment should not wait","Social worker and supported housing manager informed; safeguarding referral if missed medicines reflect neglect","Community learning disability team follow-up after discharge"],"safety":["If he tries to leave while bleeding, staff may act under the Mental Capacity Act, in his best interests, to keep him safe"]}},{"sid":"S7.5","cid":"c-t-47","area":"Neurodevelopmental","title":"A mother asks for her son's sterilisation","who":"Mother","task":"Mrs Patricia Okoye, a single mother in her fifties, has come to the learning disability clinic about her son Callum, aged 26, who has a mild intellectual disability. He was starved of oxygen at birth and born by emergency caesarean; his speech and walking were late and he went to a special school. He reads and writes little, but communicates well and needs light support. He shares a supported flat with his partner Jade, who also has a mild intellectual disability and is six months pregnant. Mrs Okoye raised him alone while working late shifts in a pub and feels exhausted and short of money. Callum was previously in trouble with police for exposing himself when stressed about money, and this has happened again amid fears the baby will be removed. She wants him sterilised and fears the baby will be disabled and become her burden. Please explore her concerns, reassure her and discuss how to manage the situation.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Mrs Okoye, Callum's mum?","p":"Yes. Patricia."},{"d":"Thank you for coming, Patricia. It sounds like you've carried a lot for many years.","p":"You've no idea."},{"d":"I'd like to hear your worries, then talk about what can realistically be done. Is that okay?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge her years of caring, agree plan"},{"h":"Her concerns","b":"Sterilisation request, baby's future, her burden, money, exhaustion"},{"h":"Callum's situation","b":"Exposure incidents, stress about baby, police involvement, current support"},{"h":"Her wellbeing","b":"Mood, sleep, isolation, finances, any thoughts of harm"},{"h":"Sterilisation explained","b":"His decision if capacity, Court of Protection otherwise, not done for others"},{"h":"Baby and parenting","b":"Not inherited automatically, pre-birth assessment, parenting support, not her duty"},{"h":"Managing the offending","b":"Forensic risk review, stress triggers, police liaison, safeguarding"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, referrals, carers' assessment, follow-up, contact"}],"bps":{"pre":"Patricia told you she has not had a day off in years and cried when describing the police visit last week. She believes the baby will \"be like Callum\" and that social services will hand it to her. You had listened and checked that she has no thoughts of harming herself. She leant forward, firm and tired.","ask":"So will you arrange the operation, and how am I meant to cope with all of this?","lead":"I can't arrange that operation, but I can help with almost everything else that's worrying you, so let me explain.","bio":["Sterilisation is Callum's own decision if he has capacity; if he lacks it, only the Court of Protection could approve it, and not for others' benefit","Contraception is a choice for Callum and his girlfriend with their GP or sexual health clinic, using accessible information","Review whether Callum's stress, anxiety or mood need treatment; SSRIs are sometimes used for sexual impulsivity, only after specialist assessment","Patricia's GP to check her own mood and sleep; treat if she is depressed"],"psych":["Forensic risk assessment of the exposure incidents by the community learning disability team, with psychology work on stress and sexual behaviour","Accessible sex and relationships education for Callum","Parenting assessment and support adapted for parents with learning disability (for example a specialist parenting programme)","Supportive counselling or Talking Therapies for Patricia"],"social":["Pre-birth assessment by children's social care: support to parent is the aim; the grandmother is not automatically responsible","Carers' assessment for Patricia under the Care Act; respite and support groups (Mencap, Carers UK)","Benefits check for Patricia and Callum; budgeting support to reduce his money stress","Police liaison and an appropriate adult for Callum if interviewed; safeguarding of the public considered","Midwife and health visitor informed so extra support starts early"],"safety":["If Patricia feels unable to cope or has thoughts of harm, contact her GP or NHS 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"S7.6","cid":"c-t-48","area":"Forensic","title":"Voices after violence on the prison wing","who":"Ward nurse","task":"Mr Shane Mulvey is a 32-year-old man three months into a prison sentence for assault. He had behaved well until a few weeks ago, when he saw one prisoner attack another and seemed shaken. Since then he has described voices from outside his head warning that he will be harmed and must defend himself, which frighten him. He also feels someone else may be controlling him and has had thoughts and urges to hurt others, though he has not acted on them. He was moved to the prison healthcare unit two days ago. Staff suspect illicit drug use, possibly GHB; he used various drugs before custody. You are the forensic psychiatry doctor covering healthcare. Nurse Kiran Bhatt has asked to speak with you. Please get a brief handover on how Mr Mulvey has been since moving, then discuss your initial plan, including whether he can go back to the wing.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the forensic psychiatry doctors. Are you Kiran, looking after Mr Mulvey?","p":"Yes, thanks for coming."},{"d":"Could you give me a quick handover: how he's been since arriving, observations, any incidents, and drug test results?","p":"Sure."},{"d":"Then I'll share my thinking and we'll agree the plan, including the wing question.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, role, SBAR handover requested, agree plan"},{"h":"Handover from nurse","b":"Sleep, eating, voices, mood, incidents, observations, drug screen, withdrawal signs"},{"h":"Risk to others","b":"Urges to harm, named targets, weapons, triggers, staff safety"},{"h":"Risk to self","b":"Self-harm, suicidal thoughts, vulnerability, bullying, debt"},{"h":"Differential","b":"Psychosis, drug-induced, GHB withdrawal, PTSD after violence"},{"h":"Initial management","b":"Stay on healthcare, observations, bloods, medication, ACCT if needed"},{"h":"Wing and transfer","b":"Not back yet, transfer to hospital if psychotic, Mental Health Act"},{"h":"Hand over and close","b":"Recap, review tomorrow, escalate, document, communicate"}],"bps":{"pre":"Kiran reported that Shane slept poorly the first night and was sweating and shaky, but this settled by day two. He still hears a voice warning him of attack, keeps his back to the wall and asked twice whether officers are reading his thoughts. There have been no assaults. A urine screen was negative, though GHB is often missed. He eats little and is polite but guarded.","ask":"So what's your plan for him, and can he go back to the wing?","lead":"He's not ready for the wing yet; I think we need to keep him here and clarify whether this is psychosis, so here's the plan.","bio":["Physical checks: observations including NEWS2, bloods (FBC, U&Es, LFTs, CK, glucose, TFTs), ECG; repeat drug screen","Watch for late GHB withdrawal (tremor, agitation, confusion); diazepam under medical advice if it recurs","If psychotic symptoms persist after a few days drug-free: start an antipsychotic such as aripiprazole 10 mg daily (or olanzapine 5-10 mg) with baseline monitoring","Short-term promethazine 25 mg for distress or sleep rather than repeated benzodiazepines"],"psych":["Daily review by the in-reach team; full mental state and trauma screen as he settles","Supportive work around the violence he witnessed; trauma-focused therapy later if PTSD develops","Substance misuse work with the prison drug and alcohol team"],"social":["Remain on the healthcare unit; not back to the wing until reassessed","If psychosis is confirmed and needs hospital treatment: refer for transfer under section 47/49 of the Mental Health Act (target 28 days)","Inform wing and security staff of risk to others on a need-to-know basis; safer cell and two officers to unlock if risk is high","Open an ACCT if any self-harm risk; involve his offender manager"],"safety":["Escalate immediately if he becomes acutely aggressive, confused or physically unwell; on-call doctor and emergency response"]}},{"sid":"S8.1","cid":"c-s-53","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Voices back, smoking cannabis to cope","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Tyrone Bailey is a 25-year-old warehouse worker with paranoid schizophrenia. After his last relapse he recovered on olanzapine, but he stopped taking it six weeks ago. The voices have since returned and he has been smoking cannabis to cope with them. His GP has referred him urgently to the community mental health clinic. Please take a brief history from Mr Bailey and agree a short-term management plan with him.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Tyrone Bailey?","p":"Yeah."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Tyrone. Your GP said things have been difficult lately and the voices are back.","p":"They are."},{"d":"Could we talk about what's been happening, then work out a plan together?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, GP referral, agree plan"},{"h":"Stopping olanzapine","b":"When, why, weight, sleepiness, felt well, belief about need"},{"h":"Current symptoms","b":"Voices content, commands, paranoia, sleep, function, work"},{"h":"Cannabis use","b":"Amount, strength, cost, how it affects voices, dependence"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Self-harm, suicide, harm to others, commands, neglect, vulnerability"},{"h":"Explain the link","b":"Relapse after stopping, cannabis worsens psychosis, both together"},{"h":"Short-term plan","b":"Restart antipsychotic, home treatment, cut down cannabis, review"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, his views, crisis line, follow-up date"}],"bps":{"pre":"Tyrone said he stopped olanzapine because he put on two stone and felt sluggish at work. Within a month he heard a voice calling him worthless and felt neighbours were watching him. He now smokes skunk most nights to calm down, about £20 a day. He has no plans to harm himself or others and no commands. He looked tired.","ask":"So what now? I'm not going back on those tablets that made me fat.","lead":"That's a fair point, and there are options that don't affect weight as much, so let's look at them together.","bio":["Restart an antipsychotic with less weight gain: aripiprazole 10 mg once daily, increasing to 15 mg after two weeks if needed","Baseline and follow-up: weight, waist, BP, HbA1c, lipids, FBC, U&Es, LFTs, prolactin; repeat at 12 weeks","Warn about restlessness (akathisia) and nausea; report early","If adherence remains difficult, discuss a long-acting injection such as aripiprazole monthly"],"psych":["Motivational interviewing about cannabis: link with paranoia and voices, set a reduction goal","CBT for psychosis (at least 16 sessions) to cope with voices once more settled","Relapse-prevention plan with early warning signs; family intervention if he agrees"],"social":["Care coordinator in the CMHT or early intervention team to see him weekly","Home Treatment Team visits for 1-2 weeks if symptoms worsen","Local drug and alcohol service referral; dual diagnosis worker","Employer: fit note or reasonable adjustments if he needs time off; DVLA advice about driving during relapse","Healthy lifestyle support: dietitian or gym referral for weight concerns"],"safety":["If voices tell him to harm himself or others, call the crisis line or NHS 111 option 2; 999 in an emergency"]}},{"sid":"S8.2","cid":"c-t-49","area":"Mania and bipolar","title":"Bipolar relapse two months off lithium","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Rhys Llewellyn is a 28-year-old graphic designer who was detained under the Mental Health Act two years ago during a manic episode with grandiose beliefs. He made a full recovery on lithium, but stopped it around two months ago. His parents brought him to his GP, who referred him urgently to the mental health clinic. He says he feels on top of the world and capable of anything, has boundless energy and sleeps around two hours a night. He trains at the gym twice a day, stays late at work and is writing a music video at home. He has spent heavily on recording and camera equipment and has been asking people he barely knows to perform in it. Family and colleagues are concerned. He denies alcohol or drugs. Please speak to Mr Llewellyn about the likely diagnosis and agree a management plan.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Rhys Llewellyn?","p":"That's right. I haven't got long, I'm busy."},{"d":"Thanks for making time, Rhys. Your GP and parents are worried, and I'd like your side of it.","p":""},{"d":"We'll be as quick as we can. Then we'll agree what happens next.","p":"Fine."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, respect his time, family concern, his view"},{"h":"Current picture","b":"Mood, energy, two hours sleep, music video, spending, strangers, irritability"},{"h":"Lithium story","b":"Why stopped, side effects, felt well, family reaction"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Debts, driving, sexual risk, exploitation, conflict, work standing, psychotic beliefs"},{"h":"Diagnosis explained","b":"Bipolar disorder, manic relapse, link to stopping lithium"},{"h":"Treatment options","b":"Antipsychotic now, restart lithium, home treatment versus admission"},{"h":"His concerns","b":"Losing energy, creativity, weight, job, being detained again"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, agreed plan, family, crisis contacts, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Rhys talked quickly about his music video and said a famous band will \"definitely\" want it. He has spent about £4,000 on credit and became irritable when you asked about money. He said he stopped lithium because it dulled him and made him shaky. He agreed he has slept little for weeks. You had explained this looks like a manic relapse.","ask":"Alright, if you're right, what would you actually want me to take or do?","lead":"My aim is to settle this quickly so you don't lose your job or your savings, so let me run through the plan.","bio":["Antipsychotic now: quetiapine starting 100 mg at night and increasing over a few days towards 400-800 mg, or aripiprazole 15 mg if he prefers less sedation","Baseline bloods: FBC, U&Es, eGFR, LFTs, TFTs, calcium, HbA1c, lipids, weight, BP; ECG","Restart lithium once agreed: 400 mg at night, level 12 hours post dose after 5-7 days, target 0.6-0.8; discuss tremor and how to reduce it","Short course of lorazepam 1 mg or promethazine 25 mg at night for sleep if needed","Stop any antidepressant; avoid alcohol and recreational drugs"],"psych":["Psychoeducation on bipolar disorder and the risk of stopping lithium suddenly","Structured individual or group psychological therapy for bipolar disorder once stable","Written relapse signature and plan shared with family, focusing on sleep loss as the first sign"],"social":["Home Treatment Team daily visits, or informal admission; Mental Health Act assessment if he refuses and risk is high","Care coordinator and CMHT follow-up","Fit note from work; a trusted relative to look after cards; debt advice through StepChange or Citizens Advice","Family meeting and carers' assessment for his parents","DVLA: stop driving while unwell and notify DVLA"],"safety":["NHS 111 option 2 or the crisis team out of hours; family to call 999 if he is at risk"]}},{"sid":"S8.3","cid":"c-u-48","area":"Depression and suicide risk","title":"Depression not lifting after two drugs","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Jeremy Pearce is a 47-year-old man referred to the community mental health clinic. He has had three previous episodes of depression, each of which resolved on fluoxetine 20 mg. About six months ago he relapsed with low mood, low energy, loss of enjoyment, poor concentration and poor appetite. His GP restarted fluoxetine 20 mg, raised it to 40 mg after six weeks without benefit, then after another six weeks with no response switched him to venlafaxine 150 mg. His mood has improved a little, but his other symptoms remain. The diagnosis has been confirmed. Please speak to Mr Pearce, take his circumstances into account, and explain the treatment options available to him.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Jeremy Pearce?","p":"Yes. Jerry."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Jerry. Your GP has explained the medicines you've tried.","p":""},{"d":"Can I check how you are and what matters to you, then go through the options?","p":"Please."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, GP letter, agree plan"},{"h":"Where he is now","b":"Mood a bit better, energy, enjoyment, sleep, appetite, concentration"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts, plans, hopelessness, alcohol, protective factors"},{"h":"His circumstances","b":"Work, family, side effects, adherence, preferences, past therapy"},{"h":"Medication options","b":"Increase venlafaxine, add mirtazapine, aripiprazole or lithium, switch"},{"h":"Therapy options","b":"CBT or behavioural activation, combined treatment best"},{"h":"If still stuck","b":"ECT if severe, rTMS, specialist affective disorders service"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, his choice, review in 2-4 weeks, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"Jerry said his mood is a notch better on venlafaxine but he still wakes exhausted, has lost interest in cycling and cannot focus at work as a surveyor. He has no suicidal thoughts. He takes the tablets daily and has had mild sweating but nothing else. He has never had talking therapy and wants to stay working.","ask":"What else is there? I've done everything the GP asked.","lead":"You've done all the right things, and there are several good next steps, so let me lay them out.","bio":["Increase venlafaxine to 225 mg daily (up to 375 mg if needed); check BP before and after each increase; warn about sweating and nausea","Or add mirtazapine 15 mg at night, increasing to 30 mg, helpful for sleep and appetite; warn about weight gain","Or augment with aripiprazole 2-5 mg daily, or lithium with levels 0.4-1.0 and kidney, thyroid and calcium checks","Avoid sudden stopping of venlafaxine because of discontinuation symptoms","Review response after 4-6 weeks on any change"],"psych":["CBT from NHS Talking Therapies or the CMHT, 16-20 sessions, combined with medication","Behavioural activation to rebuild routine, starting with short rides","Mindfulness-based cognitive therapy after recovery to reduce relapse, given three prior episodes"],"social":["Occupational health and employer adjustments: reduced hours or flexible start for a few weeks","Fit note from GP if needed","Involve his partner (with consent) in the plan","Specialist mood disorders service or ECT and rTMS referral if two further steps fail"],"safety":["Crisis line or NHS 111 option 2 if thoughts of suicide develop"]}},{"sid":"S8.4","cid":"c-s-44","area":"Forensic","title":"He believes I'm cheating and carries a knife","who":"Partner","task":"Ms Sunita Kaur is a 39-year-old woman whose partner of a year, Mr Craig Holloway, aged 44, is in A&E. He is convinced she is having an affair, probably with a neighbour. He has searched her phones, the bed and her handbag but found nothing, blames her for their infrequent sex life, and carries a knife in case he finds proof about the neighbour. Today she found him punching the bed, very agitated, and persuaded him to come to hospital. Four years ago he believed a former partner was unfaithful and stabbed her several times in their kitchen; she escaped. He was detained under the Mental Health Act and treated with risperidone 2 mg. He has now been assessed by the psychiatry team. Please explain his diagnosis and the plan to Ms Kaur and address her concerns.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in A&E. Are you Sunita Kaur?","p":"Yes. Is Craig alright?"},{"d":"He's here and safe. There's information I need to share with you because it affects your safety. Is now okay?","p":"Yes, go on."},{"d":"First, are you safe right now, and is anyone with you?","p":"My sister's on her way."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, his safety, explain need to share for her safety"},{"h":"Her understanding","b":"What she has seen, fears, the knife, what she knows of his past"},{"h":"Explain the diagnosis","b":"Delusional jealousy, fixed false belief, illness not her fault"},{"h":"Risk to her","b":"Previous stabbing, knife now, searching, agitation, risk is serious"},{"h":"Plan for Craig","b":"Admission today, Mental Health Act if refuses, antipsychotic, knife removed"},{"h":"Her safety plan","b":"Police, IDVA, MARAC, not going home alone, neighbour warned"},{"h":"Her concerns","b":"Guilt, relationship future, his anger, confidentiality"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, contacts, follow-up call, support for her"}],"bps":{"pre":"You had explained that Craig is unwell with a condition that makes him certain she is unfaithful, despite no evidence. Sunita said she had no idea about the stabbing four years ago and began to shake. She said he had been checking her phone at night and she had seen the knife in his jacket. She asked whether he would be allowed home tonight.","ask":"What are you going to do with him, and what am I meant to do now?","lead":"Your safety and his treatment both matter, and he won't be sent home tonight, so let me explain both parts of the plan.","bio":["Admission to a psychiatric ward today; Mental Health Act assessment if he will not stay voluntarily","Knife removed in A&E by security or police; search of belongings on the ward","Restart an antipsychotic: risperidone 2 mg daily, increasing to 4 mg if needed, or another with fewer side effects","Bloods (FBC, U&Es, LFTs, TFTs, glucose, prolactin), ECG, drug screen; check alcohol use"],"psych":["Once settled, CBT for psychosis focused on the jealous beliefs","Forensic risk assessment (HCR-20) before any leave or discharge","Couple work only if and when it is safe and Sunita wants it"],"social":["Police informed given the knife and threat; MARAC referral and independent domestic violence adviser","Duty to warn the neighbour, coordinated with police","Safety plan for Sunita: stay with her sister, change locks if needed, keep phone charged","Care coordinator and forensic CMHT on discharge; any release from hospital discussed with Sunita in advance"],"safety":["If he contacts or approaches her and she feels unsafe, call 999; National Domestic Abuse Helpline 0808 2000 247"]}},{"sid":"S8.5","cid":"c-t-50","area":"Forensic","title":"Doubting antidepressants: a doctor's depression plan","who":"Patient","task":"Dr Farhan Qureshi is a 40-year-old man who works as a consultant microbiologist at a nearby teaching hospital. He has a moderately severe depressive episode that began as his marriage broke down and the divorce went through. He takes mirtazapine but has noticed problems with ejaculation since starting it. Earlier tablets did little for him, and he has told the nursing staff that a paper he read claims the serotonin explanation of depression has been disproved, so he doubts any further medicine would help. He has been signed off sick for three weeks and is keen to get back to work soon. Please speak to him, respond to his worries about medication and agree a management plan with him.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Dr Farhan Qureshi?","p":"Yes. Farhan, please. No need for the title here."},{"d":"Thanks, Farhan. I understand you've been reading about the serotonin debate and have real doubts about tablets.","p":"I just don't see the point any more."},{"d":"That's fair to raise. Could we look at how you are now, then go through the evidence and options together?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, first-name terms, acknowledge his reading, agree the plan"},{"h":"His depression now","b":"Mood, sleep, appetite, divorce stress, drinking, functioning off work"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts, plans, access to hospital drugs, protective factors"},{"h":"Medicines so far","b":"Which tried, doses, durations, adherence, mirtazapine ejaculatory problem"},{"h":"The serotonin question","b":"Theory versus trial evidence; tablets help regardless of mechanism"},{"h":"Options and switch","b":"Vortioxetine or agomelatine, augmentation later, CBT alongside"},{"h":"Return to work","b":"Occupational health, phased return, Practitioner Health, fitness to practise honestly"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Shared plan, teach-back, follow-up in two weeks, crisis numbers"}],"bps":{"pre":"Farhan sat stiffly and admitted he was sleeping five hours, eating little and replaying the divorce hearing each night. He had passing thoughts that his children would cope without him but no plan, and he had not taken anything from work. He listed three failed antidepressants, then described the mirtazapine side effect with obvious embarrassment. He folded his arms and challenged the whole idea of chemical imbalance.","ask":"If serotonin isn't even the cause, what's the point of any of this?","lead":"You're right that the simple chemical-imbalance story is outdated, but the trials showing antidepressants help still stand, so let me show you what I'd suggest.","bio":["Switch mirtazapine to vortioxetine 10 mg each morning, rising to 20 mg after 2-4 weeks if tolerated; lower rate of sexual side effects; warn about nausea in week one","Alternative: agomelatine 25 mg at night (up to 50 mg) with LFTs at baseline, 3, 6, 12 and 24 weeks; little sexual dysfunction","Baseline bloods before switching: FBC, U&E, LFTs, TFTs, B12, folate, HbA1c; review alcohol intake","If no response after 4-6 weeks at a full dose, consider lithium augmentation with levels 0.6-1.0 mmol/L 12 hours post dose, plus eGFR, TFTs and calcium","Short-term sleep help only if needed: zopiclone 3.75-7.5 mg for no more than 2 weeks, given in small supplies"],"psych":["High-intensity CBT for depression, 16-20 weekly sessions with a psychologist, combined with medication as NICE advises for this severity","Behavioural activation while off work: a weekly timetable of exercise, contact with his children and small achievable tasks","Short focused sessions on the divorce: grief, guilt and rebuilding routines, possibly through counselling for separated parents"],"social":["Practitioner Health, the confidential NHS service for doctors, for ongoing treatment outside his own hospital","Occupational health referral for a phased return with reduced on-call and clear review dates","BMA wellbeing support line and peer support; legal and financial advice through the divorce","GP to keep the fit note going until psychiatry and occupational health agree he is ready"],"safety":["Crisis plan with numbers: NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, and 999 in an emergency","Agree he will not take medicines from work stock; keep home supplies limited and check alcohol use"]}},{"sid":"S8.6","cid":"c-t-51","area":"Old age","title":"Lewy body dementia: talking with the grandson","who":"Grandson","task":"Mr Gerald Okafor is an 82-year-old widower whose memory has worsened over the past 18 months. Within the last nine months he has developed parkinsonian stiffness and slowness and has been falling often. He reports vivid visual hallucinations: he sees his late wife and his own mother and chats with them as though they are present, and he also sees a cat, a fox and children's faces around the house. A DaT scan supports dementia with Lewy bodies. Rivastigmine was started and he now takes 3 mg twice a day. Lately he has become more unsettled at home, and relatives are growing anxious. His grandson, Ellis Okafor, has come to the memory clinic. Please speak with Ellis about his grandfather's behaviour and the further management options, exploring what he thinks is happening, his main fears, and his hopes for Gerald's care.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Ellis, Gerald's grandson?","p":"Yes, that's me."},{"d":"Thanks for coming in. Gerald is happy for me to discuss his care with you, so we can speak openly.","p":"Good, because we're worried sick."},{"d":"I can hear that. Could you tell me what's been happening at home, and then we'll talk about what can help?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent from Gerald, confidentiality, agree the plan"},{"h":"What Ellis has seen","b":"Hallucinations, night-time behaviour, confusion swings, falls, distress, aggression"},{"h":"Rule out new causes","b":"Infection, constipation, pain, new drugs, delirium screen, Parkinson's medicines"},{"h":"Explaining Lewy body dementia","b":"Fluctuations, visual hallucinations, parkinsonism, DaT scan result, what it means"},{"h":"Medication options","b":"Rivastigmine titration, ECG, avoid antipsychotics, memantine, quetiapine caution"},{"h":"Managing at home","b":"Do not argue, lighting, falls, routine, OT and physio"},{"h":"Risk and carers","b":"Falls, wandering, driving, carers' assessment, LPA, family strain"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, teach-back, leaflet, memory nurse, review date, who to call"}],"bps":{"pre":"Ellis described his grandfather sitting up past midnight talking warmly to his late grandmother and putting out saucers of milk for a cat nobody else can see. Gerald had fallen twice this month and one night shouted at his daughter, thinking she was an intruder. There were no signs of a new infection on recent bloods. Ellis wiped his eyes and leaned forward.","ask":"Is there anything that can actually calm these visions down without making him worse?","lead":"Yes, there is quite a lot we can do, though the safest options look a little different from other types of dementia.","bio":["Increase rivastigmine from 3 mg to 4.5 mg twice daily after a check, then to 6 mg twice daily after at least 2 weeks if tolerated; this often reduces hallucinations","Check pulse and ECG before each increase because of bradycardia and fainting risk; watch for nausea, weight loss and worsening tremor","Avoid haloperidol and other typical antipsychotics: severe sensitivity reactions can be fatal in Lewy body dementia","If hallucinations stay very distressing, specialist-led quetiapine 12.5 mg at night with slow increase and close review; add memantine (5 mg rising weekly to 20 mg) if rivastigmine is not tolerated","Review all medicines with the GP and neurologist, especially anticholinergics and any levodopa, which can worsen visions; check urine, bloods and bowels if he suddenly worsens"],"psych":["Psychoeducation for the family: the visions are part of the illness, so gently reassure or distract rather than argue","Cognitive stimulation therapy group, 14 sessions twice weekly, if his fluctuations allow","Environmental changes to reduce misperceptions: good lighting, fewer shadows and reflections, covered mirrors if they upset him","Simple daily routine with regular daytime activity to improve night-time sleep"],"social":["Occupational therapy and community physiotherapy home assessment for falls, rails and a pendant alarm","Carers' assessment from adult social care for the family, plus a review of the care package and respite","Memory clinic nurse or Admiral nurse as a single point of contact; Lewy Body Society and Alzheimer's Society information","Lasting power of attorney for health and finances while Gerald can still take part; check he is no longer driving and DVLA is informed"],"safety":["If he becomes suddenly more confused, drowsy or unwell, call the GP the same day or NHS 111 as it may be delirium","After a fall with head injury, or if he cannot get up, call 999"]}},{"sid":"S9.1","cid":"c-s-11","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Clozapine and metabolic syndrome: explaining blood results","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Tobias Mensah is a 44-year-old man with long-standing schizophrenia who has been well on clozapine for five years. He comes once a year to the rehabilitation team's physical health clinic and has just had his annual checks. Results: fasting glucose 6.1 mmol/L, blood pressure 146/90 mmHg, HDL cholesterol low at 30 mg/dL, fasting triglycerides 2.7 mmol/L and waist 108 cm. Mentally he is doing very well, with no hallucinations or unusual beliefs. Please explain these results to him, check his risk factors and talk through the plan from here. A full history and a mental state or physical assessment are not needed.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Tobias Mensah?","p":"That's right. Toby."},{"d":"Thanks, Toby. Your blood tests from the physical health clinic are back, and I'd like to go through them with you.","p":"Is something wrong?"},{"d":"Some numbers need attention, nothing urgent today. Could I first check what you know about them?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, purpose, check his understanding and worries"},{"h":"Results one by one","b":"Glucose 6.1, BP 146/90, low HDL, triglycerides 2.7, waist 108"},{"h":"What they add up to","b":"Metabolic syndrome: raised heart, stroke and diabetes risk, reversible"},{"h":"His risk factors","b":"Smoking, diet, activity, alcohol, family history, sleep, takeaways"},{"h":"Clozapine's role","b":"Clozapine contributes, but stopping risks relapse; keep it, adjust around it"},{"h":"Tests and medicines","b":"HbA1c, lipids, QRISK3, ECG, metformin, statin, repeat BP"},{"h":"Lifestyle plan","b":"Dietitian, activity, smoking cessation with clozapine level check"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, teach-back, GP letter, leaflet, review in three months"}],"bps":{"pre":"Toby listened carefully as each result was explained against the normal range. He admitted he smoked twenty a day, drank fizzy drinks constantly and rarely walked further than the shop. His father had diabetes. The term metabolic syndrome was explained and he looked worried, glancing at his clozapine card.","ask":"Does this mean I have to come off the clozapine?","lead":"No, the clozapine is keeping you well and we want to protect that, so the plan is to work on the heart and sugar risks around it.","bio":["Do not stop clozapine; check a clozapine plasma level and use the lowest effective dose","HbA1c, full lipid profile, LFTs and U&E now; ECG; calculate QRISK3 with the GP","Metformin 500 mg once daily with food, increasing to 500 mg twice daily after 1-2 weeks, then up to 1 g twice daily; check eGFR and B12 yearly; warn about stomach upset","If QRISK3 is 10% or more, offer atorvastatin 20 mg at night with LFTs at baseline and 3 months","Home or ambulatory BP readings; if hypertension is confirmed the GP starts treatment such as ramipril per NICE","If he stops smoking, clozapine levels can rise sharply: recheck the level within a week and reduce the dose if needed"],"psych":["Motivational interviewing over a few sessions with his care coordinator to choose one or two achievable changes","Structured healthy-living group in the rehabilitation service, typically weekly for 8-12 weeks, covering food, activity and sleep","Smoking cessation support with weekly behavioural sessions and varenicline or nicotine replacement"],"social":["Referral to a dietitian and to the NHS Diabetes Prevention Programme if HbA1c is 42-47 mmol/mol","Exercise on referral through the local leisure centre, possibly with a support worker for the first sessions","GP letter so the practice adds him to its serious mental illness physical health register and shares monitoring","Care coordinator to look at cooking skills, budgeting for food and access to a local walking group"],"safety":["Seek urgent help (GP same day or NHS 111) for excessive thirst, passing a lot of urine or weight loss; 999 for chest pain"]}},{"sid":"S9.2","cid":"c-s-66","area":"Anxiety, OCD, trauma and dissociation","title":"Intrusive fears of being seen as a paedophile","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Owen Pritchard is a 32-year-old man, a delivery driver, whose GP has referred him to the community mental health outpatient clinic. He told the GP he is frightened that other people might think he is a paedophile. He has also started rereading every text and message many times before sending it, to be sure he has not written anything inappropriate. The worries began after a long relationship ended and have grown steadily stronger over recent months. Please take a history from him, explore the nature of these thoughts and reach a diagnosis.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Owen Pritchard?","p":"Yes."},{"d":"Thanks, Owen. Your GP mentioned some very upsetting thoughts. Many people have thoughts like this, and you can speak freely here.","p":"It's hard to even say it."},{"d":"Take your time. Could you tell me how these thoughts first started?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, normalise, reassure about non-judgement, agree the plan"},{"h":"The thoughts themselves","b":"Content, intrusive, unwanted, own mind, resisted, distress, frequency"},{"h":"Course over time","b":"Onset after break-up, growing doubt, any certainty now"},{"h":"Checking and avoidance","b":"Rereading messages, reassurance seeking, avoiding children, time lost daily"},{"h":"Rule out other causes","b":"Delusion, overvalued idea, depression, psychosis, substance use, actual urges"},{"h":"Background","b":"Past OCD signs, family history, personality, stressors, impact on work"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts, self-neglect; harm to others; reassure obsessions not acted"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"OCD diagnosis explained, teach-back, CBT with ERP, leaflet"}],"bps":{"pre":"Owen described the first thought arriving a week after his girlfriend left: a flash of 'what if people think I'm a predator?' He found it disgusting and pushed it away, but now it comes dozens of times a day and he spends two hours checking messages. He has no sexual interest in children, no psychotic symptoms and is not depressed. The diagnosis of OCD was explained.","ask":"So is there a treatment that will make these thoughts stop?","lead":"Yes, OCD responds well to treatment, and the thoughts themselves say nothing about the kind of person you are.","bio":["Sertraline 50 mg once daily, increasing in 50 mg steps every few weeks up to 200 mg if needed; OCD often needs the higher end","Allow 12 weeks at a good dose before judging; warn about early nausea, extra anxiety in the first fortnight and sexual side effects","If two SSRIs fail with CBT, consider clomipramine 25 mg at night titrated slowly, with an ECG first","No routine bloods needed for sertraline; ask about alcohol and any other drugs"],"psych":["CBT including exposure and response prevention: around 10 therapist hours for mild OCD, more intensive (often 12-20 sessions) for severe symptoms","Delivered through NHS Talking Therapies or the CMHT psychologist, weekly","Exposure tasks such as sending a message once without rereading, then sitting with the discomfort until it settles","Psychoeducation that thoughts are not actions, and reducing reassurance seeking from family and friends"],"social":["GP to stay involved and review medication; fit note only if needed","OCD-UK and OCD Action information and peer support groups","Speak with his employer about short-term adjustments if checking slows his work","No safeguarding referral is needed: ego-dystonic intrusive thoughts carry no added risk to children"],"safety":["Crisis plan if mood drops or suicidal thoughts appear: GP, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123"]}},{"sid":"S9.3","cid":"c-u-49","area":"Forensic","title":"Love for a nurse: assessing risk","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Lewis Hartley is a 27-year-old unemployed man who lives alone. He has arrived at the hospital's main reception insisting on seeing a staff nurse he says he is in love with. He met her three months ago when she helped him in A&E during what he thought was a heart attack. When the receptionist would not give out her details, he raised his voice and became threatening, so the receptionist has called the duty psychiatry doctor. Please take a history to explore what he believes about the nurse and to judge how dangerous he may be.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Could I take your name?","p":"Lewis. Lewis Hartley. Where is she?"},{"d":"Thank you, Lewis. I can see you're upset about not getting to see someone. Shall we sit somewhere quieter?","p":"Fine. But I'm not leaving."},{"d":"That's okay. Could you tell me about this nurse and how you met?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, calm, private safe room, staff aware, exits clear"},{"h":"How it began","b":"A&E visit, her kindness, the smile, meaning he gave it"},{"h":"The belief now","b":"She loves him, evidence, certainty, signals, alternatives considered"},{"h":"Behaviour so far","b":"Contacts, letters, following, social media, her address, previous attempts"},{"h":"Response to rejection","b":"Anger, threats to receptionist, forcing attention, weapons, sexual thoughts"},{"h":"Mental state screen","b":"Mood, other delusions, hallucinations, substances, past history, forensic history"},{"h":"Dangerousness","b":"Intent, plans, weapons access, past violence, impulsivity, protective factors"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Tell him the plan, involve security, inform nurse, consultant, police"}],"bps":{"pre":"Lewis spoke for twenty minutes about the nurse's smile, convinced it was a private sign she loved him. He admitted he had waited outside the unit twice. He denied wanting to hurt her, but became hostile when told he could not see her, saying people would have to notice him and he would 'do whatever it takes', hinting at a knife.","ask":"So what happens now? Are you going to let me see her or not?","lead":"I can't arrange for you to see her, Lewis, but I do want to help with how all-consuming these feelings have become.","bio":["No routine medicine is needed today unless he is acutely agitated; if so, offer oral lorazepam 1 mg (maximum 4 mg in 24 hours)","If a fixed false belief of being loved is confirmed (erotomanic delusional disorder), offer an antipsychotic such as aripiprazole 5 mg daily, rising to 10-15 mg","Before any antipsychotic: weight, BP, glucose, lipids, prolactin and ECG; urine drug screen today"],"psych":["Full assessment by the community forensic team or CMHT within days, with collateral from GP and A&E records","Later, longer-term psychological work on loneliness, attachment and anger with a psychologist, once engaged","Clear, consistent boundaries: one named clinician, no contact with the nurse, firm explanation of why"],"social":["Inform the nurse and her line manager today (duty to warn), and offer her support and a safety plan","Hospital security and the receptionist informed; incident form completed; trust lone-working measures for the nurse","Police informed now because of veiled threats and possible weapon; consider stalking protection order advice","If a mental disorder with serious risk is present and he refuses help, arrange a Mental Health Act assessment with an AMHP"],"safety":["Do not see him alone, remove access to any weapon, and call 999 if he produces one or tries to find her"]}},{"sid":"S9.4","cid":"c-u-50","area":"Addictions","title":"Lorazepam dependence: planning a safe reduction","who":"Patient","task":"Mrs Natalie Osei, aged 38, has just moved to the area and joined a new GP practice. The GP was worried to find she has been prescribed lorazepam 4 mg a day for anxiety for about four years, and she gets shaky if a dose is delayed. She is anxious about any change and does not want to come off it. The GP has referred her to the local outpatient clinic for advice on managing the medicine. Please agree a management plan with her, including how to reduce and stop the benzodiazepine safely.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Natalie Osei?","p":"Yes. Is this about my tablets?"},{"d":"It is, Natalie. Your new GP asked us for advice. Nothing will change today without us agreeing it together.","p":"Okay. I'm already nervous."},{"d":"That's understandable. Could you tell me how the lorazepam started and how it's going now?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, reassure no sudden stop, joint decision, agree the plan"},{"h":"Lorazepam story","b":"Work stress start, four years, 4 mg, who prescribes"},{"h":"Signs of dependence","b":"Shakes when late, cravings, tolerance, extra doses, other sources"},{"h":"Anxiety and mood now","b":"Current anxiety, depression, alcohol, other drugs, suicidal thoughts"},{"h":"Why reduce","b":"Memory, falls, driving, tolerance, rebound anxiety, risk with alcohol"},{"h":"How the taper works","b":"Switch to diazepam 40 mg, small cuts fortnightly, months"},{"h":"Her fears","b":"Return of anxiety, busy life, hospital not needed, she sets pace"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Plan written down, teach-back, weekly scripts, GP letter, review"}],"bps":{"pre":"Natalie explained she was first prescribed lorazepam during a crisis at work and the GP simply kept repeating it. Now she takes 1 mg four times a day and feels shaky and sweaty if a dose is late. Her anxiety is mostly controlled, she drinks little and has no thoughts of self-harm. She shook her head firmly.","ask":"Why can't I just stay on it? And would I have to go into hospital to stop?","lead":"You won't need hospital, and we'll go at a pace you can manage; let me explain why a slow reduction is worth it.","bio":["Switch lorazepam 4 mg to diazepam 40 mg daily in stages over about a week (lorazepam 1 mg is roughly diazepam 10 mg), given in divided doses","Then reduce diazepam by about 2-4 mg every two weeks; below 20 mg reduce by 1-2 mg, and by 0.5-1 mg near the end; usually 3-6 months or longer","Never stop suddenly: warn about seizures, severe anxiety and confusion; avoid alcohol and opioids during the taper","If anxiety disorder persists, sertraline 25-50 mg daily (up to 200 mg) as NICE first-line for generalised anxiety; no other benzodiazepines or z-drugs","Weekly prescriptions from one prescriber; review at each step and hold the dose for longer if withdrawal is marked"],"psych":["CBT for generalised anxiety via NHS Talking Therapies, usually 12-15 weekly sessions, started early in the taper","Practical anxiety skills: controlled breathing, applied relaxation and a daily worry slot","Withdrawal symptom diary to show patterns and reassure her that symptoms pass"],"social":["GP letter with the full taper schedule and agreed prescribing arrangements","Driving: she must not drive if drowsy or impaired; tell DVLA if her anxiety affects driving","Employer conversation about flexible hours during the harder parts of the reduction","Local drug and alcohol service or a benzodiazepine support group if she wants extra help"],"safety":["If she has a fit, severe confusion or thoughts of self-harm, call 999 or NHS 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"S9.5","cid":"c-t-52","area":"Eating disorders","title":"Refeeding syndrome: advising the CAMHS nurse","who":"CAMHS nurse","task":"Ms Rhiannon Bell is a newly qualified CAMHS community nurse who has been visiting Amira Hughes, a 16-year-old girl with anorexia nervosa, at home for the past two weeks to monitor her weight and eating. Last week Amira's mother pressed her to eat large amounts and she put on 3 kg in seven days. Since then her hands and feet have become swollen and she is finding it hard to swallow. The GP checked bloods a week ago, which showed only a mildly reduced phosphate. Rhiannon has asked to talk with you because she is concerned. History taking is not needed. Please address her concerns, explain what is causing these symptoms and discuss management.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Rhiannon, Amira's nurse?","p":"Yes, thanks for calling back."},{"d":"I understand Amira has gained weight quickly and you've noticed some new symptoms. That's worth worrying about.","p":"Her hands and feet are puffy and she says swallowing is hard."},{"d":"Thank you. Let me check a few facts, then explain what I think is happening and what we do today.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, role, acknowledge concern, agree urgency"},{"h":"Clarify the picture","b":"3 kg in a week, swelling, swallowing, low phosphate, symptoms today"},{"h":"What this is","b":"Refeeding syndrome: fluid and salt shifts, phosphate, potassium, magnesium fall"},{"h":"Why the symptoms","b":"Oedema from fluid retention, weakness affecting swallowing, heart strain"},{"h":"Immediate action","b":"Same-day paediatric review, bloods, ECG, likely admission"},{"h":"Medical treatment","b":"Phosphate replacement, thiamine, electrolytes daily, controlled feeding plan"},{"h":"Prevention and family","b":"MEED guidance, explain to mum, not her fault, team approach"},{"h":"Hand over and close","b":"Who calls whom, consultant informed, documentation, warning signs"}],"bps":{"pre":"Rhiannon explained that Amira's mother, frightened by the weight loss, had been serving three large meals and puddings every day. Amira now has pitting ankle swelling, puffy fingers, feels weak and finds swallowing tiring. She has not been seen by a doctor since the GP bloods. Refeeding syndrome was explained and Rhiannon looked alarmed.","ask":"What should we actually do with her now? Can she stay at home?","lead":"I think Amira needs a doctor to see her today, most likely on the paediatric ward, so let me take you through why and what will happen.","bio":["Same-day paediatric assessment: bloods for phosphate, potassium, magnesium, sodium, glucose, U&E, LFTs and full blood count; repeat daily for the first week","ECG for QTc and arrhythmia; lying and standing BP, heart rate, temperature, fluid balance and daily weight","Replace phosphate (oral phosphate tablets, or IV if very low) and correct potassium and magnesium as guided by paediatricians","Thiamine (for example 100 mg daily) plus a vitamin B compound and multivitamin before and during feeding","Dietitian-led feeding plan per MEED guidance: start around 1400-2000 kcal a day and increase steadily, avoiding both overfeeding and underfeeding"],"psych":["Eating disorder-focused family therapy (FT-AN), usually 18-20 sessions over a year, starting once she is medically stable","Psychoeducation for mum on how fast refeeding should go and why, so meals feel safer for everyone","Supportive meal plans with the ward team, then CBT-ED if family therapy is not suitable"],"social":["CAMHS eating disorder team to coordinate with paediatrics through a joint care plan","School informed about absence and later return, with a meal plan in school","Beat helpline and parent support groups for the family; consider a carers' assessment","Clear communication plan: Rhiannon, the GP and the consultant agree who updates the family"],"safety":["Call 999 if Amira has chest pain, palpitations, fainting, breathlessness, confusion or a seizure before she is seen"]}},{"sid":"S9.6","cid":"c-t-53","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Schizophrenia and new trauma symptoms","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Kieran Adebayo is a 26-year-old man with paranoid schizophrenia who was discharged from an inpatient ward half a year ago following a relapse. His parents have contacted the community team because for several months he has been short-tempered, staying in his bedroom most of the day and refusing to leave the house. He is prescribed olanzapine 10 mg at night. The team assumed another relapse, but his care coordinator is not sure. He has agreed to see you in clinic but is guarded. Please take a history from him and reach a diagnosis.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Kieran Adebayo?","p":"Yeah."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Kieran. Your family have been worried you're not going out much lately.","p":"They worry about everything."},{"d":"Maybe. I'd like to hear it from you. What's been going on for you these last few months?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, open start, patience with reluctance, agree the plan"},{"h":"Recent changes","b":"Irritability, staying in room, not going out, since when"},{"h":"Trauma event","b":"Arrest during relapse, fear of dying, police, restraint, hospital"},{"h":"PTSD symptoms","b":"Flashbacks, nightmares, trigger film, avoidance, startle, hypervigilance, numbness"},{"h":"Psychosis check","b":"Voices, persecutory beliefs, adherence to olanzapine, cannabis"},{"h":"Mood and risk","b":"Low mood, motivation, sleep, suicidal thoughts, harm to others"},{"h":"Impact and supports","b":"Family, friends, work, daily routine"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"PTSD explained, teach-back, therapy plan, care coordinator, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"At first Kieran gave one-word answers, but slowly described being pinned down by police when he was unwell, sure he would die. Months later a violent arrest scene on television brought it flooding back. Since then he has nightmares, jumps at noises and avoids going out. He takes olanzapine every night, has stopped cannabis and has no voices or paranoid ideas. PTSD was explained.","ask":"So it's not my schizophrenia coming back? What do I do about this?","lead":"Your psychosis seems settled; this is a reaction to a frightening event, and it responds well to the right therapy.","bio":["Continue olanzapine 10 mg at night unchanged, as psychosis is in remission; check weight, glucose and lipids at the annual review","No routine medication for PTSD first-line; trauma-focused therapy comes first","If he prefers medication or therapy is not possible, sertraline 50 mg daily, increasing to 100-200 mg, with review at 2-4 weeks","Avoid benzodiazepines for sleep; consider short-term help with sleep hygiene first"],"psych":["Trauma-focused CBT, usually 8-12 weekly sessions with a psychologist, adapted for psychosis","EMDR as an alternative, delivered by a trained therapist over 8-12 sessions","Grounding techniques for flashbacks and a nightmare plan, taught by his care coordinator now while he waits","Psychoeducation for Kieran and his family explaining PTSD and how to support him without pushing"],"social":["Care coordinator in the CMHT to coordinate therapy and a graded plan for going out again","Family intervention sessions to reduce conflict at home","Support for staying off cannabis through the local drug service if needed","Recovery college courses or supported employment once he is more confident"],"safety":["Crisis plan: CMHT duty line in office hours, NHS 111 option 2 out of hours, 999 if in immediate danger"]}},{"sid":"S10.1","cid":"c-t-54","area":"Addictions","title":"Alcohol and clozapine: explaining to his sister","who":"Sister","task":"Mrs Leah Farrow is a 38-year-old woman whose younger brother, Mr Martin Ellery, has paranoid schizophrenia and has taken clozapine for several years. Their parents, who were his main carers, both died recently, and Martin now lives alone in the family home. Leah lives two hours away with two small children, so visits when she can. She is worried: he seems low, says he drinks more, and she has found many empty whisky and beer bottles. He has again mentioned people watching him outside, an old symptom, and she found more clozapine than there should be; he admits forgetting doses. Martin has agreed you can speak with her. Please explain a formulation of his difficulties and the possible effects of alcohol on his physical and mental health.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Leah, Martin's sister?","p":"Yes. Thanks for seeing me."},{"d":"Martin has agreed I can share information with you, so we can talk openly. I'm sorry about your parents.","p":"It's been a horrible year."},{"d":"It sounds it. Could you tell me what's worrying you most about Martin right now?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, Martin's consent, condolences, agree the plan"},{"h":"Her concerns","b":"Drinking, low mood, watched feeling, spare clozapine, living alone"},{"h":"Formulation: why now","b":"Schizophrenia, bereavement, lost carers, isolation, alcohol, missed clozapine"},{"h":"Strengths and protective factors","b":"Years of stability, Leah's support, engagement with team"},{"h":"Alcohol: physical effects","b":"Sedation, seizures, liver, falls, choking, low blood pressure"},{"h":"Alcohol: mental effects","b":"Worse mood, paranoia, relapse, suicide risk, poor adherence"},{"h":"Clozapine gaps and risk","b":"Missed over 48 hours needs retitration; stockpile overdose risk"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, teach-back, team plan, carers' assessment, crisis numbers"}],"bps":{"pre":"Leah described Martin sitting in their parents' lounge with the curtains drawn, bottles lined up by the bin. She had counted nearly two weeks of spare clozapine tablets. The formulation linking his illness, the double bereavement, losing his carers and the drinking was explained, along with the dangers of alcohol with clozapine. She nodded slowly.","ask":"So what can be done to help him before this gets worse?","lead":"Quite a lot, and the sooner the team gets involved the better; here's what I would suggest.","bio":["Urgent review by his team: clozapine plasma level, FBC with neutrophils, LFTs, GGT, MCV, U&E and an ECG","If he has missed clozapine for more than 48 hours, restart at 12.5 mg and retitrate with monitoring rather than resuming the old dose","Thiamine 100 mg three times daily while drinking heavily; AUDIT score and, if dependent, a planned assisted withdrawal with chlordiazepoxide","Assess for depression; if present, consider an antidepressant such as sertraline 50 mg daily after discussion with the team"],"psych":["Bereavement support through Cruse or the CMHT psychologist, weekly for several months","Motivational interviewing sessions on alcohol with his care coordinator or the local alcohol service","Family intervention sessions including Leah, often 10 sessions over 3-12 months, to build a shared relapse plan"],"social":["Care coordinator visits increased, and home treatment team if relapse is developing","Carers' assessment for Leah through adult social care","Social worker to look at support at home, probate and housing, and benefits now his parents have died","Local alcohol service and peer support such as Rethink Mental Illness for carers"],"safety":["Return spare clozapine to the pharmacy; weekly blister packs or supervised dispensing","If he is very drowsy, fits, talks of suicide or is very paranoid, call 999 or NHS 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"S10.2","cid":"c-t-55","area":"Depression and suicide risk","title":"Low mood with fear her husband will kill her","who":"Patient","task":"Mrs Kayleigh Doherty is a 27-year-old woman who works in a hair salon. Two workmates brought her to A&E after she told them she believes her husband means to kill her. She has spent much of the past month on sick leave with unexplained mental health problems, but turned up at the salon today unrostered asking for help. Before going off, she seemed distracted, restless and withdrawn, and was often found crying without explanation. She has never been aggressive and often looked scared. In the taxi she said she deserved to die because she was useless. Records show citalopram 20 mg for four weeks and possible depression before she moved here. Please interview her, covering the presenting complaint, a brief past history and a mental state assessment, reach a diagnosis and share your conclusions with her.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Kayleigh Doherty?","p":"Yes."},{"d":"Thanks, Kayleigh. Your colleagues brought you in because they were worried. You're safe here.","p":"I didn't know where else to go."},{"d":"You did the right thing. Could you tell me what's been happening?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, safety, calm room, agree the plan"},{"h":"Fear about husband","b":"What he did, evidence, certainty, any real violence or abuse"},{"h":"Depressive symptoms","b":"Mood, crying, sleep, appetite, concentration, energy, guilt, worthlessness"},{"h":"Psychotic features","b":"Mood-congruent delusions, voices, guilt, deserving punishment, nihilism"},{"h":"Past and treatment","b":"Earlier depression, citalopram 20 mg adherence, mania, substances"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicide plans, deserving death, self-neglect, risk from husband, children"},{"h":"Mental state and insight","b":"Appearance, retardation or agitation, mood, thought, cognition, insight"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Severe depression with psychosis explained, admission discussed, next steps"}],"bps":{"pre":"Kayleigh sat hunched, speaking slowly and barely above a whisper. She said she has ruined everything and her husband must be planning to kill her as punishment, but could give no actual threats or past violence. She has lost weight, wakes at 4am and hears no voices. She believes she deserves to die. Severe depression with psychotic symptoms was explained gently.","ask":"Can anything even help me? The tablets haven't done anything.","lead":"Yes, this is a serious but very treatable illness, and I want to start more effective treatment today while keeping you safe.","bio":["Admission to hospital, informally if she agrees, otherwise Mental Health Act assessment","Combine an antidepressant with an antipsychotic: switch citalopram to sertraline 50 mg rising to 100 mg, plus olanzapine 5 mg at night, rising to 10 mg","Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFTs, TFTs, glucose, lipids, calcium, pregnancy test; ECG for QTc before starting the antipsychotic","ECT if she stops eating or drinking, is at very high suicide risk, or does not respond, after consent or under the MHA safeguards"],"psych":["Daily supportive contact with her named nurse while on the ward","Psychoeducation for Kayleigh and, with consent, her husband about psychotic depression","High-intensity CBT for depression, around 16-20 sessions, once the psychotic features settle"],"social":["Safeguarding check: ask privately about domestic abuse, as genuine danger must be excluded before involving her husband","Children's safeguarding if she has children at home, with liaison to the GP and health visitor","Fit note and contact with her employer about sickness absence","Home treatment team for early discharge support, then CMHT follow-up with a care coordinator"],"safety":["Enhanced observation on the ward because of suicidal thoughts; remove items that could be used to harm herself"]}},{"sid":"S10.3","cid":"c-t-56","area":"Liaison","title":"Depression after a heart attack: teaching a student","who":"Medical student","task":"Mr Vikram Desai is a 57-year-old man you previously treated in clinic for moderate depression over several years; he was discharged to his GP nine months ago. He stopped his antidepressant some months back because he felt well. He has since had a heart attack and has returned to the GP with low mood, poor sleep and poor appetite. He is not attending cardiac rehabilitation, is not taking his heart medicines and keeps smoking. The GP has re-referred him for a review and ideas to get him back into rehab. Ms Freya Lindqvist, a third-year medical student on your team, will see him in clinic next week and wants to prepare to present him to her consultant. Please speak with her and address her concerns and expectations.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Freya, our medical student?","p":"Yes, hi. Thanks for making time."},{"d":"No problem. I hear you're seeing Mr Desai next week. What would be most useful for you?","p":"I want to know what to ask and what the plan might be."},{"d":"Good. Let's go through his story, why depression matters after a heart attack, then the plan.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, her goals, what she knows, agree the plan"},{"h":"His story","b":"Years of depression, stopped antidepressant, MI, low mood, not engaging"},{"h":"Why it matters","b":"Depression after MI doubles death risk, poorer adherence and rehab"},{"h":"Assessment next week","b":"Depressive symptoms, suicide risk, cognition, alcohol, beliefs about heart"},{"h":"Medication choices","b":"Sertraline safest after MI, bleeding with antiplatelets, avoid tricyclics"},{"h":"Rehab and smoking","b":"Motivational interviewing, varenicline, cardiac rehab nurse, family"},{"h":"Presenting to consultant","b":"Structure, formulation, risk, plan; confidentiality of notes"},{"h":"Hand over and close","b":"Check her understanding, reading, supervision, contact for questions"}],"bps":{"pre":"Freya had read the referral but felt unsure why a psychiatrist would worry about cardiac rehab. She learned that Mr Desai's depression was well treated until he stopped sertraline, and that untreated depression after a heart attack raises the risk of further events and death. She jotted notes about adherence and smoking and looked up.","ask":"So what would the plan for him actually look like?","lead":"It joins up his mood and his heart care, so let me walk you through it in three parts.","bio":["Restart sertraline 50 mg daily, increasing to 100 mg after 4 weeks if needed; best evidence for safety after a heart attack","SSRIs add bleeding risk with aspirin and clopidogrel or ticagrelor: add lansoprazole for stomach protection rather than omeprazole with clopidogrel","ECG, U&E, sodium (risk of hyponatraemia), LFTs and HbA1c; avoid tricyclics and use citalopram cautiously because of QTc","Smoking: varenicline 0.5 mg daily titrated to 1 mg twice daily for 12 weeks, or nicotine replacement, both safe after MI"],"psych":["NHS Talking Therapies for long-term conditions: CBT for depression, around 8-16 sessions, linked to his heart condition","Behavioural activation to rebuild activity, which also supports physical rehabilitation","Motivational interviewing sessions on medicines and smoking, exploring his own reasons to recover"],"social":["Liaise with the cardiac rehabilitation nurse to offer a fresh start date and a home-based programme if groups feel too much","GP to monitor adherence with a pharmacist medicines review and dosette box if needed","Involve his family, with consent, in supporting rehab and smoking cessation","Occupational health and phased return to work if he is employed"],"safety":["Screen carefully for suicidal thoughts, including deliberately stopping heart medicines; crisis line NHS 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"S10.4","cid":"c-t-57","area":"Neurodevelopmental","title":"Autism or psychosis? Assessing a relapse","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Jordan Ashworth is a 28-year-old autistic man who also has schizophrenia. Police brought him to A&E after his family raised concerns and after an incident with a stranger in the street. His family say he has changed recently and has seemed preoccupied and suspicious. He has taken risperidone for about ten years but may have stopped. He wants to leave as soon as possible. Please carry out a mental state assessment, explore what lies behind the change in his behaviour, and tell apart features of schizophrenia from his autistic traits.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Jordan Ashworth?","p":"Yes."},{"d":"Thank you, Jordan. I will ask some questions. You can take your time to answer.","p":"How long will this take? I need to go."},{"d":"About thirty minutes. First question: what happened before the police brought you here?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, short clear sentences, time, quiet room, agree the plan"},{"h":"What happened today","b":"Incident with public, police, family concerns, his account"},{"h":"Recent changes","b":"Life events, routine changes, timeline, stopping risperidone, sleep"},{"h":"Psychotic symptoms","b":"Spy beliefs, TV messages, persecution, voices, thought broadcasting, passivity"},{"h":"Autism versus psychosis","b":"Usual interests, literal thinking, routines; new beliefs, voices, decline"},{"h":"Mood and substances","b":"Low or high mood, cannabis, alcohol"},{"h":"Risk and insight","b":"Harm to others, self, wandering, refusing treatment, insight"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Impressions, need to stay, medication, family, Mental Health Act"}],"bps":{"pre":"Jordan answered best to short, literal questions. He explained he is a special agent waiting for a mission, that the television sends him coded instructions and that agents speak to him directly. He believes strangers are following him and that people can hear his thoughts. He stopped risperidone three months ago after his day centre closed. His usual interest in trains is unchanged. A relapse of schizophrenia was explained.","ask":"What happens to me now? I need to go home for my mission.","lead":"I think you are unwell again, Jordan, and I want to help you feel safe and settled; here is what we will do.","bio":["Restart risperidone at 2 mg daily, increasing over 1-2 weeks towards his previous effective dose (for example 4 mg)","Baseline bloods: FBC, U&E, LFTs, glucose, lipids, prolactin; ECG; weight and BP","Consider a long-acting injection, such as paliperidone, if adherence stays a problem, after discussion with Jordan and family","Avoid unnecessary sedation; if very distressed, oral lorazepam 1 mg as needed"],"psych":["Autism-adapted psychoeducation using written and visual explanations of relapse signs","CBT for psychosis adapted for autism, around 16 sessions, once settled","Relapse prevention plan made with Jordan and his family using his own early signs"],"social":["Care (Education) and Treatment Review if admission is considered, to check reasonable adjustments","Hospital passport, quiet side room, predictable routine and consistent staff","Family meeting and carers' assessment; restore day centre or alternative structured activity","If he refuses admission and risk is high, Mental Health Act assessment with an AMHP"],"safety":["Assess risk to the public after today's incident; police information shared appropriately"]}},{"sid":"S10.5","cid":"c-s-55","area":"Anxiety, OCD, trauma and dissociation","title":"Agoraphobia: talking therapy options","who":"Patient","task":"Mrs Bethan Carvalho is a 40-year-old married woman who looks after the home and her two children. Her GP referred her to the outpatient clinic because she has become very anxious about leaving the house, and she now rarely goes beyond the front gate. After assessment she has been diagnosed with agoraphobia. She is wary of medication and has heard about a step-by-step therapy for fears. Please discuss the psychological treatments available for her agoraphobia, including systematic desensitisation, and respond to her concerns.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Bethan Carvalho?","p":"Yes. It took a lot to get here."},{"d":"Well done for coming, Bethan. That's a real achievement in itself.","p":"Thank you."},{"d":"Today I'd like to talk through the therapy options for agoraphobia and answer your questions. Does that sound okay?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, praise attending, agree the plan"},{"h":"Her current difficulties","b":"Avoided places, panic attacks, safety behaviours, family impact, mood"},{"h":"Understanding agoraphobia","b":"Fear cycle, avoidance keeps it going, treatable"},{"h":"CBT and exposure","b":"Thoughts, behaviours, graded exposure, homework, 7-14 hours"},{"h":"Systematic desensitisation","b":"Relaxation training, fear ladder, step by step, pairing calm"},{"h":"Other options","b":"Guided self-help, online CBT, medication if needed"},{"h":"Her concerns","b":"Fear of panic, coping alone, attending sessions, husband's role"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, teach-back, Talking Therapies referral, leaflet, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Bethan explained she had a panic attack in a supermarket two years ago and slowly stopped going anywhere without her husband. Now she cannot walk the children to school. She is not depressed and has no thoughts of self-harm. Agoraphobia was explained as a fear cycle maintained by avoidance. She twisted her hands.","ask":"But how would therapy work if I can't even get out of the house to go to it?","lead":"That's a really common worry, and therapy can start at home and then build up gradually.","bio":["No medication is needed first-line as she prefers therapy; psychological treatment is recommended by NICE","If she later wants medication, sertraline 50 mg daily, increasing to 100-150 mg, reviewed at 2-4 weeks; warn about initial jitteriness","Avoid benzodiazepines: they reduce the benefit of exposure work and risk dependence"],"psych":["CBT with a high-intensity therapist through NHS Talking Therapies, usually 7-14 hours over 4 months, often weekly","Systematic desensitisation: first learn progressive muscle relaxation, then build a fear ladder and climb it one step at a time while staying relaxed","Graded in-vivo exposure homework between sessions, such as standing at the gate, then walking to the corner","Guided self-help or online CBT with therapist support as a first step if she prefers"],"social":["Video or telephone sessions first, then home visits or nearby appointments as she progresses","Husband involved as a co-therapist for exposure practice, gradually withdrawing support","Anxiety UK and No Panic helplines and self-help materials","School informed so a friend can help with drop-offs for now"],"safety":["If mood drops or she has thoughts of self-harm, contact the GP or NHS 111 option 2"]}},{"sid":"S10.6","cid":"c-t-58","area":"Anxiety, OCD, trauma and dissociation","title":"Dreading his own wedding","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Aaron Whitfield, 27, is a nursery assistant. He attends the outpatient clinic because he is getting married in six weeks and is extremely worried about facing the guests at the reception. He has long avoided weddings and parties, often leaving early or making excuses, and his employer has temporarily excused him from leading group activities. Please take a history from him to reach a diagnosis and explore possible causes.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Aaron Whitfield?","p":"Yes, that's me."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Aaron. Congratulations on the wedding. I gather it's also causing a lot of worry.","p":"That's an understatement."},{"d":"Could you tell me what goes through your mind when you picture the day?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, congratulate, open question, agree the plan"},{"h":"The wedding fear","b":"Speeches, being watched, guests, what he fears happening"},{"h":"Social situations generally","b":"Parties, groups, work, eating in public, phone calls"},{"h":"Physical and cognitive symptoms","b":"Dry mouth, sweating, racing heart, panic, fear of judgement, blushing"},{"h":"Avoidance and safety behaviours","b":"Leaving early, excuses, alcohol, rehearsing, avoiding eye contact"},{"h":"Onset and causes","b":"Childhood shyness, bullying, family anxiety, parenting, temperament"},{"h":"Differentials and risk","b":"Depression, panic disorder, alcohol, avoidant personality, suicidal thoughts"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Social anxiety disorder explained, teach-back, CBT, wedding plan"}],"bps":{"pre":"Aaron explained he has felt uncomfortable in groups since primary school, when classmates teased him for blushing. At gatherings his mouth dries, he sweats and his heart races, and he slips away early. His fiancée knows a little. He drinks two pints before social events but has no low mood or suicidal thoughts. Social anxiety disorder was explained.","ask":"Is there anything that can help me get through the wedding in six weeks?","lead":"Yes, and we can make a specific plan for the wedding while starting treatment for the longer-term problem.","bio":["No medication first-line: NICE recommends individual CBT for social anxiety ahead of tablets","If he declines CBT or wants added help, escitalopram 10 mg daily (up to 20 mg) or sertraline 50 mg, reviewed after 3-4 weeks; benefit takes 6-12 weeks","Beta-blockers such as propranolol are not routinely recommended for social anxiety; discuss honestly if he asks","Advise against using alcohol to cope before the wedding"],"psych":["Individual CBT for social anxiety, up to 14 sessions over about 4 months, through NHS Talking Therapies","Work on dropping safety behaviours, shifting attention outward and video feedback of speeches","Wedding-specific practice: rehearse a short speech, behavioural experiments with friends"],"social":["Involve his fiancée, with consent, in planning a manageable reception layout and breaks","Employer occupational health to make the work adjustments part of a graded return to group leading","Social Anxiety UK and Anxiety UK self-help resources"],"safety":["If mood drops or he feels hopeless, contact the GP, NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans 116 123"]}},{"sid":"S10.7","cid":"c-t-59","area":"Liaison","title":"Refusing neck wound surgery: capacity check","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Shane Mulholland is a 24-year-old man with emotionally unstable personality disorder who has been sent to the general hospital from prison, where he is serving a two-year sentence for assault occasioning actual bodily harm. He cut his neck with a piece of metal, fairly deeply, but without injuring blood vessels, nerves or muscle. The plastic surgeons recommend closing the wound under general anaesthetic, but he refuses and says he wants to return to prison without delay. Please assess his capacity to make this decision. A full mental state assessment is not required.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Shane Mulholland?","p":"Yeah. Are you here to make me have the operation?"},{"d":"No, Shane. I'm here to understand your decision and make sure it's yours to make.","p":"Fine."},{"d":"Could you tell me what the surgeons have told you about your neck?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, purpose, not forcing, agree the plan"},{"h":"Decision and context","b":"Specific decision: surgery under anaesthetic now, assumption of capacity"},{"h":"Understanding","b":"Wound depth, surgery benefits, risks of refusal: infection, poor healing, scarring"},{"h":"Retaining","b":"Repeats information later, checks key points"},{"h":"Using and weighing","b":"DBT wing transfer priority, hospital stay dislike, accepted scarring"},{"h":"Communicating and impairment","b":"Clear choice; emotional state, acute distress, intoxication"},{"h":"Self-harm risk","b":"Current thoughts, triggers, plan, means in prison"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Capacity outcome, alternatives, inform surgeons and prison, documentation"}],"bps":{"pre":"Shane calmly explained he cut himself after being told, yet again, that his transfer to the therapy wing had no date. He accurately repeated the surgeons' warnings about infection, slow healing and a large scar, and said therapy matters more than his neck. He denied current suicidal thoughts. He was judged to have capacity to refuse surgery.","ask":"So can I go back to the prison today then?","lead":"Yes, it's your decision, and I'll make sure your wound and your mental health are both looked after when you go back.","bio":["Surgeons to offer alternatives: closure under local anaesthetic as a day case, or wound management with dressings","Tetanus status check, analgesia, and antibiotics only if infection develops; prison healthcare to check the wound daily","No new psychotropic medication needed; review any current prescriptions with the prison GP"],"psych":["Expedite DBT on the therapy wing: weekly individual sessions, weekly skills group and phone coaching over about a year","Distress tolerance skills taught by the in-reach team now to bridge the wait","Brief psychological debrief about the trigger to update his crisis plan"],"social":["Prison mental health in-reach team informed today, with a clear transfer date requested","ACCT (Assessment, Care in Custody and Teamwork) plan reviewed by the prison following self-harm","Escorting officers and prison healthcare given a written handover of wound care and warning signs","Metal-working class access reviewed by the prison to reduce access to sharp off-cuts"],"safety":["Return to hospital if wound bleeds, becomes hot, red, swollen or discharges, or if he has new self-harm thoughts"]}},{"sid":"S11.1","cid":"c-t-60","area":"Medication, emergencies and side effects","title":"Mania not settling on PICU: nurse wants lithium","who":"Nurse in charge","task":"You are the on-call psychiatry registrar on a Sunday and have been asked to attend the local psychiatric intensive care unit by Aisha, the nurse in charge. Mr Connor Blackwood, a 26-year-old man with bipolar affective disorder type I, was admitted recently in a manic episode and does not seem to be improving. Aisha is worried he is at risk because he keeps intruding into other patients' personal space, and she wants to know what other treatments could be tried. Please discuss the treatment options with her, agree a plan, and address her concerns and expectations.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors on call. Are you Aisha, the nurse in charge?","p":"Yes, thanks for coming."},{"d":"You're worried about Connor not improving and other patients reacting to him.","p":"Exactly. And we want him on lithium today."},{"d":"Let's go through what's happened so far, then agree a plan together.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, role, acknowledge concern, agree the plan"},{"h":"Current presentation","b":"Elation, pressured speech, no sleep, poor eating, suspicion, intrusiveness"},{"h":"Treatment so far","b":"Olanzapine 10 mg for three days, adherence, PRN use"},{"h":"Physical health","b":"Refused bloods and observations, hydration, food intake, injuries"},{"h":"Why not lithium today","b":"Needs baseline bloods, ECG, levels; three days too early"},{"h":"Agreed medication plan","b":"Increase olanzapine, PRN lorazepam, bloods with patient's agreement"},{"h":"Ward safety","b":"Observations, low stimulus, separating patients, staffing, safeguarding"},{"h":"Hand over and close","b":"Document, inform consultant Monday, review triggers, contact details"}],"bps":{"pre":"Aisha described Connor as elated, talking rapidly, barely sleeping and eating little. He has taken olanzapine 10 mg reluctantly for three nights and refuses bloods, believing staff will experiment on his blood. Yesterday another patient nearly hit him for standing too close. The consultant had mentioned lithium if olanzapine failed. Aisha crossed her arms.","ask":"Dr Hollis said lithium if the olanzapine didn't work. Why can't we start it today?","lead":"I agree he needs more help today, but lithium isn't safe without blood tests, so let me explain what we can do right now.","bio":["Increase olanzapine to 15 mg tonight and to 20 mg within the week if tolerated; three days is too soon to judge response","PRN oral lorazepam 1-2 mg, maximum 4 mg in 24 hours, for agitation and sleep; IM options per rapid tranquillisation policy if needed","Before lithium: U&E, eGFR, TFTs, calcium, weight and ECG; then levels 12 hours post dose aiming 0.6-0.8 mmol/L, weekly until stable","Keep trying to obtain bloods with a trusted nurse and clear explanation; assess capacity and consider whether monitoring can be given under the Mental Health Act"],"psych":["Calm, consistent, low-stimulus approach with short simple explanations","Named nurse sessions to build trust around blood tests and medication","Structured, quiet activities to channel energy, and regular rest periods"],"social":["Enhanced observations (for example, within eyesight) to manage intrusive behaviour","Safeguarding of other patients: protect vulnerable patients, consider separate lounge areas","Inform family, with consent, and record a plan for consultant review on Monday","Staffing reviewed by the bleep holder if observations increase"],"safety":["Call the duty doctor urgently for violence, signs of dehydration, or if he stops eating or drinking"]}},{"sid":"S11.2","cid":"c-u-51","area":"Medication, emergencies and side effects","title":"Seclusion review: not drinking for days","who":"Ward nurse","task":"Mr Marcus Ferreira is a 27-year-old man admitted to the psychiatric intensive care unit three days ago with a relapse of paranoid schizophrenia and marked agitation. He had stopped clozapine about a week before admission. On the first day he hit a nurse and was placed in seclusion, where he has stayed. Since then he has refused medication and observations and drunk very little. He now complains of headache and a dry mouth. The nurse covering seclusion has called you to review him. Please take a handover from the nurse and agree a management plan.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Sean, covering seclusion?","p":"Yes, thanks for coming quickly."},{"d":"Could you give me the background, then what's worrying you about Marcus right now?","p":"He's barely drinking and won't let us near him."},{"d":"Thanks. Let's go through it, then decide the plan together.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, role, focus on urgent concern, agree the plan"},{"h":"Admission and seclusion","b":"Relapse, stopped clozapine, section 136, assault, restraint, reasons for seclusion"},{"h":"Intake and output","b":"600 mL then 300 mL, sips since, no urine for 12 hours"},{"h":"Symptoms and signs","b":"Headache, dry mouth, dizziness, confusion, sunken eyes, tachycardia"},{"h":"Mental state and risk","b":"Paranoia, threats, refuses unsealed food, refuses medication"},{"h":"Safe assessment plan","b":"Sealed drinks, enough trained staff, observations, bloods, ECG"},{"h":"Medication and transfer","b":"Rapid tranquillisation, A&E transfer, ambulance, police support if needed"},{"h":"Hand over and close","b":"Seclusion review schedule, document, inform consultant, timescales"}],"bps":{"pre":"Sean explained Marcus had taken roughly 600 mL in the first day, about 300 mL the next, and only sips from the tap since. He has not used the toilet for twelve hours. He refuses anything unsealed and threatens anyone who approaches. Fewer trained staff than usual are on shift. The likely dehydration was discussed. Sean looked worried.","ask":"How are we meant to check him or treat him when he won't let anyone near?","lead":"This is now a medical priority, so we need to plan carefully with enough trained staff; here's how I'd approach it.","bio":["Offer sealed bottled water, cartons and packaged food immediately, opened in front of him","Physical observations, capillary glucose, bloods (U&E, CK, FBC, LFTs, glucose) and ECG as soon as safely possible; fluid chart","If he cannot be assessed safely, rapid tranquillisation with IM lorazepam 1-2 mg, since no recent ECG; avoid IM haloperidol until ECG done","Clozapine stopped more than 48 hours ago: do not restart at the old dose; retitrate from 12.5 mg once he accepts oral medication","If AKI, rhabdomyolysis or significant dehydration, transfer to A&E for IV fluids with nursing escort and police support if needed"],"psych":["Consistent, calm verbal de-escalation by one familiar nurse at a time","Explain clearly why observations and fluids are needed, accepting his fear about his DNA","Short, regular offers of drinks and food at each nursing review"],"social":["Bleep holder or site manager to bring trained restraint staff from other wards","Medical seclusion reviews every 4 hours for the first 24 hours, then twice daily, with an independent MDT review","Inform the consultant today and the family, if Marcus agrees","Incident report and safeguarding review for the earlier assault"],"safety":["Call 999 if he becomes drowsy, confused, collapses or has a fit"]}},{"sid":"S11.3","cid":"c-s-12","area":"Medication, emergencies and side effects","title":"Weight gain on olanzapine: what can I do?","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Nathan Pryce is a 35-year-old man with paranoid schizophrenia who started olanzapine 10 mg at night about six months ago after a relapse. His psychotic symptoms have now resolved, but he has put on a lot of weight since starting the medicine and wants to do something about it. He is worried about his health and how he looks. Please talk with him about managing his weight, including changes to diet and lifestyle, and agree a plan with him.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Nathan Pryce?","p":"Yes. Nate."},{"d":"Thanks, Nate. You've done really well over the last few months. I hear the weight has been bothering you.","p":"I've gone up two jeans sizes."},{"d":"Let's look at that together and see what would help. Could you tell me more?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, praise recovery, agree the plan"},{"h":"Weight story","b":"Weight gained, appetite, cravings, timing with olanzapine, how he feels"},{"h":"Diet and activity","b":"Meals, snacks, sugary drinks, takeaways, exercise, sleep, alcohol, smoking"},{"h":"Physical health checks","b":"BMI, waist, BP, HbA1c, lipids, family history"},{"h":"Why olanzapine causes gain","b":"Appetite and metabolism effects; not his fault"},{"h":"Lifestyle changes","b":"Portions, swaps, water, walking, structured exercise, dietitian"},{"h":"Medication options","b":"Dose review, switch to aripiprazole, metformin, relapse risk"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Recap, teach-back, goals, review weight monthly"}],"bps":{"pre":"Nate explained his hunger had become constant since starting olanzapine, especially late at night, and that he has gained 14 kg. He drinks cola daily, buys takeaways most evenings and walks little. His mood is fine and he has no psychotic symptoms. His waist measured 104 cm and BP 138/86.","ask":"What can I actually do about the weight without getting ill again?","lead":"There's a lot we can do, starting with lifestyle and looking carefully at your medication together.","bio":["Check HbA1c, fasting lipids, LFTs, U&E and prolactin; ECG; weigh monthly for the next 3 months","Consider switching to aripiprazole, starting at 10 mg and cross-tapering olanzapine over 2-4 weeks, with close watch for relapse","If he prefers to stay on olanzapine, metformin 500 mg once daily with food, rising to 500 mg twice daily (off-label for antipsychotic weight gain)","Use the lowest effective olanzapine dose; avoid other sedating medicines that increase appetite"],"psych":["Motivational interviewing with his care coordinator to set small realistic goals","Behavioural weight management through a 12-week programme, such as the NHS Digital Weight Management Programme","Food and mood diary to identify late-night eating triggers"],"social":["Exercise on referral at the local leisure centre or a walking group","Dietitian appointment for meal planning and budget-friendly cooking","GP added to shared care for annual physical health checks","Peer support or recovery college healthy living courses"],"safety":["Seek help if he feels his symptoms are returning, or if he has excessive thirst or passes a lot of urine"]}},{"sid":"S11.4","cid":"c-u-52","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Voices back: why has he relapsed?","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Callum Reid is a 32-year-old warehouse worker who has paranoid schizophrenia and is maintained on olanzapine 7.5 mg at night. His partner, Leah, contacted the community mental health team because over recent weeks he has started hearing voices again and seems guarded at home. He has been offered an earlier appointment in the outpatient clinic. He lives with Leah and works part time in a warehouse. Please take a history from Mr Reid, confirm what is happening now and work out the likely reasons for this relapse.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Callum Reid?","p":"Yeah, that's me."},{"d":"Thanks for coming in, Callum. Leah was worried you've been hearing voices again, so we brought your appointment forward.","p":"She worries a lot."},{"d":"I'd like to hear how things have been, and then think together about what may have set this off. Is that okay?","p":"Fine."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, why seen early, Leah's concern, agree plan"},{"h":"Current symptoms","b":"Voices: content, commands, onset; persecutory ideas, passivity, mood, sleep"},{"h":"Tablets and adherence","b":"Olanzapine 7.5 mg taken? Missed doses, side effects, weight, beliefs about need"},{"h":"Substances","b":"Cannabis, skunk, alcohol, stimulants: amount, change recently"},{"h":"Stress and triggers","b":"Work, relationship, money, bereavement, housing, sleep loss, new medicines, smoking change"},{"h":"Physical health","b":"Infection, head injury, new drugs, stopping or starting smoking"},{"h":"Past pattern and insight","b":"Early warning signs, previous relapses, what helped, his view of illness"},{"h":"Risk","b":"To self, to Leah or others, command voices, weapons, self-neglect, driving"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Likely causes, shared plan, Leah's involvement, crisis number, early review"}],"bps":{"pre":"Callum admitted the voices had returned about a month ago, mostly two men mocking him and saying he was being watched. He had been skipping olanzapine on work nights because it left him groggy and he had put on weight. He had also started smoking skunk again with a new workmate. There were no commands to harm anyone. He looked tired and wary but agreed something had changed.","ask":"So what do we do about it? I don't want to end up in hospital again.","lead":"You've been really honest, Callum, and that gives us a clear plan to get you back on track at home.","bio":["Restart olanzapine reliably; consider moving to 10 mg at night, taken at 7-8 pm to ease morning grogginess","If missed doses continue, discuss a long-acting injection such as aripiprazole 400 mg monthly after an oral trial","Bloods now: FBC, U&E, LFT, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin; weight and BMI; urine drug screen","If weight gain is the main barrier, consider switching to aripiprazole 10 mg daily with weight review at 12 weeks","Note: stopping cigarettes raises olanzapine levels; restarting smoking lowers them"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis with a trained therapist, at least 16 sessions over 6 months, to make sense of the voices","Relapse-signature work with his care coordinator: write down his own early warning signs","Family intervention with Leah, around 10 sessions over 3-12 months","Motivational interviewing about skunk, linked to his own goal of staying out of hospital"],"social":["Care coordinator in the CMHT to see him weekly for now","Home treatment team if symptoms worsen, as an alternative to admission","Drug and alcohol service referral for cannabis support","Fit note and an occupational health chat about shift pattern if work stress is a factor","Carers' assessment for Leah and a copy of the crisis plan (with his consent)"],"safety":["Crisis plan: who to call, early signs; NHS 111 option 2 out of hours; 999 if anyone is at immediate risk","Advise him not to drive while symptoms are active and check DVLA duty"]}},{"sid":"S11.5","cid":"c-t-61","area":"Old age","title":"Low mood in an older carer","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Desmond Clarke is an 83-year-old retired bus driver who lives with his wife, Margaret. Margaret was diagnosed with vascular dementia twelve months ago and Mr Clarke looks after her at home with little outside help. His GP has referred him to the older adults community mental health team because of worsening low mood; routine blood tests came back normal. He has osteoarthritis in his knees and hands. You are visiting him at home. Please take a history of his current symptoms and carry out a thorough risk assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists from the older adults team. Are you Mr Desmond Clarke?","p":"I am. Des, please."},{"d":"Thank you for letting me visit, Des. Your GP mentioned things have felt heavy lately.","p":"It's been a long year."},{"d":"Is there somewhere we can talk privately for a while, and is Margaret settled?","p":"She's watching telly, she's fine."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, home visit, privacy, Margaret settled, agree time"},{"h":"Mood symptoms","b":"Low mood, interest, energy, sleep, appetite, weight, concentration, guilt, hopelessness"},{"h":"Caring role","b":"Margaret's needs, nights, help available, burden, resentment, guilt"},{"h":"Physical health","b":"Arthritis pain, mobility, falls, medicines, hearing, sight, memory"},{"h":"Psychotic and cognitive screen","b":"Nihilistic or guilty delusions, voices, memory concerns"},{"h":"Alcohol and medicines","b":"Drinking, painkillers, stockpiling, sleeping tablets"},{"h":"Suicide risk","b":"Thoughts, plans, means at home, final acts, older man alone"},{"h":"Risk to Margaret","b":"Neglect, frustration, harming her, joint suicide thoughts, safeguarding"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, depression likely, support for both, plan, crisis number"}],"bps":{"pre":"Des described three months of waking at 4 am, losing interest in his allotment and feeling he was failing Margaret. He had lost half a stone. He admitted he sometimes thought they would both be better off gone, but had no plan and said his grandchildren kept him going. He was exhausted by Margaret's night-time wandering and had no help. Bloods were normal and his arthritis was poorly controlled.","ask":"I don't want to be a burden. What can anybody actually do?","lead":"You're not a burden, Des; you've been carrying a great deal alone, and there are real ways to help both of you.","bio":["Sertraline 25 mg daily for one week, then 50 mg; review at 2 and 4 weeks","Check sodium 2-4 weeks after starting, as older adults are prone to hyponatraemia and falls","If on NSAIDs for arthritis, add a PPI because of bleeding risk with SSRIs; ask GP to review pain relief","Avoid tricyclics and sedating hypnotics (falls, overdose risk); mirtazapine 15 mg at night if sleep and appetite dominate"],"psych":["CBT or behavioural activation through older adult psychology or NHS Talking Therapies, around 12-16 weekly sessions","Carer support group, for example via Carers UK or local carers' centre","Admiral Nurse for dementia carer coaching and emotional support"],"social":["Carers' assessment under the Care Act 2014 by adult social care","Needs assessment for Margaret: day centre, carers at night, respite stays","Attendance Allowance and Carer's Allowance check via Age UK or Citizens Advice","Community psychiatric nurse visits weekly while mood is low"],"safety":["Remove stockpiled tablets; pharmacy to supply weekly packs","Crisis plan: Samaritans 116 123, NHS 111 option 2, 999 if in immediate danger","Safeguarding check for Margaret if his capacity to care falls further"]}},{"sid":"S11.6","cid":"c-u-53","area":"Forensic","title":"Jealousy, a knife and a speeding car","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Gareth Holloway is a 45-year-old plasterer who was arrested by police after being stopped for driving at very high speed and dangerously on the M6. He told officers he was racing to confront his wife, Donna, because he is convinced she is having an affair with her manager. A kitchen knife was found in the car. He is now in a police custody suite and the liaison and diversion team has asked you to see him. Please take a history focusing on his past and present risk factors. A detailed substance use history is not needed.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors working with the custody team. Are you Gareth Holloway?","p":"Yeah. Why do I need a psychiatrist?"},{"d":"Thanks for seeing me, Gareth. Today sounds like it's been a lot. I'm here to understand what's been going on for you.","p":"My wife is the problem, not me."},{"d":"I'd like to hear about that. What we discuss is confidential, unless I'm worried someone could be harmed.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, role, why seeing him, limits of confidentiality"},{"h":"Today's events","b":"Speeding, plan when he arrived, why the knife, what he intended"},{"h":"The jealous belief","b":"Evidence, checking phone, following, interrogating, fixity, duration"},{"h":"Violence and threats","b":"Threats to wife or manager, past violence, injunctions, domestic abuse"},{"h":"Other psychopathology","b":"Delusions elsewhere, voices, mood, alcohol briefly, cognitive change"},{"h":"Forensic and personal history","b":"Convictions, weapons, previous relationships, childhood, personality"},{"h":"Protective factors","b":"Children, work, insight, willingness to accept help, support"},{"h":"Current risk","b":"Intent now, access to wife, weapons at home, suicide risk"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, concern for safety, next steps, no ongoing contact with wife"}],"bps":{"pre":"Gareth said for two years he had been checking Donna's phone, smelling her clothes and timing her journeys. He was certain her manager's car had parked outside their house. He had once pushed her against a wall. He took the knife 'to scare him off' and still felt she deserved to be confronted. He denied voices but was low and slept badly. He had no insight.","ask":"So what happens now? I just want the truth out of her.","lead":"I can hear how tormented you've been, Gareth, and I'm worried enough about everyone's safety that we need to act today.","bio":["Assess further in hospital; if a delusional disorder is confirmed, start risperidone 1 mg at night, increasing to 2-4 mg over 1-2 weeks","Check bloods (FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, B12, glucose) and consider brain imaging if late onset or neurological signs","Prolactin, weight and ECG baseline before antipsychotic; review extrapyramidal side effects","Treat any depression or alcohol misuse found, as these increase risk"],"psych":["Detailed risk formulation by the forensic or liaison team using a structured tool such as HCR-20","Individual CBT for jealous thinking once more settled, typically 12-20 sessions","Avoid couples therapy while risk to Donna remains high"],"social":["Mental Health Act assessment in custody, given the knife, intent and lack of insight","Inform police of the risk; consider disclosure to Donna in the public interest (GMC guidance)","Domestic abuse referral: MARAC and an independent domestic violence adviser for Donna","Children's safeguarding referral if there are children in the home","DVLA: advise him not to drive; consider notification if he continues"],"safety":["No contact with Donna or her manager while risk is reviewed; police to remove weapons from home","Ongoing suicide risk check as he is distressed and in custody"]}},{"sid":"S11.7","cid":"c-u-54","area":"Perinatal","title":"New mother not feeding baby: A&E","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Amara Whitfield is a 28-year-old first-time mother with no known psychiatric history who gave birth to her son, Leo, five weeks ago. Her health visitor called an ambulance after finding her agitated at home and learning that Leo had barely been fed for a day. Ms Whitfield has been brought to A&E with Leo, who is now with paediatric staff. Her partner is on his way. Please take a history from Ms Whitfield to reach a working diagnosis and assess risk to her and to Leo.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Amara Whitfield?","p":"Where's Leo? Is he safe?"},{"d":"Leo is just next door with the children's nurses, and he's being looked after.","p":"Okay."},{"d":"Your health visitor was worried about you both today. Could you tell me how things have been since he was born?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, reassure about Leo, health visitor's concern, plan"},{"h":"Since the birth","b":"Delivery, complications, sleep, feeding, onset of change, speed"},{"h":"Psychotic symptoms","b":"Beliefs about baby, voices, perplexity, confusion, mood swings, elation"},{"h":"Mood","b":"Low or high mood, guilt, racing thoughts, energy, irritability"},{"h":"Organic screen","b":"Fever, infection, pain, thyroid, alcohol, drugs, medication"},{"h":"Background","b":"Family history bipolar or postpartum illness, previous episodes, supports"},{"h":"Risk to herself","b":"Suicide thoughts, self-neglect, eating, drinking, wandering"},{"h":"Risk to Leo","b":"Thoughts to harm, neglect, feeding, beliefs about him, infanticide"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Postpartum psychosis likely, urgency, mother and baby unit, partner"}],"bps":{"pre":"Amara spoke fast and jumped between ideas. She had barely slept for four nights and believed Leo was 'pure light' who did not need milk. She heard a woman's voice telling her to keep him from food. Her mother had been admitted after her own birth. She had no fever and no drug use. She was frightened and did not think she was unwell.","ask":"Am I going to lose him? What happens to me now?","lead":"We want to keep you and Leo together and safe, Amara, and this illness usually gets better with the right treatment.","bio":["Urgent bloods: FBC, CRP, U&E, LFT, calcium, glucose, TFT, B12; urine screen; consider CT head if atypical","Start olanzapine 5 mg at night, increasing to 10 mg as needed; check whether she plans to breastfeed","Lorazepam 0.5-1 mg up to twice daily for severe agitation and to restore sleep","Avoid valproate; consider lithium later for mood stabilisation with levels 0.6-0.8 and baby monitoring if breastfeeding"],"psych":["Clear, calm explanation to Amara and her partner of postpartum psychosis and its good recovery rate","Supported mother-baby interaction work in the unit with nursery nurses","Later perinatal psychology to process the experience and plan for future pregnancies"],"social":["Admission to a mother and baby unit, informally if possible or under the Mental Health Act if needed","Perinatal community mental health team to follow up after discharge","Children's social care referral to support safe care of Leo; health visitor and GP informed","Partner's paternity leave and family help; Action on Postpartum Psychosis peer support"],"safety":["Leo must not be left alone with her until she is better; supervised care only","1:1 nursing observation while awaiting a bed"]}},{"sid":"S12.1","cid":"c-u-55","area":"Old age","title":"Memory loss and wandering: wife's account","who":"Wife","task":"Mrs Gwen Harper is the wife of Leonard Harper, a 79-year-old retired electrician who has had worsening memory over the past year. His GP has referred him to the memory clinic, and he is about to have his own assessment with a colleague. Mrs Harper is very worried about his safety because he has recently wandered out of the house and been found lost. You are working in the memory clinic. Please take a collateral history from Mrs Harper to form an initial impression and explain to her what happens next.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the memory clinic. Are you Mrs Gwen Harper?","p":"Yes, Leonard's wife."},{"d":"Thank you for coming, Mrs Harper. Leonard has agreed for us to talk; he's with my colleague now.","p":"Good, he'd be cross otherwise."},{"d":"I understand you're worried about him getting lost. Could you tell me when you first noticed changes?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, Leonard's consent, her worries, plan"},{"h":"Memory history","b":"First signs, onset, gradual or stepwise, recent versus old memories"},{"h":"Other cognitive areas","b":"Words, getting lost, recognising people, managing money, tasks"},{"h":"Wandering and safety","b":"When, how found, cooking, gas, driving, falls, scams"},{"h":"Behaviour and mood","b":"Low mood, apathy, hallucinations, sleep, aggression, personality change"},{"h":"Medical and family history","b":"Vascular risks, stroke, alcohol, medicines, family dementia"},{"h":"Daily living and carer","b":"Washing, dressing, continence, her strain, support at home"},{"h":"Next steps","b":"Bloods, scan, memory testing, diagnosis meeting, possible medicine"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, likely dementia, safety tips, support, contact number"}],"bps":{"pre":"Mrs Harper described a gradual decline over a year: Leonard repeated questions, lost his keys daily and had twice walked to his old workplace and been brought home by neighbours. He still recognised family but struggled with the TV remote. He had high blood pressure but no stroke. He still drove short trips. She was tired and tearful. The picture suggested Alzheimer's disease.","ask":"So is it dementia? And what can we do to keep him safe?","lead":"It does sound like a memory illness, possibly Alzheimer's, and there's a clear process to confirm it and support you both.","bio":["Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, B12, folate, calcium, glucose, HbA1c; review medicines for anticholinergic burden","Cognitive testing such as ACE-III, and CT or MRI head","If Alzheimer's confirmed: donepezil 5 mg at night for 4 weeks, then 10 mg; ECG first if slow pulse; warn about nausea and vivid dreams","Optimise blood pressure with the GP"],"psych":["Cognitive stimulation therapy group, 14 weekly sessions","Post-diagnosis support sessions for Leonard and Gwen together","Life story work and memory aids such as a daily whiteboard"],"social":["Carers' assessment for Mrs Harper by adult social care","Herbert Protocol form with local police, GPS tracker and door sensors via telecare","DVLA: Leonard must notify and should stop driving pending assessment","Lasting power of attorney while he still has capacity","Alzheimer's Society dementia adviser and an Admiral Nurse"],"safety":["If he goes missing, call 999 straight away with the Herbert Protocol form","Gas cut-off valve and cooker safety device"]}},{"sid":"S12.2","cid":"c-u-56","area":"Perinatal","title":"Day three after birth: 'my baby is special'","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Leanne Barker is a 28-year-old woman on the postnatal ward who delivered a baby boy, Ellis, three days ago. Midwives are worried because she has not fed Ellis since the birth and keeps telling staff that he is 'special'. She is asking to take him home straight away. Her midwife has requested an urgent psychiatric review. Please carry out a mental state assessment of Ms Barker and a brief risk assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Leanne Barker?","p":"Yes. Can I go home now?"},{"d":"Congratulations on Ellis. I know you're keen to go home. The midwives asked me to check how you're doing.","p":"I'm fine, he's the one who matters."},{"d":"Could we talk for a little while about how you've been feeling since he arrived?","p":"If it's quick."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, congratulate, acknowledge wish to leave, purpose"},{"h":"Appearance and behaviour","b":"Self-care, eye contact, restlessness, interaction with Ellis"},{"h":"Speech and mood","b":"Rate, pressure, elation or low mood, irritability, lability"},{"h":"Thought content","b":"Why Ellis is special, delusions, religious themes, grandiosity"},{"h":"Perception","b":"Voices about baby, visions, commands"},{"h":"Cognition and insight","b":"Orientation, attention, confusion suggesting delirium, insight"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Feeding, harm to Ellis, suicide, leaving with baby"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, stay on ward, plan, partner, next review"}],"bps":{"pre":"Leanne was dishevelled, restless and spoke rapidly. She said Ellis had been sent by God to heal the world and did not need ordinary milk. She heard a voice confirming this. Her mood was elated then tearful. She knew the day and place. She wanted to leave now and saw no problem. There were no thoughts of harming Ellis, but he was not being fed.","ask":"Why can't I just take him home? What are you going to do?","lead":"I can see how much Ellis means to you, Leanne, and we want to make sure you're both safe and well.","bio":["Rule out physical causes: obs, FBC, CRP, U&E, calcium, glucose, TFT, urine; review delivery drugs and any infection","Start olanzapine 2.5-5 mg at night, titrating to 10 mg; discuss feeding choices","Lorazepam 0.5-1 mg as required for agitation and to restore sleep","Avoid valproate in women of childbearing age"],"psych":["Clear explanation to Leanne and her partner of postpartum psychosis and its usually good recovery","Supported, supervised time with Ellis; midwives or nursery nurses manage feeding","Later perinatal psychology to make sense of the episode and plan future pregnancies"],"social":["Transfer to a mother and baby unit; Mental Health Act assessment if she tries to leave","Perinatal mental health team involved today","Children's social care referral for a safety plan for Ellis","Inform GP, health visitor and community midwife"],"safety":["Ellis must not be alone with her; 1:1 observation on the ward","Security aware she may try to leave with the baby"]}},{"sid":"S12.3","cid":"c-s-58","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Is CBT for psychosis right for him?","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Kieran Doyle is a 19-year-old man who has been under the early intervention in psychosis team since a first psychotic episode six months ago. His symptoms are now settled on risperidone, although he still uses cannabis now and then. The team is considering referring him for CBT for psychosis. Please explore his past and recent vulnerability factors, build a preliminary formulation of how his illness developed, and assess whether he is suitable for CBT for psychosis.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists, working with the early intervention service. Are you Kieran Doyle?","p":"Yeah."},{"d":"Thanks for coming in, Kieran. Your team mentioned a talking therapy that might help. That's what today is about.","p":"Okay, what is it?"},{"d":"First I'd like to understand how things built up before you became unwell. Then we'll see if it fits.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, purpose, CBT for psychosis mentioned, plan"},{"h":"Predisposing factors","b":"Family history, childhood, trauma, bullying, migration, early cannabis"},{"h":"Precipitating factors","b":"Stress before onset, college deadlines, moving out, heavy cannabis, sleep loss"},{"h":"The episode","b":"Voices, beliefs, how he made sense of it, admission"},{"h":"Perpetuating factors","b":"Ongoing cannabis, isolation, worry, stopping tablets, stress"},{"h":"Protective factors","b":"Family, friends, hobbies, college, insight"},{"h":"Suitability for CBTp","b":"Insight, curiosity, willingness, homework, residual symptoms, goals"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Self-harm, harm to others, relapse signs"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Formulation shared, suitable for CBTp, next steps"}],"bps":{"pre":"Kieran described a lonely childhood after his parents split, and being bullied at school. His uncle had schizophrenia. He began smoking skunk daily at 15. In the months before he became unwell, he started at college, moved out and slept little. He had heard voices saying people were plotting. He still had brief suspicious thoughts and was curious about them. He wanted to return to college.","ask":"So would this therapy actually help me? What else is there?","lead":"You've thought about this carefully, Kieran, and you're a good fit for this kind of therapy.","bio":["Continue risperidone at current dose (2-4 mg daily); review at 12 weeks and yearly","Monitor weight, BMI, waist, blood pressure, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin; ask about sexual side effects","Discuss the link between skunk and relapse; urine drug screen if helpful"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis: at least 16 one-to-one sessions over 6 months with a trained therapist","Sessions cover making sense of suspicious thoughts, testing beliefs, coping and relapse planning","Family intervention with mum, around 10 sessions","Motivational interviewing about cannabis"],"social":["Early intervention care coordinator to link the therapy with his daily goals","IPS (individual placement and support) to help with college and work","Drug and alcohol service for young people if he wants to cut down","Peer support groups through the early intervention team"],"safety":["Crisis plan with early warning signs; NHS 111 option 2 if things worsen"]}},{"sid":"S12.4","cid":"c-u-57","area":"Child and adolescent","title":"Teenage overdose: bullying behind it","who":"Patient","task":"Miss Chloe Nwosu is a 15-year-old girl admitted to the children's ward from A&E after taking about 80 paracetamol tablets. She needed an acetylcysteine infusion but has now been declared medically fit by the paediatric team. Her mother is waiting in the parents' room. You are the psychiatry doctor covering the hospital. Please take a history about the overdose and the factors that led to it, and assess her current risk.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Chloe?","p":"Yeah."},{"d":"Thanks for talking to me, Chloe. I'm glad you're physically better. I'd like to understand what happened.","p":"Okay."},{"d":"Our chat is kept private, except if I'm worried you or someone else isn't safe. Then I'd have to share it.","p":"Fine."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, glad she's well, confidentiality and its limits"},{"h":"The overdose","b":"Tablets source, planning, notes, alone, help sought, intent"},{"h":"After the overdose","b":"Regret, still wishes to die, what's changed"},{"h":"Why now","b":"Bullying, school, online, friends, relationships, family conflict"},{"h":"Mood and mental health","b":"Depression, anxiety, eating, self-harm, sleep, substances"},{"h":"Safeguarding","b":"Abuse, exploitation, online risk, home safety"},{"h":"Protective factors","b":"Family, friends, hobbies, plans, supportive adults"},{"h":"Current risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts now, plans, means at home"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, involve mum, CAMHS, safety plan, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Chloe said that for months girls in her year had shared edited pictures of her in a group chat. She stopped going to lunch and slept poorly. She bought paracetamol from two shops after school, took them alone and messaged a friend an hour later. She said she wanted everything to stop. She was relieved to be alive but still felt low. Her mum knew nothing about the bullying.","ask":"So what happens now? Do I have to go back to school?","lead":"You've been incredibly brave telling me this, Chloe, and there's a lot we can do to make things better.","bio":["No medication for self-harm itself; LFTs checked as per the paediatric team before discharge","If moderate to severe depression persists after psychological therapy, CAMHS may add fluoxetine 10 mg daily, rising to 20 mg, with weekly checks early on","Warn about early agitation or suicidal thinking on fluoxetine; review in person"],"psych":["CAMHS follow-up within 48 hours of discharge","Therapy specifically for self-harm, such as DBT for adolescents, usually weekly for several months","CBT for low mood, around 12 sessions; family sessions with mum"],"social":["School designated safeguarding lead and anti-bullying plan; review phased return","Report online abuse; image-sharing may be a police matter","Childline 0800 1111 and Papyrus HOPELINE247","Safeguarding referral if any exploitation is found"],"safety":["Mum to lock away all medicines including paracetamol and remove sharps","Safety plan written with Chloe: triggers, distractions, people to call, 999 if at immediate risk"]}},{"sid":"S12.5","cid":"c-u-58","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Barcode fears: belief or psychosis?","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Neil Corrigan is a 45-year-old delivery driver referred by his GP to the community mental health team. When the GP was arranging some routine blood tests, Mr Corrigan said the barcode on the sample tube carried the 'mark of the beast' and was part of a worldwide plot. The GP wonders whether he has a psychotic illness. Mr Corrigan has agreed to come in to talk about his concerns. Please assess him to explore what his beliefs are, how firmly held, and whether he has a psychotic illness. You will then share your diagnostic impression.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Neil Corrigan?","p":"Yes. I don't see why I'm here."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Neil. Your GP said you had concerns about the blood tubes. I'd really like to understand your view.","p":"Most people laugh."},{"d":"I'm not here to laugh. Could you tell me how it started?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, respect his view, GP's referral, purpose"},{"h":"The belief","b":"Origin, conviction, evidence, faith links, others who share it"},{"h":"Fixity and reasoning","b":"Can he consider alternatives, distress, preoccupation, actions taken"},{"h":"Psychotic screen","b":"Voices, passivity, other delusions, thought interference, perception"},{"h":"Mood and anxiety","b":"Depression, mania, worries, OCD features, sleep"},{"h":"Background","b":"Faith community, upbringing, substances, personality, functioning"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Refusing medical care, harm to self or others, confrontations"},{"h":"Diagnostic impression","b":"Overvalued idea, not psychotic, differential, reasons"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Share view, blood test options, GP letter, open door"}],"bps":{"pre":"Neil explained that his church study group had discussed barcodes and Revelation for years, and several members held the same view. He could see why others disagreed and said he might be wrong about the tubes, but it still troubled him. He had no voices, no other strange experiences and was working normally. His mood was fine. The belief was an overvalued idea, not psychosis.","ask":"So do you think I'm mad? And what about my blood tests?","lead":"No, Neil, I see no sign of a mental illness here; your belief makes sense within your faith and community.","bio":["No antipsychotic: there is no psychotic illness","Ask the GP whether the tests are essential; options include a point-of-care fingerprick test or labels covered","Routine physical health care continues as planned with his consent"],"psych":["Respectful explanation of the difference between a strongly held belief and an illness","Offer NHS Talking Therapies (CBT for anxiety) if the worries become distressing","Re-assessment if new symptoms such as voices or broader suspicions appear"],"social":["Letter to GP explaining the impression of an overvalued idea and suggested adjustments","Discharge from the CMHT with a clear route back","Encourage support from his faith leader or church group if he wishes"],"safety":["Seek help via the GP or NHS 111 if he feels unsafe or the beliefs spread"]}},{"sid":"S12.6","cid":"c-t-62","area":"Forensic","title":"Voices return after a row at work","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Tomasz Nowak is a 34-year-old man with schizophrenia diagnosed during an admission six years ago. He has been well since on quetiapine MR 500 mg at night and works as a factory supervisor. His GP has referred him urgently because, after an argument with a colleague three weeks ago, he has begun hearing voices again. He is seen in the outpatient clinic today. Please carry out a risk assessment and agree a management and safety plan with him.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Tomasz Nowak?","p":"Yes. Tom is fine."},{"d":"Thanks for coming at short notice, Tom. Your GP said things have been tough since that work argument.","p":"It's been bad."},{"d":"I'd like to hear what's been happening, check everyone's safety, and plan together. Is that okay?","p":"Okay."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, urgent referral, work row, agree plan"},{"h":"The voices","b":"Content, commands, about colleague, frequency, distress, onset after argument"},{"h":"Other symptoms","b":"Persecutory beliefs, mood, sleep, anger, substances, adherence to quetiapine"},{"h":"Risk to others","b":"Thoughts of harming colleague, plans, weapons, past violence, intent"},{"h":"Risk to self","b":"Suicide thoughts, self-harm, self-neglect"},{"h":"Protective factors","b":"Family, insight, wish to stay well, relationship with team"},{"h":"Management plan","b":"Medication change, home treatment, time off, admission if needed"},{"h":"Safety plan","b":"Avoid workplace, warning signs, contacts, disclosure to protect colleague"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, agreed plan, crisis contacts, review in days"}],"bps":{"pre":"Tom said the argument with his colleague Gary had left him furious and sleepless. A week later, voices began telling him Gary was poisoning his tea and that he should 'deal with him'. He had thought about hitting Gary but had made no plan and was frightened by the thoughts. He had missed a few quetiapine doses. He had no weapons and wanted help. He was not suicidal.","ask":"What happens now? I don't want to hurt anyone.","lead":"Telling me this shows you want to keep everyone safe, Tom, and together we can do that.","bio":["Restart quetiapine MR 500 mg nightly; if needed increase to 600 mg, then 750 mg maximum, by 100-150 mg steps","Short-term lorazepam 1 mg up to twice daily for agitation or poor sleep","Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, glucose, lipids; ECG if increasing dose; urine drug screen","Consider a long-acting injection if missed doses continue"],"psych":["Relapse-prevention work with his care coordinator, focusing on stress and anger triggers","CBT for psychosis to address the poisoning belief, at least 16 sessions","Family intervention with his partner if he agrees"],"social":["Home treatment team daily visits; admission (informal or Mental Health Act) if risk rises","Fit note and time off; no return to work until well; occupational health input","Discuss with him sharing the risk with police or his employer; disclose in the public interest if needed (GMC)","Care coordinator in the CMHT and carers' assessment for his partner"],"safety":["Stay away from the workplace and Gary; call the crisis line if the voices command him","NHS 111 option 2 or 999 if he feels he may act on the voices"]}},{"sid":"S12.7","cid":"c-u-59","area":"Perinatal","title":"Low mood: baby at home, pregnant again","who":"Patient","task":"Mrs Priya Lawson is a 35-year-old woman referred by her GP to the perinatal mental health team because of persistent persistent sadness. She has a daughter aged seven months, Isha, and has just found out she is 10 weeks pregnant. She has been seen in the perinatal clinic today. Please take a history from Mrs Lawson about her mood and carry out a risk assessment, including risk to her children.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists, working with the perinatal service. Are you Priya Lawson?","p":"Yes, that's me."},{"d":"Thank you for coming, Priya. Your GP mentioned you've been feeling low, with Isha at home and a new pregnancy.","p":"It's a lot."},{"d":"I'd like to understand how you've been, and how to support you. Is that okay?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge baby and pregnancy, plan"},{"h":"Mood symptoms","b":"Low mood, interest, sleep, energy, appetite, guilt, concentration, onset"},{"h":"Since Isha's birth","b":"Delivery, bonding, feeding, support, earlier postnatal symptoms"},{"h":"This pregnancy","b":"Planned or not, feelings, antenatal care, morning sickness"},{"h":"Background","b":"Past depression, bipolar features, family history, alcohol, relationship"},{"h":"Psychotic and bipolar screen","b":"Elation, voices, delusional guilt"},{"h":"Risk to herself","b":"Suicide thoughts, plans, self-harm, self-neglect"},{"h":"Risk to children","b":"Thoughts of harming Isha, neglect, care of pregnancy"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, perinatal plan, support, crisis contacts"}],"bps":{"pre":"Priya said her low mood started a few months after Isha's birth and worsened when she found she was pregnant again, unplanned. She cried most days, felt she was a bad mother, and slept poorly. She sometimes wished she would not wake up but had no plan, and would never harm Isha. Her husband worked long hours. There was no history of mania.","ask":"Is there anything I can take while I'm pregnant? What can be done?","lead":"Yes, Priya; there are safe options, and you don't have to cope with this alone.","bio":["Moderate depression in pregnancy: sertraline 50 mg daily, review at 2-4 weeks; discuss small risks such as neonatal adaptation","Check TFT, FBC, ferritin; continue folic acid 400 micrograms","Avoid valproate; plan for any medication in the birth plan and inform paediatrics"],"psych":["CBT or IPT through the perinatal team or NHS Talking Therapies (priority for pregnant women), around 12-16 sessions","Mother-infant interaction support if bonding with Isha is affected","Partner involvement in sessions where helpful"],"social":["Perinatal mental health team follow-up and a pre-birth plan","Inform midwife, health visitor and GP","Home-Start volunteer support; PANDAS Foundation helpline","Carers' input from her husband; ask about paternity leave and flexible working"],"safety":["Crisis plan: NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, 999 if in danger","Urgent review if suicidal thoughts increase or she feels unable to care for Isha"]}},{"sid":"S13.1","cid":"c-t-63","area":"Depression and suicide risk","title":"Grief: 'I still see him at home'","who":"Patient","task":"Mrs Bernadette Quinn is a 53-year-old school administrator whose husband, Frank, died suddenly three months ago. Her GP has referred her to the outpatient psychiatry clinic because she mentioned that she sometimes sees Frank around the house, and the GP is unsure whether this is a sign of mental illness. Please take a relevant history from Mrs Quinn and give her a brief formulation of what she is experiencing.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Bernadette Quinn?","p":"Yes. Bernie."},{"d":"Thank you for coming, Bernie. I'm very sorry about Frank.","p":"Thank you."},{"d":"Your GP mentioned some experiences since he died. Could you tell me about Frank and how things have been?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, condolences, purpose, plan"},{"h":"The loss","b":"How Frank died, sudden or expected, funeral, relationship"},{"h":"Seeing Frank","b":"What she sees, when, insight, distress, other perceptions"},{"h":"Grief reactions","b":"Yearning, crying, guilt, anger, avoidance, sleep, appetite"},{"h":"Depression screen","b":"Persistent low mood, worthlessness, hopelessness, function"},{"h":"Psychosis and other screen","b":"Voices, delusions, alcohol use, medication"},{"h":"Supports and history","b":"Family, friends, previous losses, past mental health"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Wish to join Frank, suicide thoughts, plans, self-neglect"},{"h":"Formulation and close","b":"Normal grief, why it happens, support, plan, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Bernie described Frank's sudden heart attack. She sometimes glimpsed him in his chair or heard him in the kitchen, knew it was not real and found it oddly comforting. She cried daily, slept poorly and missed him terribly but still worked and saw friends. She sometimes wished to be with him, without plans. There was no depressive illness or psychosis.","ask":"So am I going mad? What can help with this?","lead":"No, Bernie, you're not going mad; what you describe is a common part of grieving.","bio":["No antidepressant for normal grief; review if a depressive episode develops","Avoid sleeping tablets; short-term sleep hygiene","Check alcohol use and review physical health with the GP"],"psych":["Bereavement counselling through Cruse Bereavement Support","If grief is intense and lasting beyond 6-12 months, grief-focused CBT","Supportive follow-up in clinic or with the GP in 4-6 weeks"],"social":["Cruse helpline 0808 808 1677 and local bereavement groups","Citizens Advice for Bereavement Support Payment and probate questions","Encourage contact with family, friends, and work"],"safety":["Seek help via GP, NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans 116 123 if thoughts of dying increase"]}},{"sid":"S13.2","cid":"c-u-60","area":"Depression and suicide risk","title":"CBT for depression: how it could help","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Martin Ellery is a 43-year-old operations manager at an insurance firm, where he has worked since it opened ten years ago. After the firm missed financial targets and cut staff, he had to tell several team members they were losing their jobs, although his own post was safe. Since then his mood has dropped and he has lost interest, energy and concentration, withdrawing at work and at home. He feels negative about himself and his work but has no thoughts of self-harm, and his wife is a strong support. Mirtazapine, started by his GP two months ago, has helped his sleep and appetite but not his mood. He has been signed off work for six weeks. Please talk to Mr Ellery about CBT and explain how it might help him. A mental state assessment and risk assessment are not needed.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Martin Ellery?","p":"Yes, Martin."},{"d":"Thanks for coming in, Martin. Your GP thought a talking therapy called CBT might help.","p":"I'm not sure what it involves."},{"d":"Let's start with how things have been, then I'll explain CBT and answer your questions.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, GP's suggestion, plan"},{"h":"His difficulties","b":"Redundancies, guilt, low mood, withdrawal, mirtazapine effect, time off"},{"h":"What CBT is","b":"Thoughts, feelings, behaviours, vicious cycles, his examples"},{"h":"How it helps him","b":"Guilt thoughts, withdrawal, activity, confidence returning to work"},{"h":"Structure of therapy","b":"Weekly, 16-20 sessions, homework, diaries, therapist, NHS Talking Therapies"},{"h":"Evidence and expectations","b":"Works as well as tablets, lasting gains, effort needed"},{"h":"Concerns and questions","b":"Time, emotional difficulty, confidentiality, work"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, referral, leaflet, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Martin said telling his team about redundancies had left him feeling he had betrayed them. Since then he had stopped seeing friends, found work pointless and spent days at home. Mirtazapine helped him sleep and eat but his mood was the same. He was curious about CBT but worried about 'just talking'. He had a supportive wife.","ask":"So how is talking actually going to fix this?","lead":"It's more than talking, Martin; CBT gives you practical tools to break the cycle you're stuck in.","bio":["Mirtazapine: if on 30 mg, consider 45 mg, or switch to sertraline 50 mg if little benefit after 4 weeks","Check weight and sedation; review in 4 weeks","Continue antidepressant at least 6 months after recovery"],"psych":["Individual CBT through NHS Talking Therapies, 16-20 weekly sessions","Behavioural activation within CBT: scheduling activity to rebuild energy","Work with guilt thoughts about the redundancies; mood diary homework"],"social":["Fit note and occupational health review; phased return to work","Access to Work or employer support scheme","Involve his wife for support; Mind information leaflets"],"safety":["Seek help via the GP or NHS 111 option 2 if mood worsens"]}},{"sid":"S13.3","cid":"c-t-64","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Withdrawn after rehab: what can help?","who":"Patient","task":"Miss Shanice Morrow is a 31-year-old woman with schizophrenia and recurrent depressive disorder. She spent a year on a rehabilitation unit because of ongoing delusions and negative symptoms and moved into a supported residential home a month ago. Her delusions have faded, but staff say she rarely joins activities and spends most of the day in her room. She takes clozapine 400 mg at night and sertraline 200 mg daily. Please speak to Miss Morrow about psychological and social approaches that could help these difficulties.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Shanice Morrow?","p":"Yeah."},{"d":"Thanks for seeing me, Shanice. How have you found the move here?","p":"It's okay. Quiet."},{"d":"Staff mentioned you've mostly been staying in your room. Could we talk about that and what might help?","p":"If you want."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, the move, staff concern, plan"},{"h":"Her experience","b":"Day routine, motivation, interests, enjoyment, isolation"},{"h":"Rule out other causes","b":"Depression, sedation from clozapine, ongoing psychosis, physical health"},{"h":"Explain negative symptoms","b":"Part of illness, not laziness, can improve"},{"h":"Psychological options","b":"CBT for psychosis, arts therapies, family work, social skills"},{"h":"Social options","b":"OT, day centres, recovery college, supported employment, peer support"},{"h":"Her goals","b":"What she wants to achieve, small steps"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, agreed plan, review, staff involvement"}],"bps":{"pre":"Shanice said she felt 'flat' and had no energy to get up. She once enjoyed painting and music. She denied feeling sad or having strange thoughts but felt sleepy most afternoons. She found the other residents loud. She wanted to get back to art and see her sister more.","ask":"So what's actually going to help me get going again?","lead":"There are several things we can try together, Shanice, starting with what matters to you.","bio":["Check clozapine level, FBC monitoring as per schedule; review sedation, constipation and weight","If drowsy in the afternoons, review timing or slightly reduce clozapine if level allows","Screen for depression, hypothyroidism and anaemia with bloods"],"psych":["Arts therapies, such as art therapy in a group, weekly for at least 12 weeks","CBT for psychosis, around 16 sessions, focusing on motivation and goals","Family intervention with her sister if she agrees","Social skills training and behavioural activation with the OT"],"social":["Occupational therapist to build a weekly routine with achievable steps","Recovery college courses and local art groups","IPS supported employment when ready","Care coordinator and residential staff to encourage gentle participation","PIP or benefits check"],"safety":["Contact staff or the crisis team if mood dips or old beliefs return"]}},{"sid":"S13.4","cid":"c-t-65","area":"Mania and bipolar","title":"Low after mania: what next?","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Jordan Asante is a 26-year-old man recently diagnosed with a manic episode. Six weeks ago his girlfriend brought him to A&E after two weeks of increasingly elated, reckless behaviour, and he was admitted under the Mental Health Act. He refused olanzapine and quetiapine because of weight gain worries but accepted risperidone. His mania settled over three weeks, then he quickly became depressed with marked negative thinking. Sertraline was started and increased to 150 mg daily without much benefit. He and his family are worried about his continuing low mood. Please talk through the diagnosis with him, along with the options for future management.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Jordan Asante?","p":"Yeah."},{"d":"Thanks for seeing me, Jordan. I hear things have been tough since the high spell settled.","p":"Pretty rough."},{"d":"Could we talk about what's been happening, what the diagnosis means, and options to help?","p":"Yeah, okay."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge low mood, agree plan"},{"h":"Current picture","b":"Low mood, thoughts, sleep, energy, suicide risk, current medication effects"},{"h":"The diagnosis","b":"Bipolar disorder: mania then depression, what that means"},{"h":"Medication options","b":"Stop sertraline, quetiapine, lamotrigine, lithium, weight concerns"},{"h":"Psychological options","b":"Psychoeducation, CBT for bipolar, mood diary, family work"},{"h":"Lifestyle and social","b":"Sleep routine, alcohol, drugs, work, DVLA"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicide risk, relapse signs, crisis plan"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, his choice, next steps, leaflets"}],"bps":{"pre":"Jordan said he felt empty and hopeless, slept long hours and avoided friends. He was embarrassed about things he did while high. He had brief thoughts that life was not worth it but no plan. He was frightened of weight gain and wanted a clear answer about what bipolar disorder meant for his future.","ask":"So what can I take that won't make me balloon? And will this keep happening?","lead":"Bipolar disorder is treatable, Jordan, and we can choose options that respect your worries about weight.","bio":["Taper and stop sertraline: antidepressants alone can trigger switching or mood instability","Lamotrigine: 25 mg daily for 2 weeks, 50 mg for 2 weeks, then 100-200 mg; warn about rash and stop if it appears","Long-term: lithium, aiming for 0.6-0.8 mmol/L 12 hours post-dose; baseline U&E, eGFR, TFT, calcium, weight, ECG if cardiac risk","Continue risperidone for now, with weight, glucose and lipid checks"],"psych":["Structured psychological therapy for bipolar depression, such as CBT or IPT, around 16 sessions","Group psychoeducation for bipolar disorder","Mood diary and early warning sign plan with family"],"social":["Care coordinator in the CMHT; involve his girlfriend and family with consent","DVLA: must notify after a manic episode and not drive until cleared","Work or study adjustments; Bipolar UK peer support","Advice on regular sleep and avoiding drugs and heavy drinking"],"safety":["Crisis plan with NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, 999 if in danger","Hold medicines in weekly packs while low"]}},{"sid":"S13.5","cid":"c-u-61","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Family therapy: a father's doubts","who":"Father","task":"Mr Owen Pritchard is the father of Ffion, a 15-year-old girl given a diagnosis of first-episode schizophrenia a fortnight ago and now under the early intervention service. The team has noticed frequent arguments between Ffion's parents. Mr Pritchard feels his wife is too protective and that Ffion should be pushed to leave her room and see friends. When he tries, his wife steps in, and rows follow; on those days Ffion often refuses medication and withdraws further. He worries about her future and thinks she could try harder. You are seeing him alone in clinic. Please describe what family therapy involves and respond to his worries and hopes for it. There is no need to revisit Ffion's diagnosis.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists, working with the early intervention service. Are you Mr Owen Pritchard?","p":"Yes, Ffion's dad."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Mr Pritchard. Ffion has agreed that we can share information with you about her care.","p":"Good."},{"d":"I'd like to hear your worries and then talk about family therapy. Is that okay?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent from Ffion, purpose, plan"},{"h":"His concerns","b":"Wife too soft, Ffion not trying, worries about future, rows"},{"h":"Impact at home","b":"Arguments, medication refusal, Ffion withdrawing, stress for all"},{"h":"What family therapy is","b":"Whole family, communication, problem-solving, understanding illness"},{"h":"How it works","b":"Around 10 sessions, 3-12 months, both parents, Ffion included"},{"h":"How it helps","b":"Fewer rows, less relapse, better medication taking, shared goals"},{"h":"His expectations","b":"Realistic pace, not blaming anyone, his role"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, agree to attend, other support, contact"}],"bps":{"pre":"Owen said he loved Ffion and was frightened she would 'waste her life'. He admitted shouting at his wife and pushing Ffion to go out. He noticed she refused tablets after rows but thought that was her choice. He was sceptical about therapy and worried it would blame him.","ask":"So how will sitting in a room together actually help her?","lead":"It's not about blame, Mr Pritchard; it's about helping your whole family pull in the same direction for Ffion.","bio":["Ffion continues her antipsychotic, for example aripiprazole 10 mg daily, as prescribed by the team","Team checks weight, bloods and side effects at 12 weeks and then regularly","Stable routines at home help her take medication reliably"],"psych":["Family intervention: around 10 sessions over 3-12 months with both parents and Ffion","Sessions cover understanding psychosis, communication, and problem-solving","Ffion also offered CBT for psychosis individually"],"social":["Early intervention care coordinator to link with school; phased return plan","Education welfare or home tuition while she recovers","Carers' assessment for both parents; Rethink carers' groups","Agree a joint plan for how to encourage Ffion without pressure"],"safety":["Crisis plan with the team and NHS 111 option 2 if Ffion becomes more unwell"]}},{"sid":"S13.6","cid":"c-u-62","area":"Neurodevelopmental","title":"Can he decide about sertraline?","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Liam Fairweather, aged 27, has a mild intellectual disability and lives in supported accommodation. Over recent months he has shown signs of depression and has had several sessions with the psychology team, with only small improvement. The team is now thinking about starting sertraline. He is attending the psychiatry outpatient clinic with his support worker. Please take a brief history, assess whether Mr Fairweather has capacity to consent to sertraline, explore any worries he has about it, and tell him the outcome of the capacity assessment.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Liam Fairweather?","p":"Yes, I'm Liam."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Liam. Is it okay if your support worker stays with us?","p":"Yes, Jade can stay."},{"d":"Today I want to hear about your mood and talk about a tablet that might help. I'll use simple words.","p":"Okay."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, support worker present with consent, simple language"},{"h":"Brief history","b":"Mood, sleep, appetite, interest, worries, psychology sessions"},{"h":"Explain sertraline","b":"What it is, how it helps, how long it takes"},{"h":"Understand","b":"Can Liam explain what the tablet is for, using pictures if needed"},{"h":"Retain and weigh","b":"Remember, consider benefits and side effects, alternatives"},{"h":"Communicate","b":"Express decision in any way"},{"h":"His concerns","b":"Side effects, being different, stopping, family views"},{"h":"Outcome and close","b":"Capacity outcome, his choice, next steps, review"}],"bps":{"pre":"Liam said he felt sad most days and stopped going to his drama group. He liked his psychologist but still felt down. He understood the tablet might help his mood, remembered side effects like feeling sick, and said he wanted to try it. He worried it would make him 'weird'. He had capacity.","ask":"So will it change me? What happens next?","lead":"It won't change who you are, Liam; it may help you feel more like yourself.","bio":["Start sertraline 25 mg daily for 1-2 weeks, then 50 mg if tolerated","Review at 2 weeks for agitation, stomach upset or worse mood","Easy-read leaflet; support staff to watch for side effects"],"psych":["Continue sessions with psychology, adapted CBT or behavioural activation","Use pictures and simple mood scales to track progress","Involve family or carers in plan with his agreement"],"social":["Community learning disability team and named nurse","Supported living staff to help with daily routine and activities","Reasonable adjustments: longer appointments, health passport","Encourage return to drama group or similar activities"],"safety":["Tell staff or call NHS 111 option 2 if he feels worse or has thoughts of harm"]}},{"sid":"S13.7","cid":"c-s-65","area":"Perinatal","title":"New baby and worse OCD","who":"Patient","task":"Mrs Sinead Farrell is a 35-year-old woman who had her son, Arlo, six weeks ago. She has had obsessive-compulsive disorder since her late twenties and has had CBT before; she was on clomipramine for several years then moved to sertraline three years ago as clomipramine made her drowsy. Since Arlo's birth her symptoms have worsened, and her GP has asked the perinatal mental health team to see her. Please explore her obsessional thoughts and compulsive rituals in detail and find out how they are affecting her day-to-day life.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists, working with the perinatal service. Are you Sinead Farrell?","p":"Yes, that's me."},{"d":"Congratulations on Arlo, Sinead. Your GP said some old worries have come back since he was born.","p":"They're worse than ever."},{"d":"Could you tell me what's been happening? There's no judgement here.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, congratulate, non-judgemental stance, plan"},{"h":"Obsessions","b":"Contamination worries, intrusive thoughts, harm images, content, frequency"},{"h":"Compulsions","b":"Sterilising bottles, washing, checking locks and switches, counting, time spent"},{"h":"Resistance and insight","b":"Recognises irrational, tries to resist, anxiety when stopping"},{"h":"Impact","b":"Caring for Arlo, sleep, relationships, daily tasks, time lost"},{"h":"Past OCD and treatment","b":"Onset at 29, CBT, clomipramine, sertraline dose and adherence"},{"h":"Comorbidity screen","b":"Depression, anxiety, psychosis, alcohol"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Thoughts of harm, distress, suicide, avoidance of baby"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, plan, support, follow-up"}],"bps":{"pre":"Sinead described constant worries that germs would make Arlo seriously ill. She sterilised each bottle six times and recently began checking locks and switches repeatedly. She knew the thoughts were excessive but felt unbearable anxiety if she stopped. She was exhausted and late with feeds. She was not depressed and had no thoughts of harming Arlo.","ask":"Is there anything that can help me get on top of this again?","lead":"Yes, Sinead; OCD responds well to treatment, even after a baby.","bio":["Review sertraline dose; increase gradually to 150-200 mg daily if needed for OCD","Sertraline is compatible with breastfeeding if she wishes","Review in 4-6 weeks for response and side effects"],"psych":["CBT with exposure and response prevention, around 10-20 sessions via perinatal team or Talking Therapies","Psychoeducation about postnatal OCD and intrusive thoughts","Involve partner in supporting response prevention at home"],"social":["Perinatal mental health team follow-up","Inform health visitor and GP","OCD-UK and Maternal OCD resources","Help with childcare or practical support from family"],"safety":["Crisis plan: NHS 111 option 2 if she feels overwhelmed or has harm thoughts"]}},{"sid":"S14.1","cid":"c-u-63","area":"Psychosis and schizophrenia","title":"Starting clozapine at home","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Aisha Rahman is a 32-year-old woman with schizophrenia that has not responded to two antipsychotics. She takes olanzapine 20 mg at night, yet she still hears derogatory voices and believes neighbours are plotting against her. At her last clinic review the team suggested clozapine, started in the community with daily input from the Home Treatment Team. She said no at the time, but has now told her care coordinator she is ready to give it a go and has come to the CMHT today. Please speak to her, explain what clozapine is, how it would be started at home and the side effects she needs to know about, and answer her questions.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Aisha Rahman?","p":"Yes, that's me."},{"d":"Thanks for coming in, Aisha. Your care coordinator tells me you're now thinking about clozapine. That's a big decision, and a brave one.","p":"I just want the voices to stop."},{"d":"Let's start with how things are now, then I'll talk you through clozapine step by step.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, praise her decision, agree the plan"},{"h":"How things are now","b":"Voices, persecutory beliefs, olanzapine adherence, mood, self-harm and risk"},{"h":"Why clozapine","b":"Treatment-resistant schizophrenia, two failed drugs, best evidence, chance of improvement"},{"h":"Before starting","b":"FBC, ECG, weight, glucose, lipids, smoking status, monitoring service registration"},{"h":"Starting at home","b":"12.5 mg first dose, slow rise, daily HTT visits, pulse, BP, temperature"},{"h":"Blood tests","b":"Weekly 18 weeks, fortnightly to one year, then monthly; why neutrophils matter"},{"h":"Side effects","b":"Constipation, drooling, sleepiness, weight, fever or sore throat, chest pain"},{"h":"Practical rules","b":"Missed 48 hours restart, smoking changes levels, no driving early on"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, leaflet, HTT number, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"Aisha described voices calling her worthless most evenings and a belief that next door listen through the wall. She has taken olanzapine faithfully for a year but has gained 9 kg and still feels hounded. She denied plans to harm herself. After hearing the voices were unlikely to settle with another ordinary antipsychotic, she leaned forward.","ask":"If I do try this clozapine, what actually happens, and what else can help me?","lead":"Clozapine is the main step, but it works best alongside talking therapy and practical help, so let me go through all three.","bio":["Baseline before day 1: FBC with neutrophils, ECG, weight, BMI, waist, HbA1c, lipids, U&E, LFT, troponin and CRP; register with the clozapine monitoring service","Clozapine 12.5 mg once or twice on day 1, then rise by 25-50 mg a day as tolerated towards 300 mg/day over 2-4 weeks; olanzapine reduced gradually as clozapine rises","HTT checks pulse, BP and temperature daily for the first 2 weeks; troponin and CRP weekly for 4 weeks to pick up myocarditis","FBC weekly for 18 weeks, fortnightly to 1 year, then monthly; a plasma level around 6-8 weeks aiming for at least 350 micrograms/L","Constipation can be dangerous (MHRA warning): start a laxative such as senna plus docusate early and report no bowel opening for 3 days; hyoscine 300 micrograms at night for drooling","Sore throat, fever or flu-like illness means an urgent FBC the same day; if more than 48 hours of doses are missed, titration restarts from 12.5 mg"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis from a clinical psychologist, at least 16 weekly one-to-one sessions, to make sense of and cope with the voices","Family intervention, around 10 sessions over 3-12 months, with her and her mother, covering early signs and reducing stress at home","Psychoeducation sessions with her HTT nurse during titration on what clozapine does and how to spot side effects","Relapse prevention plan written with her care coordinator once clozapine is settled"],"social":["Home Treatment Team visiting daily during titration, then handing back to her CMHT care coordinator","Clozapine clinic and pharmacist for blood results, dispensing and interaction checks","Smoking cessation service, warning that stopping or starting smoking changes clozapine levels and needs a level check","Healthy lifestyle and weight management group; benefits advice if she is off work","Carers' assessment for her mother, who will help notice side effects"],"safety":["Crisis plan with HTT and crisis line numbers; 999 or A&E for chest pain, breathlessness, fits, high fever or severe abdominal pain"]}},{"sid":"S14.2","cid":"c-s-48","area":"Forensic","title":"Son's discharge: a safety plan for mum","who":"Mother","task":"Mrs Patricia Okafor is the mother of Daniel Okafor, a 23-year-old man with newly diagnosed schizophrenia who has been on your low secure unit for several months. Daniel was admitted after he attacked his mother while unwell, at a time when he was smoking cannabis and had stopped his olanzapine. Since then he has been established on clozapine at a therapeutic dose, his psychotic symptoms have resolved and he is working well with the substance misuse team. He will soon move to supported accommodation, and the team has already met Mrs Okafor about his relapse signature. Daniel has agreed that you may talk openly with her. Please briefly check what she understands about his illness, explain his follow-up after discharge, and agree a risk management and safety plan with her.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists on Daniel's unit. Are you Mrs Okafor?","p":"Yes. Patricia."},{"d":"Thank you for coming, Patricia. Daniel has said I can speak openly with you. I know the last year has been frightening.","p":"It has. I'm worried about him coming out."},{"d":"That's exactly what I'd like to talk about: his plan and how we keep you both safe.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Consent confirmed, acknowledge the assault, agree the plan"},{"h":"Her understanding","b":"Schizophrenia, what caused the attack, cannabis, stopping olanzapine, her beliefs"},{"h":"Her own wellbeing","b":"Fear, trauma symptoms, support, contact she wants, any threats since"},{"h":"Treatment so far","b":"Clozapine, symptoms resolved, drug service engagement, insight, leave going well"},{"h":"Follow-up after discharge","b":"Section 117 aftercare, CPA, care coordinator, clozapine clinic, supported housing staff"},{"h":"Early warning signs","b":"Poor sleep, isolating, suspicion of her, missed clozapine, cannabis smell"},{"h":"Safety plan for her","b":"Who to ring, when to call 999, visits in public places first"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, carers' assessment, crisis numbers, written plan copy"}],"bps":{"pre":"Patricia said she understood Daniel had 'a chemical imbalance' but still felt the cannabis was mostly to blame. She admitted she sleeps with her bedroom door locked since the attack and dreads him turning up unannounced. She was relieved to hear he has been well on clozapine and drug-free on leave, then asked quietly about the future.","ask":"What will actually keep him well, and keep me safe, once he's out?","lead":"There are several layers of support around Daniel, and some just for you, so let me walk you through them.","bio":["Clozapine continued at his current dose, collected from the clozapine clinic, with FBC monthly and a plasma level if he stops or starts smoking","Random urine drug screens agreed with Daniel as part of his aftercare plan","Supported accommodation staff observe or prompt clozapine and alert the team if a dose is missed; more than 48 hours missed means re-titration","Monitoring of weight, glucose and lipids at least yearly, and constipation checks, through the clozapine clinic and GP"],"psych":["Relapse prevention work with his psychologist, refining his written relapse signature, which Patricia will hold a copy of","Ongoing work with the community drug and alcohol team, including cannabis relapse prevention","Family intervention sessions, around 10 over several months, if both agree, to rebuild trust safely","Talking Therapies or trauma-focused CBT for Patricia herself, through her GP or self-referral"],"social":["Discharge under Section 117 aftercare, with a CPA review, a named care coordinator in the community forensic team and fixed review dates","Supported accommodation staff with 24-hour cover who know his warning signs","Carers' assessment for Patricia from the local authority, plus contact details for a carers' support group","Contact planned at her pace: daytime visits in public places first, no keys to her home, nothing unannounced","Victim liaison or police contact if any threats are made; MAPPA discussion if the team judges it needed"],"safety":["She rings the care coordinator or out-of-hours crisis line if warning signs appear","999 straight away if she feels threatened or he arrives unwell and agitated"]}},{"sid":"S14.3","cid":"c-t-66","area":"Eating disorders","title":"Rapid weight gain in anorexia: why?","who":"Community nurse","task":"Ms Chloe Whitfield is a 24-year-old woman with anorexia nervosa who relapsed earlier this year and has been with the community eating disorder team for a month. Her BMI was 13.5 at referral. Two days ago a locum nurse visited her at home; she felt unwell, her blood pressure was 90/60 and her pulse 60. Her weight had been rising slowly but has gone up 3 kg in the past week. Today a different colleague recorded a blood pressure of 100/70. The community nurse, Ms Ruth Adeyemi, has asked to talk to you about these changes. Please speak to the nurse, work out the likely cause of the patient's physical symptoms and agree a management plan.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Ruth from the eating disorder team?","p":"Yes, thanks for calling back."},{"d":"No problem. I understand you're worried about Chloe's physical health and a sudden weight jump.","p":"Three kilos in a week. It doesn't feel right."},{"d":"Agreed. Could you take me through what you've seen, then we'll make a plan together?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Introductions, the concern, agree to gather details then plan"},{"h":"Her symptoms","b":"Swelling, breathlessness, palpitations, weakness, confusion, dizziness, chest pain"},{"h":"Eating and weight","b":"Meal plan changes, intake rise, water loading, purging, laxatives"},{"h":"Observations and bloods","b":"BP, pulse, temperature, sitting-standing BP, phosphate, potassium, magnesium, glucose"},{"h":"Likely cause","b":"Refeeding syndrome with fluid retention; differentials heart failure, water loading"},{"h":"Risk","b":"MEED red flags, cardiac risk, self-harm, low BMI"},{"h":"Immediate plan","b":"Same-day bloods, ECG, medical review, thiamine, electrolyte replacement, dietitian"},{"h":"Hand over and close","b":"Summary, who does what, recheck times, inform consultant and GP"}],"bps":{"pre":"Ruth explained the new meal plan began ten days ago and Chloe has kept to it closely. Chloe has had puffy ankles, feels weak and slightly breathless on stairs, and had palpitations last night. There is no purging. Nobody has checked bloods since the plan began. You explained the picture fits refeeding syndrome with fluid retention, and Ruth asked what to do next.","ask":"Right, so what should happen for her today?","lead":"This needs action today, so let's split it into medical steps, support for Chloe, and who coordinates it.","bio":["Same-day bloods: U&E, phosphate, magnesium, calcium, glucose, LFT, FBC; ECG for QTc, rhythm and bradycardia; sitting and standing BP, temperature","Medical admission to a general ward if phosphate, potassium or magnesium are low, ECG is abnormal or she is breathless, following the MEED (2022) guidance","Oral thiamine 100 mg three times a day plus vitamin B complex; IV Pabrinex if admitted","Phosphate replacement (e.g. Phosphate Sandoz 1-2 tablets twice a day per local protocol), potassium and magnesium as needed, with daily bloods for a week then twice weekly","Dietitian reviews the meal plan: keep feeding going, do not stop it, but adjust the rate while electrolytes are corrected; daily weight and fluid balance"],"psych":["Calm explanation to Chloe that the swelling is fluid, not fat, to stop her cutting back","Continue her eating-disorder-focused therapy (for example MANTRA or CBT-ED, around 20-40 sessions) with her therapist","Extra support at meal times from the team or family to reduce anxiety and compensatory behaviour"],"social":["Community eating disorder team links directly with the acute medical team and inpatient liaison psychiatry","Consultant and GP informed today; shared physical monitoring plan written into her care plan","Family or flatmate involved, with her consent, to help with meals and notice deterioration","Feedback to the locum service so abnormal observations are escalated the same day"],"safety":["999 or A&E for chest pain, collapse, fits, confusion or marked breathlessness","Ruth to recheck observations tomorrow and escalate any drop in pulse or BP"]}},{"sid":"S14.4","cid":"c-t-67","area":"Neurodevelopmental","title":"Adult ADHD: what treatment now?","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Kwame Asante is a 25-year-old man in his final year of a law degree. He has recently been diagnosed with ADHD and has come to clinic to talk about medication. Some months ago he saw his GP because he could not concentrate when studying and had failed several assessments, even though his tutors said he understood the material well. A friend who was diagnosed with ADHD as a child noticed the similarities and encouraged him to seek help. Please do not repeat the history. Please speak to him and agree a treatment plan, including medication, monitoring and practical support.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Kwame Asante?","p":"Yes, hi."},{"d":"Good to meet you, Kwame. You've already had your diagnosis, so today is about what we can do to help.","p":"Great, I've read a bit online."},{"d":"Let's hear what you've read and what you hope for, then I'll go through the options.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, diagnosis confirmed, agree focus on treatment"},{"h":"His goals and concerns","b":"Studying, failed assessments, expectations, worries about tablets, dependence"},{"h":"Before medication","b":"Heart history, family sudden death, BP, pulse, weight, height, substances"},{"h":"First-line medication","b":"Lisdexamfetamine 30 mg morning, weekly rises, maximum 70 mg"},{"h":"Side effects and monitoring","b":"Appetite, sleep, anxiety, BP and pulse each change, weight"},{"h":"Rules and alternatives","b":"Controlled drug, no sharing, DVLA, methylphenidate or atomoxetine if needed"},{"h":"Non-drug help","b":"Psychoeducation, CBT for ADHD, coaching, university adjustments"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, review 2-4 weeks, shared care, leaflet"}],"bps":{"pre":"Kwame said he can read a page five times and remember none of it, and that deadlines arrive before he has started. He has no heart problems, nobody in the family died suddenly, his BP was 118/74 and he drinks lightly at weekends. He has read about 'study drugs' and is curious but nervous.","ask":"So what can I actually take, and will it make me a different person?","lead":"Medication helps most people with ADHD focus without changing who they are, and it works best with some practical help alongside.","bio":["Baseline BP, pulse, weight and height recorded; ECG only if there is heart disease, a murmur or a family history of sudden death","Lisdexamfetamine 30 mg each morning, raised by 20 mg a week to a maximum of 70 mg, reviewing benefit and side effects at each step","If not tolerated or ineffective after 6 weeks, methylphenidate modified-release (e.g. 18 mg morning, titrated to up to 108 mg); atomoxetine 40 mg rising to 80 mg if stimulants unsuitable","BP and pulse at every dose change and then every 6 months; weight every 6 months; warn about poor appetite, insomnia, headache and anxiety","Controlled drug: 30 days per prescription, never shared or sold, avoid cocaine and excess alcohol; GP takes over under shared care once dose is stable"],"psych":["Psychoeducation sessions with the ADHD nurse on how ADHD affects study, time and organisation","CBT for adult ADHD, often 12-15 weekly sessions, focusing on planning, procrastination and self-esteem","ADHD coaching or study skills sessions through the university to break work into short, timed tasks"],"social":["University disability service: register for Disabled Students' Allowance, extra time and a quiet room for assessments, and deadline flexibility","Personal tutor told, with his consent, so earlier failed modules can be reviewed","DVLA: he must tell the DVLA if ADHD or his medication affects his driving; avoid driving if drowsy or unwell","Letter for travel abroad with his controlled medication; ADHD peer support groups such as ADHD UK"]}},{"sid":"S14.5","cid":"c-t-68","area":"Old age","title":"Mum's memory, whisky and driving","who":"Son","task":"Mrs Margaret Doyle is an 81-year-old widow referred to the memory clinic by her GP with short-term memory problems. A specialist nurse found she scored 50/100 on the ACE-III, mainly losing points in memory and visuospatial tasks; she looked unkempt and smelled of stale alcohol. She was told not to drive but refused. Her GP then found she has been drinking half a bottle or more of whisky daily for months, with raised ALT and GGT. She again refused to stop driving or cut down. Her son, Mr Stephen Doyle, has phoned the clinic because she is furious and he wants to know what has gone on. She has agreed that you may discuss this with him. Please explain the findings so far, address his concerns, and discuss immediate and longer-term management of her driving risk.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists at the memory clinic. Am I speaking with Stephen Doyle?","p":"Yes. What's going on with Mum?"},{"d":"Thank you for calling, Stephen. Your mum has agreed I can talk with you. I can hear this has been upsetting.","p":"She's livid. Says you're taking her car."},{"d":"Let me explain what we've found and why, then we'll plan together.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Confirm identity and consent, acknowledge anger, agree the plan"},{"h":"His concerns and observations","b":"Memory at home, self-care, drinking he has noticed, driving incidents"},{"h":"What we found","b":"ACE-III 50/100, memory and visuospatial weakness, unkempt, alcohol smell"},{"h":"The alcohol picture","b":"Half a bottle of whisky daily, raised ALT and GGT, withdrawal risk"},{"h":"What it might mean","b":"Dementia, alcohol-related brain damage or both; more tests needed"},{"h":"Driving now","b":"Unsafe, stop immediately, legal duty to tell DVLA, doctor may inform"},{"h":"Longer term","b":"Alcohol help, MRI, repeat ACE-III after abstinence, transport, LPA"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, consultant review, leaflet, contact numbers"}],"bps":{"pre":"Stephen said his mum has missed bills, scraped the car twice this spring and once got lost driving to his house. He had no idea about the whisky until a neighbour mentioned the bottles. He now understood that her drinking and her memory are both serious, and that she cannot drive safely. He sounded tired and worried.","ask":"So what happens now? How do we stop her driving and help her?","lead":"We need to deal with safety first, then the drinking and her memory, and you won't be doing this alone.","bio":["Oral thiamine 100 mg three times a day from now, as she is at risk of Wernicke's encephalopathy","No abrupt stop at home: a planned, supervised alcohol withdrawal, ideally with oxazepam or lorazepam given her age and liver results, via the community alcohol team or admission","Further tests: B12, folate, TFT, calcium, glucose, FBC, clotting and liver screen; MRI or CT head","Repeat ACE-III after 2-3 months of abstinence; hold any acetylcholinesterase inhibitor until the diagnosis is clearer","Medication review with her GP for anything sedating that worsens memory or driving"],"psych":["Motivational interviewing with an older adults' alcohol worker, weekly at first, building on her wish to stay independent","Cognitive stimulation therapy group, 14 sessions twice a week, if dementia is confirmed","Clear, simple explanation to her of why driving is unsafe, repeated by the consultant in a joint meeting with Stephen"],"social":["Driving: she must stop now and tell the DVLA; if she refuses and still drives, the doctor tells her then informs the DVLA under GMC guidance","Practical steps with her consent: keys kept elsewhere, car sold or disabled, bus pass, taxi account or community transport","Adult social care assessment for self-care, meals and shopping; carers' assessment for Stephen","Lasting Power of Attorney for health and finances while she still has capacity","Age UK and Alzheimer's Society support; safeguarding referral if she remains a danger on the road"],"safety":["If she drives while unsafe, police can be called; 999 for a fit, confusion or collapse during withdrawal"]}},{"sid":"S14.6","cid":"c-s-64","area":"Perinatal","title":"Quetiapine in pregnancy and diabetes risk","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Hannah Kowalski is a 35-year-old woman with bipolar I disorder. Her last episode, with psychotic features, was six years ago. She has stayed well on quetiapine XL 400 mg at night for several years, and took it throughout her first pregnancy; her son Theo is now three. In that pregnancy she developed gestational diabetes. At 15 weeks into this pregnancy she has asked the perinatal mental health clinic to review her medication, because she is worried about quetiapine in pregnancy and about getting gestational diabetes again. Please talk through the risks with her and agree a plan together.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, a psychiatrist with the perinatal team. Are you Hannah Kowalski?","p":"Yes. Hi."},{"d":"Congratulations on the pregnancy, Hannah. It's sensible to review your medication now.","p":"Thanks. I'm just worried about the diabetes again."},{"d":"That makes sense. Could I hear how you're doing first, then we'll weigh up the options together?","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, congratulate, agree plan"},{"h":"How she is now","b":"Mood, sleep, early warning signs, pregnancy so far, self-harm risk"},{"h":"Her worries","b":"Quetiapine and the baby, gestational diabetes last time, weight, breastfeeding"},{"h":"Risk of stopping","b":"High relapse risk, especially after birth; postpartum psychosis risk"},{"h":"Quetiapine in pregnancy","b":"No clear malformation risk, glucose and weight risk, neonatal effects"},{"h":"Options weighed","b":"Continue at lowest effective dose, avoid switching mid-pregnancy, no valproate"},{"h":"Diabetes plan","b":"Early glucose tolerance test, home glucose monitoring, diabetes antenatal clinic"},{"h":"After the birth","b":"Pre-birth planning meeting, sleep protection, breastfeeding, mother and baby unit"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, shared plan with midwife and GP, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"Hannah said her mood has been steady, though tiredness has crept in and she sometimes misses her evening dose. Last pregnancy she needed insulin from 28 weeks. She is frightened of being unwell again after the birth, as Theo's arrival was hard. You explained that stopping quetiapine now carries a real risk of relapse.","ask":"So should I stay on it, and what can be done about the diabetes?","lead":"For most women in your position staying on quetiapine is the safer choice, and we can work hard on the diabetes side.","bio":["Continue quetiapine XL at the lowest dose that keeps her well (currently 400 mg at night); do not switch mid-pregnancy as relapse risk outweighs the benefit","Early oral glucose tolerance test as soon as possible after booking (or home glucose monitoring), repeated at 24-28 weeks if normal, per NICE","If gestational diabetes recurs: diet first, then metformin or insulin through the joint diabetes antenatal clinic","Weight, BP and HbA1c checked each trimester; extra growth scans arranged by obstetrics","Birth in a unit with neonatal cover; baby observed for sleepiness or jitteriness for the first days; breastfeeding on quetiapine is generally acceptable"],"psych":["Psychoeducation with the perinatal nurse on early warning signs and the high postpartum risk in bipolar I","Relapse prevention plan agreed with her and her partner, including a sleep plan","Dietitian-led healthy eating sessions in pregnancy to limit weight gain and glucose rise"],"social":["Perinatal mental health team keyworker seeing her monthly, then more often from 32 weeks","Pre-birth planning meeting around 32 weeks with midwife, obstetrician, health visitor, GP and partner; written perinatal plan in her notes","Partner to help with night feeds (expressed milk or formula) so she gets 6 hours' unbroken sleep","Mother and baby unit identified in advance; specialist midwife and health visitor aware"],"safety":["Crisis plan: perinatal team or crisis line if sleep drops or mood lifts; 999 if confused, psychotic or unsafe after birth"]}},{"sid":"S14.7","cid":"c-s-63","area":"Perinatal","title":"Postnatal depression not getting better","who":"Health visitor","task":"Mrs Priya Nair is a 32-year-old woman whose daughter, Anya, is now five months old. Four weeks after the birth she was referred to the perinatal mental health team with low mood and diagnosed with a depressive episode. Sertraline, started by her GP, was increased step by step to 200 mg daily, and she has now taken that dose for eight weeks. She has also completed a course of intensive CBT with the team. Neither has helped much. She has no past psychiatric history, no family history, and had never taken psychiatric medication before. Her health visitor, Ms Gemma Holt, is concerned that her mood is not lifting. Please talk with the health visitor about what may be keeping the depression going, and recommend biological, psychological and social interventions.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, a psychiatrist with the perinatal team. Is that Gemma, Priya's health visitor?","p":"Yes, thanks for ringing."},{"d":"Thanks for raising this, Gemma. I gather Priya still isn't improving despite treatment.","p":"No, she seems as flat as ever."},{"d":"Let's go through what you're seeing at home, then look at what we can change.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Introductions, her concern, agree plan"},{"h":"What she sees now","b":"Mood, sleep, appetite, bonding, care of Anya, hopelessness"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts, harm to baby, neglect, safeguarding"},{"h":"Treatment so far","b":"Sertraline 200 mg eight weeks, adherence, side effects, CBT course"},{"h":"Perpetuating factors","b":"Sleep loss, feeding, relationship, abuse, money, isolation, alcohol, birth trauma"},{"h":"Physical causes","b":"Thyroid, anaemia, vitamin D, B12, postpartum thyroiditis"},{"h":"Recommended plan","b":"Medication change, parent-infant work, practical and family support"},{"h":"Hand over and close","b":"Summary, who does what, review date, consultant, crisis numbers"}],"bps":{"pre":"Gemma described Priya as tearful and slow, rarely leaving the flat. Anya feeds every two hours at night and Priya is breastfeeding alone because her husband works nights. Her own mother is in Kerala. Gemma noticed Priya rarely smiles at Anya. There have been arguments about money, no alcohol and no thoughts of harming herself or the baby. Gemma asked what could be done.","ask":"What else can we try for her?","lead":"There are several things we can change at once: her medication, the right kind of therapy, and more hands-on support at home.","bio":["Bloods: TFT for postpartum thyroiditis, FBC and ferritin, vitamin D, B12 and folate; check she is actually taking the sertraline","Switch from sertraline to mirtazapine 15 mg at night, rising to 30 mg after 2 weeks, cross-tapering; helps sleep and is generally compatible with breastfeeding","If still no response: augmentation with quetiapine 25-50 mg at night or a different antidepressant class, after consultant review and checking infant safety","Infant monitored by the health visitor for sleepiness and feeding while she breastfeeds on any new medication"],"psych":["Parent-infant work such as Video Interaction Guidance, around 6 sessions, to strengthen her bond with Anya","Interpersonal therapy (around 16 weekly sessions) focusing on role change and isolation, as CBT has not helped","Partner joins sessions to understand postnatal depression and share the load"],"social":["Health visitor visits weekly for now and liaises with the perinatal team","Night support: husband or family covering one night feed with expressed milk; Home-Start volunteer for daytime help","Children's centre groups and PANDAS or local peer support to reduce isolation","Benefits and money advice; ask directly about domestic abuse when she is alone","Referral to a mother and baby unit if she deteriorates; safeguarding referral if Anya's care slips"],"safety":["Crisis plan shared with Priya and her husband; 999 if she voices thoughts of harming herself or Anya"]}},{"sid":"S14.8","cid":"c-s-62","area":"Perinatal","title":"Valproate and planning a pregnancy","who":"Patient","task":"Ms Sophie Turnbull is a 33-year-old hairdresser with bipolar I disorder, referred by her GP to the perinatal mental health service for advice before trying to conceive. Her illness began at 27 with depression. She later had two manic episodes with psychotic features, one fuelled by cannabis and cocaine, and a severe depression with suicidal thoughts; each needed detention under the Mental Health Act. Risperidone and olanzapine worked, but she stopped olanzapine because of weight gain. Aripiprazole failed, and lithium caused urinary urgency and incontinence. Sodium valproate 1 g at night with sertraline 100 mg daily has kept her completely well and drug-free for three years. She knows the valproate risks, has yearly risk reviews and uses a contraceptive implant. She married last year and wants a baby. A medical student has already taken her history. Please agree a plan for her mental health before, during and after pregnancy, without retaking the history.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, a psychiatrist with the perinatal team. Are you Sophie Turnbull?","p":"Yes, hi."},{"d":"Congratulations on getting married, Sophie, and thank you for coming before trying for a baby. That's exactly the right time.","p":"I knew the valproate would be a problem."},{"d":"You're right, so let's plan this carefully together.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, praise planning ahead, agree plan"},{"h":"Her understanding and wishes","b":"Valproate risks known, timing hopes, partner's views, fears of relapse"},{"h":"Why valproate must change","b":"Birth defects about 1 in 10, developmental problems up to 4 in 10"},{"h":"Her relapse risk","b":"Three detentions, psychosis, high postpartum risk, sleep loss, cannabis"},{"h":"Alternative medication","b":"Risperidone or quetiapine, slow cross-taper, keep implant meanwhile"},{"h":"Before conceiving","b":"Stable 3-6 months on new drug, folic acid, sertraline continues"},{"h":"Pregnancy and birth","b":"Perinatal team, 32-week planning meeting, sleep protection, mother and baby unit"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, written plan, GP letter, crisis line"}],"bps":{"pre":"Sophie said she has felt the best of her life on valproate and is scared that changing it will make her ill again. She and her husband hope to start trying within a year. She has stayed off cannabis and cocaine. She understood clearly that valproate cannot continue into pregnancy and that coming off it carries some risk.","ask":"So what would I take instead, and how do we keep me well?","lead":"We can switch you safely before you conceive, and build a plan around the pregnancy and the months after.","bio":["Keep the contraceptive implant until she has been stable for 3-6 months on the new medicine; never stop valproate suddenly","Start risperidone 1 mg at night, raising to 2-4 mg as needed, while valproate is reduced gradually over 4-8 weeks (or quetiapine if she prefers); risperidone can raise prolactin and affect fertility, so check prolactin","Continue sertraline 100 mg, which has reassuring pregnancy data; folic acid 400 micrograms daily from 3 months before trying, 5 mg if any anticonvulsant is still needed","Monitor weight, glucose and lipids at baseline, 12 weeks and in pregnancy; early glucose testing given antipsychotic use","Consider restarting or increasing the antipsychotic immediately after delivery to protect against postpartum relapse"],"psych":["Psychoeducation with her and her husband about the high postpartum relapse risk and early warning signs","Written relapse prevention plan with a perinatal nurse, reviewed each trimester","Pre-conception counselling sessions with the perinatal team, 2-3 visits during the switch","Ongoing support to stay off cannabis and cocaine, with the drug service if needed"],"social":["Perinatal mental health team keyworker from now, with monthly contact during the switch","Shared care with her GP, midwife and obstetrician; written perinatal plan in her maternity notes","Pre-birth planning meeting at about 32 weeks; mother and baby unit place identified in advance","Husband and family to protect her night-time sleep after the birth; employer told about appointments","Annual valproate risk acknowledgement form updated as the switch proceeds"],"safety":["Crisis plan: perinatal team or 111 option 2 if sleep drops or mood rises; 999 if psychotic or unsafe"]}},{"sid":"S15.1","cid":"c-s-20","area":"Medication, emergencies and side effects","title":"Shaky and stiff on haloperidol","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Liam O'Connor is a 27-year-old man seen in A&E, where you are the junior doctor on duty. The nurses ask you to see him because he is trembling and his arms and legs feel stiff. He has been taking haloperidol 5 mg twice a day. Please ask him about his symptoms, carry out a focused physical assessment for movement side effects, explain what you have found and agree a plan.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Liam O'Connor?","p":"Yeah. I can't stop shaking."},{"d":"I'm sorry, Liam, that sounds horrible. I'd like to ask a few questions, then look at how your muscles are moving. Is that okay?","p":"Fine."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, empathy, consent, chaperone offered"},{"h":"Symptoms","b":"Tremor, stiffness, onset, recent dose change, missed or extra doses"},{"h":"Other movement effects","b":"Neck or eye spasms, restlessness, mouth movements, swallowing, falls"},{"h":"Rule out emergencies","b":"Fever, sweating, confusion, rigidity everywhere: NMS; drugs, other medicines"},{"h":"Physical assessment","b":"Observations, resting tremor, cogwheel rigidity, slowness, gait, glabellar tap"},{"h":"Mouth and face","b":"Tongue in mouth, lip smacking, grimacing, eye movements"},{"h":"Explain findings","b":"Drug-induced parkinsonism from haloperidol, reversible, plan"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Summary, teach-back, plan, team informed, safety advice"}],"bps":{"pre":"Liam said his dose doubled ten days ago after a relapse. His hands shake at rest, he walks slowly and his arms feel heavy. You found a coarse resting tremor, cogwheel rigidity at both wrists and a shuffling gait. His temperature was 36.8 and he was fully alert. You explained this is drug-induced parkinsonism.","ask":"So what can you do to stop this?","lead":"This is a known side effect and it is very treatable, so let me explain the steps.","bio":["Check CK, U&E and temperature to exclude neuroleptic malignant syndrome; ECG for QTc on haloperidol","Procyclidine 5 mg up to three times a day short term for parkinsonism (or 5-10 mg IM/IV if acute dystonia); review after 1-2 weeks","Discuss with his team reducing haloperidol to the lowest effective dose or switching to aripiprazole 10 mg daily, which has fewer movement effects","Rate severity with the Simpson-Angus scale and recheck after any change"],"psych":["Explain plainly that the shaking is a side effect, not a new illness, to protect adherence","Shared decision-making about future antipsychotic choice with his care coordinator","Brief written information on movement side effects and what to report"],"social":["Inform his CMHT care coordinator and consultant today with a discharge letter","GP letter on the procyclidine plan and that it should not continue long term","Advise against driving or operating machinery while stiff and slow; tell the DVLA if symptoms persist"],"safety":["Return or call 999 for high fever, confusion, sweating with rigidity, choking or neck spasm"]}},{"sid":"S15.2","cid":"c-s-03","area":"Medication, emergencies and side effects","title":"Restless after new tablets","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Daniel Hughes is a 32-year-old man; he has come to A&E asking to see a psychiatrist. You are the on-call psychiatry doctor. The nurses report he is pacing, irritable and agitated. The A&E doctor tells you that his GP prescribed some new tablets three days ago, and Mr Hughes is convinced his problems began after he started them. He has a history of anxiety. Please speak to him, explain what has probably happened and agree a plan.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Daniel Hughes?","p":"Yes. Finally. I need help."},{"d":"I can see you're really uncomfortable, Daniel. Feel free to stand or walk while we talk.","p":"I can't sit still anyway."},{"d":"Tell me about these new tablets and how you've felt since.","p":""}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, acknowledge distress, let him move, agree plan"},{"h":"The tablets","b":"Name, dose, why prescribed, when started, still taking or stopped"},{"h":"Inner restlessness","b":"Urge to move legs, unable to sit or lie still, onset"},{"h":"Other causes","b":"Anxiety, drugs, alcohol, caffeine, thyroid, restless legs, psychosis"},{"h":"Risk","b":"Suicidal thoughts, anger at GP, aggression, driving"},{"h":"Look for signs","b":"Pacing, rocking, crossing legs, tremor, rigidity, Barnes scale"},{"h":"Explain akathisia","b":"Antipsychotic side effect, not his fault, reversible, link to anger"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Plan, teach-back, GP letter, follow-up, crisis numbers"}],"bps":{"pre":"Daniel showed you the box: trifluoperazine 5 mg three times a day, given for anxiety at work. After a day he felt a horrible inner urge to keep moving and flushed the rest away yesterday, but still cannot sit still. He rocked from foot to foot throughout. He has used propranolol for panic in the past. He denied drugs and suicidal thoughts. You explained this is akathisia.","ask":"So how do I get rid of this, and what about my anxiety?","lead":"Akathisia usually settles within days of stopping the tablet, and there are things that help in the meantime.","bio":["Do not restart trifluoperazine; record akathisia as an adverse reaction in his GP and hospital notes","Propranolol 10 mg two or three times a day, increased if needed up to 30-80 mg a day, if no asthma, bradycardia or low BP","If still distressed, a short course of diazepam 2-5 mg up to three times a day for a few days; mirtazapine 15 mg at night is an alternative","Check TFT and a urine drug screen to exclude other causes"],"psych":["Explain akathisia clearly so he understands the anger came from the drug","Refer to NHS Talking Therapies for CBT for panic or generalised anxiety, around 8-14 weekly sessions","Guided self-help, breathing and grounding techniques to use while waiting for therapy"],"social":["Letter to the GP today explaining the reaction and recommending no antipsychotics for anxiety","Fit note for a few days off work if needed; discuss workplace stress and employer support","Avoid driving until the restlessness has gone"],"safety":["Return to A&E or call 111 option 2 if restlessness worsens or he has thoughts of harming himself or others"]}},{"sid":"S15.3","cid":"c-u-64","area":"Mania and bipolar","title":"Thyroid check on lithium","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Gareth Pugh is a 52-year-old man with bipolar disorder who takes lithium carbonate 1000 mg at night. His GP has written to say recent bloods show low T3 and T4 with a raised TSH. He attends the outpatient clinic today. Please ask about symptoms of thyroid problems, explain what the blood results mean, and carry out a physical assessment of his thyroid and related signs. Please do not take a history of his bipolar disorder.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Gareth Pugh?","p":"That's right."},{"d":"Thanks for coming, Gareth. Your GP sent us some blood results about your thyroid, so I'd like to ask a few questions and then check your neck. Is that okay?","p":"Sure. Is it the lithium?"}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, reason, consent, chaperone offered"},{"h":"Low thyroid symptoms","b":"Tiredness, weight gain, cold intolerance, constipation, dry skin, hair, low mood"},{"h":"Other points","b":"Neck swelling, swallowing, voice change, family thyroid disease, other medicines"},{"h":"Explain results","b":"Low T3 and T4, high TSH: underactive thyroid, common on lithium"},{"h":"General inspection","b":"Weight, skin, hair, puffy face, pulse rate and rhythm"},{"h":"Neck","b":"Look, swallow water, tongue out, palpate from behind, nodes, bruit"},{"h":"Eyes and reflexes","b":"Lid lag, eye movements, slow-relaxing ankle reflexes, hands"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Findings, plan, teach-back, lithium level, GP letter"}],"bps":{"pre":"Gareth described months of tiredness, feeling the cold and gaining 5 kg, and his skin is dry. On checking you found a slow pulse of 56, a small smooth goitre and slow-relaxing ankle reflexes. You explained that lithium has caused an underactive thyroid, and that this is common and treatable.","ask":"Do I have to stop the lithium? What happens now?","lead":"Usually not: we treat the thyroid and keep the lithium that has kept you well.","bio":["Continue lithium; GP starts levothyroxine, usually 50-100 micrograms each morning (25-50 micrograms if older or heart disease), recheck TSH every 6-8 weeks until normal","Check lithium level 12 hours after the dose (aim 0.6-0.8 mmol/L), U&E, eGFR and calcium now","Thyroid antibodies (TPO); thyroid ultrasound if the goitre is large, nodular or growing","Then TFTs, U&E and lithium level every 3-6 months; weight checked at each review"],"psych":["Explain that an underactive thyroid can lower mood and cause tiredness, so treating it may help his mood too","Mood diary over the next 3 months to watch for depressive dips while the thyroid settles","Refresh lithium education: toxicity signs, fluids, avoiding NSAIDs"],"social":["Letter to GP with the plan; update his lithium record book","Shared care agreement with the GP for lithium and thyroid monitoring","Advise his employer only if tiredness affects work; fit note not usually needed"],"safety":["Seek urgent help for vomiting, diarrhoea, coarse tremor, unsteadiness or confusion (possible lithium toxicity)"]}},{"sid":"S15.4","cid":"c-t-69","area":"Physical examination","title":"Arm numbness: neurological check","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Tomasz Nowak is a 45-year-old man who came onto the ward as an informal patient this morning with depression and somatisation disorder. For the past four weeks he has complained of numbness and pins and needles in his right arm. The ward team want to be sure there is no neurological cause. Please do not take a history. Please carry out a sensory and motor physical assessment of his upper limbs, comparing both sides, and report whether there is any neurological deficit.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Tomasz Nowak?","p":"Yes."},{"d":"I hear your right arm has felt numb and tingly. I'd like to check the nerves and muscles in both arms. It takes about ten minutes. Is that alright, and would you like a chaperone?","p":"That's fine."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent, chaperone, expose both arms, any pain"},{"h":"Inspection","b":"Posture, wasting, fasciculation, scars, tremor, skin changes"},{"h":"Tone","b":"Wrist and elbow, both sides, rigidity, spasticity"},{"h":"Power","b":"Shoulder, elbow, wrist, fingers, thumb; MRC grade 0-5"},{"h":"Reflexes","b":"Biceps, triceps, supinator; reinforcement; Hoffmann's sign"},{"h":"Coordination","b":"Finger-nose, rapid alternating movements, pronator drift"},{"h":"Sensation","b":"Light touch, pin prick, vibration, joint position; dermatomes C5-T1"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Thank, cover up, findings, further tests, next steps"}],"bps":null},{"sid":"S15.5","cid":"c-u-65","area":"Physical examination","title":"Leg weakness: neurological check","who":"Patient","task":"Mrs Joanne Fletcher is a 48-year-old woman admitted to the psychiatric ward last week with a depressive episode. She drinks heavily at weekends. Over the past three weeks she has told nurses that both legs feel heavy and numb, and she has tripped twice on the ward. The ward team want to exclude a neurological cause such as a peripheral neuropathy or spinal problem. Please do not take a history. Please carry out a sensory and motor physical assessment of her lower limbs and say whether there is any neurological deficit.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Joanne Fletcher?","p":"Yes, Jo."},{"d":"Thanks, Jo. The nurses say your legs feel heavy and numb. I'd like to check the nerves and muscles in both legs. Could you change into shorts, and would you like a chaperone?","p":"Okay, yes please."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent, chaperone, expose legs, any pain"},{"h":"General look","b":"Nails, skin, hair loss, pulses, joint deformity, posture"},{"h":"Inspection","b":"Wasting, fasciculation, foot shape, scars, ulcers"},{"h":"Tone","b":"Leg roll, knee lift, ankle clonus"},{"h":"Power","b":"Hip, knee, ankle, big toe; MRC grade 0-5"},{"h":"Reflexes","b":"Knee, ankle, plantar response; reinforcement"},{"h":"Coordination and gait","b":"Heel-shin, normal walk, heel-toe, Romberg's test"},{"h":"Sensation","b":"Light touch, pin prick, vibration at big toe, joint position; dermatomes L2-S1"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Thank, cover, findings, bloods, nerve tests, next steps"}],"bps":null},{"sid":"S15.6","cid":"c-s-52","area":"Physical examination","title":"Unsteady after alcohol: cerebellar check","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Sean Gallagher is a 43-year-old divorced man with a long history of severe alcohol dependence. He has been abstinent for two months, but he feels unsteady when walking and clumsy with his hands. A recent CT head scan showed cerebellar atrophy. He is being seen in the addictions clinic today. Please carry out a physical assessment looking for signs of cerebellar dysfunction and explain your findings.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Sean Gallagher?","p":"Aye, that's me."},{"d":"Two months alcohol-free is a real achievement, Sean. I gather your balance and hands aren't right. May I check your coordination, eyes, speech and walking?","p":"Go ahead."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, praise abstinence, consent, chaperone"},{"h":"General look","b":"Signs of liver disease, tremor, posture, sitting balance"},{"h":"Speech","b":"Slurred or scanning speech; 'British constitution'"},{"h":"Eyes","b":"Nystagmus, jerky pursuit, overshooting saccades"},{"h":"Arms","b":"Finger-nose with past-pointing and intention tremor, rapid alternating movements, rebound"},{"h":"Legs","b":"Heel-shin test, tone, reflexes, pendular knee jerk"},{"h":"Gait","b":"Wide-based gait, heel-toe walk, Romberg's test"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Thank, findings, link to alcohol, falls advice, next steps"}],"bps":null},{"sid":"S15.7","cid":"c-s-27","area":"Physical examination","title":"Parkinsonism check for Lewy body dementia","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Arthur Pemberton is a 78-year-old retired engineer referred to the memory clinic. Over six months he has become forgetful, has seen his late wife and brother sitting in the lounge, and has fallen several times. He scored 18/30 on the MoCA. The team suspect dementia with Lewy bodies. Please carry out a physical assessment looking for signs of parkinsonism to support or rule out this diagnosis, and explain what you are looking for.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Arthur Pemberton?","p":"I am."},{"d":"Thank you, Arthur. I'd like to check how your muscles and movements are working, including your walking. Would that be alright, and would you like your daughter to stay?","p":"Yes, she can stay."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent, chaperone or relative, pain, falls safety"},{"h":"General look","b":"Facial expression, blink rate, posture, tremor at rest"},{"h":"Tone","b":"Cogwheel or lead-pipe rigidity at wrists and elbows, with activation"},{"h":"Slowness","b":"Finger tapping, hand opening, foot tapping; decrement in speed"},{"h":"Gait and balance","b":"Start, stride, arm swing, turning, pull test"},{"h":"Other signs","b":"Glabellar tap, speech, handwriting, lying and standing BP"},{"h":"Explain findings","b":"Parkinsonism with hallucinations and fluctuations supports Lewy body dementia"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Thank, findings, avoid antipsychotics, DaT scan, next steps"}],"bps":null},{"sid":"S15.8","cid":"c-s-21","area":"Physical examination","title":"Liver check in alcohol dependence","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Kevin Marsh is a 54-year-old man, alcohol dependent, admitted to the psychiatric ward because of suicidal thoughts. He has high tolerance and gets withdrawal symptoms if he does not drink. He is worried he may have damaged his liver further and wants his health checked. Please carry out an abdominal physical assessment, including signs of hepatic encephalopathy, and explain what you find. Rectal and genital checks are not needed.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Kevin Marsh?","p":"Yes."},{"d":"You asked us to check your liver, Kevin, which is a good idea. I'd like to look at your hands, face and tummy and feel your abdomen. Is that okay, and would you like a chaperone?","p":"Yes, fine."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent, chaperone, lie flat, expose abdomen, pain"},{"h":"Hands and arms","b":"Clubbing, leuconychia, palmar erythema, Dupuytren's, flapping tremor, bruising"},{"h":"Face and chest","b":"Jaundice, parotid swelling, spider naevi, gynaecomastia, hair loss"},{"h":"Encephalopathy signs","b":"Asterixis, confusion, orientation, sweet breath, drowsiness"},{"h":"Inspect abdomen","b":"Distension, caput medusae, scars, hernias"},{"h":"Palpate and percuss","b":"Tenderness, liver edge, spleen, shifting dullness for ascites"},{"h":"Finish","b":"Leg oedema, bowel sounds, BP, temperature, bedside glucose"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Thank, findings, LFTs, clotting, ultrasound, FibroScan, next steps"}],"bps":null},{"sid":"S15.9","cid":"c-t-70","area":"Physical examination","title":"Heart and vessels in memory decline","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Desmond Clarke is a 57-year-old man admitted to the psychiatric ward with worsening cognitive difficulties. He has had type 2 diabetes for five years and has fallen several times recently. Please carry out a cardiovascular physical assessment, looking especially for signs that would support vascular dementia, and talk the medical student shadowing you through what you are looking for and why.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Desmond Clarke?","p":"Des, yes."},{"d":"Thanks, Des. I'd like to check your heart, pulse and blood vessels, as they can affect memory. A student is with me today. May we go ahead, and would you like a chaperone?","p":"That's fine."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent, chaperone, position 45 degrees, expose chest"},{"h":"General look","b":"Breathlessness, xanthelasma, corneal arcus, neglect, insulin sites"},{"h":"Hands and pulse","b":"Nicotine staining, radial pulse rate and rhythm, AF, radio-radial delay"},{"h":"Blood pressure","b":"Both arms, lying and standing for postural drop and falls"},{"h":"Neck","b":"JVP, carotid pulses, carotid bruits"},{"h":"Heart","b":"Apex beat, heaves, heart sounds, murmurs, especially aortic stenosis"},{"h":"Lungs and legs","b":"Basal crackles, ankle oedema, foot pulses, diabetic foot changes"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Thank, findings, ECG, lipids, HbA1c, MRI, next steps"}],"bps":null},{"sid":"S15.10","cid":"c-s-27","area":"Physical examination","title":"Looking for parkinsonism in hallucinations","who":"Patient","task":"Mr Raymond Ellis is a 78-year-old man admitted to the older adults' ward. His family describe six months of poor memory, frequent falls and vivid sightings of deceased relatives in the house. His MoCA score was 18/30. The consultant is considering dementia with Lewy bodies. Please carry out a focused physical assessment for parkinsonian signs to help confirm or exclude this diagnosis, and tell him what you find.","opening":[{"d":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Raymond Ellis?","p":"Ray. Yes."},{"d":"Thanks, Ray. I'd like to look at your movements, muscles and walking, as they help us understand the memory changes. Is that okay? A nurse will be with us.","p":"Alright, doctor."}],"cover":[{"h":"Opening","b":"Name, consent, nurse chaperone, check pain, walking aid nearby"},{"h":"Observe at rest","b":"Hypomimia, reduced blinking, stooped posture, pill-rolling tremor"},{"h":"Tone","b":"Rigidity at wrist and elbow; worsen by moving other hand"},{"h":"Bradykinesia","b":"Repetitive finger taps and foot taps; slowing and smaller amplitude"},{"h":"Gait","b":"Rising from chair, short steps, reduced arm swing, turning, freezing"},{"h":"Postural and autonomic","b":"Pull test with care, lying-standing BP drop"},{"h":"Explain and close","b":"Parkinsonism supports Lewy body dementia; neuroleptic sensitivity warning"},{"h":"Summary and close","b":"Thank, findings, DaT scan, team and family informed"}],"bps":null}];
